= Classical mechanics
{wiki}

<Classical mechanics> describes the motion of bodies by assigning <positions> and <velocities> and specifying how their interactions change those quantities. A <point mass> is the simplest model: its size and internal structure are ignored, leaving a <mass> and a moving <position>. A <rigid body> retains orientation and <rotational kinetic energy>. Choosing a model means deciding which features affect the question being asked; a sliding block may be treated as a <point mass>, whereas predicting its <rolling without slipping> requires its <moment of inertia>.

Three complementary questions organize the subject. <Newton's second law> asks how the net <force> determines the instantaneous <acceleration>. The <work-energy theorem> asks how that <force>, acting through a <displacement>, changes <kinetic energy>. <Conservation of mechanical energy> asks which combinations of <position> and <velocity> are accessible when the interactions admit a time-independent <potential energy> and no other <force> supplies net <power>. These descriptions are compatible, but answer different questions: an <energy> calculation may find a maximum <speed> without first solving for <position> at every <time>.

The <harmonic oscillator> below makes this unity visible. A restoring <force> produces a repeating <position> trace; the corresponding <phase plane> has a closed <ellipse>; <kinetic energy> and <potential energy> exchange while their sum stays fixed. <Lagrangian mechanics> and <Hamiltonian mechanics> provide further ways to organize the same dynamics, especially when coordinates or constraints make individual <forces> awkward to track.

The scope of <classical mechanics> also matters. <Newtonian mechanics> approximates motion at <speeds> small compared with the <speed of light>; <special relativity> changes that description when this approximation fails. <Quantum mechanics> is needed for phenomena for which a classical assignment of simultaneous <position> and <momentum> is inadequate. These limitations do not prevent <classical mechanics> from being a useful predictive model within its regime. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-01sc-classical-mechanics-fall-2016/mit8_01scs22_chapter1.pdf[MIT mechanics, Chapter 1: scope and limitations]{ref}

= Variable-mass system
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{title2=$m\dot{\mathbf v}=\mathbf F+(\mathbf u-\mathbf v)\dot m$}
{wiki}

A body exchanging <mass> with material of inertial <velocity> $\mathbf u$ satisfies the displayed <momentum conservation> law for one stream, with signed $\dot m$ positive for entry and negative for exit. The <force> $\mathbf F$ excludes the incoming or outgoing momentum flux. A short time interval gives $d(m\mathbf v)/dt=\mathbf F+\mathbf u\dot m$, proving the formula. For stationary incoming matter, $d(m\mathbf v)/dt=\mathbf F$; for matter leaving at the body's own <velocity>, $m\dot{\mathbf v}=\mathbf F$. The <rocket equation> and accreting-drop motion are applications.

= Accreting raindrop with quadratic drag
{parent=Variable-mass system}
{title2=$\dot a=cv/4,\quad\dot v=g-3(c+k)v^2/(4a)$}

For a spherical drop of constant <mass density> $\rho$, radius $a>0$ and downward <velocity> $v>0$, suppose its volume grows at rate $c\pi a^2v$ by collecting stationary mist and its upward <quadratic drag> is $k\rho\pi a^2v^2$, with $c,k>0$. The drop <mass> is $4\pi\rho a^3/3$. Its incoming <mass accretion rate> and the <variable-mass system> momentum law give the displayed equations. The accreted matter must be accelerated from rest, producing the additional $c$ term alongside drag.

= Speed-radius attractor of an accreting raindrop
{parent=Accreting raindrop with quadratic drag}
{title2=$v^2/a\longrightarrow8g/(7c+6k),\quad\dot v\longrightarrow cg/(7c+6k)$}

For the <accreting raindrop with quadratic drag>, set $q=v^2/a$ and $B=7c/8+3k/4$. Its exact equation is $\dot q=2(v/a)(g-Bq)$. Dividing by $\dot a=cv/4$ gives $q(a)=g/B+(q(a_0)-g/B)(a_0/a)^{7+6k/c}$. Thus $q$ stays between its positive initial value and positive equilibrium. Since $d\sqrt a/dt=c\sqrt q/8$, the radius grows without finite-time divergence and tends to infinity. The power correction then vanishes, proving both limits. Positive drag makes the limiting <acceleration> strictly less than $g/7$.

= Inertia
{parent=Classical mechanics}

<Inertia> is the resistance of a body's motion to change under applied force. For a particle, <Newton's second law> relates force to the rate of change of momentum; at constant mass it is $F=ma$. In an overdamped approximation the inertial acceleration term is small compared with drag over the time scales considered.

= Mechanical configuration space
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{title2=$Q$}

= Configuration space
{synonym}

The <configuration space> of a mechanical system is the space of its allowed positions or generalized coordinates. Its tangent bundle describes configurations and velocities, and its cotangent bundle describes configurations and conjugate momenta. For smooth holonomic constraints it is a manifold on which geometric Lagrangian and Hamiltonian descriptions can be built. Singular constraints or symmetry quotients can require strata rather than one global smooth manifold.

= Relational shape space
{parent=Classical mechanics}

The <relational shape space> of a particle configuration is obtained by removing collective translations and rotations; here scale is retained. For generic collision-free, noncollinear configurations of $N\ge3$ labelled particles in three dimensions, the resulting configuration dimension is $3N-6$. Collinear configurations have nontrivial rotational stabilizers and require singular strata. Removing position and orientation does not automatically remove conserved angular momentum or its centrifugal effect; a dynamically faithful reduction must retain the appropriate momentum-level data.

= Simple pendulum
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{title2=$\ddot\theta+(g/\ell)\sin\theta=0$}

A point mass constrained to a fixed-length massless arm under uniform gravitational <acceleration> is a simple pendulum. With no drag its angular equation is $\ddot\theta+(g/\ell)\sin\theta=0$. The <small-angle approximation> yields <simple harmonic motion>, whereas the full restoring force is nonlinear. Adding drag gives a <damped pendulum>.

For an ideal point mass $m$ swinging in a vertical plane, the <torque> about the pivot is $-mg\ell\sin\theta$. Dividing by its <moment of inertia> $m\ell^2$ gives the displayed equation. Both the inertia and the gravitational <torque> scale with $m$, explaining why the point mass cancels. This derivation and the small-amplitude period are given in https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/15-4-pendulums[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics, pendulums].

Released from rest at a small angular <amplitude> $\theta_0$, the linearized solution is $\theta(t)\simeq\theta_0\cos(\sqrt{g/\ell}\,t)$, with <oscillation period> $T_0=2\pi\sqrt{\ell/g}$. For $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$, lengths one and four metres give $T_0\approx2.0061\,\mathrm s$ and $4.0121\,\mathrm s$. This same scaling follows from <dimensional analysis>; the dynamical calculation supplies the coefficient $2\pi$.

Finite angular <amplitude> changes the exact ideal period. <Conservation of energy> gives $\dot\theta^2=(2g/\ell)(\cos\theta-\cos\theta_0)$ for release from rest. Integrating a quarter oscillation and substituting $\sin(\theta/2)=\sin(\theta_0/2)\sin\phi$ gives
$$
T(\theta_0)=4\sqrt{\frac\ell g}\,K\!\left(\sin\frac{\theta_0}2\right),\qquad
\frac{T(\theta_0)}{T_0}=\frac2\pi\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{d\phi}{\sqrt{1-\sin^2(\theta_0/2)\sin^2\phi}},\quad 0<\theta_0<\pi.
$$
Here $K$ is the <complete elliptic integral of the first kind>. The <small-angle approximation> gives a ratio tending to one; at $10^\circ,60^\circ,90^\circ$, the ratios are approximately $1.00191,1.07318,1.18034$. Thus almost constant period at small <amplitude> has a quantitative limit. As $\theta_0\to\pi$, the period diverges, connecting to the <pendulum separatrix approach takes infinite time>.

\Image[pendulum-period-scaling.png]
{title=Length scaling and finite-amplitude period corrections for an ideal simple pendulum}

These predictions assume the specified ideal system. Drag changes the <mechanical energy>; a massive support changes the <moment of inertia>. The distinction is useful in experiments: a change in period can test an approximation rather than merely indicate an arithmetic mistake.

= Conical pendulum
{parent=Simple pendulum}
{wiki}

A <conical pendulum> is a pendulum whose bob executes uniform horizontal <circular motion> beneath a fixed pivot, with its string at a constant <angle> $\theta$ to the downward vertical. The string sweeps a <right circular conical surface>; the bob follows its circular rim, without any physical cone supporting it. The ideal model has a <point mass> $m>0$, a taut, massless, inextensible string of length $\ell>0$, uniform <gravitational acceleration> of magnitude $g>0$, and negligible <drag force> and pivot <friction>. A nonzero circular motion requires $0<\theta<\pi/2$. This geometry and the corresponding <forces> are described in https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/301/lectures/node88.html[Richard Fitzpatrick, The conical pendulum, equations 262–267].

\Image[conical-pendulum-geometry.png]
{title=Conical pendulum geometry, horizontal orbit, and the two forces on the bob}

The <right triangle> formed by the pivot, the orbit centre, and the bob gives $r=\ell\sin\theta$ and $h=\ell\cos\theta$. Here $r$ is the orbit radius and $h$ is the vertical drop from the pivot to the bob's horizontal plane, not the bob's height above a table. Write $T$ for <tension>, $P$ for the <oscillation period> of one revolution, and $\omega=2\pi/P$ for the <angular speed>; then the bob's <speed> is $v=\omega r$. A side view compresses the horizontal circle to a line segment; the top view reveals the circle and its tangent <velocity>.

The two actual <forces> on the bob are <tension> toward the pivot and <gravitational weight> vertically downward. The vertical <acceleration> is zero, while the horizontal <centripetal acceleration> is $v^2/r=\omega^2r$. Thus <Newton's second law> gives
$$
T\cos\theta=mg,\qquad T\sin\theta=m\omega^2r=\frac{mv^2}{r}.
$$
The inward net <force> is the horizontal component of <tension>, rather than an extra interaction to add to these two <forces>. Constant <speed> still requires <acceleration> because the <velocity> changes direction. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-3-centripetal-force[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics 1, §6.3: centripetal acceleration and net force]{ref}

Dividing the two component equations and using $r=\ell\sin\theta$ gives the useful collection of predictions
$$
\omega^2\cos\theta=\frac g\ell,\qquad T=\frac{mg}{\cos\theta},\qquad v^2=g\ell\frac{\sin^2\theta}{\cos\theta},\qquad P=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{\ell\cos\theta}{g}}=2\pi\sqrt{\frac hg}.
$$
If $\phi$ is the azimuthal <angle>, then $\dot\phi=\pm\omega$; either direction of rotation satisfies $\dot\phi^2\cos\theta=g/\ell$. The <mass> cancels from the <angular speed> and <oscillation period>, but not from the <tension>. The motion is a <relative equilibrium>: in a <rotating frame> following the bob at <angular speed> $\omega$, its <position> is fixed. This is a concrete way to see how rotational <symmetry> can turn an apparently moving solution into a stationary one.

= Rotating bob experiment for a conical pendulum
{parent=Conical pendulum}

The motivating observation is a bob going around at almost constant height while its string traces a cone. The University of Iowa's demonstration places a camera above a short pendulum to reveal this motion. https://instructional-resources.physics.uiowa.edu/1d5025-conical-pendulum[University of Iowa, demonstration 1D50.25: short pendulum and overhead camera]{ref} The following proposed measurement procedure develops that observation into a test of the <conical pendulum> model; it is not a report of collected data.

Suspend a compact bob from a rigid, fixed support using a light string, and measure $\ell$ from the pivot to the bob's centre. Put a stationary marker beside the orbit as a timing reference. Displace the bob sideways and give it a horizontal push tangent to the intended circle, then let go. For a chosen starting <angle> $\theta$, the predicted initial <speed> is $v=\sqrt{g\ell\sin^2\theta/\cos\theta}$. Adjust the launch between trials until a short observation interval has nearly constant orbit radius and height. Keep the pivot fixed while recording: circling the support by hand changes the problem, and a maintained drive would introduce another <force>.

Record from above with the camera looking approximately along the vertical axis. Check that the bob's centre follows a circle and that successive returns to the marker take nearly equal times. A separate side recording checks whether the bob's height stays constant. A markedly elliptical orbit or visibly rising and falling bob is not the uniform <circular motion> assumed by the <conical pendulum> equations. Measure the orbit diameter with a scale at the orbit's height; in a level side view, use the two extreme horizontal positions, not one arbitrary frame. Halve the diameter to obtain $r$, or measure the vertical drop $h$ directly in a calibrated side view. An oblique camera view and a scale at a different depth distort apparent lengths, so arrange the geometry before interpreting the images.

Time $N$ complete returns to the same marker in the same direction and estimate $P=\Delta t/N$. Counting ten revolutions, when the orbit stays sufficiently steady, spreads an endpoint timing error over ten periods. Repeat the timing and compare the first and last few revolutions to detect drift; using a longer interval helps only if the motion remains close to uniform. From independently measured geometry, compute $\sin\theta=r/\ell$ or $\cos\theta=h/\ell$, and check $r^2+h^2\simeq\ell^2$ if both were measured. Then compare the measured <oscillation period> with $P^2=4\pi^2h/g$. MIT's 8.01X experimental assignment explicitly asks students to compare the <conical pendulum>'s period, length, and orbit radius with theory. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-01x-physics-i-classical-mechanics-with-an-experimental-focus-fall-2002/a056f6a3e071767d4f7620bca6a59d0c_expt_vs_problem.pdf[MIT 8.01X, Fall 2002, Vibrating Systems experimental problem, question 8]{ref}

Repeat at several launch radii with the same $\ell$. Larger $r$ means smaller $h=\sqrt{\ell^2-r^2}$, so the ideal <oscillation period> decreases. A plot of measured $P^2$ against independently measured $h$ should follow a straight line through the origin with slope $4\pi^2/g$; conversely, $g=4\pi^2h/P^2$ estimates <gravitational acceleration>. Do not infer $h$ from the timing equation and then use that same equation as a test. Compare discrepancies with the spread of repeated timings and the resolution of the length measurements. String stretch, a moving pivot, and appreciable bob size challenge the stated geometry; <drag force> and pivot <friction> challenge the assumption of undriven uniform motion. Observe a short interval and record any shrinking or drifting orbit rather than silently treating it as an exact <conical pendulum>.

For an illustrative calculation, assume $\ell=1.00\,\mathrm m$, $m=0.050\,\mathrm{kg}$, $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$, and $\theta=40^\circ$. The model gives $r\approx0.643\,\mathrm m$, $h\approx0.766\,\mathrm m$, $P\approx1.756\,\mathrm s$, $v\approx2.300\,\mathrm{m/s}$, and $T\approx0.640\,\mathrm N$. Its vertical <tension> component is $0.4905\,\mathrm N$, equal to the <gravitational weight>; its inward component is approximately $0.412\,\mathrm N$. These are predictions from chosen parameters, not measured data or measurements of local $g$. The next figure uses the same stipulated $\ell$ and $g$, including illustrative <angles> $15^\circ$, $40^\circ$, and $60^\circ$.

= Period and small-angle limit of a conical pendulum
{parent=Conical pendulum}
{title2=$P/P_0=\sqrt{\cos\theta},\quad P_0=2\pi\sqrt{\ell/g}$}

\Image[conical-pendulum-period.png]
{title=Ideal conical pendulum period decreases with cone angle and its square is proportional to the vertical drop}

For fixed $\ell$ and $g$, the exact <conical pendulum> <oscillation period> satisfies $P/P_0=\sqrt{\cos\theta}$. In the <small-angle approximation>, with $\theta$ in <radians>,
$$
\frac{P}{P_0}=1-\frac{\theta^2}{4}+O(\theta^4),\qquad P_0=2\pi\sqrt{\frac\ell g}.
$$
Thus the limiting revolution period equals the small-amplitude <oscillation period> of a planar <simple pendulum>. This equality has a geometric explanation: to first order, $x(t)=r\cos(\omega t)$ and $y(t)=r\sin(\omega t)$ are perpendicular <simple harmonic motions> of equal <amplitude>, a quarter period apart, with $\omega\to\sqrt{g/\ell}$. At finite <angle>, however, the <conical pendulum>'s <oscillation period> decreases with $\theta$, whereas a planar <simple pendulum>'s <oscillation period> increases with its angular <amplitude>.

The endpoint $\theta=0$ itself is a stationary bob with $r=0$ and no observable revolution; $P_0$ is a limit of nonzero circular motions, not a measured revolution period at rest. For fixed $\ell$, nonzero <conical pendulum> motion also requires $\omega>\sqrt{g/\ell}$, because $\cos\theta=g/(\ell\omega^2)<1$. At the other endpoint, $\theta\to\pi/2$ gives $h\to0$, $P\to0$, and unbounded <tension> and <speed>. These mathematical limits explain why the ideal string cannot sustain a horizontal orbit at finite <tension>; a real experiment ceases to satisfy the ideal assumptions before that limit.

= Damped pendulum
{parent=Simple pendulum}
{title2=$\ddot\theta+c\dot\theta+\sin\theta=0$}

A <damped pendulum> is a <simple pendulum> whose <mechanical energy> is removed by a resisting <torque>. Here the model is undriven, with a fixed pivot, a <point mass> $m>0$ at distance $\ell>0$, uniform <gravitational acceleration> $g>0$, and resisting <torque> $-b\,d\theta/dt$ with constant $b>0$. The <angle> $\theta$ is measured from the downward vertical in <radians>. An ideal viscous rotary <dashpot> realizes this torque law; tangential <linear drag> $-\alpha v$ on the bob gives the same equation with $b=\alpha\ell^2$. The <moment of inertia> is $m\ell^2$, so
$$
m\ell^2\frac{d^2\theta}{dt^2}+b\frac{d\theta}{dt}+mg\ell\sin\theta=0,\qquad
\omega_0=\sqrt{\frac g\ell},\quad \gamma=\frac{b}{m\ell^2},\quad \tau=\omega_0t,\quad c=\frac\gamma{\omega_0}>0.
$$
Here $t$ is physical <time>, $\omega_0$ is the undamped small-amplitude <angular frequency>, and $\gamma$ has units of inverse <time>. In the normalized equation $\ddot\theta+c\dot\theta+\sin\theta=0$, dots mean differentiation with respect to $\tau$, not $t$. The parameter $c$ is dimensionless. This separates the chosen damping law from the <small-angle approximation>: retaining $\sin\theta$ allows finite <angles> even when the resisting <torque> is linear in <angular velocity>.

\Image[damped-pendulum-decay-observation.png]
{title=Proposed viscous-damper experiment and illustrative small-angle decay: angle versus time and successive peak ratios, not measured data}

To motivate the model, release a pendulum without a push and record how its swing shrinks. A published example by Gintautas and Hubler uses a rotary sensor built from a computer mouse to measure the frequency and damping coefficient of a <physical pendulum>. https://arxiv.org/abs/0901.4319[Gintautas and Hubler, A simple, low-cost, data-logging pendulum built from a computer mouse, 2009]{ref} The following is a proposed experiment with a chosen viscous rotary <dashpot>; the plotted observations are predictions, not a reproduction of their measurements.

Mount a compact bob on a light rigid rod with a fixed pivot, couple the pivot to an adjustable viscous rotary <dashpot>, and measure $\ell$ from the pivot to the bob's centre. First record decay with the <dashpot> disconnected, then repeat with it connected at several settings; this establishes how much decay is already due to other losses. Keep the bob, rod, camera, and release <angle> unchanged between these trials. The simple model requires rod and damper-rotor <moments of inertia> to be negligible compared with $m\ell^2$. Otherwise measure the total <moment of inertia> $I$ and the distance $d$ to the assembly's centre of mass: the <physical pendulum> equation becomes $I\,d^2\theta/dt^2+b\,d\theta/dt+Mgd\sin\theta=0$, where $M$ is the total moving <mass>. A damper that also exerts an elastic restoring <torque> would require another term.

Place a camera perpendicular to the swing plane, include the pivot and bob centre in every frame, and determine their coordinates relative to the resting vertical. For horizontal and downward separations $x,y$, use $\theta=\operatorname{atan2}(x,y)$; record the frame timestamps rather than assuming a frame rate. Release from rest at about $10^\circ$, record through several complete swings, and repeat the release. For every same-side turning point, tabulate its <time> $t_n$ and angular <amplitude> $A_n=|\theta(t_n)|$. Measure the <oscillation period> from differences $t_{n+1}-t_n$; comparing several periods exposes drift. Interpolating around each observed extremum reduces frame discretization error. Check that late peaks still exceed the angular resolution; taking the <natural logarithm> of tiny, uncertain peaks amplifies their relative errors.

In the <small-angle approximation>, the <damped pendulum> becomes a <damped harmonic oscillator>. For $0<\gamma<2\omega_0$ and release from rest at $\theta_0$, its exact linear-model solution is
$$
\theta_{\mathrm{lin}}(t)=\theta_0e^{-\gamma t/2}\left(\cos\omega_dt+\frac{\gamma}{2\omega_d}\sin\omega_dt\right),\qquad
\omega_d=\sqrt{\omega_0^2-\frac{\gamma^2}{4}},\qquad T_d=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_d}.
$$
The decaying sinusoidal envelope has coefficient $|\theta_0|\omega_0/\omega_d$; because release is from rest, its successive same-side extrema occur at $t_n=nT_d$ and have $A_n=|\theta_0|e^{-\gamma nT_d/2}$. Thus the envelope and the sequence of actual peak heights are different descriptions of the same trace. The linear damping regimes and this solution are derived in https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/336k/Newton/node19.html[Richard Fitzpatrick, Damped Oscillatory Motion, equations 82–93].

The experiment tests two predictions independently: nearly equal same-side peak spacing and a straight line when plotting $\ln(A_n/A_0)$ against $t_n-t_0$. Its predicted slope is $-\gamma/2$, giving
$$
\gamma=\frac{2}{t_{n+1}-t_n}\ln\frac{A_n}{A_{n+1}},\qquad b=m\ell^2\gamma,\qquad
\omega_d^2+\frac{\gamma^2}{4}=\frac g\ell.
$$
Use several peaks and repeated trials rather than a single ratio, and compare the frequency identity with independently measured $\ell$ and $g$. For the illustrative choices $\ell=1.00\,\mathrm m$, $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$, $c=0.20$, and $\theta_0=10^\circ$, the linear model gives $\gamma\approx0.6264\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, $T_d\approx2.0162\,\mathrm s$, and $A_{n+1}/A_n\approx0.5318$. The first two positive peaks after release are therefore approximately $5.318^\circ$ and $2.828^\circ$. The undamped small-amplitude <oscillation period> is about $2.0061\,\mathrm s$: substantial loss per swing need not imply a large change in period.

Repeat at a larger release <angle> to distinguish two limitations. The relative error in replacing $\sin\theta$ by $\theta$ is about $\theta^2/6$ for small <angles>: about $0.51\%$ at $10^\circ$, but about $17.3\%$ at $60^\circ$ when compared with $\theta$. Finite-amplitude swing spacing can therefore change as the swing shrinks even if $b$ is constant. Separately, <friction> at the pivot or <quadratic drag> can invalidate the assumed linear resisting <torque>; exponential peak decay should be checked, not imposed on all recorded motion. Peters discusses why real pendulum damping can require nonlinear terms. https://arxiv.org/abs/physics/0306081[Randall D. Peters, Nonlinear Damping of the 'Linear' Pendulum, 2003]{ref} Repeatability, residual pivot motion, changes in damper <temperature>, and finite camera resolution are useful checks on this proposed measurement.

The normalized <equilibrium points> are $(\theta,\dot\theta)=(k\pi,0)$. Even multiples of $\pi$ are attracting: their <linearization> has <eigenvalues> $(-c\pm\sqrt{c^2-4})/2$, giving a <stable focus> for $0<c<2$, repeated negative <eigenvalues> at $c=2$, and a <stable node> for $c>2$. These are the underdamped, critically damped, and overdamped small-displacement regimes. Odd multiples of $\pi$ have <eigenvalues> $(-c\pm\sqrt{c^2+4})/2$ of opposite signs and are <saddle equilibria>. The <energy dissipation of a damped pendulum> explains why trajectories in the <phase portrait> spiral inward near a <stable focus> instead of closing into the recurring orbit of an undamped <simple pendulum>.

= Energy dissipation of a damped pendulum
{parent=Damped pendulum}
{title2=$\dot E=-c\dot\theta^2$}

For the normalized <damped pendulum>, the sum of <kinetic energy> and gravitational <potential energy>, divided by $mg\ell$, is
$$
E(\tau)=\frac12\dot\theta^2+1-\cos\theta,\qquad
\dot E=\dot\theta(\ddot\theta+\sin\theta)=-c\dot\theta^2\leq0.
$$
The <chain rule> and the full nonlinear equation give this identity exactly, without a <small-angle approximation>. Here $\dot\theta=d\theta/d\tau$ as in <damped pendulum>. In physical units the <mechanical energy> and the <power> of the resisting <torque> are
$$
\mathcal E(t)=\frac12m\ell^2\left(\frac{d\theta}{dt}\right)^2+mg\ell(1-\cos\theta),\qquad
\frac{d\mathcal E}{dt}=-b\left(\frac{d\theta}{dt}\right)^2.
$$
The pendulum's lost <mechanical energy> is transferred to the damper and surroundings; it does not violate <conservation of energy>. In an ideal viscous <dashpot> this loss is <frictional heating>. The exact cumulative balance is particularly useful for checking a numerical solution:
$$
Q(\tau)=c\int_0^\tau\dot\theta(s)^2\,ds,\qquad E(\tau)+Q(\tau)=E(0).
$$

\Image[damped-pendulum-nonlinear-energy.png]
{title=Illustrative nonlinear and linear damped pendulum solutions at 10 and 60 degrees, with exact mechanical energy transfer and loss; no measured data}

The figure uses $c=0.20$ and release from rest. Over $0\leq\tau\leq25$, the largest angular discrepancy between the nonlinear solution and the <damped harmonic oscillator> solution is approximately $0.000702\,\mathrm{rad}$ ($0.0402^\circ$) for a $10^\circ$ release, but $0.14995\,\mathrm{rad}$ ($8.592^\circ$) for a $60^\circ$ release. Their nonlinear initial energies are $1-\cos10^\circ\approx0.015192$ and $1-\cos60^\circ=0.5$, respectively. At $\tau=25$ the remaining fractions are approximately $0.0064080$ and $0.0063757$. These are illustrative calculations from specified initial conditions, not observations. The generator uses a <Runge-Kutta method> and integrates $Q$ alongside the motion; halving its step and independently integrating $c\dot\theta^2$ checks the result.

For $\ell=1\,\mathrm m$ and $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$, the first three nonlinear same-side swing intervals after a $60^\circ$ release are approximately $2.1008$, $2.0378$, and $2.0221\,\mathrm s$. They approach the linear-model <oscillation period> $2.0162\,\mathrm s$ as the <amplitude> shrinks. After a $10^\circ$ release the corresponding intervals are already about $2.0185$, $2.0168$, and $2.0164\,\mathrm s$. This makes the experiment's period drift a quantitative test of the <small-angle approximation>, rather than an automatic reason to change the damping coefficient.

At a turning point, $\dot\theta=0$ and $\dot E=0$ even though the swing is losing <mechanical energy> over the cycle. Its <potential energy> there is $E_n=1-\cos A_n$, so measured turning <angles> give successive energies without estimating <angular velocity>. In the proposed decay experiment, multiply these values by $mg\ell$ to obtain energies in <joules>, then subtract adjacent same-side energies to estimate the loss over each full swing. Only for small <amplitudes> is $E_n\simeq A_n^2/2$. In the linear underdamped model, same-side peak energies consequently satisfy $E_{n+1}/E_n=e^{-\gamma T_d}$, the square of the angular <amplitude> ratio. For the illustrative parameters above this energy ratio is approximately $0.2828$. Between turning points, <kinetic energy> and <potential energy> continue to exchange while their sum decreases; the energy slope vanishes briefly at each extremum and is negative whenever the bob moves.

It is therefore inaccurate to replace the exact identity by $\dot E=-cE$. Even for the linear model, $E_{\mathrm{lin}}=(\dot\theta^2+\theta^2)/2$ has $\dot E_{\mathrm{lin}}=-c\dot\theta^2$. For weak damping and small <angles>, cycle averaging gives $\langle\dot\theta^2\rangle\simeq\langle E_{\mathrm{lin}}\rangle$, so the averaged energy approximately follows $e^{-c\tau}$ while angular <amplitude> follows $e^{-c\tau/2}$. This <exponential decay> is an approximation to the energy trace; the same-side peak ratio is exact within the linear model. The figure distinguishes the actual energy curves from this averaging approximation.

The energy identity also gives a global conclusion without solving the nonlinear equation. If $E$ were constant on a positive-length interval, then $\dot\theta$ would vanish throughout it; the equation would force $\sin\theta=0$, making the entire trajectory an <equilibrium>. Every nonstationary trajectory therefore loses <mechanical energy> over every positive-length interval, even though its instantaneous <derivative> can vanish at a turning point. A nonconstant periodic motion would have to return to its initial <mechanical energy>, so it is impossible for this undriven <damped pendulum>. Oscillations near a <stable focus> are successive, shrinking swings, not a repeating cycle. Moreover, if $E(0)<2$, the <angle> cannot reach an upright <equilibrium>, whose <potential energy> is $2$; such motion stays within its original potential well. This is the usefulness of the energy description: one scalar balance controls both the observed decay and which nonlinear motions remain accessible.

= Eisenhart-Duval lift
{c}
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{title2=$g=d\mathbf x^2+2dt(du-2\mathbf A\cdot d\mathbf x-Udt)$}

= Eisenhart lift
{c}
{synonym}

= Eisenhart-Duval metric lift
{c}
{synonym}

A mechanical Lagrangian quadratic in velocities can be represented by <geodesics> of a <Lorentzian metric> with an extra null coordinate. For $L=\tfrac12|\dot{\mathbf x}|^2-2\mathbf A\cdot\dot{\mathbf x}-U$, use $g=d\mathbf x^2+2dt(du-2\mathbf A\cdot d\mathbf x-Udt)$. The null <Killing vector field> $\partial_u$ gives the conserved momentum $p_u=t\prime$. Fixing $p_u=1$ projects the <geodesic equation> to the mechanical <Euler-Lagrange equations>. This is useful for relating mechanical forces to spacetime geometry.

= Gauge transformations of an Eisenhart-Duval lift
{parent=Eisenhart-Duval lift}
{title2=$\mathbf A\mapsto\mathbf A+\nabla\chi,\quad u\mapsto u+2\chi$}

A time-independent <gauge transformation> $\mathbf A\mapsto\mathbf A+\nabla\chi$ in an <Eisenhart-Duval lift> is the coordinate change $u\mapsto u+2\chi$. It preserves the metric one-form $du-2\mathbf A\cdot d\mathbf x-Udt$ and adds only $-2d\chi/dt$ to the reduced Lagrangian.

= Null Kaluza-Klein reduction of a stationary force
{parent=Eisenhart-Duval lift}
{title2=$p_u=t\prime\ne0$}

For an <Eisenhart-Duval lift>, reduction along the null isometry fixes a nonzero conserved $p_u=t\prime$ and projects <geodesics> to $(t,\mathbf x)$. One never divides by $g_{uu}=0$. When $\mathbf E=-\nabla U$ and $\nabla\times\mathbf A=\mathbf B$, the reduced equation is $\ddot{\mathbf x}=\mathbf E+2\mathbf B\times\dot{\mathbf x}$. Every trajectory admits a null lift by choosing $\dot u=U+2\mathbf A\cdot\dot{\mathbf x}-\tfrac12|\dot{\mathbf x}|^2$.

= Newtonian mechanics
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{c}
{title2=$\mathbf F=m\mathbf a$}

Newtonian mechanics describes motion using forces, masses and acceleration in <inertial frames> with absolute time. It is the low-speed limit of <special relativity>; for constant <proper acceleration> with $|at|\ll c$, relativistic <hyperbolic motion> reduces to $x\simeq at^2/2$.

= Small-slope particle motion on a height graph
{parent=Newtonian mechanics}
{title2=$\ddot{\mathbf r}\simeq-g\nabla h$}

A particle constrained to the graph $z=h(x,y)$ has <kinetic energy> $m[|\dot{\mathbf r}|^2+(\nabla h\cdot\dot{\mathbf r})^2]/2$ and <potential energy> $mgh$. For a gently varying graph and velocities for which its geometric kinetic correction is higher order, the leading <Euler-Lagrange equations> are the displayed planar gradient-force equations. Small slope alone at arbitrarily large speed or curvature does not justify discarding the normal inertial acceleration. For a radial height, the leading planar <angular momentum> is conserved, allowing an <effective potential> reduction.

= Radial turning points for an inverse-square height well
{parent=Small-slope particle motion on a height graph}
{title2=$\dot r^2=2\mathcal E+2g/r-(L^2+2g)/r^2$}

For the height profile $h(r)=r^{-2}-r^{-1}$, set $A=L^2+2g$ and use the planar small-slope model. Its <effective potential> per unit mass is $V_{\rm eff}=A/(2r^2)-g/r$. Release with negligible radial speed at $r_0$ gives $\mathcal E=A/(2r_0^2)-g/r_0$ and the displayed radial-speed law. A bounded trajectory requires $\mathcal E<0$, equivalently $r_0>A/(2g)$. The two positive turning radii are $r_0$ and $Ar_0/(2gr_0-A)$; they coincide at the circular solution $r_0=A/g$. Zero energy is marginal escape, not bounded motion.

= Tangentially driven motion on an axisymmetric surface
{parent=Newtonian mechanics}
{title2=$\dot\phi^2=\frac{g h'(r)}{r+2r_\phi^2/r-r_{\phi\phi}}$}

Neglecting vertical acceleration and the vertical driving-force component, the radial normal reaction is $-Mg h'(r)$. A drive parallel to the horizontal velocity cancels when the radial acceleration equation is combined with $r_\phi/r$ times the azimuthal equation. For a prescribed path $r(\phi)$ this gives the displayed speed constraint. It describes the projected approximation, not arbitrary trajectories with substantial vertical motion.

// Target: classical-mechanics.bigb

= Friction
{parent=Newtonian mechanics}

<Friction> is a contact <force> tangent to the surfaces. <Kinetic friction> opposes <contact slip>; <static friction> maintains contact without <contact slip>, including <rolling without slipping>. The perpendicular contact component is the <normal force>. These two components answer different questions: how strongly the surfaces press together, and how strongly they resist sliding past one another.

The ideal Coulomb dry-contact model combines a bound on <static friction> with a sliding law for <kinetic friction>. Its parameters are the <coefficient of static friction> and the <coefficient of kinetic friction>; constant coefficients are an empirical approximation, rather than a consequence of <Newton's laws of motion>. Surface condition matters, and the static coefficient need not universally exceed the kinetic coefficient. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/I_12.html#Ch12-S2[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, The Feynman Lectures on Physics I, §12-2: friction and the limits of the empirical law]{ref}

The model's historical setting was practical machinery: Coulomb's \i[Théorie des machines simples] investigated <friction> and the stiffness of ropes. The Académie's bibliography records its 1781 prize and its appearance in volume 10, pages 161–332, of the \i[Recueil des savants étrangers]. https://www.academie-sciences.fr/pdf/dossiers/Coulomb/Coulomb_pubd1.htm[Académie des sciences: Coulomb's published works]{ref}

= Kinetic friction
{parent=Friction}
{title2=$|F_{\mathrm{fr}}|=\mu N$}

<Kinetic friction> is <friction> during nonzero <contact slip>. For one-dimensional motion along a surface, choose right as positive and write $v$ for the body's contact-point <velocity> and $U$ for the surface's contact-point <velocity>, both measured in the same <inertial frame>. The signed <relative velocity> is $w=v-U$. The ideal Coulomb law for the tangential <force> on the body is
$$
f_k=-\mu_kN\operatorname{sgn}(v-U),\qquad v-U\ne0.
$$
Here $N\ge0$ is the magnitude of the <normal force>, $\mu_k\ge0$ is the <coefficient of kinetic friction>, and $\operatorname{sgn}$ is the <sign function>. Thus <kinetic friction> opposes <contact slip>, which can differ from the body's direction of motion. In several tangential directions the same law reads $\mathbf f_k=-\mu_kN\mathbf w/|\mathbf w|$ for a nonzero tangential <relative velocity> $\mathbf w$. The magnitude law is documented in https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-2-friction[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, OpenStax University Physics 1, §6.2: static and kinetic friction].

For a stipulated toy model, take a nonrotating box with <mass> $m=1\,\mathrm{kg}$, <normal force> $N=10\,\mathrm N$, <coefficient of static friction> $\mu_s=0.5$, and <coefficient of kinetic friction> $\mu_k=0.3$. These are assumed model parameters, rather than measured properties of any particular pair of materials; the prescribed <normal force> is consistent with horizontal support under $g=10\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$ and no other vertical <forces>. On stationary ground, a box already sliding right has $f_k=-3\,\mathrm N$. With an applied horizontal <force> $F=6\,\mathrm N$ to the right, <Newton's second law> gives $a=(F+f_k)/m=3\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$, for as long as rightward <contact slip> persists.

Direction is clearer by comparing two instantaneous states of this same model. If the box has $v=1\,\mathrm{m/s}$ on stationary ground, $U=0$ and $w=+1\,\mathrm{m/s}$, so <kinetic friction> acts left with magnitude $3\,\mathrm N$. If the box still has $v=1\,\mathrm{m/s}$ but a belt beneath it has $U=2\,\mathrm{m/s}$, then $w=-1\,\mathrm{m/s}$: <kinetic friction> acts right with magnitude $3\,\mathrm N$, even while the box moves right.

\Image[kinetic-friction-relative-motion.png]
{title=The same rightward box velocity gives opposite friction directions on stationary ground and a faster moving belt}

In the belt case, the <power> delivered to the box is $P_{\mathrm{box}}=f_kv=+3\,\mathrm W$. With no other horizontal <force>, its <kinetic energy> therefore increases by the <work-energy theorem>. By <Newton's third law>, the box exerts $-f_k$ on the belt, so the contact <power> delivered to the belt is $P_{\mathrm{belt}}=-f_kU=-6\,\mathrm W$. Their sum is $-3\,\mathrm W$: <frictional heating> produces thermal <internal energy> at $3\,\mathrm W$. An ideal motor maintaining the prescribed belt <velocity> supplies $+6\,\mathrm W$ against this contact load, neglecting other losses, so its input divides into $3\,\mathrm W$ of box <kinetic energy> and $3\,\mathrm W$ of <frictional heating>. These are instantaneous rates, since the box's <velocity> subsequently changes. This balance follows directly from <power> as <force> times <velocity> and <conservation of energy>; the power law is given in https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-4-power[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, OpenStax University Physics 1, §7.4: power].

When <contact slip> vanishes, including a transition to <rolling without slipping>, the nonzero-slip law no longer applies. Determine the required <static friction> from the no-slip condition and the <equations of motion>, then test its bound. Substituting $\operatorname{sgn}(0)=0$ into the sliding law would incorrectly exclude a nonzero <static friction>.

= Coefficient of kinetic friction
{parent=Kinetic friction}
{title2=$\mu_k$}

The <coefficient of kinetic friction> is the <dimensionless quantity> $\mu_k=|f_k|/N$ for sliding contact with positive <normal force> $N$ in the ideal <kinetic friction> model. It characterizes the pair of contacting surfaces and their condition. A constant $\mu_k$ means the model neglects dependence on <contact slip> speed; unlike the <coefficient of static friction>, it specifies a sliding <force> magnitude rather than a no-slip upper bound.

// Target: classical-mechanics.bigb

= Two-stage stopping under linear drag and dry friction
{parent=Kinetic friction}
{title2=$t=\frac M\lambda\log\frac U{u_*}+\frac{u_*}{\mu g},\quad d=\frac M\lambda(U-u_*)+\frac{u_*^2}{2\mu g}$}

For a body of mass $M$ initially at speed $U$, suppose the total resistance is <linear drag> $\lambda u$ until speed $u_*>0$, followed by <kinetic friction> $\mu Mg$ until rest. The first stage solves $M\dot u=-\lambda u$, giving duration $(M/\lambda)\log(U/u_*)$ and distance $(M/\lambda)(U-u_*)$. Constant deceleration in the second stage gives duration $u_*/(\mu g)$ and distance $u_*^2/(2\mu g)$. The stopping distance is finite because the switch removes the infinite exponential stopping time of pure linear drag.

= Contact slip
{parent=Friction}
{title2=$\mathbf w$}

<Contact slip> is the tangential <relative velocity> between the two material points instantaneously in contact. For a nonrotating box on a belt it is $w=v-U$; for a <rigid body>, use the <velocity> of its contact point, including the contribution from <angular velocity>. Zero <contact slip> defines <rolling without slipping> at a rolling contact, even when the <center of mass> moves.

= Frictional heating
{parent=Friction}
{title2=$\dot E_{\mathrm{thermal}}=\mu_kN|w|$}

In the ideal <kinetic friction> model, the loss of combined <mechanical energy> of the two contacting bodies becomes thermal <internal energy> at rate
$$
\dot E_{\mathrm{thermal}}=-f_k(v-U)=\mu_kN|v-U|\ge0.
$$
This follows by adding the contact <powers> $f_kv$ and $-f_kU$ from <Newton's third law>. It depends on <contact slip>, whereas the contact <power> received by either body separately depends on its <velocity> in the chosen <inertial frame>. Thus positive <frictional heating> is compatible with increasing <kinetic energy> of one body, as the moving-belt example in <kinetic friction> shows. Heating need not cause a <temperature> rise if thermal <energy> escapes by <heat transfer>.

= Newton–Cartan theory
{parent=Newtonian mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

Newton–Cartan theory describes <Newtonian mechanics> geometrically using absolute time, a degenerate spatial inverse metric, and a compatible torsion-free <affine connection>. It is not a theory with a nondegenerate Lorentzian metric. Gravitationally accelerated particles are geodesics of that connection, whose curvature measures tidal gravity.

= Newton–Cartan gravitational connection
{parent=Newton–Cartan theory}
{c}
{title2=$\Gamma^i{}_{00}=\partial_i\Phi$}

In Cartesian spatial coordinates and absolute time $t$, the <gravitational potential> $\Phi(t,x)$ determines an <affine connection> by the displayed coefficients, all others zero. The <geodesic equation> with affine time is $d^2x^i/dt^2=-\partial_i\Phi$. It preserves $dt$ and the spatial inverse metric $h^{ij}=\delta^{ij}$, reproducing freely falling <Newtonian mechanics> in a curved spacetime connection.

= Newtonian tidal curvature
{parent=Newton–Cartan gravitational connection}
{title2=$R^i{}_{0j0}=\partial_i\partial_j\Phi,\quad R_{00}=\nabla^2\Phi$}

Using $[\nabla_c,\nabla_d]V^a=R^a{}_{bcd}V^b$, the <Newton–Cartan gravitational connection> has only the displayed Hessian components and their antisymmetric partners in the last two indices. The <geodesic deviation> equation gives relative acceleration $-\partial_i\partial_j\Phi\,\delta x^j$. The <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity> is therefore $R_{ab}=4\pi G\rho\,(dt)_a(dt)_b$. A spatially uniform gravitational acceleration has zero tidal curvature even if the connection coefficients do not vanish in the original coordinates.

= Equation of motion
{parent=Classical mechanics}

= Equations of motion
{synonym}

An equation of motion determines how a physical state evolves. A variational action gives <Euler-Lagrange equations>; a <phase-space action> instead gives canonical <Hamiltonian mechanics> equations together with any imposed <constraints>.

= Time reversal in classical mechanics
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{title2=$x(t)\mapsto x(-t)$}

<Time reversal in classical mechanics> reverses the order of a trajectory's states and the sign of its velocities. A coordinate is time-even, while its velocity is time-odd. <Microscopic reversibility> compares a path with this physical reversal, including the correct equilibrium initial weights.

= Newton's law of universal gravitation
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{c}
{title2=$\mathbf F=-GMm\mathbf r/|\mathbf r|^3$}
{wiki}

<Newton's law of universal gravitation> gives the attractive <force> between two <point masses>. With source <mass> $M$ at the origin and relative <position> $\mathbf r$ of <mass> $m$, write $r=|\mathbf r|>0$:
$$
\mathbf F_{M\to m}=-\frac{GMm}{r^3}\mathbf r,\qquad |\mathbf F_{M\to m}|=\frac{GMm}{r^2}.
$$
Here $G$ is the <gravitational constant>. The minus sign points toward $M$; the magnitude follows an <inverse-square law>, so doubling the separation quarters the <force>. The opposite <force> on $M$ satisfies <Newton's third law>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/13-1-newtons-law-of-universal-gravitation[OpenStax: mutual gravitational forces]{ref}

Both <masses> accelerate. Applying <Newton's second law> to each in the isolated <two-body problem> gives
$$
\ddot{\mathbf r}=-\frac{\mu}{r^3}\mathbf r,\qquad \mu=G(M+m).
$$
The relative <standard gravitational parameter> includes both <masses>; replacing it by $GM$ requires the <test particle> approximation $m\ll M$.

Historically, Newton's Book III, Proposition VII argues that gravitational attraction applies to all bodies and scales with their <masses>; its second corollary states the <inverse-square law> for individual particles. https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Mathematical_Principles_of_Natural_Philosophy_(1729)/Proposition_7:_Gravity_tending_to_all_Bodies[Newton, Principia, Book III, Proposition VII, Motte's 1729 translation]{ref}

= Free fall
{parent=Newton's law of universal gravitation}
{wiki}

Free fall is motion under <gravity> alone: there is no supporting <normal force>, engine thrust or appreciable <drag force>. In the <Newtonian mechanics> model, a <test particle> of <mass> $m$ obeys $m\ddot{\mathbf r}=m\mathbf g(\mathbf r)$, hence $\ddot{\mathbf r}=\mathbf g(\mathbf r)$. The <mass> cancels. Bodies released at the same <position> with the same <velocity> follow the same path in this model, even if their <gravitational weights> differ. https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/free-falling-objects/[NASA Glenn: free-falling objects and the cancellation of mass]{ref}

A dropped stone is one example; a coasting satellite is another. Free fall does not require motion straight down or decreasing distance to the attracting body. An ideal <circular orbit> has constant radius while its <velocity> continually turns under <centripetal acceleration>. The <circular orbit as continuous free fall> connects the familiar drop with orbital motion.

Near a surface, treating the <gravitational acceleration> as constant gives a useful first prediction. Over larger heights, the <inverse-square law> changes the acceleration during the fall. <Radial free fall from rest> uses <conservation of mechanical energy> to account for that change exactly, within the specified <Newtonian gravitational field>. The contrast matters: the simplest formula is an approximation to a physical model, rather than a law with the same numerical $g$ everywhere.

History also makes the idealization tangible. Galileo's *Two New Sciences* (1638), Third Day, Theorem II, derives distance proportional to the square of time for uniformly accelerated motion from rest; its first corollary gives successive distances in the ratios $1,3,5,7,\ldots$ over equal time intervals. This is a kinematic statement, predating the gravitational <inverse-square law>. https://galileoandeinstein.phys.virginia.edu/tns_draft/tns_160to243.html[Galileo, Two New Sciences, Third Day, Theorem II and Corollary I; Crew and de Salvio's 1914 translation, pp. 174–176]{ref}

On Apollo 15's final lunar surface excursion, David Scott released a hammer and a feather together. The near-<vacuum> removed the large difference in <drag force> familiar on Earth. NASA's report describes their arrival together within the accuracy of the release; this demonstration illustrates the <free fall> prediction without establishing infinite experimental precision. https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-apollo-15-hammer-feather-drop/[NASA's original footage and Joe Allen's account in the Apollo 15 Preliminary Science Report, p. 2-11]{ref}

\Video[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oYEgdZ3iEKA]
{title=NASA footage of David Scott's Apollo 15 hammer-and-feather free-fall demonstration}

= Free fall in a constant gravitational field
{parent=Free fall}
{title2=$s(t)=\tfrac12gt^2,\quad v(t)=gt$}

Take downward <displacement> $s$ as positive, assume a constant <gravitational acceleration> $g>0$, and release a <point mass> from rest at $s=0$. Integrating $\ddot s=g$ twice gives
$$
v(t)=gt,\qquad s(t)=\frac12gt^2.
$$
For a downward distance $h>0$, the arrival time and <speed> are
$$
t_{\mathrm{fall}}=\sqrt{\frac{2h}{g}},\qquad v_{\mathrm{arrival}}=\sqrt{2gh}.
$$
For the model values $h=20\,\mathrm m$ and $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$, these are approximately $2.0193\,\mathrm s$ and $19.8091\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Doubling the height multiplies both quantities by $\sqrt2$; it does not double them. The same <speed> follows from the <work-energy theorem>, $mgh=mv^2/2$.

For intervals of length $\Delta t$, the successive downward <displacements> are
$$
s((j+1)\Delta t)-s(j\Delta t)=\frac12g(\Delta t)^2(2j+1),\qquad j=0,1,2,\ldots.
$$
Thus equal spacing in time produces increasingly separated positions. This is the odd-number pattern in Galileo's result. It assumes constant <acceleration> and release from rest; a nonzero initial <velocity> adds a linear term to $s(t)$.

The approximation can be estimated for a spherical source of radius $R$ and <standard gravitational parameter> $\mu$. Writing $g_0=\mu/R^2$, the gravitational magnitude at height $h$ is
$$
\frac{g(R+h)}{g_0}=\left(1+\frac hR\right)^{-2}.
$$
For $h/R\ll1$, a <Taylor expansion> gives $g(R+h)/g_0=1-2h/R+O((h/R)^2)$. Small height relative to the source's radius makes nearly constant gravity plausible; negligible <drag force> remains a separate requirement.

= Radial free fall from rest
{parent=Free fall}
{title2=$v(r)=\sqrt{2\mu(1/r-1/r_0)}$}

Let a <test particle> start at rest at radius $r_0$ outside a fixed spherically symmetric source. The <spherical shell theorem> gives the exterior <Newtonian gravitational potential> $\Phi(r)=-\mu/r$, where $\mu=GM$ is the source's <standard gravitational parameter>. Zero initial transverse <velocity> makes the motion radial. <Conservation of mechanical energy> per unit <mass> gives
$$
\frac{\dot r^2}{2}-\frac{\mu}{r}=-\frac{\mu}{r_0},\qquad
\dot r=-\sqrt{2\mu\left(\frac1r-\frac1{r_0}\right)}.
$$
The minus sign selects inward motion; the positive square root is its <speed>. For a fall from $r_0=2R$ to the surface $r=R$, that <speed> is $\sqrt{\mu/R}$. In the limit of release from rest infinitely far away, the familiar <free-fall speed> is $\sqrt{2\mu/r}$, equal in magnitude to the local <escape velocity>. A finite starting radius gives a smaller speed.

The arrival time follows from the same first integral. Set $r=r_0\cos^2\eta$. Then
$$
t(\eta)=\sqrt{\frac{r_0^3}{2\mu}}\left(\eta+\sin\eta\cos\eta\right),\qquad
\eta=\arccos\sqrt{\frac r{r_0}},\qquad 0\leq\eta<\frac\pi2.
$$
For arrival at $R>0$ this is finite, and the field formula is used only outside the source. For $r_0=2R$, $\eta=\pi/4$ gives
$$
t_{\mathrm{arrival}}=\left(1+\frac\pi2\right)\sqrt{\frac{R^3}{\mu}}\approx2.570796\sqrt{\frac{R^3}{\mu}}.
$$
Keeping the smaller initial gravitational magnitude $g(r_0)=\mu/(4R^2)$ constant would predict $\sqrt8\,\sqrt{R^3/\mu}$, about $10.0\%$ longer. The actual <acceleration> increases during the fall.

\Image[radial-free-fall-gravity-variation.png]
{title=Radial free-fall height and arrival time compared with constant gravity at the release point}

The curves compare a shallow fall, $h/R=0.02$, with a fall from $r_0=2R$, so $h/R=1$. Each comparison holds the release height and initial gravitational magnitude fixed. The constant-gravity model is close for the shallow fall and increasingly inaccurate for the larger one. This is also a practical use of <nondimensionalization>: the normalized trajectories depend on $h/R$, while $\sqrt{R^3/\mu}$ supplies the dimensional time scale.

= Rotational deflection of a falling body
{parent=Free fall}

In an Earth-fixed <rotating reference frame>, the horizontal displacement of a freely falling body receives contributions from <Coriolis acceleration> and <centrifugal acceleration>. A short-fall approximation substitutes the unperturbed downward <velocity> into the Coriolis term and the Earth's surface <position> into the centrifugal term. If <gravitational acceleration> excludes centrifugal acceleration, both contributions must be included explicitly.

= Newtonian gravitational field
{parent=Newton's law of universal gravitation}
{c}
{title2=$\mathbf g$}

The <Newtonian gravitational field> is the gravitational <force> per unit <mass> of a <test particle>. For a <point mass> $M$ at the origin,
$$
\mathbf g(\mathbf r)=\frac{\mathbf F}{m}=-\frac{GM}{r^3}\mathbf r,\qquad g(r)=|\mathbf g|=\frac{GM}{r^2}.
$$
The <test particle>'s <mass> cancels: different small <masses> have the same <gravitational acceleration> at the same <position>. In <SI>, the <Newtonian gravitational field> has units $\mathrm{N\,kg^{-1}}=\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. The same exterior expression holds for a spherically symmetric source, by the <spherical shell theorem>.

Choose a reference radius $r_0>0$, set $g_0=GM/r_0^2$, and use <nondimensionalization> with $z=r/r_0$. Then $g/g_0=z^{-2}$: at $z=1,2,4$, the magnitudes are $1,1/4,1/16$. This makes the spatial variation visible without specifying a planet. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/13-1-newtons-law-of-universal-gravitation[OpenStax: inverse-square attraction and spherical sources]{ref}

\Image[gravity-force-and-potential.png]
{title=Normalized Newtonian gravitational field and point-mass potential}
{description=Doubling the radius quarters the magnitude of the <Newtonian gravitational field>, but halves the magnitude of the negative <Newtonian gravitational potential>. Both curves approach zero as radius increases; the inward <gravitational acceleration> is minus the <gradient> of the <Newtonian gravitational potential>.}

= Uniform gravitational field in an off-center spherical cavity
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}
{title2=$g=-4\pi G\rho c/3$}

Remove a spherical void centered at $c$ from a uniform sphere centered at the origin. At points inside the void, <superposition> combines the sphere's interior field $-4\pi G\rho x/3$ with the removed sphere's field $+4\pi G\rho(x-c)/3$. Their sum is the displayed constant <Newtonian gravitational field>. A second removed mass outside this void adds a nonuniform contribution.

= Cavity equilibrium destabilized by a second void
{parent=Uniform gravitational field in an off-center spherical cavity}

A uniform <Newtonian gravitational field> inside one spherical void can be cancelled at a point by the repulsive field representing another removed sphere. The latter field has a <Jacobian matrix> proportional to $I-3\hat r\hat r^T$: its axial eigenvalue is negative and both transverse eigenvalues positive. The equilibrium is restoring along the axis but unstable transversely. A zero force is thus not enough for stable rest.

= Newtonian gravitational stress tensor
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}
{c}
{title2=$T_{ij}$}

For a <Newtonian gravitational potential> obeying the <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity>, define $T_{ij}=[\partial_i\Phi\partial_j\Phi-\delta_{ij}|\nabla\Phi|^2/2]/(4\pi G)$. Commutation of mixed <partial derivatives> gives $\partial_jT_{ij}=\rho\partial_i\Phi$. Thus the gravitational force density can be put in conservative form as $-\partial_jT_{ij}$. The sign is chosen so that $T_{ij}$ is added to the momentum flux; reversing the stress convention reverses the divergence sign.

= Gravitational stress contribution to angular-momentum transport
{parent=Newtonian gravitational stress tensor}
{title2=$rT_{r\phi}$}

In cylindrical coordinates, the <Newtonian gravitational stress tensor> gives radial <angular-momentum flux> $rT_{r\phi}=r(\partial_r\Phi)(r^{-1}\partial_\phi\Phi)/(4\pi G)$. Nonaxisymmetric structure can correlate the field components and transport <angular momentum>. The isotropic negative-pressure part has zero off-diagonal component, so it gives no direct radial transport. This separates a shear flux from a contribution to normal force balance.

= Self-gravity
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}

Self-gravity is the <gravitational acceleration> produced by a system's own <mass density>. It is obtained by solving the <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity> for that density, rather than treating the <Newtonian gravitational potential> as an externally fixed field. It competes with <pressure> and rotational support in <gravitational instability of an astrophysical disk>.

= Standard gravitational parameter
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}
{title2=$\mu$}
{wiki}

= Gravitational parameter
{synonym}

The product $\mu=GM$ of the <gravitational constant> and a central <mass> sets the strength of the exterior <Newtonian gravitational field>. Its dimensions are length cubed divided by time squared. In the exact relative <two-body problem>, the parameter is $G(M_1+M_2)$; using $GM$ for one source assumes the <test particle> approximation.

The combination is useful because orbital and falling-body observations constrain $\mu$ directly. A gravitational <circular orbit> of radius $a$ has <oscillation period> $T=2\pi\sqrt{a^3/\mu}$, so measuring $a$ and $T$ gives $\mu=4\pi^2a^3/T^2$. That measurement alone does not separately determine $G$ and $M$. Similarly, <radial free fall from rest> has a natural time scale $\sqrt{r_0^3/\mu}$.

Other interactions require a different model. Inverse-square <radiation pressure> can reduce the effective attraction for a dust <Kepler orbit>; calling that effective coefficient a gravitational parameter must not hide the added <force>.

= Tidal tensor
{title2=$T_{jk}$}
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}

The acceleration tidal tensor is $T_{jk}=\partial_k g_j=-\partial_j\partial_k\Phi$ for <gravitational acceleration> $\mathbf g=-\nabla\Phi$. It describes differential acceleration $\Delta\mathbf g\simeq T\Delta\mathbf r$. Some authors instead call the positive <Hessian matrix> $\partial_j\partial_k\Phi$ the tidal tensor, reversing this sign convention. For a point mass and the positive potential convention $\mathbf g=\nabla\psi$, the tensor is $Gm(3\mathbf R\mathbf R^{\mathsf T}/R^5-\mathbf1/R^3)$.

The <tidal tensor> is the <Jacobian matrix> of the <Newtonian gravitational field>, rather than the <gravitational acceleration> itself. Its entries have units $\mathrm{s^{-2}}$: multiplying by a <displacement> produces an <acceleration>. For a smooth <Newtonian gravitational potential>, the <tidal tensor> is a <symmetric matrix>, since mixed <partial derivatives> commute. A spatially uniform <gravitational acceleration> has zero <tidal tensor>, however large that <acceleration> is.

= Point-mass tidal stretching and squeezing
{parent=Tidal tensor}
{title2=$T=\mu(3\mathbf n\mathbf n^{\mathsf T}-I)/R^3$}

For a source <point mass> $M$ at the origin, let $\mathbf R=R\mathbf n$, where $\mathbf n$ is an outward <unit vector>, and let $\mu=GM$ be the source's <standard gravitational parameter>. Differentiating $g_i=-\mu R_i/R^3$ gives the <tidal tensor>
$$
T_{ij}=\frac{\mu}{R^3}(3n_i n_j-\delta_{ij}),\qquad T=\frac{\mu}{R^3}(3\mathbf n\mathbf n^{\mathsf T}-I),
$$
where $\delta_{ij}$ is the <Kronecker delta> and $I$ is the <identity matrix>. In an <orthonormal basis> with first axis radial, the <tidal tensor> is
$$
T=\frac{\mu}{R^3}\begin{pmatrix}2&0&0\\0&-1&0\\0&0&-1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The radial <eigenvalue> $2\mu/R^3$ separates nearby freely falling <test particles>; the two transverse <eigenvalues> $-\mu/R^3$ bring them together. The farther radial <test particle> falls inward less rapidly, so its <acceleration> relative to the nearer one points outward. Transverse <gravitational accelerations> converge toward the source. Thus an initially resting small cloud stretches radially and squeezes in both transverse directions. These are instantaneous relative <accelerations>; the subsequent shape also depends on initial <velocities> and the changing <tidal tensor> along the cloud's <free fall>.

Outside the source, the <matrix trace> is $2\mu/R^3-\mu/R^3-\mu/R^3=0$. This matches $\operatorname{tr}T=-\nabla^2\Phi=0$ in a region with zero <mass density>, by the <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity>. Zero <matrix trace> means no leading change of a small initially resting cloud's <volume> at order $t^2$, not that each separation stays fixed. Inside matter, the same sign convention gives $\operatorname{tr}T=-4\pi G\rho$ instead.

= Quadrupolar point-mass tidal potential
{parent=Tidal tensor}
{title2=$\psi_{\mathrm{tid}}=\tfrac12r_iT_{ij}r_j$}

Subtracting the constant and uniform-acceleration terms from the point-mass <relative potential> gives $\psi_{\mathrm{tid}}=Gm_p[3(\mathbf r\cdot\widehat{\mathbf r}_p)^2-r^2]/(2r_p^3)$, with force convention $\nabla\psi$. Its <tidal tensor> is $Gm_p(3\widehat{\mathbf r}_p\widehat{\mathbf r}_p^T-I)/r_p^3$. This is the leading term for target size small compared with separation.

= Tidal disruption
{parent=Tidal tensor}

Disruption of a self-gravitating body when differential gravitational <acceleration> across it exceeds its binding <acceleration>. For a body of radius $R$, <mass> $m$ at distance $r$ from a much more massive body $M$, the competing scales are $GMR/r^3$ and $Gm/R^2$; their equality gives $r\sim R(M/m)^{1/3}$, up to geometry and internal-structure factors. The <tidal tensor> gives the directional differential <acceleration>. A <tidal disruption event> near a black hole is a specific astronomical application.

= Tidal force
{parent=Tidal tensor}
{wiki}

Differential gravitational <acceleration>, multiplied by the affected <mass>, across a spatially extended body. In the linear approximation the <acceleration> difference is $T\Delta\boldsymbol r$, where $T$ is the <tidal tensor>. Stellar <tidal forces> limit the domain of a <circumplanetary orbit>; sufficiently strong ones can produce <tidal disruption>.

To isolate a <tidal force>, subtract the <gravitational acceleration> shared by the body as a whole. Let a reference <test particle> follow $\ddot{\mathbf R}=\mathbf g(\mathbf R)$, and let another have <position> $\mathbf R+\mathbf x$. In a <translating accelerated reference frame> with parallel, nonrotating axes,
$$
\ddot{\mathbf x}=\mathbf g(\mathbf R+\mathbf x)-\mathbf g(\mathbf R)=T(\mathbf R)\mathbf x+O\!\left(\frac{\mu|\mathbf x|^2}{R^4}\right)
$$
for a source <point mass>, when $|\mathbf x|/R\ll1$. Here $\mu=GM$ is its <standard gravitational parameter> and the last term uses <Big O notation>. This <Taylor expansion> removes the common inward pull and keeps the spatial variation. The near side has an excess pull toward the source; the far side has a deficit of inward pull, which is an outward relative <acceleration>. Both sides can stretch apart while both still accelerate toward the source.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume I by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 7: The Theory of Gravitation>[§7–4, discussion of stronger near-side and weaker far-side attraction]{ref}

\Image[tidal-field-difference-and-stretching.png]
{title=Subtracting common gravitational acceleration reveals radial stretching and transverse squeezing}
{description=Blue arrows show the exact <Newtonian gravitational field> at several <positions> near a reference point. Orange arrows show its linear residual, calculated with the <tidal tensor>. Arrow lengths use separate scales in the two panels; this is a comparison of direction and spatial variation, not two equal-magnitude fields.}

For a material element of <mass> $m$, the residual external <force> in this <translating accelerated reference frame> is $m[\mathbf g(\mathbf R+\mathbf x)-\mathbf g(\mathbf R)]$. Actual deformation also depends on <elasticity>, <pressure> and <self-gravity>. A <rigid body> approximation suppresses deformation but can still have internal <forces> that resist the <tidal force>. The <point-mass tidal stretching and squeezing> pattern describes freely falling, noninteracting <test particles>, rather than promising the same shape for every solid, star or ocean. A <rotating reference frame> introduces <centrifugal acceleration> and <Coriolis acceleration>, which must be included separately.

= Exact tidal acceleration between separated masses
{parent=Tidal force}
{title2=$\Delta a=2\mu L/[R^3(1-L^2/(4R^2))^2]$}

Stipulate a fixed source <point mass> $M=10^{12}\,\mathrm{kg}$, two equal <test particles> of <mass> $m=1\,\mathrm{kg}$, and $G=6.67430\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm{m^3\,kg^{-1}\,s^{-2}}$. This is a chosen model, not a measurement of a particular object. Place the <test particles> on the outward radial axis at $R-L/2$ and $R+L/2$, with midpoint distance $R=100\,\mathrm m$ and separation $L=2\,\mathrm m$. Neglect their mutual attraction, other sources and all supporting <forces>.

Each signed radial <gravitational acceleration> is inward:
$$
a_-=-\frac{\mu}{(R-L/2)^2}\approx-0.0068098153\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}},\qquad a_+=-\frac{\mu}{(R+L/2)^2}\approx-0.0065427899\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}.
$$
Their separation has outward relative <acceleration>
$$
\ddot L=a_+-a_-=\mu\left[\frac{1}{(R-L/2)^2}-\frac{1}{(R+L/2)^2}\right]=\frac{2\mu L}{R^3}\left(1-\frac{L^2}{4R^2}\right)^{-2}.
$$
The exact value is $2.6702540\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. The midpoint <tidal tensor> predicts $2\mu L/R^3=2.6697200\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$, an underestimate of $0.019999\%$ relative to the exact value. For these equal $1\,\mathrm{kg}$ <masses>, the difference of external <forces> has the same numerical value in the <SI> unit of <force>, the <newton (unit)>. It is not an additional mutual <force> between the <test particles>.

The exact formula requires $L<2R$ and describes the instantaneous relative <acceleration>. Because the two sample <positions> are symmetric about the midpoint, the <Taylor expansion> error relative to the linear result starts at order $(L/R)^2$; a one-sided comparison with a reference point generally has relative error of order $L/R$. If either <test particle> enters an extended source, use that source's interior <Newtonian gravitational field> instead of extrapolating the singular <point-mass> model.

= Gravity magnitude versus tidal gradient
{parent=Tidal force}
{title2=$g=\mu/R^2,\quad \lambda_r=2\mu/R^3$}

For a fixed source <point mass>, the magnitude $g=\mu/R^2$ of the <gravitational acceleration> measures the local pull; the radial <eigenvalue> $\lambda_r=2\mu/R^3$ of the <tidal tensor> measures how that pull changes across space. Doubling $R$ divides $g$ by four but $\lambda_r$ by eight. For a fixed small radial separation $L$, the linear relative <acceleration> is $\Delta a\simeq\lambda_r L$, so
$$
\frac{\Delta a}{g}\simeq\frac{2L}{R}.
$$
A small body can therefore experience strong common <gravitational acceleration> with a comparatively small <tidal force>. A measurement of $g$ alone does not determine the <tidal tensor>: sources at different distances can have the same $g$ but different spatial variation.

\Image[tidal-gravity-distance-scaling.png]
{title=Gravity falls as inverse distance squared, while point-mass tidal stretching falls as inverse distance cubed}
{description=The left panel compares independently normalized <gravitational acceleration> and radial <tidal tensor> <eigenvalue> for a fixed source. The right panel compares exact and linear relative <accelerations> for two radially separated <test particles> at a fixed midpoint, showing the finite-separation correction as their separation grows.}

The local stretching time scale is $\lambda_r^{-1/2}=\sqrt{R^3/(2\mu)}$: it marks when an initially resting separation could change by order itself if the <tidal tensor> stayed fixed. During <free fall>, $R$ changes, so this scale is not a complete trajectory or a universal disruption time. <Tidal disruption> additionally compares the <tidal force> with binding by <self-gravity> and material <forces>; $g$ by itself supplies no such comparison.

= Love number
{c}
{title2=$k_2$}
{wiki}
{parent=Tidal force}

A Love number measures a body's tidal response to an applied potential. The degree-two potential response $k_2$ is sensitive to radial density structure and central concentration, and can complement mass and radius constraints on an <exoplanet interior>. Inferring it from orbital precession requires separating other contributions and assumptions about rotation and the tidal response.

= Gravity gradiometry
{parent=Tidal tensor}

<Gravity gradiometry> measures spatial differences of <gravitational acceleration>. Two simultaneous measurements along a <unit vector> $\mathbf e$, separated by $L\mathbf e$, give
$$
\frac{\mathbf e\cdot[\mathbf g(\mathbf R+L\mathbf e)-\mathbf g(\mathbf R)]}{L}\simeq\mathbf e^{\mathsf T}T\mathbf e.
$$
This measures one directional component of the <tidal tensor>, not its whole <matrix>. Any identical platform <acceleration> cancels in the difference. Unequal instrument responses, changing geometry and <rotating reference frames> can spoil that cancellation. For a large baseline, the quotient is an average of the directional <tidal tensor> along the baseline, rather than its exact value at one point.

= Atom-cloud gravity-gradient measurement
{parent=Gravity gradiometry}

A laboratory <gravity gradiometry> experiment used two vertically separated clouds of <chemical atoms> in <vacuum>, launched into <free fall>. Shared <laser> beams applied three pulses ($\pi/2,\pi,\pi/2$), splitting and recombining <matter waves>. Balanced <photodiodes> measured probe-light absorption to infer internal-state populations; scanning the last pulse's <wave phase> produced an <interference> fringe.<Sensitive absolute-gravity gradiometry using atom interferometry by J. M. McGuirk and others>[§I, pp. 033608-1–2; §II E 1, p. 033608-6; §II F 1, p. 033608-7]{ref}

The difference of fringe <wave phases> obeys $\delta\phi=\mathbf k_{\mathrm{eff}}\cdot\Delta\mathbf g\,\tau^2$, with effective <wave vector> $\mathbf k_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and pulse spacing $\tau$: shared reference motion cancels, leaving differential <gravitational acceleration>. Eight lead bricks, each approximately $12.5\,\mathrm{kg}$, were placed symmetrically around the lower chamber, about $0.2\,\mathrm m$ from the fountain apex. The measured signal was $(8.1\pm2.1)\times10^{-9}g$, versus a calculated $8.2\times10^{-9}g$, where $g$ denotes terrestrial <gravitational acceleration>.<Sensitive absolute-gravity gradiometry using atom interferometry by J. M. McGuirk and others>[§I, p. 033608-2; §III A 2, p. 033608-10, mass detection]{ref}

Controls deliberately shook the shared reference reflector and compared actively isolated and unisolated operation. Tilting tests exposed sensitivity to <Coriolis acceleration> and <centrifugal acceleration>. Thus shared translation is rejectable, while rotation and finite measurement uncertainty remain limitations. The brick signal detects spatially varying <gravity>; it does not establish a precision value of the <gravitational constant>.<Sensitive absolute-gravity gradiometry using atom interferometry by J. M. McGuirk and others>[§III A, p. 033608-9; §III B, pp. 033608-10–11]{ref}

= Quadrupolar tidal forcing
{parent=Tidal tensor}

For a point-mass companion at separation $a$, subtract the constant and linear terms of its <Newtonian gravitational potential> in a frame accelerating with the target's center. The leading residual is
$$
\Phi_{\mathrm{tide}}=-\frac{GM_2}{2a^3}\left[3(\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf r)^2-r^2\right].
$$
It is degree two in <spherical harmonics>. In a circular orbit with angular frequency $\omega_o$, its time-dependent $m=\pm2$ components force at $2\omega_o$ in a frame with no spin; there is also a static $m=0$ component. This is the leading approximation in $r/a$, and the tidal amplitude may be absorbed into a harmonic-dependent constant.

= Gravitational acceleration
{title2=$g$}
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}
{wiki}

= Newtonian gravity
{synonym}
{c}

Gravitational acceleration is the <acceleration> of a <test particle> in <free fall> in a <Newtonian gravitational field>. It is a vector pointing along that field; $g$ commonly denotes its positive magnitude. Near Earth's surface, the magnitude is approximately constant over shallow vertical scales. For a fixed spherical source it instead varies as $g(r)=\mu/r^2$, where $\mu$ is the <standard gravitational parameter>.

This is distinct from <gravitational weight>, the <force> $m\mathbf g$, and from <apparent weight>, the support force a scale measures. An orbiting body can have substantial <gravitational acceleration> while its <apparent weight> is zero.

= Newtonian central configuration
{c}
{parent=Gravitational acceleration}
{title2=$\nabla_iW=\Lambda m_i\mathbf a_i$}

A collision-free positive-mass configuration satisfies the displayed equation when every gravitational acceleration points proportionally toward a common centre. With $W=-\sum_{i<j}Gm_im_j/|\mathbf a_i-\mathbf a_j|$, homogeneity gives $\Lambda I=-W>0$, $I=\sum_i m_i|\mathbf a_i|^2$. Pairwise force cancellation implies $\sum_i m_i\mathbf a_i=0$. Such a configuration generates a nonrotating <homothetic gravitational motion> by multiplying all position vectors by one positive scale factor.

= Homothetic gravitational motion
{parent=Newtonian central configuration}
{title2=$\ddot a=-\Lambda/a^2$}

For a <Newtonian central configuration>, set $\mathbf x_i(t)=a(t)\mathbf a_i$ with $a>0$. The gravitational force scales as $a^{-2}$, reducing all equations to the displayed scalar <ordinary differential equation>. Its first integral is $\dot a^2/2-\Lambda/a=k/2$. The total <energy> is $kI/2$, so the scalar energy constant exactly tracks the many-body energy for the fixed shape.

= Gravitational constant
{title2=$G$}
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}
{wiki}

The gravitational constant is the proportionality constant in <Newton's law of universal gravitation>.

= Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity
{c}
{title2=$\nabla^2\Phi=4\pi G\rho$}
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}

For <mass density> $\rho$, the <Newtonian gravitational potential> satisfies the <Poisson equation> $\nabla^2\Phi=4\pi G\rho$. The gravitational acceleration is $-\nabla\Phi$. An isolated system also requires the exterior vacuum solution and decay at infinity; solving the interior equation alone does not fix a globally physical field.

= Dyadic spherical shells with inverse-radius density scaling
{parent=Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity}
{title2=$M(2^{-N})=M(1)\,4^{-N}$}

Suppose the shell $2^{-n}<r<2^{1-n}$ has <mass density> $2^{n-1}\rho_0$. Its mass is $(14\pi\rho_0/3)4^{-n}$, by integrating $4\pi r^2\rho\,dr$. Summing the <geometric series> gives total mass $14\pi\rho_0/9$. At $r=2^{-N}$ the enclosed shells have indices $n\geq N+1$, so the enclosed mass is the total mass times $r^2$. The magnitude of the <Newtonian gravitational field> at every such boundary is therefore the same, $14\pi G\rho_0/9$. This does not assert that the field is constant throughout each shell.

= Newtonian gravitational potential
{title2=$\Phi$}
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}
{c}

The <Newtonian gravitational potential> $\Phi$ is <potential energy> per unit <mass> of a <test particle>. Its <gradient> determines the <Newtonian gravitational field>:
$$
U=m\Phi,\qquad \mathbf g=-\nabla\Phi,\qquad \mathbf F=-\nabla U=-m\nabla\Phi.
$$
In <SI>, $U$ has units $\mathrm J$, while $\Phi$ has units $\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}}=\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-2}}$; its <gradient> has units of <acceleration>, not <force>. Adding a constant to $\Phi$ leaves the <Newtonian gravitational field> unchanged. For an isolated <point mass>, the convenient reference is $\Phi(\infty)=0$. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/16-07-dynamics-fall-2009/0bb19205a6316ba88f88284bf7257ec6_MIT16_07F09_Lec16.pdf[Peraire and Widnall, MIT Dynamics, Lecture 16: potential per unit mass]{ref}

Increasing the radius from $r_0$ to $r_1>r_0$ raises the <Newtonian gravitational potential energy>:
$$
\Delta U=GMm\left(\frac1{r_0}-\frac1{r_1}\right)>0.
$$
An external agent supplies this positive <work> if the initial and final <kinetic energies> agree; gravity does <work> $-\Delta U$. For $r_1=2r_0$, the required <work> is $GMm/(2r_0)$. <University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others/13.3 Gravitational Potential Energy and Total Energy>{ref}

= Planetary quadrupole coefficient
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential}
{title2=$J_2$}

The dimensionless coefficient $J_2$ describes the leading axisymmetric departure of a planet's external <Newtonian gravitational potential> from a monopole: $\Phi=-GM_p/r+[GM_pJ_2R_p^2/r^3]P_2(\cos\vartheta)$, where $\vartheta$ is colatitude from the spin axis. An oblate mass distribution has positive $J_2$. The <disturbing function> is minus the nonspherical potential term. On a circular inclined <Kepler orbit>, $\cos\vartheta=\sin I\sin M$, so <orbit averaging> gives $\overline{\mathcal R}_{J_2}=GM_pJ_2R_p^2a^{-3}(1/2-3\sin^2I/4)$. The exact circular nodal rate is $-3nJ_2(R_p/a)^2\cos I/2$; its small-tilt limit is $-3nJ_2(R_p/a)^2/2$.

= Oblate-quadrupole epicyclic precession
{parent=Planetary quadrupole coefficient}
{title2=$\kappa^2=n^2(1-3\alpha^2/R^2),\quad\nu^2=n^2(1+9\alpha^2/R^2)$}

For $\Phi=-\mu/r+\alpha^2\mu(3z^2-r^2)/r^5$, a nearly circular equatorial orbit has $n^2=\mu/R^3$ and azimuthal frequency $\Omega_c^2=n^2(1+3\alpha^2/R^2)$. The <radial epicyclic frequency> is $\kappa^2=R\,d\Omega_c^2/dR+4\Omega_c^2$, and the <vertical epicyclic frequency> is $\nu^2=\partial_z^2\Phi|_{z=0}$. Radial stability requires $R^2>3\alpha^2$. The <nodal precession> rate $\Omega_c-\nu$ is negative and the <apsidal precession> rate $\Omega_c-\kappa$ positive. These sign statements concern small eccentricity and inclination. For an arbitrary inclined orbit, the averaged <argument of periapsis> instead evolves as $(3/2)n\alpha^2[5\cos^2i-1]/[a^2(1-e^2)^2]$ and can regress.

= Axisymmetric logarithmic gravitational potential
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential}
{title2=$\Phi=\tfrac12V^2\log(R^2+z^2/q^2)$}

An axisymmetric logarithmic <Newtonian gravitational potential> has spheroidal equipotential surfaces and a <flat galaxy rotation curve>: $R\Phi_R(R,0)=V^2$. The singular model is defined away from the origin; a fixed reference length makes the logarithm dimensionless. Its central $r^{-2}$ <mass density> cusp is locally integrable, but its total <mass> diverges at large radius.

= Density positivity for an axisymmetric logarithmic potential
{parent=Axisymmetric logarithmic gravitational potential}
{title2=$q^2\geq1/2$}

The <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity> gives
$$
\rho=\frac{V^2}{4\pi Gq^2}\frac{R^2+(2-q^{-2})z^2}{(R^2+q^{-2}z^2)^2}.
$$
For $V>0$, nonnegative <mass density> away from the origin is equivalent to $q^2\geq1/2$. Strict positivity requires $q^2>1/2$. This condition does not by itself guarantee a nonnegative <galactic distribution function>.

= Power-law gravitational potential
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential}
{title2=$\Phi=-Ar^{-\beta}$}

A radial <Newtonian gravitational potential> $\Phi=-Ar^{-\beta}$ produces inward acceleration $A\beta r^{-\beta-1}$. The point-mass case has $\beta=1$ and $A=GM$. Treating the <power-law gravitational potential> as prescribed does not require it to be sourced by the flowing gas.

= Gravitational quadrupole potential of a point-mass binary
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential}
{title2=$\Phi_2=-G\mu[3(\mathbf x\cdot\mathbf r)^2-x^2r^2]/(2x^5)$}

For a <binary star> viewed far from its <centre of mass>, let $\mathbf r$ be the separation and $\mu$ the <reduced mass>, $M=M_1+M_2$ the total <mass>, $r=|\mathbf r|$ and $x=|\mathbf x|$. The <Taylor expansion> of the point-mass <Newtonian gravitational potential> has zero dipole because $\sum_A M_A\mathbf x_A=0$. Its second moment is $\sum_A M_Ax_{Ai}x_{Aj}=\mu r_i r_j$, so
$$
\Phi=-\frac{GM}{x}-\frac{G\mu}{2x^5}\left[3(\mathbf x\cdot\mathbf r)^2-x^2r^2\right]+O(GMr^3/x^4).
$$
This holds for an instantaneous separation in any orbit, not only a <circular orbit>. The <mass quadrupole moment> convention $Q_{ij}=\mu(r_ir_j-r^2\delta_{ij}/3)$ gives $\Phi_2=-3GQ_{ij}x_ix_j/(2x^5)$.

= Flattened power-law gravitational potential
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential}
{title2=$\Phi=\Phi_0[R_0/(R^2+\lambda^2z^2)^{1/2}]^\beta$}

A flattened power-law <Newtonian gravitational potential> has equipotential spheroids $R^2+\lambda^2z^2=\text{constant}$. For $\Phi_0<0$ and $0<\beta<1$, its midplane <circular orbit> angular frequency satisfies $\Omega^2=-\beta\Phi_0R_0^\beta R^{-\beta-2}$.

= Density positivity for a flattened power-law potential
{parent=Flattened power-law gravitational potential}

Applying the <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity> to a <flattened power-law gravitational potential> yields
$$
\rho=-\frac{\beta\Phi_0R_0^\beta}{4\pi G}\frac{(\lambda^2-\beta)R^2+\lambda^2[2-(\beta+1)\lambda^2]z^2}{(R^2+\lambda^2z^2)^{(\beta+4)/2}}.
$$
For $0<\beta<1$, nonnegative <mass density> everywhere outside the origin requires and is ensured by $\beta\leq\lambda^2\leq2/(\beta+1)$. The central density singularity is locally integrable and carries no point mass, although the total mass diverges at large radius in this scale-free model.

= Newtonian potential of a point mass
{title2=$\Phi(r)=-GM/r$}
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential}
{c}

= Gravitational potential
{synonym}

The <Newtonian potential of a point mass> $M$ at the origin is
$$
\Phi(r)=-\frac{GM}{r},\qquad r>0,\qquad \Phi(\infty)=0.
$$
It is the free-space <Green function of the Laplacian> with the normalization of the <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity>. Differentiating gives inward <gravitational acceleration> $-GM/r^2$. With $z=r/r_0$, the normalized <Newtonian gravitational potential> is $\Phi/(GM/r_0)=-1/z$; at $z=1,2,4$ it equals $-1,-1/2,-1/4$.

For a radially outward launch from $r_0$, <conservation of mechanical energy> in the fixed-source <test particle> approximation gives the minimum <escape velocity>:
$$
\frac{E}{m}=\frac{v_0^2}{2}-\frac{GM}{r_0}=0
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad v_{\mathrm{esc}}=\sqrt{\frac{2GM}{r_0}}.
$$
At this threshold the <speed> tends to zero only at infinite separation. The model neglects air resistance, external <tidal forces>, and <general relativity>. <University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others/13.3 Gravitational Potential Energy and Total Energy>{ref}

A <circular orbit> at $r_0$ instead requires tangential <speed> $v_c=\sqrt{GM/r_0}$. Choosing units with $G=M=r_0=1$ gives $g_0=1$, $v_c=1$, and $v_{\mathrm{esc}}=\sqrt2\simeq1.41421$. A radial launch at <speed> $1$ reaches $r=2$ and returns; the same <speed> directed tangentially gives a <circular orbit>. <University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others/13.4 Satellite Orbits and Energy>{ref}

\Image[gravitational-escape-energy.png]
{title=Energy balance and outward radial speeds below, at, and above escape}
{description=Left: at the <escape velocity>, positive <kinetic energy> exactly balances negative <Newtonian gravitational potential energy>. Right: radial launches at normalized <speeds> $1$, $\sqrt2$, and $1.6$ respectively turn at $r/r_0=2$, approach zero <speed> at infinity, and retain a positive limiting <speed>. The plotted trajectories use a fixed source and the <test particle> approximation.}

= Spherically symmetric potential
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential}

A spherically symmetric potential depends only on distance $r$ from one point. Its force is central, so angular momentum is conserved.

= Newtonian gravitational potential energy
{c}
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}

Two point masses $m_1,m_2$ separated by distance $r$ have <potential energy>
$$
V(r)=-\frac{Gm_1m_2}{r}.
$$
The corresponding attractive force is $-\nabla V$.

This is the Newtonian point-mass specialization of <gravitational energy>, with the reference value chosen at infinite separation.

= Spherical binding-energy mass integral
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential energy}
{title2=$W=-\dfrac G2\int_0^\infty M(r)^2r^{-2}\,dr$}

Assembling a spherical mass distribution in shells gives <gravitational potential energy> $W=-G\int M(r)\,dM(r)/r$. Integration by parts gives the displayed identity when $M(r)^2/r$ vanishes at both endpoints. It includes each interacting pair once; no extra factor of one half belongs in the original shell-assembly integral.

= Minimum-separation binding-energy bounds
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential energy}
{title2=$B_0\leq rU\leq A_0$}

For positive <masses>, let $r$ be the actual minimum pairwise separation and use positive binding <energy> $U=\sum_{j<k}Gm_jm_k/r_{jk}$. If $m_{(1)},m_{(2)}$ are the smallest <masses>, then $Gm_{(1)}m_{(2)}\leq rU\leq G\sum_{j<k}m_jm_k$. Thus $U^{-1}$ is comparable to $r$ with constants depending only on the masses.

= Negative-energy minimum-separation upper bound
{parent=Minimum-separation binding-energy bounds}
{title2=$r\leq A_0/|E|$}

With $E=T-U<0$ and $T\geq0$, the positive binding <energy> satisfies $U\geq|E|$. The <minimum-separation binding-energy bounds> imply $r\leq A_0/|E|$. At least one pair stays close; this does not bound every particle's distance or exclude a <negative-energy gravitational collision>.

= Cavendish experiment
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}
{c}
{wiki}

The <Cavendish experiment> makes the attraction predicted by <Newton's law of universal gravitation> measurable between laboratory <masses>. Two small test <masses> hang at the ends of a horizontal rod in a <Cavendish torsion balance>; nearby larger source <masses> exert a tiny <torque>. A thin suspension fiber supplies an opposing <torque> as it twists. Measuring its twist and calibrating its <torsion constant> lets an experimenter infer the gravitational <force> and, in a modern analysis, the <gravitational constant>. https://www.phys.uconn.edu/~hamilton/phys258/N/cav.pdf[University of Connecticut, Physics 258/259, The Cavendish Experiment: apparatus and equations of motion, pp. 1–3]{ref}

The beautiful feature is that the two horizontal <forces> can cancel as vectors while their <torques> add. The instrument converts weak attraction into a rotation, with sensitivity set by the fiber's <torsion constant>. Earth's much larger downward <gravitational weight> is supported by the suspension rather than supplying the intended <torque> about its vertical axis. A laboratory measurement can therefore probe the same <Newtonian gravitational field> that governs the <two-body problem>.

Henry Cavendish's original paper was \i[Experiments to determine the Density of the Earth], published in \i[Philosophical Transactions], volume 88 (1798), pp. 469–526, and read on 21 June 1798. On p. 469 he credits John Michell with the apparatus idea and explains that Michell died before using it; the instrument reached Cavendish through Francis John Hyde Wollaston. Cavendish's stated aim was Earth's mean <mass density>. Calling the experiment a determination of the <gravitational constant> is a modern interpretation, not Cavendish's own formulation of his result. https://www.famaf.unc.edu.ar/~wolf/FisicaI/Phil.%20Trans.%20R.%20Soc.%20Lond.-1798-Cavendish-rstl.1798.0022.pdf[Cavendish (1798), original paper, opening p. 469; DOI 10.1098/rstl.1798.0022]{ref}

The connection between Earth's mean <mass density> and the <gravitational constant> follows in a spherical, nonrotating model of <Earth>. With Earth <mass> $M_E$, radius $R_E$, and surface <gravitational acceleration> $g$, the <spherical shell theorem> and <Newton's law of universal gravitation> give
$$
g=\frac{GM_E}{R_E^2},\qquad \overline\rho_E=\frac{3M_E}{4\pi R_E^3},\qquad G=\frac{3g}{4\pi R_E\overline\rho_E}.
$$
Thus a local comparison of gravitational attractions, together with knowledge of Earth's size and surface <gravitational acceleration>, reaches a property of the entire planet. These are modern equations explaining the connection, not equations quoted from Cavendish's paper.

For a worked example, use an explicitly simplified two-near-pair model. Each test <point mass> is $m$, each source <point mass> is $M$, and each <lever arm> is $L$, so the rod's full <length> is $2L$. Neglect the rod's <mass>. At zero twist put the test <masses> at $(-L,0)$ and $(L,0)$, and the sources at $(-L,-r)$ and $(L,r)$. Here $r$ is the near-pair center separation. Ignore each source's attraction on the test <mass> at the opposite end. Evaluate the two near-pair <forces> at zero twist and treat their <torque> as constant for a small twist, requiring $L|\theta|\ll r$. This is a teaching model, not a precision model of Cavendish's actual apparatus.

By <Newton's law of universal gravitation>, each retained <force> has magnitude $F=GmM/r^2$. The left <force> points down and the right <force> points up. Using <torque> about the suspension axis, both contributions are counterclockwise when viewed from above:
$$
\tau_{z,\mathrm{left}}=(-L)(-F)=LF,\qquad \tau_{z,\mathrm{right}}=LF,\qquad |\tau_g|=2L\frac{GmM}{r^2}.
$$
The net retained horizontal <force> is zero, yet the net <torque> is positive. Moving the sources to $(-L,r)$ and $(L,-r)$ reverses both <forces> and hence the <torque>. In a real apparatus, the opposite source also attracts each test <mass>; that correction is explicitly included in the university derivation. https://www.phys.uconn.edu/~hamilton/phys258/N/cav.pdf[University of Connecticut, The Cavendish Experiment, p. 2, equation (1): opposite-source correction]{ref}

\Image[cavendish-torsion-balance-schematic.png]
{title=Two near-pair forces give the same counterclockwise torque about a vertical suspension fiber}

For illustrative parameters, take $m=0.010\,\mathrm{kg}$, $M=1.0\,\mathrm{kg}$, $r=0.050\,\mathrm m$, $L=0.10\,\mathrm m$, and <torsion constant> $\kappa=1.0\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{N\,m/rad}$. Use $G=6.67430\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm{m^3\,kg^{-1}\,s^{-2}}$, the central value in NIST's 2022 CODATA table; that table gives standard uncertainty $0.00015\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm{m^3\,kg^{-1}\,s^{-2}}$. This is an input for the example, not a measurement made with these assumed parameters. <Fundamental Physical Constants — Complete Listing by National Institute of Standards and Technology and others>{ref}
$$
F=\frac{(6.67430\times10^{-11})(0.010)(1.0)}{(0.050)^2}=2.66972\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm N,\qquad |\tau_g|=2(0.10)F=5.33944\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm{N\,m}.
$$
This sub-nanonewton <force> produces a measurable twist in the <Cavendish torsion balance>. The displayed digits make the toy calculation reproducible; they do not describe experimental precision.

= Cavendish torsion balance
{parent=Cavendish experiment}
{c}

A <Cavendish torsion balance> is a <torsion balance> arranged to measure gravitational <torque> from nearby source <masses>. Its suspended horizontal rod rotates about a vertical fiber. In the linear elastic regime, a twist <angle> $\theta$, measured from the fiber's untwisted orientation, gives a restoring <torque> $\tau_{\mathrm{fiber}}=-\kappa\theta$, where $\kappa>0$ is the <torsion constant>. The minus sign is essential: the fiber resists the twist. Under an approximately constant gravitational <torque>, the rotational equation using <moment of inertia> $I$ and <angular acceleration> $\ddot\theta$ is
$$
I\ddot\theta=\tau_g-\kappa\theta,\qquad \theta_{\mathrm{eq}}=\frac{\tau_g}{\kappa}.
$$
At <equilibrium>, the two <torques> balance. The <angle> is a displacement from the fiber's reference orientation, not the largest excursion during an <oscillation>. https://www.phys.uconn.edu/~hamilton/phys258/N/cav.pdf[University of Connecticut, The Cavendish Experiment, pp. 2–3: restoring torque and equilibrium deflection]{ref}

For the toy parameters in the <Cavendish experiment>, the predicted <equilibrium> twist is
$$
\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}=\frac{5.33944\times10^{-11}}{1.0\times10^{-8}}=0.00533944\,\mathrm{rad}\simeq0.3059^\circ.
$$
Here $L|\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}|/r\simeq0.0107$, consistent with using a small geometric displacement in the toy model, but not an estimate of its total <measurement error>. Reversing the source positions ideally takes the <equilibrium> <angle> from $+\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}$ to $-\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}$: the magnitude of the change is $2|\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}|\simeq0.6119^\circ$. A stable zero offset then cancels in the difference. A real reading may track a reflected light spot; the <law of reflection> doubles a mirror's rotation in the outgoing beam, so the spot's angular change must not be confused with the rod's <angle>. https://sciencedemonstrations.fas.harvard.edu/presentations/cavendish-experiment[Harvard Natural Sciences Lecture Demonstrations, Cavendish Experiment: mirror readout and source reversal]{ref}

How can the fiber's <torsion constant> be found without already knowing the gravitational <force>? Measure the <oscillation period> and calculate the <moment of inertia>. For the same toy rod, neglecting its <mass> and treating both test <masses> as <point masses>, $I=mL^2+mL^2=2mL^2$. With the source <torque> absent or treated as constant, let $\phi=\theta-\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}$ be a small departure from <equilibrium>. Neglect <damping> and any change of gravitational <torque> with <angle>. The rotational equation becomes <simple harmonic motion>:
$$
I\ddot\phi+\kappa\phi=0,\qquad \phi(t)=A\cos(\omega t+\delta),\qquad \omega=\sqrt{\frac{\kappa}{I}},\qquad T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac I\kappa},\qquad \kappa=\frac{4\pi^2I}{T^2}.
$$
Here $A$ is the angular amplitude, $\delta$ specifies the initial phase, and $\omega$ is the <angular frequency>. A softer fiber gives a larger <equilibrium> twist but also a longer <oscillation period>: greater sensitivity costs observation time. An undamped <oscillation> does not settle at <equilibrium>; <damping> is needed for settling. https://www.phys.uconn.edu/~hamilton/phys258/N/cav.pdf[University of Connecticut, The Cavendish Experiment, p. 3, equation (7): period calibration neglecting damping]{ref}

The toy <moment of inertia> and undamped <oscillation period> are
$$
I=2(0.010)(0.10)^2=2.0\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2},\qquad T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{2.0\times10^{-4}}{1.0\times10^{-8}}}\simeq888.5766\,\mathrm s\simeq14.81\,\mathrm{min}.
$$
Timing this <oscillation period> would recover the assumed <torsion constant>. The units check explains the result: <torque> divided by <torsion constant> is an <angle> in <radians>, and $I/\kappa$ has units of <time> squared when <radians> are treated as dimensionless in <SI>.

\Image[torsion-balance-period-calibration.png]
{title=Undamped small torsional oscillations calibrate the restoring torque: a softer fiber has a longer period}

Combining the <equilibrium> twist with the period calibration, the two-near-pair model would infer the <gravitational constant> as
$$
G=\frac{\kappa|\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}|r^2}{2LmM}=\frac{4\pi^2Lr^2|\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}|}{MT^2}.
$$
The test <mass> cancels in the second expression because it enters both the gravitational <torque> and the toy <moment of inertia>. If the measured quantity is the magnitude $|\Delta\theta|$ of the change between opposite source positions, use $|\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}|=|\Delta\theta|/2$. Substituting the toy twist and <oscillation period> returns the input $G$; it is a consistency check, not independent experimental evidence.

The sensitivity of a <Cavendish torsion balance> also explains its practical difficulty. Air motion can exert unwanted <torque>; unequal <temperatures> can drive that motion. Cavendish discusses this effect on p. 471 and describes observing from outside a closed room to avoid disturbing the instrument. https://www.infinitoteatrodelcosmo.it/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/cavendish-1997-xxi-experiments-to-determine-the-density-of-the-earth.pdf[Cavendish (1798), original paper, p. 471: temperature-driven air currents and remote observation]{ref} Mechanical vibrations can excite the same slow <oscillations> used to calibrate the <torsion constant>, while changes in the fiber or source separations alter the inferred <force>. Precision therefore depends on resolving the <equilibrium> <angle>, calibrating the full suspended <moment of inertia> and <torsion constant>, and modeling the actual source geometry. The two-near-pair model omits opposite-source attraction, rod contributions, finite-size effects, <damping>, and the variation of gravitational <torque> as the rod turns. https://sciencedemonstrations.fas.harvard.edu/presentations/cavendish-experiment[Harvard Cavendish demonstration: sensitivity, settling, and separation-dependent measurement]{ref}

= Torsion balance
{parent=Classical mechanics}

A <torsion balance> measures a weak <torque> by suspending a body from a thin fiber and reading its twist <angle>. The fiber supports the body's <gravitational weight> while permitting rotation about the suspension axis. In the range of <linear elasticity>, a twist $\theta$ in <radians>, measured from the untwisted reference orientation, produces a restoring <torque>
$$
\tau_{\mathrm{fiber}}=-\kappa\theta,\qquad \kappa>0.
$$
The <torsion constant> $\kappa$ sets the sensitivity. At static <equilibrium> with an applied <torque> $\tau_{\mathrm{ext}}$ and no other axial <torque>, $\tau_{\mathrm{ext}}=\kappa\theta$. A perpendicular <force> $F$ at <lever arm> $a$ therefore gives $\theta=aF/\kappa$. The <torsion balance> measures a turning effect: a <force> whose line of action passes through the suspension axis gives no axial <torque>. The <Cavendish torsion balance> is the gravitational application; <Coulomb's law> supplies an electrical one. https://cdn.pasco.com/product_document/Coulombs-Law-Apparatus-Manual-ES-9070.pdf[Bruce Lee, PASCO ES-9070 Coulomb Balance manual, pp. 1 and 7: twist measurement of electric force]{ref}

\Image[torsion-balance-readout.png]
{title=Torsion balance readout: fiber twist, force geometry, and returning the body to a fixed angle}

The original schematic separates two ways of reading the same <torque>. In deflection operation the top attachment stays fixed and the body rotates. In null operation the experimenter rotates the top attachment by $\alpha$ while returning the body's laboratory <angle> $\beta$ to its original value. If the initially untwisted configuration has $\alpha=\beta=0$, the fiber twist is $\theta=\beta-\alpha$, not $\beta$ alone. Thus
$$
\tau_{\mathrm{fiber}}=-\kappa(\beta-\alpha),\qquad \tau_{\mathrm{ext}}=\kappa(\beta-\alpha).
$$
For a positive applied <torque>, holding $\beta=0$ requires $\alpha=-\tau_{\mathrm{ext}}/\kappa$. Keeping the body at the same <angle> keeps the <lever arm> and source geometry fixed, making comparison of different applied <forces> easier. Either readout still requires a calibrated <torsion constant> and a stable reference orientation.

An illustrative calculation shows the amplification: with $a=0.050\,\mathrm m$, $F=2.0\times10^{-9}\,\mathrm N$, and $\kappa=1.0\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{N\,m/rad}$, the applied <torque> is $1.0\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm{N\,m}$ and the <equilibrium> twist is $0.010\,\mathrm{rad}\simeq0.573^\circ$. These are stipulated values, not measured data. Reducing the <torsion constant> tenfold increases this twist tenfold, provided the fiber remains in its calibrated <linear elasticity> range. The cost is a slower response, explained by the <oscillation period> in <torsion constant>.

A concrete laboratory experiment is PASCO's Coulomb balance: level its counterbalanced rod, discharge both conducting spheres, and set the unloaded zero. Charge the spheres at maximum separation; switch off the charging supply. Move the second sphere to a known center separation $R$, then turn the suspension dial until the rod returns to its index mark. Record the dial change and $R$, recharge, and repeat at several separations. The manual recommends a draft-free room, clean insulating supports, and recharging before each reading: leakage, especially in humid conditions, otherwise changes the <electric charges> during the sequence. At close separations the conducting spheres redistribute charge, so the <point charge> approximation needs correction. https://cdn.pasco.com/product_document/Coulombs-Law-Apparatus-Manual-ES-9070.pdf[Bruce Lee, PASCO ES-9070 manual, pp. 4–8: setup, force-versus-distance procedure, and corrections]{ref}

For constant <electric charges> and a fixed perpendicular <lever arm>, the <Coulomb's law> prediction is $|\theta|\propto R^{-2}$. Plotting the <logarithm> of twist magnitude against the <logarithm> of separation gives $\log|\theta|=\text{constant}-2\log R$, so the predicted slope is $-2$. Absolute <force> measurement additionally needs the <torsion constant>; comparing ratios can eliminate it if it remains stable. Repeating a reference separation between other settings distinguishes a separation effect from a changing signal. A declining reading at the reference separation would invalidate the assumption of unchanged <electric charges>, even if a distance plot looks smooth.

The historical electrical example is Coulomb's first memoir of 1785. His apparatus enclosed a suspended needle with a small ball and a second ball; an adjustable head twisted the suspension. After charging the balls by contact, he reported angular separations $36^\circ$, $18^\circ$, and $8.5^\circ$ with head settings $0^\circ$, $126^\circ$, and $567^\circ$. The total twists were approximately $36^\circ$, $144^\circ$, and $576^\circ$: fourfold restoring <torque> accompanied approximately halved separation. These are his reported observations, not data from our diagrams. https://cnum.cnam.fr/pgi/redir.php?ident=8CA121-1&onglet=c[Coulomb, Premier mémoire (1785), reprinted in Mémoires de Coulomb, pp. 104–109, CNAM digitization]{ref} A precise reconstruction must use center distances and the changing <lever arm>, rather than identify angular separation exactly with distance; Alberto A. Martínez's replication explicitly analyzes that geometry. https://martinezwritings.com/m/Coulomb_files/CoulombExperiment-1.pdf[Martínez, Replication of Coulomb's Torsion Balance Experiment (2006), pp. 522–525]{ref}

Sensitivity also exposes changes of the apparatus itself. Fleischer and colleagues' cryogenic <torsion balance> used a temperature-controlled enclosure to reduce vacuum-vessel top-plate <temperature> fluctuations to approximately $\pm0.01\,\mathrm K$, limiting changes in suspension geometry from <thermal expansion>. They used a mild bake-out near $40^\circ\mathrm C$ to speed initial fiber relaxation and reduce slow zero motion. Their optical readout compared the pendulum mirror with an internal reference mirror to reject shared optical motion. Room-temperature <thermal radiation> entering through the optical opening heated the pendulum; a cold filter reduced that load. The authors could not directly measure the pendulum's <temperature> without disturbing it, and explicitly distinguished the cold shield's <temperature> from the body's. Cooling therefore requires checking the actual noise and thermal environment, rather than assuming that the fiber and body share the shield's <temperature>. https://arxiv.org/pdf/2206.02890[Fleischer et al., A Cryogenic Torsion Balance Using a Liquid-Cryogen Free, Ultra-Low Vibration Cryostat (2022), §§II B–C and III A]{ref}

A useful control strategy is to record unloaded readings and <temperature> before and after each signal sequence, recalibrate the <torsion constant> under the same conditions, and compare forward and reverse settings. The algebra of <torsion balance zero drift> shows precisely which offsets such comparisons can cancel. A fine angular scale alone does not establish a small <measurement error>.

= Torsion constant
{parent=Torsion balance}
{title2=$\kappa$}

= Torsional spring constant
{synonym}

The <torsion constant>, or <torsional spring constant>, is the positive restoring <torque> per unit twist <angle> of a complete suspension in its <linear elasticity> range:
$$
\tau_{\mathrm{fiber}}=-\kappa\theta,\qquad \kappa=-\frac{d\tau_{\mathrm{fiber}}}{d\theta}>0.
$$
Its <SI> unit is $\mathrm{N\,m/rad}$. The minus sign describes restoration toward the untwisted orientation. The derivative specifies a local stiffness if the response is not globally linear; using one constant over a finite range additionally requires a straight calibration curve. Return to the same zero after unloading tests recoverable <elastic deformation>; repeat loading in both directions to check whether the response depends on its previous loading. Staying below permanent deformation is necessary, but does not alone prove proportionality between <torque> and twist.

For an ideal uniform circular wire of <length> $\ell$, radius $r$, and <shear modulus> $G_s$, the <filament torsional stiffness> is $C=G_sJ_p$, with polar <second moment of area> $J_p=\pi r^4/2$. Uniform twist gives $\theta/\ell$ as the twist per unit <length>, so
$$
\kappa=\frac C\ell=\frac{\pi G_s r^4}{2\ell},\qquad U=\frac12\kappa\theta^2.
$$
Here $U$ is stored <elastic energy>. Doubling the wire's radius multiplies the ideal <torsion constant> by $16$; doubling its <length> halves it. This explains why a long thin fiber is useful for weak <torque> measurement. The formula assumes <linear elasticity> with uniform material and circular section; it does not calibrate arbitrary ribbons, clamps, or a compound suspension. Within this ideal model, the <equipartition theorem> predicts an equilibrium thermal mean-square angular displacement $\langle\theta^2\rangle=k_B\mathcal T/\kappa$, where $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant> and $\mathcal T$ the uniform absolute <temperature>. A softer suspension magnifies both the signal and its <thermal fluctuations>.

Static calibration supplies known <torques> without assuming the unknown interaction being measured. Apply a known perpendicular <force> $F$ at <lever arm> $a$, record the change from the unloaded twist, and use
$$
\tau_{\mathrm{cal}}=aF,\qquad \kappa=\frac{\tau_{\mathrm{cal}}}{\Delta\theta}.
$$
Several positive and negative settings test proportionality; fit the slope of applied <torque> against twist and retain a possible intercept. A nonzero intercept must not be silently absorbed into the slope. Convert degrees to <radians> before reporting $\mathrm{N\,m/rad}$. PASCO's procedure turns the Coulomb balance on its side and adds small known <masses> to generate a <gravitational weight> at the sphere's <lever arm>, returning the rod to its index mark by turning the dial. Its tabulated coefficient is <force> per degree: multiply by the <lever arm> and $180/\pi$ to obtain our <torsion constant>. https://cdn.pasco.com/product_document/Coulombs-Law-Apparatus-Manual-ES-9070.pdf[Bruce Lee, PASCO ES-9070 manual, pp. 9–10: mass calibration and force-per-degree convention]{ref}

\Image[torsion-balance-calibration.png]
{title=Toy torsion calibrations: static slope and a period corrected for viscous damping}

The figure's ideal static calibration uses $\kappa=1.0\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{N\,m/rad}$: an applied <torque> of $2.0\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm{N\,m}$ gives $0.020\,\mathrm{rad}$. Both signs lie on the same straight line. The plotted points are calculated examples, not experimental observations; they cannot demonstrate a real fiber's linearity.

Dynamic calibration instead measures the <oscillation period> $T_0$ with known total suspended <moment of inertia> $I$. For negligible <damping>, small free <oscillations> about a fixed reference obey <simple harmonic motion>:
$$
I\ddot\theta+\kappa\theta=0,\qquad T_0=2\pi\sqrt{\frac I\kappa},\qquad \kappa=\frac{4\pi^2I}{T_0^2}.
$$
Include the rod, mirrors, and any attachments in $I$ unless their contributions are demonstrably negligible. Keep the applied <torque> absent or independent of <angle>. If an external <torque> has derivative $g_\tau=d\tau_{\mathrm{ext}}/d\theta$ at <equilibrium>, linearization gives effective stiffness $\kappa-g_\tau$: timing alone then measures that combination, and stability requires $\kappa-g_\tau>0$. A constant applied <torque> merely shifts <equilibrium>. Non-negligible <damping> changes the measured period; <damped torsional oscillation> gives a correction under a specified damping model. Agreement between static and dynamic calibrations is a useful check on the assumed <moment of inertia>, reference stability, and restoring law, rather than a reason to ignore their assumptions.

= Damped torsional oscillation
{parent=Torsion constant}
{title2=$I\ddot\theta+b\dot\theta+\kappa\theta=0$}

A <damped torsional oscillation> is rotation about a fiber's fixed reference orientation with a restoring <torque> and dissipative <torque>. In the ideal viscous model, <damping> contributes $-b\dot\theta$ with constant $b\geq0$. This is a rotational <damped harmonic oscillator>. For known constant <moment of inertia> $I$ and <torsion constant> $\kappa$, define $\gamma=b/(2I)$. In the oscillatory regime $\gamma^2<\kappa/I$,
$$
\theta(t)=A e^{-\gamma t}\cos(\omega_dt+\varphi),\qquad \omega_d^2=\frac\kappa I-\gamma^2,\qquad T_d=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_d}.
$$
$A$ and $\varphi$ are set by the initial state. Successive positive peaks measured from the same <equilibrium> have magnitudes $p_n$ and $p_{n+1}$; their ratio gives $\delta=\ln(p_n/p_{n+1})=\gamma T_d$. Consequently a measured damped <oscillation period> and decay together recover
$$
\boxed{\kappa=I\left[\left(\frac{2\pi}{T_d}\right)^2+\gamma^2\right]=\frac I{T_d^2}(4\pi^2+\delta^2).}
$$
Using only $4\pi^2I/T_d^2$ underestimates the <torsion constant> in this model. The toy curve in <torsion constant> sets $I=2.0\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2}$, $\kappa=1.0\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{N\,m/rad}$, and $\gamma=0.020\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. It gives $T_0\simeq88.86\,\mathrm s$, $T_d\simeq92.64\,\mathrm s$, and $\delta\simeq1.853$. Ignoring the decay recovers $9.2\times10^{-9}\,\mathrm{N\,m/rad}$, an $8\%$ underestimate; including it recovers the stipulated <torsion constant>.

These equations assume a single rotational mode, a stationary zero, <linear elasticity>, and viscous <damping>. Internal fiber losses need not behave as a constant viscous coefficient: Fleischer and colleagues model their precision apparatus using a loss angle. Their response is evidence for specifying a damping model, not for applying the viscous correction universally. https://arxiv.org/pdf/2206.02890[Fleischer et al. (2022), §I, equation (1): thermal torque noise and internal damping represented by a loss angle]{ref}

= Torsion balance zero drift
{parent=Torsion balance}
{title2=$\beta_{\mathrm{eq}}=\beta_0(t)+\tau_{\mathrm{ext}}/\kappa$}

<Torsion balance zero drift> is slow motion of the unloaded reference <angle> $\beta_0(t)$ during a measurement. If the <torsion constant> remains fixed, a constant applied <torque> gives $\beta_{\mathrm{eq}}=\beta_0(t)+\tau_{\mathrm{ext}}/\kappa$. For two equal and opposite applied <torques> measured at the same reference zero, their <equilibrium> <angles> differ by $2\tau_{\mathrm{ext}}/\kappa$. A stable offset cancels; a changing zero contributes $\beta_0(t_+)-\beta_0(t_-)$ and does not cancel automatically.

A simple symmetric sequence makes the limitation explicit. Let $b_1,b_2,b_3$ be settled <equilibrium> <angles> for applied <torques> $+\tau,-\tau,+\tau$ at equally spaced times $t_0-h,t_0,t_0+h$. If the unloaded zero is linear in <time>, $\beta_0(t)=u+vt$, and $\kappa$ and $\tau$ remain fixed, then
$$
\widehat\tau=\frac\kappa2\left(\frac{b_1+b_3}{2}-b_2\right)=\tau.
$$
The arithmetic average of the outer zero values equals the middle zero, so both the constant offset and linear drift cancel. Curved zero motion, unequal time spacing, changing <torsion constant>, changing signal strength, or unsettled <oscillations> violate these assumptions. This is an algebraic control example, not a claim that every <torsion balance> follows a linear drift law.

= Degree of freedom
{parent=Classical mechanics}

= Degrees of freedom
{synonym}

A degree of freedom is an independent coordinate needed to specify a mechanical configuration after all <mechanical constraints> have been imposed.

= Constraint
{disambiguate=mechanics}
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

= Mechanical constraint
{synonym}

= Constraint
{synonym}

A mechanical constraint restricts the allowed configurations or velocities of a system and reduces its <degrees of freedom>.

= Bead on a parabolic wire
{parent=Constraint (mechanics)}
{title2=$T=2\sqrt{2a/g}\int_{-1}^1\sqrt{\frac{1+(h/a)u^2}{1-u^2}}\,du$}

For a smooth stationary wire $z=x^2/(4a)$ and release from rest at height $h$, the <normal reaction> does no work. The energy equation is $[1+x^2/(4a^2)]\dot x^2/2+gx^2/(4a)=gh$. Integrating the travel time between turning points gives the displayed period. Its small-amplitude expansion is $T=2\pi\sqrt{2a/g}[1+h/(4a)+O((h/a)^2)]$. The mass cancels because both gravitational and inertial forces are proportional to it.

= Constraint surface
{parent=Constraint (mechanics)}
{title2=$C_a=0$}

The common zero locus of constraint functions $C_a$ in <phase space> is the constraint surface. A physical trajectory lies on this surface, but off-surface identities are often needed when analyzing a <constraint algebra>.

= Holonomic constraint
{parent=Constraint (mechanics)}
{wiki}

A <holonomic constraint> is a relation $f(q,t)=0$ involving generalized coordinates and possibly time, rather than a genuinely nonintegrable velocity relation. Independent smooth constraints of full row rank define a local configuration submanifold. Ideal constraint forces are normal to its tangent virtual displacements and can be represented by <Lagrange multipliers>. A time-dependent <holonomic constraint> need not conserve mechanical <energy>.

= First-class constraint
{parent=Constraint (mechanics)}
{title2=$\varphi_i\approx0$}

= First class
{synonym}

A first-class constraint has <Poisson brackets> with all constraints that vanish on the constraint surface. For a regular constrained <Hamiltonian> system, first-class constraints generate <gauge transformations>; each independent constraint and its gauge direction remove one <canonical pair>.

= Regular first-class phase-space reduction
{parent=First-class constraint}
{title2=$\dim\Gamma_{\mathrm{phys}}=2N-2n$}

For $n$ independent regular <first-class constraints> in a $2N$-dimensional phase space, imposing the equations removes $n$ dimensions and quotienting independent gauge orbits removes another $n$. Reducibility, singular strata or vanishing gauge directions can invalidate that elementary count. Closure of the <constraint algebra> is necessary but does not by itself establish regularity.

= Canonical gauge transformation
{parent=First-class constraint}
{title2=$\delta F=\{F,\epsilon^i\varphi_i\}$}

A <first-class constraint> generates an infinitesimal <canonical transformation> $\delta F=\{F,\epsilon^i\varphi_i\}$. For the <phase-space action> $I=\int(p\dot q-\lambda^i\varphi_i)dt$, the multiplier transformation $\delta\lambda^k=\dot\epsilon^k-\lambda^i\epsilon^jC_{ij}{}^k$ cancels the bulk variation when $\{\varphi_i,\varphi_j\}=C_{ij}{}^k\varphi_k$. The remaining temporal boundary term vanishes for parameters that vanish at the endpoints.

= Constraint algebra
{parent=First-class constraint}
{title2=$\{\varphi_i,\varphi_j\}=f_{ij}{}^k\varphi_k$}

The <Poisson brackets> of <first-class constraints> close on the constraints. When the constraints are independent and the coefficients are constant, the <Jacobi identity for the Poisson bracket> makes the coefficients <structure constants> of a <Lie algebra>.

= Structure functions of a constraint algebra
{parent=Constraint algebra}
{title2=$C_{ij}{}^k(q,p)$}

The coefficients $C_{ij}{}^k(q,p)$ in $\{\varphi_i,\varphi_j\}=C_{ij}{}^k\varphi_k$ are structure functions. A finite real span of <first-class constraints> is a <Lie algebra> when it closes with constant coefficients. General structure functions instead describe closure with phase-space-dependent coefficients, while still permitting <canonical gauge transformations>.

= Position
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki=Position_(geometry)}

Position specifies the location of a particle relative to a coordinate system.

= Plane polar coordinates
{title2=$(r,\theta)$}
{parent=Position}

Plane polar coordinates describe a point by its radial distance $r$ and polar angle $\theta$.

These are the planar <polar coordinates>; the general coordinate-system definition and transformations belong to that canonical concept.

= Acceleration in polar coordinates
{parent=Plane polar coordinates}
{title2=$\mathbf a=(\ddot r-r\dot\theta^2)\widehat{\mathbf r}+(2\dot r\dot\theta+r\ddot\theta)\widehat{\boldsymbol\theta}$}

Differentiating $\mathbf r=r\widehat{\mathbf r}$ twice and using $\dot{\widehat{\mathbf r}}=\dot\theta\widehat{\boldsymbol\theta}$ and $\dot{\widehat{\boldsymbol\theta}}=-\dot\theta\widehat{\mathbf r}$ gives the displayed <acceleration>. For a <central force>, its angular component vanishes, so $r^2\dot\theta$ is constant and expresses <conservation of angular momentum>.

= Velocity
{parent=Position}
{title2=$\mathbf v=d\mathbf r/dt$}
{wiki}

<Velocity> is the <vector> giving the rate of change of <position> with <time>, measured in a specified <reference frame>. For a differentiable path $\mathbf r(t)$, its instantaneous <velocity> and its average <velocity> between two <times> are
$$
\mathbf v(t)=\frac{d\mathbf r}{dt},\qquad \mathbf v_{\mathrm{avg}}=\frac{\mathbf r(t_2)-\mathbf r(t_1)}{t_2-t_1},\qquad t_2>t_1.
$$
The <displacement> in the numerator retains direction. In <Cartesian coordinates>, each <vector component> is a <derivative>, for example $v_x=dx/dt$; its sign tells whether the corresponding coordinate is increasing or decreasing. The <SI> unit of <velocity> is $\mathrm{m/s}$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-2-instantaneous-velocity-and-speed[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics Volume 1, section 3.2: instantaneous velocity and speed]{ref}

For a concrete reversal, let $x(t)=(1\,\mathrm m)+(1\,\mathrm{m/s^2})t^2-(2\,\mathrm{m/s})t$ for $0\le t\le2\,\mathrm s$. Its signed <velocity> is $v_x(t)=(2\,\mathrm{m/s^2})t-2\,\mathrm{m/s}$: at $t=0$ it is $-2\,\mathrm{m/s}$, at $t=1\,\mathrm s$ it is zero, and at $t=2\,\mathrm s$ it is $+2\,\mathrm{m/s}$. The opposite endpoint <velocities> describe opposite directions, although the endpoint <speeds> agree. The <position> moves from $1\,\mathrm m$ to $0$ and back to $1\,\mathrm m$.

Recovering <displacement> from a measured <velocity> is an <integration> problem: $\mathbf r(t_2)-\mathbf r(t_1)=\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\mathbf v(t)\,dt$, with the <integral> taken component by component. In the reversal example, $\int_0^{2\,\mathrm s}v_x(t)\,dt=0$: the negative and positive contributions cancel. This distinction is useful in navigation, where the final <position> depends on direction as well as <speed>.

= Normal velocity
{parent=Velocity}
{title2=$v_n=v\cdot n$}

The <normal velocity> is the scalar component of a <velocity> in a specified unit-normal direction. Its sign depends on the orientation of the normal. At a material interface, no penetration equates the fluid and interface <normal velocities>; it does not require their tangential components to agree. <Surface acoustic impedance> uses a fixed inward normal-velocity convention to determine the sign of energy absorbed by a boundary.

= Speed
{parent=Velocity}
{title2=$v=|\mathbf v|$}
{wiki}

<Speed> is the nonnegative magnitude of <velocity>, a <scalar> rather than a <vector>. In orthonormal <Cartesian coordinates>,
$$
v(t)=|\mathbf v(t)|=\sqrt{v_x(t)^2+v_y(t)^2+v_z(t)^2}.
$$
Its <SI> unit is $\mathrm{m/s}$. In the reversal example under <velocity>, the endpoint <velocities> $-2\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and $+2\,\mathrm{m/s}$ both have <speed> $2\,\mathrm{m/s}$, but the <speed> falls to zero between them. A <speed> reading alone therefore cannot tell which way the body is moving.

For a smooth path, the total distance traveled is its <arc length>, $L=\int_{t_1}^{t_2}v(t)\,dt$. Unlike the <integral> of a signed <velocity> component, this <integral> cannot cancel outward and return motion. The reversal example travels $2\,\mathrm m$ in $2\,\mathrm s$, giving an average <speed> of $1\,\mathrm{m/s}$, while its average <velocity> is zero. This makes <speed> useful for estimating travel duration or fuel use, while <velocity> also tracks progress toward a destination. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-2-instantaneous-velocity-and-speed[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, section 3.2: distance traveled versus displacement]{ref}

= Acceleration
{parent=Velocity}
{title2=$\mathbf a=d\mathbf v/dt$}
{wiki}

<Acceleration> is the <time> <derivative> of <velocity>, equivalently the second <derivative> of <position>:
$$
\mathbf a(t)=\frac{d\mathbf v}{dt}=\frac{d^2\mathbf r}{dt^2}.
$$
It is a <vector> with <SI> unit $\mathrm{m/s^2}$. A change in direction produces <acceleration> even if <speed> stays constant. For example, a car rounding a bend at steady <speed> needs an inward resultant <force> by <Newton's second law>. Thus <acceleration> does not mean only speeding up. When $v>0$, the <dot product> gives $dv/dt=\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf a/v$: a perpendicular <acceleration> changes direction without changing <speed> at that instant.

Take counterclockwise <circular motion> about the origin with <radius> $R=2\,\mathrm m$ and constant <angular speed> $\omega=0.5\,\mathrm{rad/s}$. The <position>, <velocity> and <acceleration> are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf r(t)&=(R\cos(\omega t),R\sin(\omega t)),\\
\mathbf v(t)&=(-R\omega\sin(\omega t),R\omega\cos(\omega t)),\\
\mathbf a(t)&=(-R\omega^2\cos(\omega t),-R\omega^2\sin(\omega t))=-\omega^2\mathbf r(t).
\end{aligned}
$$
The <speed> is $R\omega=1\,\mathrm{m/s}$ throughout, while the inward <centripetal acceleration> has magnitude $R\omega^2=v^2/R=0.5\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$. At the four cardinal points, reached at $t=0,\pi,2\pi,3\pi\,\mathrm s$, the <velocities> in $\mathrm{m/s}$ are $(0,1),(-1,0),(0,-1),(1,0)$; the <accelerations> in $\mathrm{m/s^2}$ are $(-0.5,0),(0,-0.5),(0.5,0),(0,0.5)$. The <vectors> turn together, remaining perpendicular. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/4-4-uniform-and-nonuniform-circular-motion[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, section 4.4: uniform circular motion]{ref}

\Image[constant-speed-circular-acceleration.png]
{title=Constant speed with changing velocity and inward acceleration at four points of a circle}

The proof outline is just two applications of the <chain rule> to $\mathbf r(t)$; $\sin^2(\omega t)+\cos^2(\omega t)=1$ then fixes both magnitudes. The usefulness is geometric: turning requires a <force> even when a <speed> reading remains steady, and doubling <speed> at fixed <radius> quadruples the required <centripetal acceleration>. The same mechanism appears in a <circular orbit>.

Historically, Newton's \i[Principia], Book I, Section II, Proposition IV, relates the inward <forces> of uniform <circular motion> to the squared arcs traversed in equal <times>, divided by their <radii>. In modern notation, its dependence on <speed> and <radius> is $v^2/R$; Newton presents a geometric argument rather than the coordinate <derivatives> above. https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Mathematical_Principles_of_Natural_Philosophy_(1729)/Book_1/Section_2[Newton, Principia, Book I, Proposition IV; Motte's 1729 translation]{ref}

= Jerk
{parent=Acceleration}
{disambiguate=kinematics}
{title2=$\mathbf j=d\mathbf a/dt=d^3\mathbf r/dt^3$}
{wiki=Jerk_(physics)}

= Kinematic jerk
{synonym}

The kinematic jerk is the time derivative of <acceleration>. For a <Kepler orbit> with relative position $\mathbf r$, relative velocity $\mathbf v$, $r=|\mathbf r|$, $\dot r=\mathbf r\cdot\mathbf v/r$ and total <mass> $M$, differentiation of <Newton's law of universal gravitation> gives $\mathbf j=-GM(\mathbf v-3\dot r\,\mathbf r/r)/r^3$.

= Constant speed
{parent=Velocity}

A path has <constant speed> when the magnitude of its <velocity> does not vary with <time>. Its <acceleration> may still be nonzero: uniform <circular motion> has turning <velocity> and inward <centripetal acceleration>. Constant <velocity> is stronger, requiring unchanged direction as well as unchanged <speed>.

= Terminal velocity
{parent=Velocity}
{title2=$\mathbf v_T$}
{wiki}

<Terminal velocity> is a constant limiting <velocity> approached when a <velocity>-dependent resistance balances the other applied <forces>. A falling body may keep moving while its net <force> and <acceleration> tend to zero. For downward motion in a stationary medium, its limiting <speed> is set by balancing <gravitational weight> against <drag force>, including <buoyancy> if appreciable. The balance, rather than a universal numerical value, is the defining idea. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-4-drag-force-and-terminal-speed[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, section 6.4: drag and terminal speed]{ref}

The <terminal velocity under linear drag> gives a simple example with an explicit approach to the limit. Real falling objects need an appropriate resistance model: <linear drag> is useful for slow motion of a small sphere in <Stokes flow>, whereas many faster falling objects require <quadratic drag>. Changing <mass> also changes the question: the existing <accreting raindrop with quadratic drag> has a <speed-radius attractor of an accreting raindrop>, rather than the fixed-<mass> balance used below.

= Terminal velocity under linear drag
{parent=Terminal velocity}
{title2=$v_T=Mg/b,\quad\tau=M/b$}

Consider a <point mass> of fixed <mass> $M>0$ released from rest in a stationary medium. Choose downward as positive, assume constant <gravitational acceleration> $g>0$, neglect <buoyancy>, and impose <linear drag> $F_d=-bv$ with constant <linear friction coefficient> $b>0$. Here $v$ is the signed downward <velocity> component; it equals <speed> during this descent. <Newton's second law> becomes
$$
M\frac{dv}{dt}=Mg-bv,\qquad v(0)=0,\qquad v_T=\frac{Mg}{b},\quad\tau=\frac{M}{b}.
$$
The <terminal velocity> balances the two <forces>, and the <time constant> $\tau$ controls how quickly that balance is approached:
$$
v(t)=v_T(1-e^{-t/\tau}),\qquad a(t)=\frac{dv}{dt}=ge^{-t/\tau},\qquad t\ge0.
$$
For the illustrative parameters $M=1\,\mathrm{kg}$, $b=2\,\mathrm{kg/s}$ and $g=9.8\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$, the <time constant> is $0.5\,\mathrm s$ and the <terminal velocity> is $4.9\,\mathrm{m/s}$ downward. At $t=1\,\mathrm s=2\tau$, the downward <velocity> is $4.9(1-e^{-2})\,\mathrm{m/s}\simeq4.23686\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and the downward <acceleration> is $9.8e^{-2}\,\mathrm{m/s^2}\simeq1.32629\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$. This is a toy <linear drag> model, not a claim that every $1\,\mathrm{kg}$ falling object has this resistance law.

\Image[linear-drag-terminal-velocity.png]
{title=Exponential approach to terminal velocity and decay of acceleration in an illustrative linear drag model}

For a proof outline, subtract the equilibrium value: $u=v-v_T$ obeys $du/dt=-u/\tau$. The <exponential decay> solution and the initial condition give $u=-v_Te^{-t/\tau}$; one <derivative> gives the <acceleration>. Every <time constant> multiplies the remaining gap $v_T-v$ by $e^{-1}$. The exact limit is reached only as $t\to\infty$. This makes the model useful for estimating a settling duration without mistaking vanishing <acceleration> for vanishing <velocity>.

Newton already analyzed vertical motion under uniform <gravity> and resistance proportional to <velocity> in Book II, Section I, Proposition III of the \i[Principia]. His geometric construction includes a greatest downward <velocity>, illustrating that a limiting rate can emerge from balancing a driving <force> and resistance. https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Mathematical_Principles_of_Natural_Philosophy_(1729)/Book_2/Section_1[Newton, Principia, Book II, Proposition III; Motte's 1729 translation]{ref}

= Momentum
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{title2=$\mathbf p$}
{wiki}

= Linear momentum
{synonym}

= Momenta
{synonym}

For a nonrelativistic <point mass> of fixed <mass> $m>0$, measured in an <inertial frame>, its <momentum> is
$$
\mathbf p=m\mathbf v.
$$
<Momentum> is a <vector> pointing along the <velocity>, with <International System of Units> unit $\mathrm{kg\,m/s}$. Doubling either <mass> or <velocity> doubles <momentum>. In one dimension, a chosen positive direction makes $p=mv$ a signed quantity: a $2\,\mathrm{kg}$ body moving right at $3\,\mathrm{m/s}$ has $p=+6\,\mathrm{kg\,m/s}$; moving left at the same <speed> gives $p=-6\,\mathrm{kg\,m/s}$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-1-linear-momentum[OpenStax University Physics Volume 1, section 9.1: Linear Momentum]{ref}

For a collection, total <momentum> is the <vector> sum $\mathbf P=\sum_i m_i\mathbf v_i$. Two equal <masses> moving with opposite <velocities> can have zero total <momentum> while both have positive <kinetic energy>. Direction is therefore essential when using <momentum conservation>; adding <speeds> instead of signed <velocities> gives the wrong balance.

<Newton's second law> connects the net <force> on one fixed-<mass> <point mass> to its <momentum> through $\mathbf F_{\mathrm{net}}=d\mathbf p/dt$. Integrating that change gives <impulse>. Here $\mathbf p$ denotes <mechanical momentum>; when <canonical momentum> differs from <mass> times <velocity>, the distinction in <mechanical momentum> below applies. The nonrelativistic formula also has a limited regime: <special relativity> is needed at <speeds> comparable with the <speed of light>.

= Mechanical momentum
{parent=Momentum}
{title2=$\boldsymbol\pi=\mathbf p-q\mathbf A$}

For a charged particle minimally coupled to a magnetic <vector potential>, the mechanical momentum is $\boldsymbol\pi=\mathbf p-q\mathbf A$, with $\mathbf p$ the <canonical momentum>. It is the <mass> times <velocity> in nonrelativistic mechanics.

= Momentum conservation
{parent=Momentum}

= Conservation of momentum
{synonym}

<Momentum conservation> states that total <momentum> stays constant when the net <force> exerted from outside the chosen collection is zero. Use a closed collection, meaning the same fixed-<mass> <point masses> are followed throughout, and an <inertial frame>. Then
$$
\mathbf P=\sum_i\mathbf p_i,\qquad
\frac{d\mathbf P}{dt}=\sum_i\mathbf F_i^{\mathrm{ext}}.
$$
To see why, sum <Newton's second law> over the members. If $\mathbf F_{ij}$ is the <force> on member $i$ from member $j$, <Newton's third law> assumes $\mathbf F_{ij}=-\mathbf F_{ji}$. Each pair cancels in the total:
$$
\frac{d\mathbf P}{dt}
=\sum_i\mathbf F_i^{\mathrm{ext}}
+\sum_{i<j}(\mathbf F_{ij}+\mathbf F_{ji})
=\sum_i\mathbf F_i^{\mathrm{ext}}.
$$
The internal <forces> act on different bodies and can change their individual <momenta>; their contributions cancel only in the collection's sum. An <isolated system> has no external interaction, but balanced external <forces> also suffice for <momentum conservation>. A <closed system> merely excluding matter exchange need not have conserved <momentum>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-3-conservation-of-linear-momentum[OpenStax University Physics Volume 1, section 9.3: Conservation of Linear Momentum]{ref}

The usefulness is that the detailed internal <forces> during a <collision> need not be known. Both an <elastic collision> and a <perfectly inelastic collision> obey the same isolated <momentum> balance; <kinetic energy> conservation is an additional condition for the <elastic collision>. If the external <impulse> is small but nonzero, the total <momentum> change is correspondingly small. For a <variable-mass system> exchanging matter, the entering or leaving <momentum> must also be included.

In a translation-invariant field theory, integrating over the spacetime zero mode enforces momentum conservation at interactions and scattering amplitudes.

= Rocket equation
{parent=Momentum}

= Tsiolkovsky rocket equation
{c}
{synonym}

The ideal <rocket equation> gives the change in signed <velocity> produced by ejecting <rocket propellant>. Assume one-dimensional <Newtonian mechanics> in an <inertial frame>, no external <force>, and a fixed positive <rocket exhaust speed> $u$ directed rearward relative to the instantaneous rocket. The rocket's instantaneous <mass> $M$ includes its remaining <rocket propellant>; burning it gives $dM<0$. For initial and final <masses> $M_{\mathrm{initial}}\ge M_{\mathrm{final}}>0$,
$$
M\,dv=-u\,dM,\qquad
\Delta v=v_{\mathrm{final}}-v_{\mathrm{initial}}
=u\ln\frac{M_{\mathrm{initial}}}{M_{\mathrm{final}}}.
$$
Here $\ln$ is the <natural logarithm>, and its argument is the <rocket mass ratio>. This is the constant-<rocket exhaust speed>, zero-pressure-correction version of the ideal <rocket equation>. https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/ideal-rocket-equation/[NASA Glenn Research Center: Ideal Rocket Equation]{ref}

For a toy rocket with $u=2\,\mathrm{km/s}$, $M_{\mathrm{initial}}=1000\,\mathrm{kg}$ and <rocket dry mass> $M_{\mathrm{final}}=250\,\mathrm{kg}$ after all <rocket propellant> is spent, the <rocket mass ratio> is $4$ and
$$
\Delta v=2\ln4\,\mathrm{km/s}\simeq2.7725887\,\mathrm{km/s}.
$$
Burning from $1000$ to $500\,\mathrm{kg}$ gains $2\ln2\,\mathrm{km/s}\simeq1.3863\,\mathrm{km/s}$. Burning from $500$ to $250\,\mathrm{kg}$ gains exactly the same <velocity> increment, using only $250\,\mathrm{kg}$ of <rocket propellant> instead of $500\,\mathrm{kg}$. Both burns halve the remaining <mass>. The second burn accelerates a lighter rocket; equal fractional <mass> losses give equal <velocity> gains.

\Image[rocket-mass-ratio-delta-v.png]
{title=Equal mass halvings give equal ideal velocity gains for an exhaust speed of 2 kilometres per second}

The <natural logarithm> makes the <rocket equation> both useful and revealing: multiplying successive <rocket mass ratios> adds their <velocity> gains. Conversely, the required <rocket mass ratio> is the <exponential function> $M_{\mathrm{initial}}/M_{\mathrm{final}}=e^{\Delta v/u}$. Each extra $u\ln2$ of ideal <velocity> gain doubles the required initial <mass> for fixed final <mass>. This explains why a larger <rocket exhaust speed> is valuable and why carrying ever more <rocket propellant> has diminishing returns. Changing the burn duration alone does not change this ideal endpoint result.

For a short proof outline, follow the rocket together with a tiny parcel about to be expelled. Before ejection, their total <momentum> is $Mv$. After ejection, the remaining rocket has <mass> $M+dM$ and <velocity> $v+dv$, while the parcel has positive <mass> $-dM$ and, to first order, inertial <velocity> $v-u$. <Momentum conservation> gives
$$
Mv=(M+dM)(v+dv)+(-dM)(v-u),\qquad
0=M\,dv+u\,dM+dM\,dv.
$$
Discarding the second-order product $dM\,dv$ gives $M\,dv=-u\,dM$; taking the <integral> gives $\Delta v=-u\int_{M_{\mathrm{initial}}}^{M_{\mathrm{final}}}dM/M$. Evaluating the <integral> yields the <natural logarithm> above. This is a <variable-mass system> calculation: the rocket alone is not a <closed system>, and its <momentum> changes as the expelled material carries away <momentum>. The fixed collection consisting of the rocket and all its expelled material has conserved total <momentum> when external <forces> vanish.

\Image[rocket-momentum-ejection.png]
{title=Momentum balance for a tiny expelled parcel: rearward relative exhaust can still move forward in the inertial frame}

Fixed <rocket exhaust speed> means fixed relative <speed> at release, not fixed inertial <velocity>. As the rocket's <velocity> $v$ changes, newly emitted material has changing inertial <velocity> $v-u$; previously released parcels coast at their own release <velocities> in this ideal model. If $v>u$, the exhaust even moves forward in the <inertial frame>, although it is emitted rearward relative to the rocket. No surrounding medium is needed for this exchange of <momentum>.

Real flight adds <gravity>, <drag force> and possibly a <pressure> correction to <rocket thrust>. A varying <rocket thrust> due solely to a changing <mass loss rate> still gives the same ideal <rocket equation> if $u$ is constant; it changes the <acceleration> and burn duration. A changing <rocket exhaust speed> instead requires $\Delta v=-\int u\,dM/M$, and external <forces> require their own contribution to the <velocity> change. The existing <constant mass-loss propulsion with quadratic drag> and <constant-burn rocket with ground friction> illustrate such additional <forces>; the <forward-exhaust rocket equation> reverses the ejection direction and therefore the sign of the recoil.

= Rocket exhaust speed
{parent=Rocket equation}
{title2=$u>0$}

The <rocket exhaust speed> is the magnitude of the emitted material's <velocity> relative to the instantaneous rocket. In the ideal rearward-ejection convention of the <rocket equation>, a rocket with inertial <velocity> $v$ emits material with inertial <velocity> $v-u$ to first order. A constant $u$ does not make $v-u$ constant.

= Rocket propellant
{parent=Rocket equation}

<Rocket propellant> is the onboard material expelled to produce <rocket thrust>. A chemical rocket normally carries both fuel and oxidizer; both contribute to its <mass> and to the expelled material. In the ideal <rocket equation>, $M(t)=M_{\mathrm{dry}}+M_{\mathrm{propellant}}(t)$, with fixed <rocket dry mass> and decreasing remaining <rocket propellant>. https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/rocket-thrust/[NASA Glenn Research Center: Rocket Thrust]{ref}

= Rocket dry mass
{parent=Rocket propellant}
{title2=$M_{\mathrm{dry}}$}

In this ideal <rocket equation> model, <rocket dry mass> is the <mass> remaining after all usable <rocket propellant> has been expelled: structure, engines and payload. It is the final <mass> in the worked example; if <rocket propellant> remains at shutdown, the final <mass> is larger than the <rocket dry mass>.

= Rocket mass ratio
{parent=Rocket equation}
{title2=$R=M_{\mathrm{initial}}/M_{\mathrm{final}}$}

The <rocket mass ratio> compares the initial and final total rocket <masses> for a burn. It is <dimensionless>, and the ideal <rocket equation> becomes $\Delta v=u\ln R$. For the same fixed <rocket exhaust speed>, two successive burns with <rocket mass ratios> $R_1$ and $R_2$ have combined <rocket mass ratio> $R_1R_2$ and total <velocity> gain $u\ln R_1+u\ln R_2$.

= Rocket thrust
{parent=Rocket equation}
{title2=$T=u(-\dot M)$}

<Rocket thrust> is the propulsive <force> associated with ejecting <rocket propellant>. In the ideal <rocket equation> with no <pressure> correction, $T=u(-\dot M)$, where $\dot M=dM/dt<0$ is the signed rate of change of rocket <mass> and $-\dot M$ is the positive <mass loss rate>. For $u=2000\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and $\dot M=-5\,\mathrm{kg/s}$, $T=10000\,\mathrm N$. With no other <force>, the <acceleration> is $T/M$: initially $10\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$ for $M=1000\,\mathrm{kg}$, rising as <mass> falls if this <rocket thrust> stays constant.

A real exhaust outlet can also contribute a <pressure> term: $T=u(-\dot M)+A_e(p_e-p_a)$, with exit <area> $A_e$, exit <pressure> $p_e$ and ambient <pressure> $p_a$. The ideal formula here assumes that term vanishes; it must not silently absorb this correction while still interpreting $u$ as the actual relative <rocket exhaust speed>. https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/rocket-thrust/[NASA Glenn Research Center: Rocket Thrust and its pressure correction]{ref}

= Constant-burn rocket with ground friction
{parent=Rocket equation}
{title2=$q(t)\dot v=su-\mu gq(t)$}

For fuel mass rate $s>0$ and backward relative exhaust speed $u$, a rocket sliding on a horizontal plane has remaining mass $q(t)$ and satisfies the displayed <rocket equation> with <kinetic friction>. Integration over a sliding interval gives $v(t)-v(t_0)=u\log[q(t_0)/q(t)]-\mu g(t-t_0)$. From initial rest, immediate forward sliding additionally requires thrust to overcome <static friction>. If both friction coefficients equal $\mu$, motion starts only when $su>\mu gq(t)$. An algebraically negative sliding speed is not the physical solution at rest.

= Constant mass-loss propulsion with quadratic drag
{parent=Rocket equation}
{title2=$m\dot u=QV-\alpha u^2$}

A body ejecting <mass> at constant rate $Q>0$ and relative exhaust <speed> $V>0$, against <quadratic drag> $\alpha u^2$, has remaining <mass> $m=m_0-Qt$. A short-interval <momentum> balance gives $m\dot u=QV-\alpha u^2$. From rest, <separation of variables> gives
$$
u(t)=\sqrt{\frac{QV}{\alpha}}\tanh\left(\sqrt{\frac{\alpha V}{Q}}\log\frac{m_0}{m_0-Qt}\right).
$$
For final <mass> $m_f>0$, this formula applies until $t=(m_0-m_f)/Q$. The zero-drag limit is the <rocket equation> $u_f=V\log(m_0/m_f)$. Two <dimensionless variables> are $\alpha V/Q$ and $m_0/m_f$.

= Zero-relative-velocity mass shedding
{parent=Rocket equation}
{title2=$q\dot v=F_{\rm ext}$}

When matter leaves a moving body with zero instantaneous relative <velocity>, it carries the body's current <momentum> per unit mass but creates no additional recoil term. A short-interval <momentum> balance including the newly released material gives $q\dot v=F_{\rm ext}$ for the remaining body's mass $q(t)$. The expression $\frac d{dt}(qv)=F_{\rm ext}$ omits the outgoing momentum flux. Nonzero relative ejection <velocity> instead produces the thrust term of the <rocket equation>.

= Dust-collecting rocket
{parent=Rocket equation}
{title2=$m\dot v=\alpha u-\beta v^2$}

A classical rocket ejecting fuel at rate $\alpha>0$ and relative exhaust <speed> $u>0$, while collecting stationary dust of <mass> $\beta\,dx$ per traveled distance, obeys
$$
\dot m=\beta v-\alpha,\qquad m\dot v=\alpha u-\beta v^2.
$$
These are <mass conservation> and <momentum conservation> for the rocket, incoming dust and outgoing gas. The dust-loading term supplies the <momentum> needed to accelerate incoming stationary material. Put $\lambda=\sqrt{\alpha/(\beta u)}$ and $V=\lambda u$. While the engines operate and $0\leq v<V$, an initially stationary rocket satisfies
$$
\frac m{m_0}=\left(\frac{V-v}{V}\right)^{(\lambda-1)/2}\left(\frac{V+v}{V}\right)^{-(\lambda+1)/2}.
$$
For $\lambda>1$, continuing to $V$ would drive total <mass> to zero. For $0<\lambda<1$, total <mass> instead grows without bound and $V$ is approached only after infinite time under indefinitely maintained thrust. In both cases a finite fuel supply runs out before that limiting <speed>. Total stored <mass> must not be confused with remaining fuel.

= Rocket equation with stationary mass accretion
{parent=Dust-collecting rocket}
{title2=$M\dot v=u\dot m-v\dot D$}

For a <rocket equation> with exhaust mass rate $\dot m$ and incoming stationary mass rate $\dot D$, mass balance is $\dot M=\dot D-\dot m$. A short-interval <momentum conservation> calculation gives $M\dot v=u\dot m-v\dot D$. Constant time rates $\dot D=\alpha$, $\dot m=\beta$ give $M\dot v+\alpha v=u\beta$, unlike a constant accretion rate per distance, whose incoming mass rate is proportional to speed. For unequal rates and initial rest, $v=(u\beta/\alpha)[1-(M/M_0)^{\alpha/(\beta-\alpha)}]$.

= Equal-rate rocket burning and accretion
{parent=Rocket equation with stationary mass accretion}
{title2=$v(t)=u(1-e^{-\alpha t/M_0})$}

If stationary mass arrives at the same constant time rate as fuel is ejected, total rocket mass stays fixed. The <rocket equation with stationary mass accretion> is then $M_0\dot v+\alpha v=\alpha u$. For initial rest, speed approaches the relative exhaust speed exponentially with relaxation time $M_0/\alpha$. This is the regular equal-rate limit of the unequal-rate mass-power formula.

= Relativistic rocket equation
{parent=Rocket equation}
{title2=$m\gamma^2\,dv+u\,dm=0$}

For collinear motion without external forces, a rocket ejecting material backwards at constant speed $u$ in its instantaneous rest frame obeys $m\gamma^2\,dv+u\,dm=0$. Here $m$ is the rocket's <rest mass>, including stored <internal energy>, and $\gamma$ is its <Lorentz factor> in the chosen <inertial frame>. The equation follows from <momentum conservation>, <conservation of relativistic energy>, and the <relativistic velocity-addition formula>; expelled rest mass is not simply the decrease in rocket rest mass.

= Two-stage rocket with proportional fuel and body masses
{parent=Rocket equation}
{title2=$v_2=u\log\frac{M+m_0}{M+(1-k)m_0}+u\log\frac{M+m_0}{M}$}

For zero initial <velocity>, body <masses> $kM,(1-k)M$ and fuel <masses> $km_0,(1-k)m_0$, the <rocket equation> gives the displayed final second-stage <speed> after burning the first-stage fuel, discarding its body without impulse, and burning the second-stage fuel. It assumes $M>0$ and $0\le k<1$. The speed exceeds the single-stage value when $0<k<1$ and fuel is positive. Its limit as $k\to1^-$ concerns a surviving second stage of vanishing <mass>; at $k=1$ no such stage exists.

= Forward-exhaust rocket equation
{parent=Rocket equation}

If a vehicle moving at speed $v$ ejects mass forward at relative speed $u>0$, then
$$
M\,dv=u\,dM.
$$
As $dM<0$, the vehicle slows down.

= Angular momentum
{title2=$\mathbf L=\mathbf r\times\mathbf p$}
{parent=Momentum}
{wiki}

= Angular momenta
{synonym}

<Angular momentum> measures how a <point mass>'s <momentum> passes around a chosen origin. Motion straight toward or away from that origin contributes zero; transverse <momentum> contributes more when it passes farther away. The picture shows the sign convention before the calculation.

\Image[angular-momentum-lever-arm.png]
{title=Positive, negative and zero angular momentum about the same origin}

Fix an origin $O$ and nonrotating axes in an <inertial frame>. For a nonrelativistic <point mass> of constant <mass> $m>0$, let $\mathbf r$ be its <position> from $O$ and $\mathbf p=m\mathbf v$ its <mechanical momentum> in that same <reference frame>. Its <angular momentum> is the <cross product>
$$
\mathbf L_O=\mathbf r\times\mathbf p,\qquad
|\mathbf L_O|=rp\sin\phi.
$$
Here $r=|\mathbf r|$, $p=|\mathbf p|$, and $\phi\in[0,\pi]$ is the <angle> between them. The <right-hand rule> gives the direction: curl from $\mathbf r$ toward $\mathbf p$, and the thumb points along $\mathbf L_O$. Thus <angular momentum> is an <axial vector>, perpendicular to both <position> and <momentum>. Its <International System of Units> unit is $\mathrm{kg\,m^2/s}$. A <point mass> need not follow a <circle> to have <angular momentum>.<University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others/11.2 Angular Momentum>[section 11.2: Angular Momentum]{ref}

With $x$ rightward, $y$ upward, and $+z$ out of the page, the <cross product> gives $L_z=xp_y-yp_x$. Take
$$
\mathbf r=(2,0,0)\,\mathrm m,\qquad
\mathbf p=(0,3,0)\,\mathrm{kg\,m/s}.
$$
Then $\mathbf L_O=(0,0,6)\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2/s}$. Reversing the <momentum> makes $L_z=-6\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2/s}$; radial <momentum> $\mathbf p=(3,0,0)\,\mathrm{kg\,m/s}$ gives $\mathbf L_O=\mathbf0$. The factor $r\sin\phi$ is the <lever arm> to the line through the particle parallel to its <momentum>.

For motion in the $xy$ plane, <plane polar coordinates> give $\mathbf v=\dot r\widehat{\mathbf r}+r\dot\theta\widehat{\boldsymbol\theta}$, with $\widehat{\mathbf r}\times\widehat{\boldsymbol\theta}=\widehat{\mathbf z}$. Consequently
$$
L_z=mr^2\dot\theta,\qquad r^2\dot\theta=\frac{L_z}{m}.
$$
The signed <angular velocity> $\dot\theta$ is positive counterclockwise viewed from $+z$ toward the plane, with $\theta$ measured in <radians>. Radial <velocity> does not enter the <cross product>. Under <conservation of angular momentum>, this relation predicts faster angular motion at smaller $r$ and constant <areal velocity> $\dot A=L_z/(2m)$ for consistently oriented swept <area>. That is the geometric content of <Kepler's second law>.

For several <point masses>, add their <angular momenta> about the same origin: $\mathbf L_O=\sum_i\mathbf r_i\times\mathbf p_i$. The origin matters: <angular momentum under an origin shift> explains why the same moving particle can have different values about two fixed points. The <central axis of a momentum system> and <angular momentum decomposition about the centre of mass> make this dependence useful.

= Angular momentum under an origin shift
{parent=Angular momentum}
{title2=$\mathbf L_{O'}=\mathbf L_O-\mathbf a\times\mathbf P$}

Move the fixed origin from $O$ to $O'=O+\mathbf a$, keeping the axes parallel and the <inertial frame>'s <velocity> measurements unchanged. The new <positions> are $\mathbf r'_i=\mathbf r_i-\mathbf a$, so distributivity of the <cross product> gives
$$
\mathbf L_{O'}=\sum_i(\mathbf r_i-\mathbf a)\times\mathbf p_i
=\mathbf L_O-\mathbf a\times\mathbf P,\qquad
\mathbf P=\sum_i\mathbf p_i.
$$
Here $\mathbf P$ is total <linear momentum>. For the <angular momentum> example with $\mathbf a=(1,0,0)\,\mathrm m$, the new <position> is $(1,0,0)\,\mathrm m$ and $L_{O',z}=6-3=3\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2/s}$. If total <momentum> is zero, <angular momentum> is independent of this origin shift. A moving origin additionally contributes $-\dot{\mathbf a}\times\mathbf P$ when this expression is differentiated while retaining the original <momenta>; see <angular momentum about the centre of mass> for an important case where that term vanishes.

= Central axis of a momentum system
{parent=Angular momentum}
{title2=$\mathbf b=\mathbf P\times\mathbf L/|\mathbf P|^2+\lambda\mathbf P$}

For nonzero total <momentum> $\mathbf P$, translating the origin by $\mathbf b$ changes <angular momentum> to $\mathbf L-\mathbf b\times\mathbf P$. The displayed line of origins makes this vector parallel to $\mathbf P$, equal to $(\mathbf L\cdot\mathbf P)\mathbf P/|\mathbf P|^2$. The invariant $\mathbf L\cdot\mathbf P$ cannot be removed by translation; if it is zero, angular momentum vanishes on this line.

= Gyroscope
{parent=Angular momentum}
{wiki}

A <gyroscope> is a rotating instrument whose rapidly spinning, nearly symmetric rotor provides a direction reference through its <angular momentum>. In an <inertial frame>, negligible external <torque> approximately preserves that direction by <conservation of angular momentum>. A transverse <torque> turns the <angular momentum> instead: the surprising sideways motion of a supported spinning wheel is <precession>. An ideal freely falling test gyroscope in <general relativity> has its spin undergo <parallel transport> along its <timelike geodesic>. Comparing that direction with distant reference axes measures <Lense-Thirring precession>; the direction need not remain fixed in arbitrary rotating coordinates.

A <gyroscope direction reference> is useful precisely because its direction can persist while its housing turns. A <spinning-wheel precession experiment> deliberately supplies gravitational <torque> to reveal how that persistence fails. The <gyroscope vector geometry> explains the direction, and <slow steady gyroscope precession> gives a numerical prediction with a check on its assumptions.

= Gyroscope vector geometry
{parent=Gyroscope}
{title2=$\boldsymbol\tau_O=d\mathbf L_O/dt$}

Choose a fixed support point $O$ and nonrotating axes in an <inertial frame>. All <angular momenta>, <torques>, and <moments of inertia> below refer to $O$. Let $\widehat{\mathbf e}$ point along the axle from $O$ toward the <centre of mass>, and let $\widehat{\mathbf z}$ point vertically upward. Write $\theta$ for their <angle>. For a rapidly spinning <symmetric top>, the dominant <angular momentum> is $\mathbf L_O\simeq L_s\widehat{\mathbf e}$, where $L_s=I_3\omega_3$ is signed and $I_3$ is the axial <principal moment of inertia>. The sign of the axial <angular velocity> $\omega_3$ follows the <right-hand rule>.

\Image[gyroscope-wheel-torque-vector-geometry.png]
{title=A fixed wheel support separates the downward gravitational force from the transverse torque and sideways precession}

With <mass> $M$, <centre of mass> distance $d$, and constant <gravitational acceleration> $g$, the gravitational <force> and its <torque> are
$$
\mathbf r=d\widehat{\mathbf e},\qquad \mathbf F_g=-Mg\widehat{\mathbf z},\qquad
\boldsymbol\tau_O=\mathbf r\times\mathbf F_g=Mgd\,\widehat{\mathbf z}\times\widehat{\mathbf e}.
$$
An ideal support applies a <force> at $O$, so its <lever arm> and <torque> about $O$ vanish. At the instant the axle lies in the $xz$ plane with a positive $x$ component, the gravitational <torque> points along $+y$. It adds $d\mathbf L_O=\boldsymbol\tau_O\,dt$ toward $+y$, rather than along the downward <force>. The <dot product> identity $d|\mathbf L_O|^2/dt=2\mathbf L_O\cdot\boldsymbol\tau_O$ shows why perpendicular <torque> changes direction without instantly changing magnitude. This vector balance and the slow-turning relation $\boldsymbol\tau_O=\boldsymbol\Omega\times\mathbf L_O$ are developed in <The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume I by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 20: Rotation in space> (sections 20-2 and 20-3, equations 20.13–20.15).

For steady <precession> about the vertical, $\boldsymbol\Omega=\Omega\widehat{\mathbf z}$ and $d\widehat{\mathbf e}/dt=\Omega\widehat{\mathbf z}\times\widehat{\mathbf e}$. Equating the two <cross products> gives
$$
\Omega\simeq\frac{Mgd}{L_s}=\frac{Mgd}{I_3\omega_3},\qquad
|\boldsymbol\tau_O|=Mgd\sin\theta.
$$
The <angular momentum> tip traces a <circle> of <radius> $|L_s|\sin\theta$: its sideways <speed> is $|\Omega L_s|\sin\theta$. Both that <radius> and the gravitational <lever arm> contain $\sin\theta$, explaining its cancellation for $0<\theta<\pi$. Exactly vertical alignment has zero gravitational <torque> and no distinguishable azimuthal <precession> to infer by this cancellation. Positive $L_s$ gives positive <precession>, counterclockwise viewed from above; reversing the spin reverses the slow <precession>. <University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others/11.4 Precession of a Gyroscope>[University Physics Volume 1, section 11.4, equation 11.12 and the torque and precession-circle derivation]{ref}

= Spinning-wheel precession experiment
{parent=Gyroscope}

https://www.physics.purdue.edu/demos/display_page.php?item=1Q-30[Purdue's Bicycle Wheel Gyroscope demonstration] uses a bicycle wheel on a long axle, with a ball at one end seated in a freely rotating socket on a heavy stand. Spin the wheel by hand or with the supplied motor, seat the ball, and release the nearly horizontal axle. The readout is the axle sweeping around the vertical while the wheel continues spinning. Compare with the stopped wheel, which falls when one end is released; reverse the spin with the same support and the <precession> reverses. These are the page's documented apparatus, presentation, and reversal checks. <Bicycle Wheel Gyroscope by Purdue University, Department of Physics and Astronomy>[1Q-30, apparatus paragraph, Directions, and Suggestions for Presentation; page updated 30 November 2023]{ref}

An alternative in https://sprott.physics.wisc.edu/demobook/CHAPTER1.HTM[Physics Demonstrations by Julien Clinton Sprott] (section 1.3, Materials and Procedure) suspends one axle end by stainless steel wire and a fishing-line swivel. The swivel allows <precession> without winding up the suspension. Sprott describes the axle bobbing as <nutation> and <precession> becoming faster as spin decreases. A suspended support can move, whereas the calculation here assumes a fixed $O$; use the socket version for the closest realization. <Physics Demonstrations: A Sourcebook for Teachers of Physics (1996 online manuscript) by Julien Clinton Sprott/Bicycle Wheel Gyroscope (section 1.3)>[section 1.3, Bicycle Wheel Gyroscope: Materials, Procedure, and Discussion]{ref}

A proposed quantitative extension is to record an overhead view of an axle marker against a fixed <angle> scale, together with a side view for $\theta(t)$. Time successive overhead passages to estimate $\Omega=2\pi/T$; count passages of a rim mark to estimate $|\omega_3|=2\pi f$. Record the spin sense too. Measure $M$ and the <centre of mass> offset $d$ of the whole moving assembly, and determine its axial <moment of inertia> $I_3$ independently. Wheel, axle, support, release <angle>, and observation interval should stay the same between spin trials. Measuring both $\Omega$ and $\theta(t)$ distinguishes azimuthal <precession> from <nutation>; an overhead movie alone can miss the latter. These camera readouts and parameter checks are proposed here, not reported measurements from the demonstration pages.

For slow <precession>, compare $\Omega I_3\omega_3$ with $Mgd$, and test whether reversing $\omega_3$ reverses $\Omega$ with nearly unchanged magnitude. A brief interval minimizes spin loss from <friction>; repeat at several starting spin rates rather than interpreting an entire slowing run as one steady state. A suspension restoring <torque> governed by its <torsion constant>, socket <friction>, support motion, a misplaced <centre of mass>, and a push at release can all supply extra <torque> or change the initial conditions. The swivel reduces the twist that activates the suspension's <torsion constant>; it does not demonstrate zero <torque>.

Steady <precession> keeps $\theta$ constant; <nutation> changes $\theta$ while the axle may also precess. Sudden release generally excites both. A small initial drop is consistent with <conservation of energy> and conserved vertical <angular momentum>: some <gravitational potential energy> supplies the motion around the vertical, and the axial contribution to vertical <angular momentum> adjusts. Bearing <friction> may damp the bobbing, but an ideal undamped <heavy symmetric top> need not settle into steady <precession>. <The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume I by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 20: Rotation in space> explicitly distinguishes uniform <precession> from release transients and <nutation> (section 20-3, paragraphs following equation 20.15).<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume I by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 20: Rotation in space>[section 20-3: sudden release, nutation, and the small drop needed for precessional angular momentum]{ref}

= Slow steady gyroscope precession
{parent=Gyroscope}
{title2=$\Omega_{\mathrm{slow}}\simeq Mgd/(I_3\omega_3)$}

Model the assembly as a <heavy symmetric top> with a fixed frictionless support, a uniform <gravitational field>, and <centre of mass> on its symmetry axis. Assume constant spin, negligible <damping>, and initial conditions prepared for <uniform precession of a heavy symmetric top>. Large spin means the transverse <angular momentum> from <precession> is small: $I_1|\Omega|\sin\theta\ll|I_3\omega_3|$. Also require $|\Omega|\ll|\omega_3|$. A large spin rate alone does not select steady motion after an arbitrary release.

For an original illustrative calculation, stipulate
$$
M=2.0\,\mathrm{kg},\quad d=0.15\,\mathrm m,\quad g=9.81\,\mathrm{m/s^2},\quad
I_3=0.080\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2},\quad I_1=I_2=0.090\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2},\quad
\omega_3=100\,\mathrm{rad/s},\quad\theta=60^\circ.
$$
The transverse <principal moments of inertia> $I_1,I_2$ are about the pivot, including the <parallel axis theorem> contribution. These inputs give
$$
L_s=8.0\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2/s},\quad K=Mgd=2.943\,\mathrm{N\,m},\quad
|\boldsymbol\tau_O|=K\sin60^\circ=2.5487\,\mathrm{N\,m},
$$
$$
\Omega_0=K/L_s=0.367875\,\mathrm{rad/s},\qquad T_0=2\pi/\Omega_0\simeq17.080\,\mathrm s.
$$
The ratio $\Omega_0/\omega_3=0.00368$ is small; the more direct <angular momentum> ratio $I_1\Omega_0\sin60^\circ/L_s=0.00358$ is small too. These are model calculations, not experimental results.

Check the approximation against the existing exact <uniform precession of a heavy symmetric top> condition. Its total <angular momentum> is
$$
\mathbf L_O=I_1\Omega(\widehat{\mathbf z}-\cos\theta\widehat{\mathbf e})+L_s\widehat{\mathbf e}.
$$
At constant $\theta,\Omega,L_s$, differentiating this expression and equating it to the gravitational <torque> gives
$$
I_1\cos\theta\,\Omega^2-L_s\Omega+K=0.
$$
For $L_s>0$ the slow solution can be evaluated without subtracting two nearly equal numbers:
$$
\Omega_{\mathrm{slow}}=\frac{2K}{L_s+\sqrt{L_s^2-4I_1K\cos\theta}}
\simeq0.368639\,\mathrm{rad/s}.
$$
It exceeds $\Omega_0$ by about $0.208\%$. The other solution is about $177.409\,\mathrm{rad/s}$ and violates the assumed slow <precession>; the quadratic's two roots must not be conflated. Its <discriminant> must be nonnegative for these steady solutions to exist.

\Image[gyroscope-precession-spin-torque-sweep.png]
{title=Synthetic steady-precession calculations: larger spin slows the drift and larger transverse torque speeds it up}

The original curves hold $I_1,I_3,\theta$ fixed and vary spin and the imposed <torque> coefficient $K$ in $\boldsymbol\tau_O=K\widehat{\mathbf z}\times\widehat{\mathbf e}$. For gravity alone, $K=Mgd$; the other curves are hypothetical changes in the transverse <torque>, not observations of this wheel. Solid curves use the slow quadratic root; dashed curves use $K/(I_3\omega_3)$. The second panel displays their relative difference. Increasing spin improves this approximation for the parameters shown. It does not remove <nutation> caused by inappropriate initial conditions.

= Gyroscope direction reference
{parent=Gyroscope}

For a rotor spinning about a <principal axis> with its support at the <centre of mass>, uniform <gravity> supplies no <torque> about that centre. With other external <torques> negligible, <conservation of angular momentum> preserves its direction in an <inertial frame>. A mount that permits free axis motion lets the housing rotate relative to that persistent direction, giving an orientation reference when external landmarks are unavailable. This is the useful limit opposite to the deliberately off-centre support in the <spinning-wheel precession experiment>. It supplies a direction, not a position or a full three-axis orientation from one axis alone.

For a small accumulated transverse <torque> about the <centre of mass>, the change in the direction <unit vector> is, to first order,
$$
\Delta\widehat{\mathbf L}\simeq\frac{1}{L}\int\boldsymbol\tau_\perp\,dt,\qquad
\boldsymbol\tau_\perp=\boldsymbol\tau-(\boldsymbol\tau\cdot\widehat{\mathbf L})\widehat{\mathbf L},\qquad L=|\mathbf L|.
$$
Here $L$ is approximately constant over the short interval. Large <angular momentum> reduces drift for the same integrated transverse <torque>; parallel <torque> instead changes its magnitude. For $L=8.0\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2/s}$, a constant transverse <torque> of $0.010\,\mathrm{N\,m}$ over $1.0\,\mathrm s$ produces approximately $0.00125\,\mathrm{rad}$, or $0.0716^\circ$, of direction change. This is another synthetic calculation. The persistent direction belongs to an <inertial frame>; its components can change in a rotating <reference frame> even with zero external <torque>.

= Magnetic axial angular-momentum invariant
{parent=Angular momentum}
{title2=$L_z+qB\rho^2/2$}

A charged particle in a uniform field $\boldsymbol B=B\boldsymbol e_z$ and an axially symmetric potential conserves the displayed canonical angular momentum, with $\rho$ the distance from the field axis and $q$ its charge. It follows directly by differentiating the expression and using the <Lorentz force>. For charge $q=-e$, the equivalent invariant is $m(\dot{\boldsymbol r}\times\boldsymbol r)\cdot\boldsymbol B+e|\boldsymbol r\times\boldsymbol B|^2/2$. A radial restoring force has no axial torque.

= Angular momentum decay under uniform linear drag
{parent=Angular momentum}
{title2=$L(t)=L(0)e^{-kt/m}$}

Consider particles of the same constant mass $m$, interacting through equal-and-opposite central internal forces, with external forces $F_i=-k\dot r_i$ for constant $k$. About a fixed point $a$, internal pair <torques> cancel and the external <torque> is $G_a=-k\sum_i(r_i-a)\times\dot r_i=-(k/m)L_a$. Hence $\dot L_a=-(k/m)L_a$ and the displayed exponential decay follows. The same result holds for <angular momentum about the centre of mass>, because the moving-origin term $-\dot R\times P=-\dot R\times M\dot R$ vanishes. Unequal masses do not generally permit factoring out a single $k/m$.

= Generalized angular momentum in a radial monopole field
{parent=Angular momentum}
{title2=$\mathbf L=m\mathbf r\times\dot{\mathbf r}-\mu\widehat{\mathbf r}$}

For a radial conservative force plus the magnetic-type force $\dot{\mathbf r}\times(\mu\mathbf r/r^3)$, the torque equals $\mu\,d\widehat{\mathbf r}/dt$. Subtracting $\mu\widehat{\mathbf r}$ from ordinary <angular momentum> therefore produces a constant vector. Its dot product with $\widehat{\mathbf r}$ is $-\mu$, so motion lies on a cone about that vector. At $\mu=0$, ordinary planar <central force> motion is recovered. This is the classical radial <magnetic monopole> angular-momentum correction.

= Conservation of angular momentum
{parent=Angular momentum}
{title2=$d\mathbf L_O/dt=\boldsymbol\tau_O^{\mathrm{ext}}=\mathbf0$}

= Angular momentum conservation
{synonym}

<Conservation of angular momentum> says that total <angular momentum> stays fixed when the net external <torque> about the chosen fixed inertial origin vanishes. Individual <angular momenta> and <angular velocities> can change. The <radius change at constant angular momentum> below makes this distinction visible: pulling two rotating <point masses> inward lowers the <moment of inertia>, raises the <angular velocity>, and supplies <work> that increases the <kinetic energy>.

\Image[angular-momentum-radius-change.png]
{title=Pulling two point masses inward preserves angular momentum and adds three joules of kinetic energy}

Follow the same constant-<mass> <point masses> throughout, in an <inertial frame>, with all <positions> measured from a fixed origin $O$. If the internal pair <forces> are opposite and central, meaning directed along each pair's separation, then
$$
\mathbf L_O=\sum_i\mathbf r_i\times\mathbf p_i,\qquad
\frac{d\mathbf L_O}{dt}=\boldsymbol\tau_O^{\mathrm{ext}}
=\sum_i\mathbf r_i\times\mathbf F_i^{\mathrm{ext}}.
$$
Thus zero net external <torque> throughout an interval implies constant total <angular momentum> throughout that interval. A vanishing external <torque> component, such as $\tau_z^{\mathrm{ext}}=0$, conserves the corresponding $L_z$ even when the other components change. This is the balance law for a fixed collection; <angular-momentum flux> must also be accounted for when matter crosses its boundary. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/11-3-conservation-of-angular-momentum[OpenStax University Physics Volume 1, section 11.3: Conservation of Angular Momentum]{ref}

The central qualification is essential. Write $\mathbf F_{ij}$ for the internal <force> on member $i$ from member $j$. Under <Newton's third law>, $\mathbf F_{ji}=-\mathbf F_{ij}$, but the sum of their <torques> is
$$
\mathbf r_i\times\mathbf F_{ij}+\mathbf r_j\times\mathbf F_{ji}
=(\mathbf r_i-\mathbf r_j)\times\mathbf F_{ij}.
$$
This vanishes when $\mathbf F_{ij}$ is parallel to $\mathbf r_i-\mathbf r_j$. Equal-and-opposite <forces> alone do not suffice: two opposite transverse <forces> with separated lines of action form a <couple> with nonzero <torque>. Cancellation of the internal pair <torques>, together with <Newton's second law>, proves the total balance above.

For one <point mass> under a <central force> about $O$, $\mathbf F=f(r)\widehat{\mathbf r}$ gives $\boldsymbol\tau_O=\mathbf r\times\mathbf F=\mathbf0$. The <force> may still change the <speed> and <kinetic energy>; its zero <torque> preserves <angular momentum>. With nonzero $\mathbf L_O$, $\mathbf r\cdot\mathbf L_O=0$ keeps the motion in a fixed plane. Choosing the $z$ axis perpendicular to that plane, $r^2\dot\theta=L_z/m$ governs its angular progress. This removes an unknown from the <central-force radial turning point> calculation and helps reduce the <Kepler orbit> to a radial problem.

There is a historical geometric version of this result. In Book I, Section II, Proposition I of the \i[Principia], Newton proves that motion under a <central force> toward a fixed centre stays in a plane and sweeps equal <areas> in equal <times>, using successive central <impulses> and equal <triangle> <areas>. https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Mathematical_Principles_of_Natural_Philosophy_(1846)/BookI-II[Newton, Principia, Book I, Proposition I, Motte translation, 1846 edition]{ref} In the modern notation above, constant <areal velocity> is $\dot A=L_z/(2m)$: the old geometric proof and the <angular momentum> calculation express the same invariant.

= Radius change at constant angular momentum
{parent=Conservation of angular momentum}
{title2=$I\omega=L_z,\quad K_{\mathrm{rot}}=L_z^2/(2I)$}

Place two equal <point masses> of $1\,\mathrm{kg}$ each on opposite sides of a fixed origin, at equal <radius> $r$, and let them share a signed <angular velocity> $\omega=\dot\theta$ in a plane. Their total <mass> is $M=2\,\mathrm{kg}$ and their <moment of inertia> about the perpendicular axis is $I=Mr^2$. Radial pulling <forces> have zero <torque> about that origin, so
$$
L_z=I\omega=Mr^2\omega=\mathrm{constant},\qquad
K_{\mathrm{rot}}=\frac12I\omega^2=\frac{L_z^2}{2I}.
$$
Take an initial <radius> $r_i=1\,\mathrm m$ and <angular velocity> $\omega_i=1\,\mathrm{rad/s}$, and a final <radius> $r_f=0.5\,\mathrm m$. Then
$$
I_i=2\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2},\quad I_f=0.5\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2},\quad
L_z=2\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2/s},\quad \omega_f=4\,\mathrm{rad/s},
$$
and
$$
K_i=1\,\mathrm J,\qquad K_f=4\,\mathrm J,\qquad W=K_f-K_i=+3\,\mathrm J.
$$
These are endpoint states with $\dot r_i=\dot r_f=0$. During the pull, the full <kinetic energy> also contains radial motion: $K=M\dot r^2/2+K_{\mathrm{rot}}$. Assume the pulling apparatus supplies all net <work> to the two <point masses>; the <work-energy theorem> then fixes its net <work> at $+3\,\mathrm J$, including any radial acceleration and braking. Each <mass> spirals inward during the pull, allowing its inward <force> to do positive <work>. <Conservation of angular momentum> therefore predicts the final <angular velocity> without requiring <kinetic energy> conservation. For an apparatus included in a larger system, its stored <energy> supplies the increase consistently with <conservation of energy>. OpenStax uses the same mechanism to explain a skater pulling her arms inward. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/11-3-conservation-of-angular-momentum[OpenStax, section 11.3: work during a change of moment of inertia]{ref}

= Angular-momentum flux
{parent=Conservation of angular momentum}
{title2=$\dot J$}

The rate at which <angular momentum> crosses a surface, including advection and stresses. With positive inward <mass accretion rate>, the net inward flux in a steady <accretion disk> is $\dot M h-\mathcal G$.

= Central-force radial turning point
{parent=Conservation of angular momentum}

For unit mass in a <central force> with potential $\Phi(r)$ and conserved angular momentum $L$, a radial turning point $r_*$ has zero radial speed and therefore satisfies
$$
E=\frac{L^2}{2r_*^2}+\Phi(r_*).
$$

= Attractive and repulsive inverse-square closest approaches
{parent=Central-force radial turning point}
{title2=$p_-=\sqrt{b^2+q^2}-q,\quad p_+=\sqrt{b^2+q^2}+q$}

For unit <mass>, incoming <speed> $v>0$, <impact parameter> $b>0$, and <potential energy> $\mp\kappa/r$, put $q=\kappa/v^2>0$. At closest approach, <conservation of energy> and <conservation of angular momentum> give the displayed radii and tangential <speeds> $u_\mp=bv/p_\mp$. Consequently $p_-p_+=b^2$ and $u_-u_+=v^2$. These relations compare equal force magnitudes. The attractive radial case $b=0$ is a singular collision, so the speed-product statement does not extend to that endpoint.

= Angular momentum transport
{parent=Angular momentum}

Angular momentum transport moves angular momentum between locations through matter, stresses, waves, or fields. An <accretion disk> must transport angular momentum outward for most of its mass to move inward.

= Torque
{title2=$\boldsymbol\tau_O=\mathbf r\times\mathbf F$}
{parent=Angular momentum}
{wiki}

= Moment of force
{synonym}

<Torque> measures a <force>'s turning effect about a specified origin. The geometry in the <angular momentum> diagram applies with <force> replacing <momentum>: transverse <forces> give positive or negative <torque>, while a radial <force> gives zero. A longer perpendicular <lever arm> makes the same <force> more effective, explaining why pushing near the end of a handle is useful.

For a <force> $\mathbf F$ applied at <position> $\mathbf r$ from a fixed origin $O$ in an <inertial frame>, its <torque>, or <moment of force>, is
$$
\boldsymbol\tau_O=\mathbf r\times\mathbf F,\qquad
|\boldsymbol\tau_O|=rF\sin\phi=F\ell.
$$
Here $F=|\mathbf F|$, $\phi$ is the <angle> between <position> and <force>, and $\ell=r\sin\phi$ is the perpendicular <lever arm>. Direction follows the <right-hand rule>; in the $xy$ plane $\tau_z=xF_y-yF_x$, positive counterclockwise as viewed from $+z$. The <International System of Units> unit is $\mathrm{N\,m}=\mathrm{kg\,m^2/s^2}$. Although <torque> and <energy> have the same <physical dimensions>, <torque> is an <axial vector> and <energy> is a <scalar>; their roles differ.

For $\mathbf r=(2,0,0)\,\mathrm m$ and $\mathbf F=(0,3,0)\,\mathrm N$, $\tau_z=+6\,\mathrm{N\,m}$. Applying the same <force> at $(1,0,0)\,\mathrm m$ gives $+3\,\mathrm{N\,m}$; reversing it gives a negative <torque>; directing it along $\mathbf r$ gives zero. More generally, sum $\mathbf r_i\times\mathbf F_i$ to find the total <torque> about one common origin.

For a constant-<mass> <point mass>, the <product rule> and <Newton's second law> show why net <torque> changes <angular momentum>:
$$
\frac{d\mathbf L_O}{dt}
=\mathbf v\times(m\mathbf v)+\mathbf r\times\frac{d\mathbf p}{dt}
=\mathbf r\times\mathbf F_{\mathrm{net}}
=\boldsymbol\tau_O^{\mathrm{net}}.
$$
The first <cross product> vanishes because its factors are parallel. For a collection with canceling internal <torques>, the remaining sum is external <torque>, giving <conservation of angular momentum> when it vanishes.<University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others/11.2 Angular Momentum>[section 11.2: torque and the angular momentum balance]{ref}

A nonzero <torque> can turn the direction of <angular momentum> as well as change its magnitude. Indeed, the <dot product> gives $d|\mathbf L_O|^2/dt=2\mathbf L_O\cdot\boldsymbol\tau_O$: a perpendicular <torque> changes direction without instantaneously changing the magnitude. This is the geometry behind <gyroscope> <precession>. For a rapidly spinning <symmetric top> with a fixed tip, <centre of mass> distance $d$ from the tip, <mass> $M$, and spin-dominated <angular momentum> magnitude $L\simeq I\omega$, the slow steady <precession> approximation gives $\Omega\simeq Mgd/L$, where $g$ is <gravitational acceleration>. <University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others/11.4 Precession of a Gyroscope>[OpenStax University Physics Volume 1, section 11.4: Precession of a Gyroscope]{ref} This is an approximation with $\Omega\ll\omega$; the <uniform precession of a heavy symmetric top> retains contributions omitted by the spin-dominated estimate.

= Lever arm
{parent=Torque}
{title2=$\ell=r\sin\phi$}

= Moment arm
{synonym}

The <lever arm> of a nonzero <force> about an origin is the shortest distance from that origin to the line through the application point parallel to the <force>. Its length is $\ell=|\mathbf r\times\widehat{\mathbf F}|$, with <unit vector> $\widehat{\mathbf F}=\mathbf F/|\mathbf F|$. Thus <torque> magnitude is $F\ell$, maximal at a right <angle> for fixed $r$ and $F$, and zero if the line passes through the origin. Sliding the application point along that line leaves <torque> unchanged, since $(\mathbf r+s\widehat{\mathbf F})\times\mathbf F=\mathbf r\times\mathbf F$. The same perpendicular-distance construction gives <angular momentum> magnitude $p\ell$ for a line parallel to nonzero <momentum> through the particle.

= Couple
{disambiguate=mechanics}
{parent=Torque}
{title2=$\mathbf G=(\mathbf x_1-\mathbf x_2)\times\mathbf F$}
{wiki}

= Force couple
{synonym}

= Couple
{synonym}

A pair of equal and opposite <forces> acting along distinct lines has zero resultant <force> but a nonzero <torque>. If $\mathbf F$ acts at $\mathbf x_1$ and $-\mathbf F$ at $\mathbf x_2$, the total <torque> about any origin is $(\mathbf x_1-\mathbf x_2)\times\mathbf F$. Shifting that origin changes the moment by the <cross product> of the shift with the resultant <force>, which is zero. A prescribed <couple> therefore specifies a pure turning moment independently of its reference point. In <Stokes flow> it can drive a <rotating sphere in Stokes flow> without a directly imposed translational <force>.

= Moment balance
{parent=Torque}

Moment balance equates the rate of change of angular momentum to applied torques. A static slender rod obeys $\partial_s\mathbf M+\mathbf t\times\mathbf F+\mathbf m=0$.

= Areal velocity
{title2=$\dot A$}
{parent=Angular momentum}
{wiki}

Areal velocity measures how fast a <position> <vector> from a chosen origin sweeps <signed swept area>. It connects a geometric picture of motion to <angular momentum>: a long <position> <vector> with slow transverse motion can sweep the same <area> per unit <time> as a short one with fast transverse motion. Constancy requires a dynamical condition, given by the <torque criterion for constant areal velocity>.

Choose a fixed origin $O$ in an <inertial frame> and a fixed <plane> through $O$, with constant <unit vector> $\mathbf n$ normal to it. For a differentiable trajectory in that <plane>, let $\mathbf r(t)$ be <position> relative to $O$ and $\mathbf v=\dot{\mathbf r}$ its <velocity>. Positive swept <area> follows the <right-hand rule> about $\mathbf n$. In positively oriented Cartesian coordinates in the <plane>, and in <plane polar coordinates> with <angle> $\theta$ in <radians>,
$$
\dot A=\frac12\mathbf n\cdot(\mathbf r\times\mathbf v)
=\frac12(x\dot y-y\dot x)
=\frac12r^2\dot\theta.
$$
Here the <dot product> selects the normal component of the <cross product>. Motion away from or towards $O$ alone contributes no <area>. The signed <specific angular momentum> component $h_n=\mathbf n\cdot(\mathbf r\times\mathbf v)$ obeys
$$
\dot A=\frac{h_n}{2}=\frac{L_n}{2m}
$$
for a constant <mass> $m$. If $\mathbf n$ is chosen along a nonzero conserved <specific angular momentum> <vector>, then $h_n=h=|\mathbf r\times\mathbf v|$ and the familiar positive formula is $\dot A=h/2$. Reversing the orientation changes the sign; the instantaneous unsigned swept-<area> rate is $|\dot A|$.

= Signed swept area
{parent=Areal velocity}
{title2=$A(t)-A(t_0)=\frac12\int_{t_0}^t(x\dot y-y\dot x)\,ds$}

Signed swept area counts the <area> traversed by a <position> <vector>, positively or negatively according to its direction of turning. For a continuously differentiable planar trajectory, its accumulated value relative to $t_0$ is the <line integral>
$$
A(t)-A(t_0)=\frac12\int_{t_0}^t\mathbf n\cdot(\mathbf r(s)\times\dot{\mathbf r}(s))\,ds.
$$
A short segment contributes the oriented <triangle> <area> $\tfrac12\mathbf n\cdot(\mathbf r\times d\mathbf r)$; taking its <time> <derivative> gives <areal velocity>. Repeated turns count repeated <area>; a reversal subtracts it. Thus this quantity need not equal the <area> of the union of all previously swept regions. The origin matters: the relevant origin for <Kepler's second law> is the attracting focus of the <ellipse>, rather than its geometric centre.

= Equal-time sectors of a Kepler ellipse
{parent=Areal velocity}

Take an illustrative <elliptic Kepler orbit> with <semi-major axis> $a=1$, <orbital eccentricity> $e=0.6$ and <gravitational parameter> $\mu=1$, in consistent dimensionless units. Its <semi-minor axis> is $b=a\sqrt{1-e^2}=0.8$, <mean motion> is $n=\sqrt{\mu/a^3}=1$, and <specific angular momentum> magnitude is $h=\sqrt{\mu a(1-e^2)}=0.8$. About the attracting focus,
$$
\dot A=0.4,\qquad \Delta A=0.4\,\Delta t.
$$
The two sectors below each span $\Delta t=0.5$, giving $\Delta A=0.2$. Near <periapsis> the short radius sweeps a wide <angle>; near <apoapsis> the long radius sweeps a narrow <angle>. Constant <areal velocity> therefore does not require constant <angular velocity> or constant <speed>.

\Image[kepler-ellipse-equal-time-swept-sectors.png]
{title=Equal <time> intervals sweep equal <areas> about the focus of an illustrative <elliptic Kepler orbit>}

To compute the sectors without mistaking equal <angles> for equal <times>, parametrize the <ellipse> by <eccentric anomaly> $E$:
$$
x=a(\cos E-e),\qquad y=b\sin E,\qquad
A(E_2)-A(E_1)=\frac{ab}{2}\bigl(E_2-E_1-e(\sin E_2-\sin E_1)\bigr).
$$
This follows by integrating $\tfrac12(x\,dy-y\,dx)$. <Kepler's equation> gives $E-e\sin E=M=n(t-t_{\mathrm p})$, with <mean anomaly> $M$ measured from <periapsis> <time> $t_{\mathrm p}$. Consequently $\Delta A=abn\Delta t/2=h\Delta t/2$. The displayed sectors use <mean anomaly> intervals $[-0.25,0.25]$ and $[\pi-0.25,\pi+0.25]$, rather than equal <eccentric anomaly> increments.

= Orbital observations behind the area law
{parent=Areal velocity}

Why infer an <area> law from <astronomy>? In the authored account <The great Martian catastrophe and how Kepler fixed it by Owen Gingerich>, a 13 August 1593 <Mars> observation has <ecliptic longitude> $346^\circ7.5'$, against predicted $342^\circ0'$ and $351^\circ26'$. These are Earth-based directions with a date, not measured Sun-based <areas>. The observer's <armillary sphere> used graduated circles and sighting arms to read <declination>; reversing it allowed a second reading. Comparing <stars> at different altitudes exposed <refraction>, and a planet-based check revealed an erroneous solar correction table. The apparent <Mars> distance signal had been contaminated by that correction. <Johannes Kepler> subsequently corrected the observer's <Earth> <orbit>, then tested <Mars> trajectories. The account describes typical angular errors of $2'$ or less ($120$ <arcseconds>): small residuals needed care.<The great Martian catastrophe and how Kepler fixed it by Owen Gingerich>[pp. 51-53: Fig. 2, refraction checks, 13 August log table, Earth-orbit correction and angular errors]{ref}

The Tufts class notes <The Newtonian Revolution – Part One; Philosophy 167: Science Before Newton's Principia/Kepler's Astronomia Nova and the Orbit of Mars> explain the key limitation: an <area> requires a trajectory and distances, so <Kepler's second law> cannot be directly tested from angular observations alone. They describe checking the combined <ellipse> and <area> law against 28 dated observations around seven oppositions during 1583-1595, using an <Earth> model to predict Earth-based directions; agreement improved greatly but some discrepancies remained.<The Newtonian Revolution – Part One; Philosophy 167: Science Before Newton's Principia/Kepler's Astronomia Nova and the Orbit of Mars>[p. 20, items 3-5, and pp. 26-27, section IV.D: predictions, residuals and dependence of the area test on a trajectory]{ref}

The inferential chain is useful today too: recover relative <positions> and <times>, compare swept <areas>, and ask what <forces> are compatible with their evolution. Agreement with equal <areas> supports zero <torque> about the chosen origin under the assumptions below; it alone does not identify an <inverse-square law>. Recovering the radial <force> also needs the trajectory and its timing, as in <inferring an inverse-square force from Kepler laws>. The two diagrams here are calculated examples, not the historical measurements.

= Armillary sphere
{parent=Orbital observations behind the area law}
{wiki}

= Ecliptic longitude
{parent=Orbital observations behind the area law}
{title2=$\lambda$}

An angular coordinate around the <celestial sphere> measured along the <Earth>'s orbital reference <plane>. An Earth-based <ecliptic longitude> specifies a viewing direction; it does not by itself give a body's distance or its Sun-based <position>.

= Torque criterion for constant areal velocity
{parent=Areal velocity}
{title2=$\ddot A=\tau_n/(2m)$}

Assume a constant positive <mass> $m$, a twice differentiable trajectory in the fixed <plane>, a fixed origin in an <inertial frame>, and <Newton's second law> for the net <force>. The <product rule> gives the short proof
$$
2\ddot A=\mathbf n\cdot\frac{d}{dt}(\mathbf r\times\mathbf v)
=\mathbf n\cdot(\mathbf r\times\mathbf a)
=\frac{\tau_n}{m},\qquad
\boldsymbol\tau=\mathbf r\times\mathbf F_{\mathrm{net}}.
$$
The term $\mathbf v\times\mathbf v$ vanishes. This is the planar <angular momentum> balance written in <area> language.<University Physics Volume 1>[Section 11.2, equations 11.5-11.6: particle angular momentum and its torque balance]{ref}

On a connected <time> interval, <areal velocity> is constant if and only if $\tau_n=0$ throughout. Since both <position> and <acceleration> lie in the fixed <plane>, the net <torque> has only that normal component. For $r\ne0$, zero net <torque> is therefore equivalent to net <force> parallel to $\mathbf r$: its instantaneous direction is central, possibly inward, outward or zero. This diagnoses the net <force> along this motion, not its dependence on <position> or <time> elsewhere. Opposing transverse <forces> may cancel.

A <central force> about a fixed origin gives <conservation of angular momentum>, irrespective of its radial dependence: an <inverse-square law> is sufficient but unnecessary. With nonzero conserved <angular momentum>, $\mathbf r\cdot\mathbf L=0$ fixes the <orbital plane>. Zero <angular momentum> gives radial motion and zero <areal velocity>; it does not select a unique <orbital plane>. The converse needs the fixed-plane assumption: a constant projected <areal velocity> in three dimensions only constrains one <torque> component. Also, $r=0$ makes the instantaneous <torque> vanish regardless of <force> and makes <plane polar coordinates> singular. A moving origin or changing plane requires additional terms.

= Torque-driven circular swept area
{parent=Torque criterion for constant areal velocity}

A path along a <circle> can have variable <areal velocity>. As an illustrative controlled trajectory, keep a <point mass> on a <circle> of radius $R$ with <angle>
$$
\theta(t)=\omega_0t+\frac12\alpha t^2.
$$
Its <acceleration in polar coordinates> and the required net <force> are
$$
\mathbf a=-R(\omega_0+\alpha t)^2\mathbf e_r+R\alpha\mathbf e_\theta,
\qquad \mathbf F_{\mathrm{net}}=m\mathbf a,
$$
where $\mathbf e_r$ and $\mathbf e_\theta$ are the radial and transverse <unit vectors>. A circular guide can supply the radial <force>, while a tangential drive supplies $mR\alpha$. Hence
$$
\tau_n=mR^2\alpha,\qquad
\dot A=\frac{R^2}{2}(\omega_0+\alpha t),\qquad
\ddot A=\frac{R^2\alpha}{2}.
$$
For $R=m=\omega_0=\alpha=1$ in illustrative units, the three equal <time> intervals $[0,0.5]$, $[0.5,1]$ and $[1,1.5]$ sweep <areas> $0.3125$, $0.4375$ and $0.5625$. Their difference comes from the transverse <force>'s positive <torque>, even though the radius and path shape stay fixed. The <area> under each <areal velocity> graph segment equals the corresponding swept <area>.

\Image[torque-driven-circular-area-rate.png]
{title=A transverse <force> gives increasing <areal velocity> and unequal swept <areas> in equal <times>}

= Energy
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

Energy is a conserved scalar quantity associated with time-translation symmetry.

= Energy function
{parent=Energy}

For a Lagrangian $L(q,\dot q,t)$, its energy function is
$$
E=\sum_i\dot q_i\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q_i}-L.
$$
It is conserved along Euler-Lagrange trajectories when $L$ has no explicit time dependence.

= Mechanical energy
{title2=$E=T+V$}
{parent=Energy}
{wiki}

Mechanical energy is the sum of <kinetic energy> and <potential energy>. It is conserved when all forces are conservative and the potential has no explicit time dependence.

= Conservation of mechanical energy
{parent=Mechanical energy}
{title2=$T+V=\mathrm{constant}$}

For constant masses, a time-independent <potential energy> $V$ and forces $\boldsymbol F_i=-\nabla_iV+\boldsymbol R_i$ with zero total extra power $\sum_i\boldsymbol R_i\cdot\boldsymbol v_i=0$, <Newton's second law> gives $dT/dt=-dV/dt$. Thus <mechanical energy> is conserved. Smooth stationary constraint reactions and a purely magnetic <Lorentz force> supply zero power. This is more restrictive than <conservation of energy> in general, which may include thermal or other internal forms.

= Work
{parent=Energy}
{title2=$W=\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r$}
{wiki=Work_(physics)}

<Work> measures the <energy> transferred by a <force> as its point of application moves. For a <point mass> following a path $C$, add the contribution of the <force> along each small <displacement>:
$$
W=\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
=\int_{t_i}^{t_f}\mathbf F(t)\cdot\mathbf v(t)\,dt.
$$
This <line integral> uses the actual path, and the <dot product> selects the component of <force> along the motion. A large <force> perpendicular to every small <displacement> does zero <work>; holding something still also does zero mechanical <work> on it, even if holding it feels tiring. The <SI> unit is $\mathrm J=\mathrm{N\,m}$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-1-work[OpenStax University Physics 1, §7.1: Work]{ref}

The usefulness of <work> is that it connects a description in terms of <forces> and distances to one in terms of <energy>. The <work-energy theorem> gives the change in <kinetic energy> from the total net <work>, often without solving for the entire motion. The <work> of one selected <force> equals that change only when the other <forces> contribute zero total <work>.

= Work done by a constant force
{parent=Work}
{title2=$W=Fd\cos\theta$}

For a fixed <force> $\mathbf F$ and endpoint <displacement> $\Delta\mathbf r$, the <line integral> reduces to a <dot product>:
$$
W=\mathbf F\cdot\Delta\mathbf r=Fd\cos\theta,
\qquad F=|\mathbf F|,\quad d=|\Delta\mathbf r|.
$$
Here $\theta$ is the <angle> between the <force> and the <displacement>. For straight motion, $d$ is also the distance traveled. A $3\,\mathrm N$ <force> acting along a $4\,\mathrm m$ <displacement> does $12\,\mathrm J$ of <work>. Turning the same <force> perpendicular to the <displacement> gives $\cos(\pi/2)=0$ and zero <work>; reversing it gives $\cos\pi=-1$ and $-12\,\mathrm J$. At $\theta=\pi/3$, the <work> is $6\,\mathrm J$. These signs describe <energy> transferred to or from the moving body. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-1-work[OpenStax University Physics 1, §7.1: Constant-force work]{ref}

\Image[work-force-and-displacement.png]
{title=<Work> from parallel, perpendicular, and opposing <forces>}
{description=Each left panel shows a $3\,\mathrm N$ <force> and a rightward $4\,\mathrm m$ <displacement>. Each right panel plots the along-path <force> component against distance; its signed area is the <work>: $12$, $0$, or $-12\,\mathrm J$. The arrows compare directions; their lengths do not compare quantities with different units. The three panels prescribe motion to illustrate <work>; they are separate cases, not successive stages of one journey.}

= Work-energy theorem
{parent=Work}
{title2=$W_{\mathrm{net}}=\Delta T$}

For a <point mass> of constant <mass> $m$ observed in an <inertial frame>, the net <work> along its actual path equals its change in <kinetic energy>:
$$
W_{\mathrm{net}}=\int_{t_a}^{t_b}\mathbf F_{\mathrm{net}}(t)\cdot\mathbf v(t)\,dt
=\int_a^b\mathbf F_{\mathrm{net}}\cdot d\mathbf r
=\frac12m|\mathbf v_b|^2-\frac12m|\mathbf v_a|^2.
$$
The middle expression is a <line integral>; the <dot product> selects the component of <force> along the <displacement>. Positive net <work> increases <speed>, negative net <work> decreases it, and zero net <work> leaves the endpoint <speeds> equal. Equal endpoint <speeds> do not imply equal <velocities>: a perpendicular <force> can continually change direction while doing zero <work>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-01sc-classical-mechanics-fall-2016/mit8_01scs22_chapter13.pdf[MIT mechanics, Chapter 13, section 13.11]{ref}

The bridge to <Newton's second law> is short: take the <dot product> of $m\dot{\mathbf v}=\mathbf F_{\mathrm{net}}$ with <velocity>. The <chain rule> gives $d(m|\mathbf v|^2/2)/dt=\mathbf F_{\mathrm{net}}\cdot\mathbf v$, equating the rate of change of <kinetic energy> to instantaneous <power>. Integrating gives the theorem. No <conservative force> assumption entered this argument; <friction> also contributes to the net <work>.

For the <harmonic oscillator> with <spring> stiffness $k=2\,\mathrm{N/m}$, release a <mass> $m=0.5\,\mathrm{kg}$ from rest at $x=0.3\,\mathrm m$. The <spring> exerts $F=-kx$. Its <work> before the first passage through $x=0$ is
$$
W=\int_{0.3\,\mathrm m}^{0}(-kx)\,dx
=\frac12k(0.3\,\mathrm m)^2=0.09\,\mathrm J.
$$
Thus $mv^2/2=0.09\,\mathrm J$ gives <speed> $0.6\,\mathrm{m/s}$ there. The <velocity> is negative on this passage because the <position> is decreasing. The <energy> calculation alone determines its magnitude, so the direction still requires information about the motion.

For a time-independent <potential energy> $V$, the <conservative force> contributes <work> $-\Delta V$. Separating all other <work> as $W_{\mathrm{other}}$ gives $\Delta(T+V)=W_{\mathrm{other}}$. Consequently <conservation of mechanical energy> requires zero net other <work>; the <work-energy theorem> itself does not require that conservation. With <damping>, <mechanical energy> decreases even though the theorem remains valid. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-01sc-classical-mechanics-fall-2016/mit8_01scs22_chapter14.pdf[MIT mechanics, Chapter 14: mechanical energy and nonconservative work]{ref}

= Power
{parent=Work}
{title2=$P=dW/dt$}
{wiki=Power_(physics)}

<Power> describes how quickly <energy> is transferred. For mechanical <work>, the instantaneous <power> supplied by a <force> is
$$
P(t)=\frac{dW}{dt}=\mathbf F(t)\cdot\mathbf v(t),
\qquad W=\int_{t_i}^{t_f}P(t)\,dt.
$$
The <SI> unit is $\mathrm W=\mathrm{J/s}=\mathrm{N\,m/s}$, distinct from the <velocity> unit $\mathrm{m/s}$. The italic symbol $W$ for <work> and the upright unit $\mathrm W$ for <power> mean different things. Two processes can transfer the same <energy> while requiring different <powers>: doing $12\,\mathrm J$ of <work> in $2\,\mathrm s$ requires an average $6\,\mathrm W$, whereas doing it in $4\,\mathrm s$ requires an average $3\,\mathrm W$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-4-power[OpenStax University Physics 1, §7.4: Power]{ref}

= Instantaneous power
{parent=Power}
{title2=$P(t)=\mathbf F(t)\cdot\mathbf v(t)$}

<Instantaneous power> is <power> at a particular moment. Consider a <point mass> with <mass> $m=2\,\mathrm{kg}$, initially at rest, acted on by a constant net <force> $F=3\,\mathrm N$ along a straight path. <Newton's second law> gives constant <acceleration> $a=F/m=1.5\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$, hence <velocity> $v(t)=at$ and
$$
P(t)=Fat=(4.5\,\mathrm{W/s})t.
$$
The <instantaneous power> starts at zero even though the <force> is nonzero, because the initial <velocity> is zero. At distance $4\,\mathrm m$, the <kinetic energy> example below gives <speed> $\sqrt{12}\,\mathrm{m/s}$, so the final <instantaneous power> is $3\sqrt{12}\,\mathrm W\approx10.3923\,\mathrm W$. Negative <instantaneous power> means the selected <force> is removing <energy> at that moment.

= Average power
{parent=Power}
{title2=$\overline P=W/(t_f-t_i)$}

<Average power> divides the <work> over a nonzero interval by the elapsed <time>. For the same $2\,\mathrm{kg}$ <point mass> driven from rest through $4\,\mathrm m$ by a net $3\,\mathrm N$ <force>, the <displacement> is $x(t)=at^2/2$. Thus
$$
t_f=\sqrt{\frac{2(4\,\mathrm m)}{1.5\,\mathrm{m/s^2}}}
=\sqrt{16/3}\,\mathrm s\approx2.309401\,\mathrm s,
\qquad \overline P=\frac{12\,\mathrm J}{t_f}
\approx5.19615\,\mathrm W.
$$
This <average power> is half the final <instantaneous power> because $P(t)$ is a <linear function> of <time>, starting at zero. Its <graph of a function> bounds a <triangle> of area $P(t_f)t_f/2=12\,\mathrm J$; the horizontal line at <average power> encloses a <rectangle> with the same area. In general, an endpoint value of <instantaneous power> does not determine <average power>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-4-power[OpenStax University Physics 1, §7.4: Average and instantaneous power]{ref}

= Kinetic energy
{parent=Energy}
{title2=$K=\frac12mv^2$}
{wiki}

<Kinetic energy> is the <energy> associated with motion. For a nonrelativistic <point mass> of <mass> $m>0$, measured in a specified <inertial frame>,
$$
K=\frac12m\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf v=\frac12mv^2,
\qquad v=|\mathbf v|.
$$
Here $\mathbf v$ is <velocity> and $v$ is <speed>; $T$ is another common symbol for <kinetic energy>. The <dot product> makes $K$ nonnegative regardless of the direction of <velocity>. At fixed <mass>, doubling <speed> quadruples <kinetic energy>: a $2\,\mathrm{kg}$ <point mass> at $2\,\mathrm{m/s}$ has $4\,\mathrm J$, whereas at $4\,\mathrm{m/s}$ it has $16\,\mathrm J$. The <SI> units are $\mathrm{kg\,m^2/s^2}=\mathrm J$. Different <reference frames> can assign different <velocities> and therefore different <kinetic energies>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-2-kinetic-energy[OpenStax University Physics 1, §7.2: Kinetic energy]{ref}

A short outline explains why <kinetic energy> has this formula. In a nonrelativistic <inertial frame>, constant <mass> and <Newton's second law> give, using the <chain rule>,
$$
\frac{dK}{dt}=m\mathbf v\cdot\frac{d\mathbf v}{dt}
=\mathbf F_{\mathrm{net}}\cdot\mathbf v.
$$
Integrating over <time> proves the <work-energy theorem>, $W_{\mathrm{net}}=K_f-K_i$. The net <force> is the sum of all <forces>, and $W_{\mathrm{net}}$ is their total <work>, not merely the <work> of a chosen driving <force>. This route to final <speed> is useful when the <forces> as <functions> of <position> are known but the full motion as a function of <time> would be harder to find. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-3-work-energy-theorem[OpenStax University Physics 1, §7.3: Work–energy theorem]{ref}

In the running example, a $2\,\mathrm{kg}$ <point mass> starts at rest and receives a constant net $3\,\mathrm N$ <force> through $4\,\mathrm m$. Its net <work> is $12\,\mathrm J$, so
$$
K_f=12\,\mathrm J,
\qquad v_f=\sqrt{\frac{2K_f}{m}}
=\sqrt{12}\,\mathrm{m/s}\approx3.4641\,\mathrm{m/s}.
$$
At any intermediate distance $x\ge0$, $K(x)=(3\,\mathrm N)x$ and $v(x)=\sqrt{2Fx/m}$: <kinetic energy> is a <linear function> of distance, while <speed> is proportional to the <square root> of distance. If a net opposing $3\,\mathrm N$ braking <force> subsequently acts for $4\,\mathrm m$, it does $-12\,\mathrm J$ of <work> and brings this <point mass> to rest. Negative net <work> decreases <kinetic energy>; it does not make <kinetic energy> negative. Beyond the stopping point the motion must be reconsidered, since the <point mass> cannot keep moving forward with a negative $K$.

\Image[kinetic-energy-work-and-power.png]
{title=<Kinetic energy>, <velocity>, and <power> for a constant net <force>}
{description=A $2\,\mathrm{kg}$ <point mass> starts at rest under a net $3\,\mathrm N$ <force>. The left two panels show rightward <velocity> $v(x)=\sqrt{2Fx/m}$ and <kinetic energy> $K(x)=Fx$ over $0\le x\le4\,\mathrm m$, with separate axes for their different units. The right panel shows <instantaneous power> $P(t)=Fat$ up to $t_f\approx2.309401\,\mathrm s$ and <average power> $\overline P\approx5.19615\,\mathrm W$. The shaded area is $12\,\mathrm J$ of <work>. All values follow from the stated idealized model.}

<Kinetic energy> also makes the exchange with <potential energy> intuitive: a falling body's loss of <gravitational potential energy> can appear as increased <kinetic energy>. The formula above applies when <speed> is small compared with the <speed of light>; at higher <speeds>, <special relativity> changes the expression. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/I_04.html[Feynman Lectures I, chapter 4, §§4–2 and 4–3: Energy of position and motion]{ref}

= Kinetic energy decomposition about the center of mass
{parent=Kinetic energy}
{title2=$T=\frac12M|\dot R|^2+\frac12\sum_i m_i|\dot\rho_i|^2$}

= König theorem for kinetic energy
{c}
{synonym}

Writing particle positions relative to the <center of mass> as $\rho_i=r_i-R$ makes $\sum_i m_i\rho_i=0$, so the cross term in the squared <velocities> vanishes. The total <kinetic energy> splits into center-of-mass translation and relative motion as displayed. For a <rigid body>, the latter is $\tfrac12 I\omega^2$, where $I$ is the <moment of inertia> about the instantaneous rotation axis through the <center of mass>.

= Unit-direction kinetic-energy identity
{parent=Kinetic energy}
{title2=$v^2=(\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v)^2-r^2\mathbf u\cdot\ddot{\mathbf u}$}

For a twice differentiable position away from the origin, write $\mathbf r=r\mathbf u$ with radial <unit vector> $\mathbf u$. Its unit-length constraint gives $\mathbf u\cdot\dot{\mathbf u}=0$ and $\mathbf u\cdot\ddot{\mathbf u}=-|\dot{\mathbf u}|^2$, separating radial and directional contributions to speed and <kinetic energy>. The identity is kinematic and does not require a particular force law.

= Kinetic energy density
{parent=Kinetic energy}

The kinetic energy density of a continuum with mass density $\rho$ and velocity $\mathbf v$ is $\rho|\mathbf v|^2/2$. For a displacement field, the material velocity is its time derivative.

= Rotational kinetic energy
{title2=$K=\frac12I\omega^2$}
{parent=Kinetic energy}
{wiki}

<Rotational kinetic energy> is the <kinetic energy> of a <rigid body>'s rotation. For pure rotation about an axis fixed in an <inertial frame>,
$$
K_{\mathrm{rot}}=\frac12I\omega^2,
\qquad I=\int r_\perp^2\,dm,
\qquad \omega=|\boldsymbol\omega|.
$$
Here $r_\perp$ is the perpendicular distance of a <mass> element $dm$ from that axis, $I$ is the <moment of inertia> about that same axis, and $\omega$ is the common <angular speed>, measured in <radians> per second. The <rigid body> assumption fixes the relative positions of the <mass> elements; they share <angular velocity> but have different <speeds> $v=\omega r_\perp$. Summing their <kinetic energies> $dm\,v^2/2$ gives the formula. This is a nonrelativistic model; deformation or internal motion can contribute additional <kinetic energy>.<University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others/10.4 Moment of Inertia and Rotational Kinetic Energy>[§10.4]{ref}

For a computed example, take a uniform disc and an ideal thin ring, each of <mass> $M=2\,\mathrm{kg}$ and radius $R=0.30\,\mathrm m$, rotating about its central perpendicular axis at <angular speed> $10\,\mathrm{rad/s}$. The <moment of inertia of a uniform disc> is $MR^2/2=0.090\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2}$, while the ring has <moment of inertia> $MR^2=0.180\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2}$. Their <rotational kinetic energies> are therefore $4.5\,\mathrm J$ and $9.0\,\mathrm J$. The ring stores twice the <energy> at the same <angular speed>, because more <mass> lies farther from the axis. At the same nonzero <rotational kinetic energy>, its <angular speed> would instead be $1/\sqrt{2}$ times the disc's. This makes radial placement useful for storing <energy> in a rotating wheel, although a real design must also withstand the stresses of rotation.

For fixed-axis rotation with constant <moment of inertia>, write the signed rotation rate as $\Omega=\dot\theta$, with <angle> $\theta$ in <radians>. The axial component of net external <torque> obeys $\tau_{\mathrm{axis}}=I\dot\Omega$, so the <work-energy theorem> becomes
$$
\frac{dK_{\mathrm{rot}}}{dt}=\tau_{\mathrm{axis}}\Omega,
\qquad \Delta K_{\mathrm{rot}}=\int\tau_{\mathrm{axis}}\,d\theta.
$$
An opposing <torque> removes <rotational kinetic energy>; bearing <friction> must be included in the net <torque>. The signed $\Omega$ matters when the direction reverses. Rotation about a fixed axis need not be about a <principal axis>: the scalar <energy> formula still holds, but transverse bearing <torques> can be needed to maintain that axis.

For general <rigid body> motion, use the <inertia tensor> $\mathsf I_G$ about the <centre of mass> $G$:
$$
K_{\mathrm{total}}=\frac12M|\mathbf V_G|^2+\frac12\boldsymbol\omega^{\mathsf T}\mathsf I_G\boldsymbol\omega,
\qquad \mathbf L_G=\mathsf I_G\boldsymbol\omega.
$$
This is the <kinetic-energy decomposition about the centre of mass>. In a <principal body frame>, the rotational term is $\tfrac12(I_1\omega_1^2+I_2\omega_2^2+I_3\omega_3^2)$, using the <principal moments of inertia>. Away from a <principal axis>, <angular momentum> and <angular velocity> need not be parallel. A freely rotating asymmetric <rigid body> can change the direction of <angular velocity> relative to the body even while <rotational kinetic energy> remains constant; <Euler equations for a torque-free rigid body> govern that motion. One constant axial <moment of inertia> is then insufficient.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume I by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 20: Rotation in space>{ref}

= Potential energy
{parent=Energy}
{title2=$\mathbf F=-\nabla V$}
{wiki}

<Potential energy> assigns <energy> to a system's arrangement, so a change of arrangement accounts for the <work> done by a <conservative force>. For a time-independent <potential energy> $V(\mathbf r)$,
$$
\mathbf F=-\nabla V,
\qquad W_{\mathrm{conservative}}=V_i-V_f=-\Delta V.
$$
The <gradient> points toward increasing <potential energy>; the <force> points toward decreasing <potential energy>. Only differences matter: adding a constant to $V$ changes neither the <force> nor its <work>. This endpoint rule follows by substituting $-\nabla V$ into the <line integral> for <work>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-2-conservative-and-non-conservative-forces[OpenStax University Physics 1, §8.2: Conservative forces and potential energy]{ref}

Near <Earth>'s surface, stipulate constant <gravitational acceleration> $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$ and measure height $h$ upward from a chosen zero. Then <gravitational potential energy> is $V=mgh$. Raising a $2\,\mathrm{kg}$ <mass> by $0.5\,\mathrm m$ increases its <gravitational potential energy> by $9.81\,\mathrm J$, while <gravity> does $-9.81\,\mathrm J$ of <work>. These are computed model values, not measurements of a particular lift. An ideal <spring> obeying <Hooke's law> instead has $V=kx^2/2$, with <displacement> $x$ from its relaxed state and <spring constant> $k$: $k=100\,\mathrm{N/m}$ and $x=0.2\,\mathrm m$ give $V=2\,\mathrm J$ relative to $x=0$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-1-potential-energy-of-a-system[OpenStax University Physics 1, §8.1: Gravity and spring examples]{ref}

= Repulsive inverse-square potential
{parent=Potential energy}
{title2=$V(r)=c/r^2,\quad c>0$}

This <potential energy> generates a repulsive <central force> $F_r=-V'(r)=2c/r^3$. It is an inverse-square potential but an inverse-cube force; the powers must not be confused with the Coulomb or gravitational potential proportional to $1/r$.

= Scattering in a repulsive inverse-square potential
{parent=Repulsive inverse-square potential}
{title2=$r=r_{\min}\sec[\beta(\theta-\theta_0)]$}

For $V(r)=c/r^2$ with $c>0$, nonzero physical <angular momentum> $h$ and positive <energy> $E$, put $\beta=\sqrt{1+2mc/h^2}>1$ and $r_{\min}=\sqrt{(c+h^2/(2m))/E}$. Direct integration of the <effective potential> radial equation gives the displayed <orbit>. It has one radial minimum and two straight-line asymptotes separated by polar angle $\pi/\beta$. The repulsive scattering deflection is $\pi-\pi/\beta$; no bounded or circular orbit exists at finite positive energy.

= Gravitational energy
{parent=Potential energy}
{title2=$E_{\rm grav}$}
{wiki}

= Gravitational potential energy
{synonym}

The potential energy associated with gravitational interactions. For a self-gravitating spherical body with a fixed dimensionless density profile, $E_{\rm grav}=-C GM^2/R$ for a positive profile-dependent constant $C$; a uniform sphere has $C=3/5$. The $R^{-1}$ scaling competes with the same radius dependence of ultrarelativistic <electron degeneracy pressure> in a white dwarf, giving the <Chandrasekhar mass> scale.

= Available potential energy
{parent=Potential energy}
{title2=$A$}

The portion of a fluid system's <potential energy> that can be released by admissible rearrangement relative to a stable reference state. In a uniformly stratified incompressible fluid, the quadratic density is $A=\rho_0N^2\xi_z^2/2=\rho_0b^2/(2N^2)$, where $\xi_z$ is vertical <fluid displacement> and $b$ the buoyancy perturbation. It is positive for stable stratification and measures the restoring energy of <internal gravity waves>.

= Background potential energy
{parent=Available potential energy}
{title2=$P_b$}

The background potential energy is the <gravitational potential energy> of the statically stable state obtained by adiabatically sorting a fluid's parcels. The difference $A=P-P_b$ is <available potential energy>. Pure rearrangement changes $A$ but not $P_b$; irreversible density mixing can increase $P_b$. Molecular exchange with <internal energy> must be separated when defining an irreversible mixing rate.

= Potential function
{parent=Potential energy}

A potential function $V$ assigns a <scalar> to each point of a configuration or spatial domain. In mechanics it represents <potential energy>, and its negative <gradient> gives the corresponding <conservative force>.

= Central potential
{parent=Potential energy}
{wiki}

A central potential depends only on distance from a fixed centre, $V(\mathbf x)=V(|\mathbf x|)$. Its force is radial, and rotational symmetry implies conservation of <angular momentum>.

= Conservative force
{parent=Potential energy}
{wiki}

A force is conservative when its work depends only on the endpoints. On a simply connected domain this is equivalent to $F=-\nabla V$ for a <potential energy> $V$.

= Nonconservative force
{parent=Conservative force}

A nonconservative force has path-dependent work. On a simply connected domain, a continuously differentiable force is nonconservative exactly when its curl does not vanish identically.

= Inverse-square potential
{title2=$V(r)=-k/r$}
{parent=Potential energy}

Here <inverse-square potential> means the <potential energy> associated with an attractive <inverse-square law> for a <central force>: the <potential energy> itself varies as inverse distance. For $k>0$ and <position> $\mathbf r$ with $r=|\mathbf r|>0$,
$$
V(r)=-\frac{k}{r},\qquad V(\infty)=0,\qquad \mathbf F=-\nabla V=-\frac{k}{r^2}\widehat{\mathbf r}=-\frac{k\mathbf r}{r^3}.
$$
The <gradient> makes the distinction explicit: doubling $r$ halves the magnitude of <potential energy> but quarters the magnitude of <force>. The neighboring <repulsive inverse-square potential> instead has <potential energy> proportional to $r^{-2}$ and an inverse-cube <force>; the two naming conventions must be distinguished.

For <Newton's law of universal gravitation>, $k=GMm$, where $G$ is the <gravitational constant>. Treat the attracting <mass> $M$ as an isolated fixed <point mass>, or as a body with <spherical symmetry> and exterior <test particle> of constant <mass> $m\ll M$. The <spherical shell theorem> gives the same exterior <Newtonian gravitational potential energy> $U=-GMm/r$ without requiring uniform <mass density>. Here $r$ is measured from the centre, and the formula applies outside the body; it does not describe its interior. The <Newtonian gravitational potential> per unit <mass> is $\Phi=U/m=-GM/r$.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume I by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 13: Work and Potential Energy (A)>[§13–2, equations 13.11–13.12: radial work and conserved energy; §13–4: gravitational field outside a sphere]{ref}

The negative sign records binding relative to infinite separation, rather than a negative <kinetic energy>. Moving from $r_1$ to $r_2>r_1$ increases <potential energy> by
$$
\Delta V=k\left(\frac1{r_1}-\frac1{r_2}\right)>0,\qquad W_{\mathrm{gravity}}=-\Delta V.
$$
Because the <force> is a <conservative force>, this <work> depends on the endpoints, including for a curved path. Integrating $dV/dr=k/r^2$ with zero at infinity gives $V=-k/r$; adding a constant leaves the <force> unchanged but shifts the numerical escape <energy> threshold by that same constant. This is why the reference at infinity must accompany an <escape velocity> calculation.

For an exact synthetic comparison, stipulate the <standard gravitational parameter> $\mu=GM=8\,\mathrm{m^3/s^2}$ and a $1\,\mathrm{kg}$ <test particle>. At $r=2\,\mathrm m$, $U=-4\,\mathrm J$ and the inward <force> has magnitude $2\,\mathrm N$; at $r=4\,\mathrm m$, $U=-2\,\mathrm J$ and the inward <force> has magnitude $0.5\,\mathrm N$. The increase is $2\,\mathrm J$ of <potential energy>. These stipulated parameters are an arithmetic example, not observations of a particular body. The <escape-energy balance diagram> uses the same parameters.

= Angular momentum and the escape energy threshold
{parent=Inverse-square potential}
{title2=$\varepsilon=\tfrac12\dot r^2+\tfrac{h^2}{2r^2}-\tfrac\mu r$}

In the isolated model, <conservation of energy> and <angular momentum conservation> reduce the exterior <test particle>'s motion to
$$
\varepsilon=\frac{E}{m}=\frac{\dot r^2}{2}+\frac{h^2}{2r^2}-\frac{\mu}{r},\qquad h=|\mathbf r\times\mathbf v|,\qquad \mu=GM.
$$
Here $\varepsilon$ is <specific orbital energy> and $h$ is <specific angular momentum>. The <effective potential> is $\Phi_{\mathrm{eff}}=h^2/(2r^2)-\mu/r$. A zero radial <velocity> is a radial <turning point>, and need not mean zero total <speed>: at the <periapsis> or <apoapsis> of a nonradial <Kepler orbit>, tangential <velocity> remains. Thus the vertical gap between $\varepsilon$ and $-\mu/r$ represents total <kinetic energy> per unit <mass>, whereas the gap above $\Phi_{\mathrm{eff}}$ represents its radial part.

For $h>0$, the <orbital eccentricity> obeys $e^2=1+2\varepsilon h^2/\mu^2$. Negative $\varepsilon$ gives an <elliptic orbit>, including a <circular orbit> at its minimum allowed <energy>; zero $\varepsilon$ gives a <parabolic trajectory>; positive $\varepsilon$ gives a <hyperbolic Kepler orbit>. The <effective potential> tends to zero from below at infinity, so its outer region creates no extra positive escape barrier. This explains why <escape velocity> depends on <energy> even though <angular momentum> changes the path. The $h=0$ case is radial motion, rather than a nondegenerate <parabola> or <hyperbola>; inward radial motion can end in collision.

= Falling bodies and lunar motion as tests of gravity
{parent=Inverse-square potential}

One motivating laboratory demonstration compares two balls initially at the same height. A switch releases one vertically when the other leaves a horizontal track; arranging their paths to meet gives a collision as the readout of equal downward <displacement> over equal <time>. Equal initial height and synchronized release control the comparison. This tests the local independence of horizontal motion and <free fall>, under nearly constant <gravitational acceleration>; it does not measure an <inverse-square law>.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume I by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 7: The Theory of Gravitation>[§7–4, figure 7–3: synchronized release and midair collision]{ref}

A complementary astronomical comparison uses the <Moon>'s distance and <orbital period> to infer inward <centripetal acceleration>, $a=4\pi^2r/T^2$, and compares it with surface <gravitational acceleration> scaled by $(R/r)^2$. The approximately sixtyfold distance ratio gives the approximately $1/3600$ prediction. The inference links terrestrial <free fall> to a lunar <orbit>. Distance calibration matters; the <circular orbit> approximation and the <Sun>'s <tidal force> limit this elementary comparison.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume I by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 7: The Theory of Gravitation>[§7–4: lunar distance, period, and the inverse-square comparison]{ref}

= Lunar laser ranging
{parent=Inverse-square potential}

<Lunar laser ranging> gives a more discriminating <orbit> readout. An observatory directs short <laser> pulses at a <retroreflector> array on the <Moon>, and a detector records returning <photons>. Round-trip <time>, statistically combined over several returns, constrains distance; <pressure> and <temperature> accompany the readout. The fitted model includes <Newtonian gravity>, terrestrial and lunar rotation, geophysical effects, and corrections from <general relativity>. Comparing <orbit determination> residuals across stations and model choices checks consistency. The inspected study reports $1.8\,\mathrm{cm}$ weighted <root mean square> residuals for its 2005–2011 ranges, rather than for a bare isolated <inverse-square potential>. It tests the <equivalence principle> using the <Earth> and <Moon>'s response to the <Sun>; it is not a direct <escape velocity> measurement. Uneven coverage near new and full <Moon>, <radiation pressure>, and thermal expansion of reflector mounts limit that inference.<Lunar Laser Ranging Tests of the Equivalence Principle by James G. Williams and others>[§§2–3, pp. 3–6: pulse timing, normal points, model and figure 1; §4, pp. 7–8: consistency checks and radiation/thermal effects]{ref}

= Retroreflector
{parent=Lunar laser ranging}

A <retroreflector> returns incident <light> approximately toward its source. Corner-cube arrays make the <lunar laser ranging> round trip possible.

= Exponential cusp potential
{parent=Potential energy}

The even potential
$$
V(x)=V_\infty(1-e^{-|x|/\lambda})
$$
has a cusp at the origin and approaches the finite value $V_\infty$. Motions below that value oscillate between symmetric turning points; motions above it escape.

= Escape velocity
{parent=Potential energy}
{wiki}
{title2=$v_{\mathrm{esc}}=\sqrt{2GM/r}$}

= Escape speed
{synonym}

The <escape velocity> is the least initial <speed> that permits a freely coasting body to move arbitrarily far from an isolated attracting body. Despite its conventional name, it is a <speed>, not a unique <velocity> vector. For a fixed <point mass> $M$, or outside a body with <spherical symmetry>, set <Newtonian gravitational potential energy> zero at infinity. An exterior <test particle> of constant <mass> $m\ll M$ then has
$$
U(r)=-\frac{GMm}{r},\qquad E=\frac12mv^2-\frac{GMm}{r},\qquad v_{\mathrm{esc}}(r)=\sqrt{\frac{2GM}{r}}=\sqrt{\frac{2\mu}{r}}.
$$
Here $\mu=GM$ is the <standard gravitational parameter>, and $r$ is distance from the centre, not height above the surface. Set $r=R$ for surface <escape velocity>. Evaluate <velocity> in the body's rest <inertial frame>. Neglect <drag>, later <rocket thrust>, other bodies' <gravity>, and relativistic effects.<University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others/13.3 Gravitational Potential Energy and Total Energy>[§13.3, equations 13.4–13.6 and “Escape velocity”: infinity reference, small test mass and exterior launch]{ref}

<Conservation of energy> gives the threshold by requiring $E\geq0$: at infinity the <potential energy> tends to zero and the <kinetic energy> cannot be negative. If $E=0$, the outward motion loses <speed> continuously toward zero. If $E>0$, the asymptotic <speed> is $v_\infty=\sqrt{2E/m}=\sqrt{v_0^2-v_{\mathrm{esc}}(r_0)^2}$. If $E<0$, infinity is inaccessible. For a radial outward launch, its outer <turning point> is $r_{\max}=-GMm/E$. For nonradial motion use <angular momentum and the escape energy threshold> to distinguish zero radial <velocity> from zero total <speed>.

Direction still determines the <orbit> and whether the body hits an obstacle. An initially inward <velocity> can cause collision even when $E\geq0$. For an isolated spherical body, an exterior outward launch, or a tangential launch at or above <escape velocity>, has an outward escape branch without intersecting the body. A <parabolic trajectory> is the nonradial threshold path; a <hyperbolic Kepler orbit> has positive <energy>. The formula specifies their required local <speed>, not their orientation.<University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others/13.3 Gravitational Potential Energy and Total Energy>[§13.3, “Energy and gravitationally bound objects”: scalar energy and finite-body collisions]{ref}

= Escape-energy balance diagram
{parent=Escape velocity}

Use the synthetic <standard gravitational parameter> $\mu=8\,\mathrm{m^3/s^2}$ and launch distance $r_0=2\,\mathrm m$. Then <escape speed> is $2\sqrt2\,\mathrm{m/s}$. Three outward radial launches give exact comparisons: $v_0=2\,\mathrm{m/s}$ has <specific orbital energy> $-2\,\mathrm{J/kg}$ and stops outward at $r=4\,\mathrm m$; $v_0=2\sqrt2\,\mathrm{m/s}$ has zero <specific orbital energy>; $v_0=2\sqrt3\,\mathrm{m/s}$ has <specific orbital energy> $+2\,\mathrm{J/kg}$ and asymptotic <speed> $2\,\mathrm{m/s}$. The second case has <speed> $2\,\mathrm{m/s}$ at $r=4\,\mathrm m$. Doubling $r_0$ divides <escape speed> by $\sqrt2$, whereas doubling $M$ at fixed $r_0$ multiplies it by $\sqrt2$. Changing the <test particle>'s <mass> changes $E$ but cancels from the <escape speed>.

\Image[inverse-square-escape-energy-balance.png]
{title=The <inverse-square potential> and <kinetic energy> budget for three radial launch <speeds>}
{description=Computed curves use $x=r/r_0$ and $\mathcal E=\varepsilon/(\mu/r_0)$. The left panel compares $U/m=-\mu/r$ with constant <specific orbital energies> $\mathcal E=-1/2,0,+1/2$. The right panel shows <kinetic energy> per unit <mass>, divided by $\mu/r_0$, equal to $\mathcal E+1/x$. Only the physically allowed outward branch is drawn for negative <energy>, ending at $x=2$. These are synthetic predictions, not measurements.}

= Tangential launch trajectories across the escape threshold
{parent=Escape velocity}
{title2=$r=\tfrac{h^2/\mu}{1+e\cos f}$}

The same initial distance can lead to strikingly different <orbits>. Let $s=v_0/\sqrt{\mu/r_0}\geq1$ and launch tangentially in one <orbital plane>. Then <periapsis> is the launch point, <specific angular momentum> is $h=r_0v_0$, and
$$
\frac{\varepsilon}{\mu/r_0}=\frac{s^2}{2}-1,\qquad e=s^2-1,\qquad \frac{r}{r_0}=\frac{s^2}{1+(s^2-1)\cos f},
$$
where $f$ is <true anomaly>. These follow by combining the initial <specific orbital energy> with the <orbital eccentricity> relation and the <Kepler orbit> equation. With $\mu=8\,\mathrm{m^3/s^2}$ and $r_0=2\,\mathrm m$, the initial <speeds> $2$, $\sqrt6$, $2\sqrt2$, and $2\sqrt3\,\mathrm{m/s}$ give $e=0,1/2,1,2$: a <circular orbit>, an <elliptic orbit> with <apoapsis distance> $6\,\mathrm m$, a <parabolic trajectory>, and a <hyperbolic Kepler orbit>. The two open paths have asymptotic <speeds> zero and $2\,\mathrm{m/s}$ respectively. Their different directions at large distance are part of the <Kepler orbit>, not an extra scalar escape threshold.

\Image[escape-threshold-tangential-kepler-trajectories.png]
{title=Tangential launches give circular, elliptic, parabolic, and hyperbolic <Kepler orbits>}
{description=All panels use equal spatial scales, a fixed attracting centre at the origin, a launch point $(r_0,0)$, and an initially upward <velocity>. The gray body's radius is stipulated as $R=r_0/2$, so all displayed paths remain exterior. Colored solid curves follow motion after launch; dashed curves on the open <orbits> are their incoming extensions, not an additional part of the launch. Open curves continue beyond the viewing window. The normalized launch <speeds> and <specific orbital energies> are computed from the stated isolated model.}

= Zero-energy escape takes infinite time
{parent=Escape velocity}
{title2=$r(t)=[r_0^{3/2}+\tfrac32\sqrt{2\mu}\,t]^{2/3}$}

“Arrives at infinity with zero <speed>” is an asymptotic statement. For outward radial motion at zero <specific orbital energy>,
$$
\dot r=\sqrt{\frac{2\mu}{r}},\qquad t(r)=\frac{2}{3\sqrt{2\mu}}\left(r^{3/2}-r_0^{3/2}\right),\qquad r(t)=\left(r_0^{3/2}+\frac32\sqrt{2\mu}\,t\right)^{2/3}.
$$
Every finite distance takes finite <time>, but $t(r)\to\infty$ as $r\to\infty$. There is no instant of rest at an infinitely distant destination. Differentiating the displayed solution gives $\ddot r=-\mu/r^2$, independently checking <Newton's second law>. In the synthetic example, reaching $r=4\,\mathrm m$ takes exactly $(4-\sqrt2)/3\,\mathrm s\approx0.861929\,\mathrm s$. For nonzero <specific angular momentum>, the <Barker equation> gives the corresponding infinite-<time> limit along a <parabolic trajectory>. Positive-<energy> escape also takes infinite <time> to reach infinity; it retains nonzero asymptotic <speed>.

= Escape speed, circular speed, and propulsion
{parent=Escape velocity}

For a <circular orbit>, <gravity> supplies <centripetal acceleration>, giving $v_c^2/r=\mu/r^2$. Thus
$$
v_c=\sqrt{\frac{\mu}{r}},\qquad v_{\mathrm{esc}}=\sqrt2\,v_c,\qquad \varepsilon_c=-\frac{\mu}{2r}.
$$
A body in a <circular orbit> is bound despite its continuous <free fall>. At the same $r$, escape needs twice its <kinetic energy> per unit <mass>. An instantaneous forward tangential <velocity> increment from the <circular orbit> requires $\Delta v=(\sqrt2-1)v_c$, rather than the full <escape speed> from rest. In the synthetic example this is exactly $2(\sqrt2-1)\,\mathrm{m/s}\approx0.828427\,\mathrm{m/s}$.<University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others/13.4 Satellite Orbits and Energy>[§13.4, “Circular Orbits” and “Energy in Circular Orbits”: centripetal balance and negative total energy]{ref}

<Escape speed> is a coasting threshold, not a required continuously maintained <speed> or a measure of <rocket propellant>. A powered vehicle can start at rest and acquire <energy> gradually. The <rocket equation> relates an ideal <velocity> increment to <rocket exhaust speed> and <rocket mass ratio>; <rocket thrust>, <drag>, and the timing and direction of propulsion affect a real ascent. Rotation also changes the relation between surface-relative <velocity> and the <inertial frame> used in the calculation.

Reaching another body is a finite-destination <spacecraft trajectory> problem. In the synthetic radial example, a launch at $2\,\mathrm{m/s}$ reaches $r=4\,\mathrm m$ while remaining bound. A spacecraft can similarly encounter the <Moon> on a bound geocentric transfer; actual planning must include the <Moon>'s <gravity>, target motion, arrival <velocity>, and any propulsion needed for capture. Escaping <Earth> alone does not imply escaping the <Sun>, because the relevant <reference frame> and attracting <mass> have changed. The isolated formula describes one gravitational well; a full mission requires more than its scalar threshold.

= Spacecraft tracking and the limits of the escape model
{parent=Escape velocity}

Operational evidence comes from reconstructing a spacecraft's motion. A ground antenna transmits a coded signal; the spacecraft returns it. Corrected round-trip <time> constrains distance, while the <Doppler effect> of a coherent carrier constrains the line-of-sight <relative velocity>. NASA's navigation account describes calibration of onboard turnaround delays before launch, calibration of ground delays before use, and corrections for the <terrestrial atmosphere>. Measurements over multiple stations and epochs constrain the <orbit>; range and <Doppler effect> alone do not directly give all transverse motion. Navigators compare a fitted <spacecraft trajectory> with later tracking and remeasure after propulsion to assess the maneuver. The account identifies <radiation pressure> and imperfect propulsion as disturbances. This provides practical checks of a dynamical model and its corrections, rather than a laboratory measurement of a universal <escape speed>. The simple <inverse-square potential> predicts an unpowered exterior arc; accurate navigation includes additional <forces> and measurement delays.<Basics of Space Flight (2017 edition) by Dave Doody/Chapter 13: Navigation>{ref}

= Spacecraft trajectory
{parent=Escape velocity}

A <spacecraft trajectory> is the path traced by a spacecraft's <position> over <time>. An unpowered exterior arc can approximate a <Kepler orbit>; other bodies' <gravity>, <radiation pressure>, and propulsion can change it.

= Orbit determination
{parent=Spacecraft trajectory}

<Orbit determination> infers a body's <position> and <velocity> from observations and an <equation of motion>. It is an <inverse problem>; a <residual of an inverse problem> compares an observed readout with its modeled prediction.

= Escape and circular speeds inside an isolated uniform sphere
{parent=Escape velocity}
{title2=$v_e^2=V_c^2(3r_0^2/r^2-1)$}

For a sphere of radius $r_0$ and uniform <mass density> $\rho_0$, with no surrounding <mass> and <gravitational potential> zero at infinity, the <spherical shell theorem> gives $V_c^2=4\pi G\rho_0r^2/3$ inside. Matching to the exterior point-mass potential gives $\Phi(r)=-(2\pi G\rho_0/3)(3r_0^2-r^2)$. Since $v_e^2=-2\Phi$, the displayed relation follows for $0<r\leq r_0$. Exterior shells leave the interior <circular speed> unchanged but deepen the potential and change the <escape velocity>; a uniform core alone is insufficient to determine escape.

= Equipotential surface
{parent=Potential energy}

An equipotential surface is a level set of a scalar potential. Its gradient and the corresponding conservative force are normal to every smooth level surface.

= Equipotential curve
{parent=Equipotential surface}
{title2=$\phi(x,y)=C$}

An equipotential curve is a planar level curve of a scalar potential. Where the gradient is nonzero, its tangent is perpendicular to that gradient. In <potential flow>, it is therefore perpendicular to the <velocity field>; this distinguishes equipotential curves from <streamlines>.

= Turning point
{parent=Energy}

A turning point of one-dimensional motion is a position where the kinetic energy and instantaneous velocity vanish before the direction of motion reverses.

= Period of an oscillation
{title2=$T$}
{parent=Classical mechanics}

= Oscillation period
{synonym}

The period is the time required for a periodic motion to complete one full cycle.

= Force
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

A force is an interaction that changes momentum; in Newtonian mechanics it satisfies $F=ma$ for constant mass.

= Weight
{disambiguate=physics}
{parent=Force}
{wiki=Weight}
{title2=$\mathbf W=m\mathbf g$}

= Gravitational weight
{synonym}

Here <gravitational weight> means the <force> on a body due to <gravity>. In the Newtonian model, a body of <mass> $m$ in a uniform <gravitational acceleration> $\mathbf g$ has <gravitational weight> $\mathbf W=m\mathbf g$, with magnitude $W=mg$. Its direction follows $\mathbf g$; a <buoyant force> is a separate contribution to the body's net <force>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-4-mass-and-weight[OpenStax: mass and gravitational weight]{ref}.

= Apparent weight
{parent=Weight (physics)}
{title2=$N=m(g+a)$}
{wiki}

Apparent weight measures support rather than the gravitational <force> itself. In an ideal scale model, it is the magnitude $N$ of the <normal force> exerted on the body. A scale calibrated to display <mass> divides that support force by its calibration value of $g$.

Take upward as positive in a constant downward <gravitational acceleration> of magnitude $g$. If a body stays in contact with a horizontal scale and accelerates upward at $a$, <Newton's second law> gives $N-mg=ma$, hence $N=m(g+a)$. At rest, $N=mg$. In common <free fall>, $a=-g$, so $N=0$ despite the nonzero <gravitational weight> $mg$. If the formula would require $N<0$, ordinary supporting contact cannot realize it: the body loses contact instead.

= Weightlessness
{parent=Apparent weight}
{wiki}

Weightlessness is the absence of supporting or constraining <forces> that would give a body an <apparent weight>. It does not require zero <gravitational acceleration>. A freely falling cabin and a small freely falling object inside share the same acceleration in a uniform field, so neither needs to support the other.

The <circular orbit as continuous free fall> gives a sustained example. Real spacecraft have small residual relative accelerations from <tidal forces>, atmospheric <drag force> and onboard disturbances; the ideal common-fall explanation does not assert that every point in an extended spacecraft experiences exactly the same <Newtonian gravitational field>. https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20000083212[Ramachandran, Numerical and Experimental Investigation of the Effects of Acceleration Disturbances on Microgravity Experiments (2000), abstract: vibration, atmospheric drag and gravity-gradient effects]{ref}

= Restoring force
{parent=Force}
{wiki}
{title2=$F(x)=-kx$}

A restoring force acts toward an <equilibrium> when a body is displaced from it. In one dimension, taking <displacement> $x=0$ at <equilibrium>, this means $xF(x)<0$ for sufficiently small nonzero $x$. An ideal linear <spring> has $F(x)=-kx$ with positive <spring constant> $k$, producing <simple harmonic motion> for a constant <mass> without <damping>. More general restoring forces need not be linear.

= Tension
{parent=Force}
{title2=$T$}

Tension is a pulling <force> transmitted along a taut string, rope, or similar body. For an ideal uniform string with <linear mass density> $\mu$, constant tension $T$ sets the small transverse <wave> speed $c=\sqrt{T/\mu}$.

= Van der Waals force
{c}
{parent=Force}
{wiki}

The Van der Waals force is a distance-dependent nonbonded interaction between atoms or molecules, including dispersion and dipolar contributions. In the attractive nonretarded pair model, an interaction energy $U(r)=-\beta/r^6$ gives force magnitude $6\beta/r^7$. Macroscopic integration introduces a <Hamaker constant>; it does not replace the sign change and molecular cutoff needed at very short distances.

= Sphere-plane dispersion force
{parent=Van der Waals force}
{title2=$F\simeq Aa/(6d^2)$}

For a sphere of radius $a$ at separation $d\ll a$ from a half-space, pairwise integration of the attractive $r^{-6}$ molecular potential gives $F\simeq Aa/(6d^2)$. Equivalently, integrate the planar <disjoining pressure> magnitude $A/(6\pi h^3)$ over the local gap $h=d+r^2/(2a)$. Across a third medium the effective <Hamaker constant> can instead correspond to repulsion; state its sign convention explicitly.

= Static friction
{parent=Force}
{title2=$|f_s|\le\mu_sN$}

<Static friction> is the tangential contact <force> that maintains zero <contact slip>. “Static” describes the contact state: both surfaces can move together in the chosen <inertial frame>. In the ideal Coulomb dry-contact model, the allowable magnitude is bounded by
$$
|f_s|\le\mu_sN,
$$
where $N\ge0$ is the magnitude of the <normal force> and $\mu_s\ge0$ is the <coefficient of static friction>. The actual <static friction> is whatever the <equations of motion> require to maintain zero <contact slip>, provided it lies within this bound. Equality specifies limiting <static friction>; a smaller load generally requires a smaller contact <force>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-2-friction[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, OpenStax University Physics 1, §6.2: responsive static friction]{ref}

Use the stipulated toy model from <kinetic friction>: <mass> $m=1\,\mathrm{kg}$, <normal force> $N=10\,\mathrm N$, <coefficient of static friction> $\mu_s=0.5$, and <coefficient of kinetic friction> $\mu_k=0.3$. A box initially at rest on stationary horizontal ground is pushed right by $F\ge0$. To remain at rest, <Newton's second law> requires $F+f_s=ma=0$, hence $f_s=-F$. For $F=4\,\mathrm N$, this gives $f_s=-4\,\mathrm N$ and $a=0$, within the $5\,\mathrm N$ bound. Automatically setting $|f_s|=\mu_sN$ would predict the wrong <force> and <acceleration>.

For $F=6\,\mathrm N$, maintaining rest would require $|f_s|=6\,\mathrm N>5\,\mathrm N$, so rest cannot persist. Once the box slides right, <kinetic friction> is $f_k=-3\,\mathrm N$ and the <acceleration> is $a=(6-3)/1=3\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$. At exactly $F=5\,\mathrm N$, the inequality still permits rest with $f_s=-5\,\mathrm N$. An already right-sliding box under the same applied <force> instead has $f_k=-3\,\mathrm N$ and $a=2\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$. Applied <force> alone therefore does not specify the <friction> branch: the contact state and motion history matter.

\Image[static-friction-versus-applied-force.png]
{title=State-dependent friction magnitudes: a resting box can balance up to 5 N, while a box already sliding right has 3 N of friction}

The diagram compares conditional states, rather than specifying a force-ramp trajectory. The static branch includes its $5\,\mathrm N$ endpoint; the sliding branch also exists below that load when rightward <contact slip> is already present. To solve a new problem, first impose zero <contact slip>, find the required <static friction> and <normal force> from the <equations of motion>, and check the inequality. If that contact condition fails, use <kinetic friction> with the actual slip direction.

This distinction makes <static friction> useful for traction. For an ideal driven tire undergoing <rolling without slipping> on stationary ground, the road can exert a forward <static friction> that accelerates the vehicle while the tire's contact point has zero <velocity>. Thus <static friction> can point along the <center of mass> motion; its role is to prevent <contact slip>, rather than always to oppose the motion of the whole body.

= Coefficient of static friction
{parent=Static friction}
{title2=$\mu_s$}

= Granular friction coefficient
{synonym}

The <static friction> force at impending sliding has magnitude $\mu_s$ times the normal contact <force>. In a simple grain model this coefficient represents resistance to downstream motion; the model excludes lift and detailed contact torques.

= Force balance
{parent=Force}

Force balance equates the rate of change of momentum to the sum of applied forces. A static continuum segment with internal resultant $\mathbf F(s)$ and force density $\mathbf f(s)$ obeys $\partial_s\mathbf F+\mathbf f=0$.

= Newton's laws of motion
{c}
{parent=Force}
{wiki}

<Newton's laws of motion> consist of inertia in an <inertial frame>, the relation between net <force> and change of <momentum>, and equal-and-opposite pair forces. The following three children state the individual laws.

= Newton's first law
{parent=Newton's laws of motion}
{c}

In an <inertial frame>, a body subject to no net force remains at rest or moves with constant velocity.

= Newton's second law
{parent=Newton's laws of motion}
{c}
{title2=$\mathbf F_{\mathrm{net}}=m\mathbf a$}

In an <inertial frame>, a <point mass> of constant <mass> $m>0$ obeys
$$
\mathbf F_{\mathrm{net}}=\sum_i\mathbf F_i
=\frac{d\mathbf p}{dt}=m\mathbf a,
\qquad \mathbf p=m\mathbf v,
\qquad \mathbf a=\frac{d\mathbf v}{dt}.
$$
The net <force> is the sum of all <forces> acting on that body; <momentum>, <velocity> and <acceleration> refer to the same <reference frame>. A <force> changes <velocity>, rather than sustaining it: zero net <force> implies constant <velocity>, including a nonzero one. Doubling the net <force> at fixed <mass> doubles <acceleration>; doubling <mass> at fixed net <force> halves it. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-01sc-classical-mechanics-fall-2016/mit8_01scs22_chapter7.pdf[MIT mechanics, Chapter 7, section 7.3]{ref}

For example, an otherwise balanced block of <mass> $0.5\,\mathrm{kg}$ receives a constant horizontal net <force> $2\,\mathrm N$ for $0.5\,\mathrm s$. Its <acceleration> is $4\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$, so its <velocity> changes by $2\,\mathrm{m/s}$. Equivalently, the <impulse> is $F\Delta t=1\,\mathrm{N\,s}$, and $\Delta v=\Delta p/m$. Starting from rest, its <displacement> during the pulse is $a(\Delta t)^2/2=0.5\,\mathrm m$. After the pulse, zero net <force> leaves it moving at constant <velocity>.

Applying the law therefore requires both a specification of the net <force> and <initial conditions>. In the <harmonic oscillator> example, the <force> is $-kx$, so <Newton's second law> becomes a <differential equation> for <position>. In an accelerating <reference frame>, the measured relative <acceleration> includes a contribution from the frame's own motion; the <equation of motion in a rotating frame> illustrates the extra terms required.

Constant <mass> is essential to the displayed $m\mathbf a$ form. A <variable-mass system> exchanging matter also exchanges <momentum>; its balance includes the entering or leaving matter's <velocity>. Simply differentiating $m\mathbf v$ and equating it to external <force> on that open body can omit this flux. The existing <variable-mass system> equation specifies the needed correction.

In the 1687 \i[Principia], <Newton's second law> was stated in terms of change of motion proportional to impressed <force>, directed along that <force>. Its original statement is verbal; the <derivative> equation above is modern notation. https://www.newtonproject.ox.ac.uk/view/texts/normalized/NATP00076[Newton, Axiomata sive leges motus (1687), Lex II]{ref}

= Avalanche front with linear mass entrainment
{parent=Newton's second law}
{title2=$\ddot x\longrightarrow g\sin\alpha/3$}

An idealized front at distance $x>0$ down a uniform slope entrains stationary material so that its moving mass is proportional to $x$. Its <linear momentum> balance is $d(x\dot x)/dt=Ax$, where $A=g\sin\alpha>0$. Multiplying by $x\dot x$ gives $x^2\dot x^2=2Ax^3/3+c$. Every forward-moving branch tends to $\dot x=\sqrt{2Ax/3}$ as $x\to\infty$, and $\ddot x=A/3-c/x^3\to A/3$. Acceleration is smaller than the constant-mass downslope value because some momentum accelerates newly entrained material.

= Newton's third law
{parent=Newton's laws of motion}
{c}

For an isolated pair of particles, the force of particle 1 on particle 2 is equal and opposite to the force of particle 2 on particle 1.

= Normal force
{parent=Force}
{wiki}

= Normal reaction
{synonym}

A normal force is a contact <force> perpendicular to the constraining surface. It supplies support without being identical to <gravitational weight>: its magnitude depends on the other <forces> and the body's <acceleration>. For a body at rest on a horizontal scale with no other vertical forces, it equals $mg$. A freely falling body has no supporting normal force, despite its nonzero <gravitational weight>; this distinction defines the scale's <apparent weight>.

= Equation of motion in a rotating frame
{parent=Classical mechanics}

= Equations of motion in a rotating frame
{synonym}

For a frame rotating with constant angular velocity $\boldsymbol\omega$,
$$
\mathbf a_{\rm inertial}
=\ddot{\mathbf x}+2\boldsymbol\omega\times\dot{\mathbf x}
+\boldsymbol\omega\times(\boldsymbol\omega\times\mathbf x).
$$
The extra terms produce the Coriolis and centrifugal forces in the rotating-frame equation.

= Astroid motion of a harmonic oscillator in a rotating frame
{parent=Equation of motion in a rotating frame}
{title2=$(x',y')=(\cos^3\omega t,\sin^3\omega t)$}

For an isotropic <harmonic oscillator> with inertial frequency $2\omega$, viewed in a frame rotating at $\omega$, initial rotating coordinates $(1,0)$ and zero relative <velocity> produce the displayed <astroid> trajectory. The complex coordinate satisfies $\ddot\zeta+2i\omega\dot\zeta+3\omega^2\zeta=0$, with solution $\zeta=\tfrac34e^{i\omega t}+\tfrac14e^{-3i\omega t}$. The relative <speed> is $(3|\omega|/2)|\sin2\omega t|$, attaining its first maximum at $\pi/(4|\omega|)$ for nonzero $\omega$.

= Circular motion
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

Uniform circular motion of radius $a$ and angular frequency $\omega$ has centripetal acceleration of magnitude $a\omega^2$ toward the centre.

= Centripetal acceleration
{parent=Circular motion}
{title2=$\mathbf a=-\omega^2\mathbf r$}

For uniform <circular motion> of radius $R$ and <angular speed> $\omega$, the <acceleration> is directed toward the centre and has magnitude $\omega^2R=v^2/R$. Writing $\mathbf r$ for the <position> relative to the centre gives $\ddot{\mathbf r}=-\omega^2\mathbf r$. The required resultant <force> is $m\ddot{\mathbf r}$; it may be supplied by a <normal force>, gravity, or another physical interaction.

= Circular orbit
{parent=Circular motion}

A circular orbit maintains constant distance from its centre of attraction. Its <velocity> changes direction even when its <speed> stays constant. The inward <centripetal acceleration> has magnitude $v^2/r$, so the radial <force> must satisfy $F_r=-mv^2/r$. For a <Newtonian gravitational field> with <standard gravitational parameter> $\mu$, this gives $v=\sqrt{\mu/r}$.

= Circular orbit as continuous free fall
{parent=Circular orbit}
{title2=$\mathbf r(t)=a(\cos\omega t,\sin\omega t),\quad\omega^2=\mu/a^3$}

For a fixed spherical gravitating source, place a <test particle> at $(a,0)$ with initial <velocity> $(0,\sqrt{\mu/a})$, where $a$ is outside the source. Its exact <circular orbit> is
$$
\mathbf r(t)=a(\cos\omega t,\sin\omega t),\qquad \omega=\sqrt{\frac{\mu}{a^3}}.
$$
Differentiating twice gives $\ddot{\mathbf r}=-\omega^2\mathbf r=-\mu\mathbf r/a^3$, precisely the <Newtonian gravitational field> at each point of that circle. There is no outward physical <force> to cancel gravity in this <inertial frame>. Gravity supplies the inward <centripetal acceleration> that continually changes the <velocity>'s direction.

\Image[circular-orbit-continuous-free-fall.png]
{title=A circular free-fall orbit, inward acceleration, and the straight path the same initial velocity would produce without gravity}

Over a short interval, the displacement from the straight tangent path begins inward with magnitude $\tfrac12(\mu/a^2)t^2$. The <curvature> of the orbit matters at the same order: the particle moves sideways while continually falling away from its instantaneous tangent. The full circular solution remains at radius $a$ rather than striking the source.

Its <speed> is $\sqrt{\mu/a}$, its <oscillation period> is $2\pi\sqrt{a^3/\mu}$, and its <specific orbital energy> is $-\mu/(2a)$. Removing gravity while keeping the initial <velocity> would give the straight path $(a,a\omega t)$, rather than a stationary particle. This contrast explains why orbital <weightlessness> is compatible with a substantial gravitational <force>: a freely coasting spacecraft and its contents fall together. https://www.nasa.gov/centers-and-facilities/glenn/what-is-microgravity/[NASA Glenn: orbit as continuous free fall and the origin of weightlessness]{ref}

= Circular-orbit energy gradient
{parent=Circular orbit}
{title2=$d\varepsilon=\boldsymbol\Omega\cdot d\mathbf h$}

In a spherically symmetric <gravitational potential> $\Phi(r)$, a <circular orbit> has $r\Omega^2=\Phi'(r)$, <specific orbital energy> $\varepsilon=\Phi+r^2\Omega^2/2$ and <specific angular momentum> $\mathbf h=r^2\Omega\mathbf n$, with $|\mathbf n|=1$. Differentiating gives $\varepsilon'=2r\Omega^2+r^2\Omega\Omega'=\Omega(r^2\Omega)'$. Changes in orbital orientation have $\mathbf n\cdot d\mathbf n=0$, so $\boldsymbol\Omega\cdot d\mathbf h=\Omega\,d(r^2\Omega)=d\varepsilon$. For a particle of variable <mass> $M$ and total <angular momentum> $\mathbf H=M\mathbf h$, this implies $d(M\varepsilon)=(\varepsilon-\Omega h)dM+\boldsymbol\Omega\cdot d\mathbf H$. Moreover $d(\varepsilon-\Omega h)/dr=-h\Omega'$, which is positive when the orbital <angular velocity> decreases outward.

= Circular orbits in an exponential central potential
{parent=Circular orbit}
{title2=$l^2=r^3e^{-r}$}

For unit mass with specific <angular momentum> $l$ in $V(r)=-e^{-r}$, the <effective potential> is $-e^{-r}+l^2/(2r^2)$. A positive-radius <circular orbit> satisfies the displayed equation. Since $r^3e^{-r}$ has its unique maximum $27/e^3$ at $r=3$, exactly two radii occur for $0<l^2<27/e^3$. Their radial stability follows from $V_{\rm eff}''=e^{-r}(3/r-1)$: the inner orbit is stable at fixed <angular momentum>, the outer unstable. The case $l=0$ has no finite-radius <circular orbit> despite satisfying the upper bound.

= Circular-orbit stability for a power-law central force
{parent=Circular orbit}
{title2=$\Omega_r^2=(n+3)ka^{n-1}$}

For the radial <acceleration> $-kr^n$ with $k>0$, a <circular orbit> of radius $a$ has squared <specific angular momentum> $h^2=ka^{n+3}$. At fixed $h$, its radial <small oscillations> have squared frequency $(n+3)ka^{n-1}$, so the orbit is stable for $n>-3$. This is a special case of the <effective potential stability criterion>.

= Circular orbit in a quadratic central potential
{parent=Circular orbit}
{title2=$r_0^4=h^2/(2km^2)$}

For $V(r)=kmr^2$, fixed nonzero <angular momentum> $h$ gives the <effective potential> $V_{\rm eff}=h^2/(2mr^2)+kmr^2$. A positive-radius circular orbit exists when $k>0$, at the displayed radius, and $V_{\rm eff}''(r_0)=8km>0$ proves radial stability by the <effective potential stability criterion>. Its angular speed has magnitude $\sqrt{2k}$ and its small radial oscillation frequency is $\sqrt{8k}$. If $k\le0$, no such orbit with nonzero angular momentum exists; for $h=0,k>0$, the sole minimum is at the origin, not a positive-radius circular orbit.

= Inverse-square force
{parent=Classical mechanics}

An inverse-square central force has magnitude proportional to the reciprocal square of the separation.

= Repulsive inverse-square force
{parent=Inverse-square force}

The repulsive inverse-square force $\mathbf F=k\mathbf r/r^3$ has <potential energy> $V(r)=k/r$ and scatters positive-energy trajectories along hyperbolic conics.

= Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector
{title2=$\mathbf A$}
{c}
{parent=Inverse-square force}
{wiki=Laplace–Runge–Lenz_vector}

= Runge-Lenz vector
{c}
{synonym}

For the signed <inverse-square force> $\boldsymbol F=-\kappa\boldsymbol r/r^3$ on a particle of <mass> $m$, with Hamiltonian $H=p^2/(2m)-\kappa/r$, the <Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector> $\boldsymbol A=\boldsymbol p\times\boldsymbol L-m\kappa\widehat{\boldsymbol r}$ is conserved. Attraction has $\kappa>0$ and repulsion has $\kappa<0$. It obeys $\boldsymbol A\cdot\boldsymbol L=0$ and $A^2=m^2\kappa^2+2mHL^2$. For unit <mass> in a <Kepler orbit>, $\kappa=GM$.

= Kepler dynamical symmetry algebra
{c}
{parent=Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector}
{title2=$\mathfrak{so}(4),\quad\mathfrak e(3),\quad\mathfrak{so}(3,1)$}

For $H=p^2/2-\kappa/r$, the <angular momentum> and <Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector> obey $\{K_i,K_j\}=-2H\epsilon_{ijk}L_k$. On negative or positive energy regions use $K/\sqrt{|2H|}$ to obtain the compact or Lorentz <Lie algebra>, respectively. At zero energy the algebra on the characteristic orbit quotient is the Euclidean motion algebra. Global <group actions> require complete generators or collision regularization; simply replacing $H$ by a constant inside a Hamiltonian-vector-field derivative is not valid on the unreduced energy hypersurface.

= Polar equation of a repulsive inverse-square orbit
{parent=Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector}

Taking the dot product of the <Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector> with $\widehat{\mathbf r}$ gives $A\cos\theta=L^2/r+mk$. Thus
$$
r=\frac{\lambda}{e\cos\theta-1},
\qquad
\lambda=\frac{L^2}{mk},
\qquad
e=\frac{A}{mk}.
$$

= Displacement
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki=Displacement_(geometry)}

Displacement is the change in position between two points.

= Time in physics
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

= Time
{synonym}

Time parametrizes physical change and orders events.

= Mass
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

Mass measures inertia and acts as the source of Newtonian gravity.

= Point mass
{parent=Mass}
{title2=$\rho=m\delta^{(3)}(\mathbf x-\mathbf z)$}

A point mass idealizes a localized body's mass density as $\rho(\mathbf x,t)=m\delta^{(3)}(\mathbf x-\mathbf z(t))$, using the <Dirac delta function>. Its <second mass moment tensor> is $I_{ij}=mz_i z_j$. The approximation ignores its internal size and structure.

= Rolling acceleration with rotational inertia
{parent=Classical mechanics}

For a round body with $I=\gamma Ma^2$ rolling without slipping down an incline, static friction gives
$$
a_{\rm cm}=\frac{g\sin\alpha}{1+\gamma}.
$$

= Angular momentum about the centre of mass
{parent=Classical mechanics}

The derivative of angular momentum about the moving centre of mass equals the total external torque about that centre, because total relative momentum vanishes and internal central torques cancel.

= Angular momentum decomposition about the centre of mass
{parent=Angular momentum about the centre of mass}
{title2=$\boldsymbol L=M\boldsymbol X\times\boldsymbol V+\sum_i m_i\boldsymbol r_i'\times\boldsymbol v_i'$}

Write each position and velocity as its <centre of mass> value plus a relative value. Expanding the total <angular momentum> gives the displayed split into orbital angular momentum of the centre and internal angular momentum about it. The cross terms vanish because $\sum_i m_i\boldsymbol r_i'=\sum_i m_i\boldsymbol v_i'=0$.

= Legendre transform in mechanics
{c}
{parent=Classical mechanics}

For a regular Lagrangian, $p=\partial L/\partial\dot q$ and $H=p\dot q-L$. Regularity means the velocity Hessian is invertible.

= Regular Lagrangian
{parent=Legendre transform in mechanics}
{title2=$\det(L_{v^iv^j})\ne0$}

A <Lagrangian> is regular when its velocity <Hessian matrix> is invertible. The <inverse function theorem> then makes the fiber derivative $p_i=L_{v^i}$ locally invertible, allowing a local <Legendre transform in mechanics> to a <Hamiltonian>. If that fiber derivative is globally invertible, the <Lagrangian> is called a <hyperregular Lagrangian>. Regularity alone does not assert global invertibility, and singular <Lagrangians> may give constrained rather than ordinary unconstrained <Hamilton's equations>.

= Hyperregular Lagrangian
{parent=Regular Lagrangian}
{title2=$\mathbb FL:TQ\overset{\sim}{\longrightarrow}T^*Q$}

A <Lagrangian> is hyperregular when its fiber derivative, sending a velocity to its conjugate momentum, is a global <diffeomorphism> from the <tangent bundle> to the <cotangent bundle>. This gives a globally defined <Legendre transform in mechanics> and a global equivalence between the <Euler-Lagrange equations> and <Hamilton's equations>. With time dependence this requirement is imposed at each time, with a smooth inverse depending on time.

= Singular Legendre transform of a degree-one velocity Lagrangian
{parent=Legendre transform in mechanics}

For $L=mc|\dot x|-V(x)$,
$$
p=mc\frac{\dot x}{|\dot x|},
\qquad |p|=mc,
\qquad H=V(x).
$$
The momentum loses the speed, so the velocity cannot be recovered from $(x,p)$ and the ordinary inverse Legendre transform does not exist.

= Hamiltonian of a charged particle
{c}
{parent=Classical mechanics}

For $L=m\dot r^2/2-e\phi+e\dot r\cdot A$, $p=m\dot r+eA$ and $H=|p-eA|^2/(2m)+e\phi$.

= Virial theorem
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}
{title2=$2\langle K\rangle+\langle U\rangle=0$}

The virial theorem relates the <average value of a function> along a trajectory to the <forces> that bind it. For an <isolated system> of particles interacting through <Newtonian gravitational potential energy>, it gives $2\langle K\rangle+\langle U\rangle=0$: the average <kinetic energy> is half the magnitude of the average negative <potential energy>. An eccentric <Kepler orbit> makes the distinction vivid: <kinetic energy> and <potential energy> vary throughout the <orbit>, although their sum is constant. The relation concerns their <time> averages, rather than their values at every instant. It underlies the <virial mass estimate> and the <negative heat capacity of a virialized gravitational system>.

Use constant positive particle <masses> $m_i$, <positions> $\mathbf r_i$, mechanical <momenta> $\mathbf p_i=m_i\dot{\mathbf r}_i$, and total <kinetic energy> $K=\sum_i\mathbf p_i^2/(2m_i)$. The auxiliary quantity $G(t)$ is a sum of <dot products>, not the <gravitational constant>, which we denote here by $G_{\!N}$:
$$
G(t)=\sum_i\mathbf p_i(t)\cdot\mathbf r_i(t),\qquad
\langle A\rangle_T=\frac1T\int_0^T A(t)\,dt,\qquad
2\langle K\rangle_T+\left\langle\sum_i\mathbf r_i\cdot\mathbf F_i\right\rangle_T
=\frac{G(T)-G(0)}{T}.
$$
This finite-$T$ identity is exact wherever <Newton's second law> holds along a regular trajectory; $\mathbf F_i$ must include every <force>. The endpoint term is what a finite observation window leaves behind. For the infinite-<time> theorem, suppose the averages on the left exist and $(G(T)-G(0))/T\to0$. A sufficient condition is uniformly bounded <positions> and <velocities> for finitely many particles. More generally $G(T)=o(T)$ suffices, even without those individual bounds. Bounded <positions> alone do not supply a bound on <momenta>, especially near a singular <potential energy>; nor does a small endpoint term prove <thermodynamic equilibrium>. Periodic motion with $G(T)=G(0)$ gives the identity without an endpoint term at that finite $T$. For an <isolated system>, use an inertial <centre of mass> frame so translational drift does not contribute bulk <kinetic energy>.<Cosmology by David Tong/1 The Expanding Universe>[Cosmology by David Tong, §1.4.3, “The Virial Theorem and Galaxy Clusters”: bounded positions and velocities, existence of averages, equation (1.74) and endpoint limit]{ref}

For a differentiable <potential energy> that is a <homogeneous function> of degree $q$, choose its additive constant so that the scaling law really holds. With <conservative forces>, the <Euler theorem for homogeneous functions> gives
$$
U(\lambda\mathbf r_1,\ldots,\lambda\mathbf r_N)=\lambda^qU(\mathbf r_1,\ldots,\mathbf r_N),\qquad
\sum_i\mathbf r_i\cdot\mathbf F_i=-qU,\qquad
2\langle K\rangle_T-q\langle U\rangle_T=\frac{G(T)-G(0)}T.
$$
Thus a <harmonic oscillator> has $q=2$ and $\langle K\rangle=\langle U\rangle$. An <isolated system> with <Newtonian gravitational potential energy> has
$$
U=-G_{\!N}\sum_{i<j}\frac{m_im_j}{|\mathbf r_i-\mathbf r_j|},\qquad q=-1,\qquad
2\langle K\rangle+\langle U\rangle=0,\qquad
E=\langle K\rangle+\langle U\rangle=-\langle K\rangle.
$$
Each interacting pair occurs once, and $U=0$ at infinite separation. Here the total <energy> $E=K+U$ is conserved. An external field or a mixture of different scaling powers requires its own <force> term; one cannot assign the whole <potential energy> a single degree $q$. The <virial theorem for a homogeneous pair potential> states the corresponding general <energy> relation.

= Virial theorem on a Kepler ellipse
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$\langle K\rangle=\kappa/(2a),\quad\langle U\rangle=-\kappa/a$}

Consider the exact <two-body problem> with <masses> $m_1,m_2$. In the inertial <centre of mass> frame, its internal <kinetic energy> is $K=m|\dot{\mathbf r}|^2/2$, where $\mathbf r$ is their relative <position>, $m=m_1m_2/(m_1+m_2)$ is the <reduced mass>, and $U=-\kappa/r$ with $\kappa=G_{\!N}m_1m_2$. Take a noncollision <elliptic Kepler orbit> with <semi-major axis> $a$ and <orbital eccentricity> $0<e<1$. Write $\eta$ for <eccentric anomaly>, to avoid confusing it with total <energy> $E$. With <mean motion> $n=\sqrt{\kappa/(ma^3)}$ and <orbital period> $P=2\pi/n$, <Kepler's equation> and the <orbit> geometry give
$$
nt=\eta-e\sin\eta,\qquad r=a(1-e\cos\eta),\qquad
K=\frac{\kappa}{r}-\frac{\kappa}{2a},\qquad U=-\frac{\kappa}{r},\qquad E=-\frac{\kappa}{2a}.
$$
The <time> origin is <periapsis>. Since $dt=(1-e\cos\eta)\,d\eta/n$, the <time> average of $1/r$ over one <orbital period> is exactly $1/a$. Therefore $\langle K\rangle_P=\kappa/(2a)$ and $\langle U\rangle_P=-\kappa/a$, independently of <orbital eccentricity>. Equal steps in <eccentric anomaly> are not equal <time> steps; unweighted averaging of a drawing of the <ellipse> gives the wrong answer.

\Image[virial-kepler-ellipse-energy-time-averages.png]
{id=virial-kepler-ellipse-energy-time-averages}
{title=Instantaneous <kinetic energy> and <potential energy>, their <orbital period> averages, and the finite-window virial residual on an eccentric <Kepler orbit>}

The original dimensionless example in both diagrams has $m=a=\kappa=1$ and $e=0.6$. At <periapsis>, $r=0.4$, $K=2$, $U=-2.5$; at <apoapsis>, $r=1.6$, $K=0.125$, $U=-0.625$. Both have $E=-0.5$, but $2K+U$ is respectively $1.5$ and $-0.375$. Only the <orbital period> averages are $\langle K\rangle_P=0.5$ and $\langle U\rangle_P=-1$. The instantaneous zero of $2K+U$ occurs at $r=a$, not at every point of the <orbit>.

\Image[virial-kepler-ellipse-orbit-configuration.png]
{id=virial-kepler-ellipse-orbit-configuration}
{title=Relative <orbit> of the same <two-body problem>, with equal-<time> markers, the attracting focus, and <periapsis> and <apoapsis> <energies>}

The endpoint term can also be evaluated exactly: $G=m\mathbf r\cdot\dot{\mathbf r}=mna^2e\sin\eta$. Starting at <periapsis>, a window ending at <eccentric anomaly> $\eta$ has
$$
2\langle K\rangle_T+\langle U\rangle_T
=\frac{\kappa}{a}\frac{e\sin\eta}{\eta-e\sin\eta}.
$$
At $T=P/4$, <Kepler's equation> gives $\eta\simeq2.09133$ and a positive residual $\simeq0.3314\kappa/a$. At $T=P/2$ or $P$, $G$ returns to zero and the residual vanishes exactly. The lower panel shows the running residual and its endpoint expression coinciding; the shaded quarter-<orbital period> window explains why a short observation need not satisfy the infinite-<time> theorem.

= Virial mass estimate
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$M\simeq3R_g\sigma_{\rm los}^2/G_{\!N}$}

Motion can weigh a system whose <mass> is otherwise inaccessible. For a self-gravitating <isolated system> in <virial equilibrium>, let $K$ and $U$ denote equilibrium averages and use its <gravitational radius> $R_g=-G_{\!N}M^2/U$ and a mass-weighted mean squared <speed> relative to its <centre of mass>. Then $K=M\langle v^2\rangle/2$ gives $M=R_g\langle v^2\rangle/G_{\!N}$. If random <velocities> are <isotropic> and mean streaming has been removed, $\langle v^2\rangle=3\sigma_{\rm los}^2$, where $\sigma_{\rm los}$ is the one-dimensional line-of-sight <velocity dispersion>. Observations sample many objects at one epoch; replacing a <time> average by that sample requires a representative, approximately stationary population, beyond the trajectory identity itself.<Cosmology by David Tong/1 The Expanding Universe>[Cosmology by David Tong, §1.4.3, “The Virial Theorem and Galaxy Clusters”: self-averaging assumption and mass estimate (1.75)]{ref}

A practical measurement route uses an <optical telescope> to locate members and a <spectrograph> to measure <Doppler shifts> of identified <spectral lines>. Their shifts relative to the system's mean give radial <velocities>; angular separations plus a distance estimate constrain its size. Subtracting instrumental broadening and measurement variance, testing membership, and looking for coherent rotation or subgroups help control the inference. The projected luminous size need not equal $R_g$, and luminous tracers need not have the same distribution as all gravitating <mass>. Unknown <velocity-anisotropy parameters>, interlopers, mergers, external <tidal forces> and appreciable boundary stresses can bias a naive estimate. A large <velocity dispersion> alone does not establish <virial equilibrium>.

= Boundary-pressure correction to the virial theorem
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$2\langle K\rangle+\langle U\rangle=3\langle P_s\mathcal V\rangle$}

An external <pressure> changes the balance even if the system remains confined. For a material boundary acted on by spatially uniform inward <pressure> $P_s$, the surface <force> contributes $-P_s\int_{\partial\mathcal V}\mathbf r\cdot\mathbf n\,dS=-3P_s\mathcal V$, by the <divergence theorem>. For <Newtonian gravitational potential energy>, the finite-window identity becomes
$$
\frac{G(T)-G(0)}T=2\langle K\rangle_T+\langle U\rangle_T-3\langle P_s\mathcal V\rangle_T.
$$
Here $\mathcal V$ is the enclosed <volume>, $\mathbf n$ the outward <unit normal>, and $K$ includes the microscopic translational <kinetic energy> of the confined particles. Do not also add the same random motions as a separate internal <pressure> term. A nonuniform external stress needs its surface integral; a fixed observational boundary crossed by matter also needs flux terms.<Negative Specific Heat in Astronomy, Physics and Chemistry by D. Lynden-Bell>[Negative Specific Heat in Astronomy, Physics and Chemistry by D. Lynden-Bell, p. 2, §1: external-pressure virial relation]{ref}

With negligible surface <pressure>, the <negative heat capacity of a virialized gravitational system> follows from $E=-\langle K\rangle$: after a slow loss of total <energy> and readjustment to <virial equilibrium>, the average <kinetic energy> rises. For fixed $N$ classical particles with three translational <degrees of freedom>, define kinetic <temperature> $\Theta$ by $\langle K\rangle=3Nk_B\Theta/2$. Along this isolated equilibrium sequence, $dE/d\Theta=-3Nk_B/2$. The system contracts and its binding <potential energy> becomes more negative, supplying both the outgoing <energy> and the increased <kinetic energy>. This is an endpoint comparison, not a claim that every transient contraction obeys the average relation. A <collisionless stellar system> need not have a common thermodynamic <temperature>; a pressure-confined system instead has $E=-\langle K\rangle+3\langle P_s\mathcal V\rangle$. A stable <canonical ensemble> in contact with a large <thermal reservoir> does not support this negative <heat capacity> branch.<Negative Specific Heat in Astronomy, Physics and Chemistry by D. Lynden-Bell>[Negative Specific Heat in Astronomy, Physics and Chemistry by D. Lynden-Bell, pp. 2-3, §1: isolated-system energy, negative heat capacity and instability in a large heat bath]{ref}

= Virial theorem derivation and history
{parent=Virial theorem}

The proof is a single <time derivative>. Apply the <product rule> to each <dot product> and use <Newton's second law>:
$$
\dot G=\sum_i\dot{\mathbf p}_i\cdot\mathbf r_i+\sum_i\mathbf p_i\cdot\dot{\mathbf r}_i
=\sum_i\mathbf F_i\cdot\mathbf r_i+2K.
$$
Integrating from $0$ to $T$ gives the endpoint identity; the stated sublinear condition removes the endpoint term only in the infinite-<time> limit. Equivalently, for the scalar inertia $I=\tfrac12\sum_i m_i|\mathbf r_i|^2$ used in the <pairwise moment-of-inertia identity>, $\dot I=G$ and $\ddot I=2K+\sum_i\mathbf r_i\cdot\mathbf F_i$. Specifying this factor of one half avoids confusion with another common inertia convention.

In <On a Mechanical Theorem applicable to Heat by R. Clausius> (1870, pp. 123-124), the author defines stationary motion through bounded <positions> and <velocities>, includes planetary <orbits> and elastic vibrations, and names the average negative half of the force-position sum the “virial”. That historical convention differs from the auxiliary $G(t)$ defined above. His proof on pp. 126-127 keeps an endpoint term, then explains why it vanishes over a complete period or becomes small in a long bounded irregular motion. The same paper separates internal and external <forces> and includes uniform boundary <pressure> on pp. 125-126: confinement was already part of the formulation, rather than a reason to omit external stresses.<On a Mechanical Theorem applicable to Heat by R. Clausius>[On a Mechanical Theorem applicable to Heat by R. Clausius (1870), pp. 123-127; p. 122 footnote dates the lecture to 13 June 1870]{ref}

= Virial mean escape speed
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$\langle v_{\rm esc}^2\rangle=4\langle v^2\rangle$}

For an isolated equilibrium of equal-mass points, the <virial theorem> gives $\langle v^2\rangle=-W/M$. A particle's instantaneous <escape velocity> satisfies $v_{\rm esc}^2=-2\Phi$ with zero potential at infinity. Since $W=\tfrac12\sum_i m\Phi_i$, averaging gives $\langle v_{\rm esc}^2\rangle=-4W/M$. These are three-dimensional mean-square speeds, not one-component <velocity dispersions>.

= Virial theorem for a homogeneous pair potential
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$2\langle K\rangle=q\langle W\rangle$}

For a differentiable total pair <potential energy> homogeneous of degree $q$ in all particle positions, the <Euler theorem for homogeneous functions> gives $\sum_i\boldsymbol r_i\cdot\nabla_iW=qW$. Since $\boldsymbol F_i=-\nabla_iW$, the force virial is $-qW$. The bounded-motion time-averaged <virial theorem> consequently gives the displayed equation. For $q\ne0$, total energy is $E=(q+2)\langle K\rangle/q$. A positive kinetic-temperature coefficient makes its <heat capacity> negative exactly when $-2<q<0$. For a pair potential proportional to $r^{p+1}$ this means $-3<p<-1$.

= Negative heat capacity of a virialized gravitational system
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$dE/d\Theta=-3Nk_B/2$}

An isolated Newtonian system in <virial equilibrium> has total <energy> $E=K+W=-K$. Along an equilibrium sequence at fixed particle number, define the kinetic <temperature> by $K=3Nk_B\Theta/2$. Its <heat capacity> is therefore negative, as displayed: losing total energy increases the kinetic temperature. A <collisionless stellar system> need not have a thermodynamic temperature; this is a kinetic-temperature statement unless thermal equilibrium is additionally specified. Boundary pressure and external confinement can change the energy relation.

= Radiative energy of virialized gravitational collapse
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$E_{\rm rad}\simeq3M\sigma^2/2$}

For an isolated final <stellar population> with isotropic one-dimensional <velocity dispersion> $\sigma$, kinetic energy is $K=3M\sigma^2/2$. The <virial theorem> gives $W=-2K$ and total energy $E=-K$. Collapse from a diffuse nearly unbound gas reservoir therefore requires removal of approximately $K$ of energy. The radiated fraction can differ if there are outflows, appreciable initial binding energy, or boundary stresses.

= Virial equilibrium
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$2K+W=0$}

A bounded gravitational system in statistical equilibrium satisfies the time-averaged <virial theorem>. For inverse-distance gravity with negligible boundary stresses, $2K+W=0$. This relation does not require static particles or a homogeneous density profile, and reaching it does not erase the system's dynamical history.

= Positive-energy linear diameter growth
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$\liminf_{t\to\infty}R(t)/t\geq\sqrt{E/(2A_1)}$}

For an isolated Newtonian point-mass system, $\ddot I=T+E\geq E>0$. Twice integrating gives a quadratic lower bound on $I$. The <maximum-separation inertia bounds> then give at-least-linear asymptotic growth of the diameter $R$ for a trajectory existing for arbitrarily large times. The bound does not assert instantaneous monotonicity or escape of every particle.

= Pairwise moment-of-inertia identity
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$I=\frac1{2M}\sum_{j<k}m_jm_kr_{jk}^2$}

For positive <masses> in their <centre of mass> frame, $\sum_{j<k}m_jm_k|\boldsymbol r_j-\boldsymbol r_k|^2=M\sum_km_kr_k^2$. Thus the scalar inertia $I=\tfrac12\sum_km_kr_k^2$ equals $(2M)^{-1}\sum_{j<k}m_jm_kr_{jk}^2$. The identity compares total spatial extent with pairwise separations.

= Maximum-separation inertia bounds
{parent=Pairwise moment-of-inertia identity}
{title2=$B_1R^2\leq I\leq A_1R^2$}

Let $R$ be the actual largest pairwise separation of positive <masses> in their <centre of mass> frame. The <pairwise moment-of-inertia identity> gives $m_{(1)}m_{(2)}R^2/(2M)\leq I\leq R^2\sum_{j<k}m_jm_k/(2M)$. Consequently the scalar inertia is comparable to $R^2$, independently of configuration.

= Virial temperature
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$T_{\rm vir}$}

The <virial temperature> characterizes the thermal energy of gas supported in a gravitational potential. It is of order $\mu m_pGM/(k_BR)$, with a structural coefficient determined by the density profile. For a uniform self-gravitating gas sphere the <virial theorem> gives $k_BT=GM\mu m_p/(5R)$; a conventional halo circular-speed definition often uses $k_BT\simeq\mu m_pv_c^2/2$.

= Virial mass contours in a cooling diagram
{parent=Virial temperature}
{title2=$n_H\propto T^3/M^2$}

Combining a virial-temperature relation with the density of a uniform self-gravitating sphere gives $M\propto T^{3/2}n_H^{-1/2}$. Thus contours of constant mass in a density-temperature plane satisfy $n_H\propto T^3/M^2$. Structural coefficients, composition and the distinction between baryonic and total gravitating mass matter when giving numerical labels.

= Fixed-energy virial constraint between steady states
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$T=-E,\quad W=2E$}

Two finite isolated steady gravitational systems at the same conserved energy obey $2T+W=0$ at each endpoint, so both have $T=-E$ and $W=2E$. Redistribution can increase central concentration without making the global potential energy more negative. Escaping matter or an external reservoir requires a different energy accounting.

= Gravitational radius
{title2=$R_g$}
{parent=Virial theorem}
{wiki}

For a self-gravitating system of mass $M$ and <Newtonian gravitational potential energy> $W<0$, its gravitational radius is defined by
$$
R_g=-\frac{GM^2}{W}.
$$
It converts the total binding energy into a characteristic size without assuming a particular density profile.

= Gravitational rms escape and collapse speeds
{parent=Gravitational radius}
{title2=$\langle v_{\rm esc}^2\rangle=4\langle v_{\rm eq}^2\rangle,\quad\langle v_{\rm coll}^2\rangle=2\langle v_{\rm eq}^2\rangle$}

For identical point masses of total mass $M$, let $W=-GM^2/R_g$ exclude self-interactions. The <virial theorem> gives $\langle v_{\rm eq}^2\rangle=GM/R_g$. Since the sum of individual potential energies counts each pair twice, the mass-weighted mean frozen-potential <escape velocity> squared is $-4W/M$. A dissipation-free collapse from zero total <energy> instead has kinetic energy $-W$, hence mean squared speed $-2W/M$. These are three-dimensional speeds relative to the <centre of mass>; a coherent collapse speed is not a local random <velocity dispersion>.

= Hamiltonian mechanics
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

Hamiltonian mechanics describes phase-space evolution through a Hamiltonian and the symplectic structure.

= Adiabatic invariant
{parent=Hamiltonian mechanics}
{title2=$I$}

= Adiabatic invariance
{synonym}

A quantity conserved to leading order when a parameter varies slowly compared with the natural motion period. The <action variable> of a regular periodic Hamiltonian orbit is a standard example, as explained by <adiabatic invariance of the action>.

= Circular-shell adiabatic contraction
{parent=Adiabatic invariant}
{title2=$r_iM_i(r_i)=r_fM_f(r_f)$}

In a slowly changing spherical <gravitational potential>, <angular momentum> is exactly conserved and the <radial action> is an <adiabatic invariant>. An initially <circular orbit> remains circular in the adiabatic limit, so $J_r=0$ and $L^2=GrM(r)$ give the displayed shell relation. If dark-matter shells do not cross, their enclosed dark mass is also conserved. A density profile alone does not specify this orbital model: eccentric-orbit populations require their full action distribution.

= Inner NFW contraction to a flat rotation curve
{parent=Circular-shell adiabatic contraction}
{title2=$\rho'_D\propto r^{-5/3}$}

An inner <NFW profile> has $M_i(r_i)=Ar_i^2$, where $A=2\pi\rho_s r_s$. If the final total <circular speed> is $V$, <circular-shell adiabatic contraction> gives $r_i=(V^2/(GA))^{1/3}r^{2/3}$ and $M'_D=A^{1/3}(V^2/G)^{2/3}r^{4/3}$. Thus $\rho'_D=A^{1/3}(V^2/G)^{2/3}r^{-5/3}/(3\pi)$. The total density producing the flat curve is $V^2/(4\pi Gr^2)$, so the dark fraction tends to zero as $r^{1/3}$ despite its increase relative to the original cusp.

= Particle-speed invariant between slowly approaching walls
{parent=Adiabatic invariant}
{title2=$v\ell\approx\mathrm{constant}$}

An <elastic collision> with a wall moving toward the particle at speed $V$ changes the particle speed by approximately $2V$ when recoil is negligible. The collision rate is approximately $v/\ell$ for $v\gg V$, and $\dot\ell=-2V$, yielding $\dot v/v=-\dot\ell/\ell$. For a freely moving finite wall of mass $M$, the neglected recoil changes the increment by order $(m/M)v$, so relative accuracy also requires $(m/M)(v/V)\ll1$.

// Target: classical-mechanics.bigb

= Hamiltonian system
{c}
{parent=Hamiltonian mechanics}

A dynamical system on a <symplectic manifold> whose vector field is generated by a <Hamiltonian>. In <canonical variables> it obeys <Hamilton's equations>.

= Inverted quartic oscillator phase portrait
{parent=Hamiltonian system}
{title2=$K=x^2+y^2-y^4$}

The <autonomous planar system> $\dot x=y-2y^3$, $\dot y=-x$ preserves $K$. Its origin is a <center equilibrium> and $(0,\pm1/\sqrt2)$ are <saddle equilibria>. The level $K=1/4$ is $x=\pm(y^2-1/2)$: its two inner arcs are <heteroclinic orbits> enclosing periodic motion, while its outer branches connect saddles to escaping trajectories. Since $|\dot y|\sim y^2$ on escaping levels, infinity is reached in finite time. Adding $\alpha x/2$ to $\dot x$ gives $\dot K=\alpha x^2$, so positive $\alpha$ produces outward crossing of central levels and breaks the equal-energy heteroclinic connections.

= Conformal mechanics
{parent=Hamiltonian mechanics}

A one-dimensional particle with $H=p^2/2+g/x^2$ admits conserved time-translation, dilation and special-conformal charges. Their <Poisson brackets> form the <sl2R Lie algebra>. The homogeneity of the <inverse-square potential> is what allows the symmetry.

= Special conformal charge of inverse-square mechanics
{parent=Conformal mechanics}
{title2=$K=t^2E-txp+x^2/2$}

For the extended canonical variables with $\{t,E\}=-1$ and $\{x,p\}=1$, the charge $K=t^2E-txp+x^2/2$ generates a special conformal transformation. Its time derivative is $2te(E-H)$, so it is conserved on the <constraint surface>. With a time-dependent parameter $\beta$, the multiplier varies as $\delta e=-2te\beta$ and the action variation is $\dot\beta K-d(\beta x^2/2)/d\lambda$.

= Hamilton-Jacobi equation
{parent=Hamiltonian mechanics}
{c}
{title2=$\partial_tS+H(q,\partial_qS,t)=0$}

A principal function $S(q,t)$ obeys the <Hamilton-Jacobi equation> $\partial_tS+H(q,\partial_qS,t)=0$. A complete solution generates <canonical momentum> components $p_j=\partial_jS$ and local canonical coordinates constant along motion. For a time-independent <Hamiltonian>, $S=W(q)-Et$ reduces the equation to $H(q,\partial_qW)=E$. Additive separation of $W$ yields conserved separation constants, subject to local regularity and independence.

= Phase-space action
{parent=Hamiltonian mechanics}
{title2=$I=\int dt\,(p_I\dot q^I-H)$}

A phase-space <action> treats <canonical variables> as independent fields. Varying them gives <Hamilton's equations>; <Lagrange multipliers> can impose constraints.

= Relativistic particle phase-space action
{parent=Phase-space action}
{title2=$I=\int dt\,[p_m\dot x^m-\tfrac e2(p^2+M^2)]$}

The <worldline einbein> imposes the relativistic <mass-shell condition>. With the <Minkowski metric> of signature $(-,+,\ldots,+)$, its <first-class constraint> is $\varphi=(p^2+M^2)/2$.

= Pseudoclassical spinning particle
{parent=Relativistic particle phase-space action}
{title2=$\mathcal L=\dot x\cdot p+\tfrac i2\zeta\cdot\dot\zeta-\tfrac e2p^2-i\chi p\cdot\zeta$}

A pseudoclassical spinning particle supplements ordinary position and momentum by odd <Grassmann variables>. An even multiplier imposes $p^2=0$ and an odd multiplier imposes $p\cdot\zeta=0$. The odd symplectic term gives $\{\zeta^m,\zeta^n\}_{D}=-i\eta^{mn}$. Quantization represents these variables by a <Clifford algebra>, producing a spinor wavefunction.

= Spinning-particle Dirac quantization
{parent=Pseudoclassical spinning particle}
{title2=$\widehat\zeta^m=\gamma^m/\sqrt2$}

Quantizing the odd <Dirac brackets> yields $\{\widehat\zeta^m,\widehat\zeta^n\}_+=\eta^{mn}$. The <constraint> $p\cdot\zeta=0$ is then the <massless Dirac equation> on a spinor. Its square enforces $p^2=0$, matching the even <constraint>. The spin bilinear is represented by $-i[\gamma^m,\gamma^n]/4$ in this action convention.

= Spin from Grassmann bilinears
{parent=Pseudoclassical spinning particle}
{title2=$S^{mn}=-i\zeta^m\zeta^n$}

The odd particle variables form an even antisymmetric tensor through their bilinear. Adding it to orbital angular momentum gives a conserved Lorentz <Noether charge>. The particle and odd-variable equations make the two time derivatives cancel. After <spinning-particle Dirac quantization>, the bilinear becomes the spinor Lorentz generator rather than an ordinary commuting classical spin vector.

= Light-cone Hamiltonian
{parent=Relativistic particle phase-space action}
{title2=$H=(p_i^2+\mu^2)/(2p^+)$}

With $ds^2=-2dx^+dx^-+dx_i^2$, $x^+=t$, and $p_-\ne0$, the <mass-shell condition> gives $H=-p_+=-(p_i^2+\mu^2)/(2p_-)$. In the future-directed sector $p^+=-p_->0$, this is $(p_i^2+\mu^2)/(2p^+)$. Its <Schrodinger equation> is equivalent to the <Klein-Gordon equation> after multiplication by $2p_-$.

= Worldline einbein
{parent=Relativistic particle phase-space action}
{title2=$e(t)$}

An einbein specifies a one-dimensional <metric tensor> along a <worldline>. In the <relativistic particle phase-space action> it is the <Lagrange multiplier> for the <mass-shell condition> and transforms as $\delta e=\dot\epsilon$ under the corresponding canonical <gauge transformation>. After eliminating the <momentum>, its geometric interpretation depends on the normalization of the particle <action>.

= Proper-time gauge for a massive worldline einbein
{parent=Worldline einbein}
{title2=$ds=e\,d\lambda$}

For the <relativistic particle> action $S=(m/2)\int[g_{\mu\nu}\dot x^\mu\dot x^\nu/e-e]d\lambda$, varying the <worldline einbein> gives $g_{\mu\nu}\dot x^\mu\dot x^\nu=-e^2$. On the positive-einbein branch the <proper time> satisfies $ds=e\,d\lambda$. A <reparametrization> with $\lambda'=s$ sets the transformed einbein to one, making the <geodesic equation> affinely parametrized. This gauge choice is made after retaining the einbein <mass shell> constraint; fixing the action first would discard that equation. If the parameter interval is held fixed at both ends, its integrated <proper length> is a modulus and cannot be arbitrarily changed by endpoint-preserving transformations.

= Proper-time modulus
{parent=Worldline einbein}
{title2=$s$}

The constant mode of the <worldline einbein> is not removed by <gauge transformations> whose parameters vanish at the ends of a fixed interval. This Schwinger proper-time parameter remains integrated over after <gauge fixing>. For $\varphi=(p^2+M^2)/2$, the geometric on-shell proper length is $M\int e\,dt$.

= Worldline gauge-orbit determinant
{parent=Proper-time modulus}
{title2=$\Delta_{\mathrm{FP}}=\det{}^{\prime}\partial_t$}

On a unit interval with gauge parameters vanishing at its ends, $e=s+\dot\epsilon$ separates the <worldline einbein> into its invariant average $s$ and a <canonical gauge transformation>. The Jacobian for nonconstant modes is $\det\partial_t$ between the appropriate boundary-condition spaces. On a circle its prime excludes the constant gauge-parameter zero mode. This <Faddeev-Popov determinant> can be represented by anticommuting $b,c$ fields with an action proportional to $\int b\dot c\,dt$.

= Canonical variables
{parent=Hamiltonian mechanics}
{title2=$(q^I,p_I)$}

= Canonical coordinates
{synonym}

<Phase space> coordinates are canonical when their <Poisson brackets> have the form $\{q^I,p_J\}=\delta^I_J$, with the position-position and momentum-momentum brackets zero.

= Canonical pair
{parent=Canonical variables}
{title2=$(q,p)$}

A canonical pair consists of a coordinate and its <canonical momentum>, with <Poisson bracket> $\{q,p\}=1$.

= Leapfrog integration
{parent=Hamiltonian mechanics}
{wiki}

= Leapfrog integrator
{synonym}

The leapfrog integrator alternates half-steps for momentum with a full step for position and a final momentum half-step. It is reversible, symplectic, and second-order accurate.

= Hamiltonian flow
{c}
{parent=Hamiltonian mechanics}

Hamiltonian flow is the phase-space evolution generated by <Hamilton's equations> for a fixed <Hamiltonian>.

= Isotropic harmonic oscillator flow
{parent=Hamiltonian flow}
{title2=$S_t=\begin{pmatrix}\cos\omega t\,I&(\sin\omega t/\omega)I\\-\omega\sin\omega t\,I&\cos\omega t\,I\end{pmatrix}$}

For $H_\omega=(|v|^2+\omega^2|x|^2)/2$, the displayed linear <Hamiltonian flow> solves $\dot X=V$, $\dot V=-\omega^2X$. Its inverse is $S_{-t}$, its determinant is one and it preserves $H_\omega$. The zero-frequency limit is the shear $(x,v)\mapsto(x+tv,v)$.

= Uniform support bound from a coercive conserved energy
{parent=Hamiltonian flow}
{title2=$\operatorname{supp}f_t\subseteq\{H\leq E_*\}$}

For homogeneous <Hamiltonian Liouville equation> transport with autonomous $H$, compact initial support remains in the fixed energy sublevel determined by its maximum initial energy. If these sublevels are compact, this proves uniform compact support without solving the flow. The harmonic energy $H_\omega=(|v|^2+\omega^2|x|^2)/2$ has this property exactly when $\omega\ne0$; at zero frequency spatial free streaming can escape every compact set.

= Global characteristic flow for a Hamiltonian with bounded Hessian
{parent=Hamiltonian flow}
{title2=$\|D_z^2H(t,z)\|\leq L_T$}

If $H$ is $C^2$ and its spatial <Hessian matrix> is uniformly bounded on every finite time interval, its <Hamiltonian vector field> is globally <Lipschitz continuous> in <phase space> with at most linear growth. The <Picard-Lindelof theorem> and <Gronwall inequality> give a unique <Hamiltonian flow> for all finite forward and backward times. Bounds on $\nabla_zH(t,0)$ follow from time continuity on compact intervals.

= Quadratic rotational Lie-Poisson dynamics
{parent=Hamiltonian flow}
{title2=$\{x_i,x_j\}=-\epsilon_{ijk}x_k,\quad H=Ax_1^2+Bx_2^2+Cx_3^2$}

With the displayed signed <Lie-Poisson bracket> and evolution $\dot F=\{F,H\}$, the equations are $\dot x_1=2(C-B)x_2x_3$, $\dot x_2=2(A-C)x_3x_1$, and $\dot x_3=2(B-A)x_1x_2$. The energy and the <Casimir function of a Poisson manifold> $x_1^2+x_2^2+x_3^2$ are conserved. For $A=1/(2I_1)$, $B=1/(2I_2)$, $C=1/(2I_3)$ with positive inertias, these are the <Euler equations for a torque-free rigid body>. Reversing the bracket while fixing $H$ reverses time orientation.

= Integral of motion
{parent=Hamiltonian mechanics}
{wiki}

= Integrals of motion
{synonym}

An integral of motion is a function on <phase space> that remains constant along every trajectory. If it has no explicit time dependence, $I(q,p)$ is an integral exactly when its <Poisson bracket> with the <Hamiltonian> vanishes: $\{I,H\}=0$.

= Jacobi integral of a rigidly rotating potential
{c}
{parent=Integral of motion}
{title2=$E_J=E-\Omega L_z$}

For $\Phi(R,\phi-\Omega t,z)$ with constant pattern angular velocity $\Omega$, ordinary specific <energy> satisfies $\dot E=\partial_t\Phi=-\Omega\partial_\phi\Phi$ and specific axial <angular momentum> satisfies $\dot L_z=-\partial_\phi\Phi$. Their combination $E_J$ is conserved. This is the rotating-frame <integral of motion>; in the <circular restricted three-body problem>, the usual <Jacobi constant> is an affine rescaling of it. Thus time dependence in an inertial frame does not alone imply the absence of all integrals.

= Isolating integral
{parent=Integral of motion}

An isolating integral is a regular single-valued <integral of motion> whose fixed value restricts accessible <phase space> to a lower-dimensional level set. Functional independence of several such integrals means their differentials are linearly independent on the region considered. A central potential has <specific orbital energy> and the three components of <specific angular momentum> as four generically independent integrals. A <Kepler orbit> has a fifth independent integral supplied by its fixed apsidal direction, encoded by the <Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector>; its vector components obey two constraints with energy and angular momentum. Circular and radial orbits are degenerate cases of this count.

= Isolating integrals and orbital closure dimension
{parent=Isolating integral}
{title2=$N=6-d$}

In three-dimensional autonomous mechanics, a regular common level set of $N$ independent <isolating integrals> has dimension $6-N$ in <phase space>. If an <orbit> is dense in a $d$-dimensional <invariant torus> and the integrals characterize its closure, $N=6-d$. A generic three-frequency regular <orbit> has three integrals; a spherical nonclosed rosette has energy and three components of <specific angular momentum>, with two-dimensional closure and four integrals. A periodic nonstationary <orbit> has one-dimensional closure and can be isolated by five independent <orbit> labels on the invariant family. The maximum is five wherever the <Hamiltonian vector field> is nonzero, since all integral differentials annihilate that field. A single resonant <orbit> need not yield an additional smooth single-valued integral on an open neighborhood; global superintegrability is stronger than orbital closure.

= Conserved quantity
{parent=Integral of motion}
{wiki}

= Constant of motion
{synonym}

A conserved quantity is a quantity that remains constant along the time evolution of a system. In <Hamiltonian mechanics>, a possibly time-dependent phase-space function $F$ is conserved exactly when $\partial_tF+\{F,H\}=0$.

= Canonical momentum
{title2=$p_i$}
{parent=Hamiltonian mechanics}
{wiki}

= Canonical momenta
{synonym}

= Conjugate momentum
{synonym}

= Conjugate momenta
{synonym}

The canonical momentum conjugate to a generalized coordinate $q_i$ is $p_i=\partial L/\partial\dot q_i$.

= Canonical momentum sign in the affine particle action
{parent=Canonical momentum}
{title2=$P_\mu^c=-m g_{\mu\nu}\dot x^\nu$}

For the affine action $S=-\tfrac m2\int g_{\mu\nu}\dot x^\mu\dot x^\nu d\tau$ with signature $(-+++)$, differentiating with respect to velocity gives the displayed <canonical momentum>. Future-oriented proper time in a conformally flat metric yields $P_i^c=-a\gamma mv_i$, whereas the conventional future-directed mechanical momentum has $q_i=a\gamma mv_i$. Changing the action's overall sign interchanges these canonical labels but leaves its classical geodesics unchanged. Both labels are conserved by spatial <translation invariance>, and either gives the same physical momentum magnitude proportional to $a^{-1}$. The sign should be stated when comparing a worldline canonical covector with a positive-frequency field-mode label.

= Boundary-term shift of canonical field momenta
{parent=Canonical momentum}

If the spatially integrated <Lagrangian> changes by $dB[\Phi]/dt$, where $B$ depends on fields and their spatial derivatives but not velocities, the <canonical momentum> components change by $\pi_a\mapsto\pi_a+\delta B/\delta\Phi_a$. This follows by varying $dB/dt$ and using spatial <integration by parts>. The canonical one-form changes by the exact variation $\delta B$, so the <symplectic form> and <canonical commutation relations> are preserved. For the two <Feynman gauge> Maxwell densities, $B=\int A_0\partial_iA_i\,d^3x$ gives shifts $\Delta\pi^0=\partial_iA_i$ and $\Delta\pi^i=-\partial_iA_0$. Boundary flux must vanish for this spatial functional calculation.

= Phase space
{parent=Hamiltonian mechanics}
{wiki}

Phase space has one position coordinate $q_i$ and one conjugate momentum $p_i$ for every degree of freedom; a point $(q,p)$ specifies an instantaneous mechanical state.

= Velocity space
{parent=Phase space}
{title2=$\mathbf v\in\mathbb R^3$}

Velocity space is the space of velocity vectors at a fixed spatial position. Integrating a <galactic distribution function> over velocity space produces <mass density>; its normalized moments determine mean velocity and <velocity dispersion>.

= Phase-space area
{parent=Phase space}
{title2=$\oint p\,dq$}

The symplectic area enclosed by a closed orbit in a two-dimensional phase plane. With the orientation of Hamiltonian motion it is $\oint p\,dq=2\pi I$, where $I$ is the <action variable>.

= Phase-space trajectory
{parent=Phase space}

An <integral curve of a vector field> in phase space, representing the positions and momenta of a moving system. A periodic one-dimensional Hamiltonian orbit encloses the area used to define the <action variable>.

= Hamiltonian
{parent=Hamiltonian mechanics}

The Hamiltonian is a function of canonical position and momentum that generates time evolution through Hamilton's equations.

= Hamilton's equations
{c}
{parent=Hamiltonian}
{wiki}

= Hamilton equations
{c}
{synonym}

For canonical coordinates $(q_i,p_i)$ and Hamiltonian $H$,
$$
\dot q_i=\frac{\partial H}{\partial p_i},
\qquad
\dot p_i=-\frac{\partial H}{\partial q_i}.
$$

= Poisson bracket
{parent=Hamiltonian mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

In canonical coordinates,
$$
\{F,G\}=\sum_i\left(
\frac{\partial F}{\partial q_i}\frac{\partial G}{\partial p_i}
-\frac{\partial F}{\partial p_i}\frac{\partial G}{\partial q_i}
\right).
$$

= Poisson-commuting functions
{parent=Poisson bracket}
{c}
{title2=$\{F_i,F_j\}=0$}

= Poisson commutation
{synonym}
{c}

Functions $F_i$ for which $\{F_i,F_j\}=0$ for all pairs. Their <Hamiltonian vector fields> commute, and each is constant along the flows of the others. When they are <integrals of motion> of a <Hamiltonian>, they are <first integrals in involution>.

= Dirac bracket
{parent=Poisson bracket}
{c}

Second-class constraints $\chi_a$ with invertible bracket matrix can be eliminated with a modified bracket. For an ordinary even constraint set it is $\{F,G\}_D=\{F,G\}-\{F,\chi_a\}(C^{-1})^{ab}\{\chi_b,G\}$, $C_{ab}=\{\chi_a,\chi_b\}$. Graded constraints require the matching graded sign convention.

= Graded Dirac bracket
{parent=Dirac bracket}
{title2=$\{\psi^m,\psi^n\}_D=-i\eta^{mn}$}

A <Dirac bracket> on a graded phase space eliminates second-class constraints while respecting the <graded Poisson bracket>. The fermionic kinetic term $i\psi^m\dot\psi_m/2$ gives $\{\psi^m,\psi^n\}_D=-i\eta^{mn}$. Quantization then gives $\{\widehat\psi^m,\widehat\psi^n\}_+=\eta^{mn}$.

= Graded Poisson bracket
{parent=Poisson bracket}

An even Poisson bracket on a space with commuting and anticommuting variables obeys $\{F,G\}=-(-1)^{|F||G|}\{G,F\}$ and the graded Leibniz rule. In particular the bracket of two odd functions is symmetric. Quantization turns the odd-odd bracket into an anticommutator.

= Jacobi identity for the Poisson bracket
{parent=Poisson bracket}
{c}

The Poisson bracket satisfies
$$
\{F,\{G,H\}\}+\{G,\{H,F\}\}+\{H,\{F,G\}\}=0.
$$

= Poisson bracket of conserved quantities
{parent=Jacobi identity for the Poisson bracket}

If $F$ and $G$ have no explicit time dependence and satisfy $\{F,H\}=\{G,H\}=0$, the Jacobi identity gives $\{\{F,G\},H\}=0$. Their Poisson bracket is therefore also conserved.

= Hamiltonian conservation law
{parent=Poisson bracket}

Along a trajectory generated by the <Hamiltonian> $H$,
$$
\frac{dF}{dt}=\frac{\partial F}{\partial t}+\{F,H\}.
$$
Thus a time-independent observable is conserved exactly when its <Poisson bracket> with $H$ vanishes.

= Hamiltonian energy balance
{c}
{parent=Hamiltonian conservation law}
{title2=$\frac d{dt}H(t,X,V)=\partial_tH(t,X,V)$}

Along <Hamilton's equations>, spatial derivatives of the <Hamiltonian function> cancel in its total time derivative. Thus $dH/dt=\partial_tH$. An autonomous <Hamiltonian function> is conserved; explicit time dependence generally prevents conservation even though <phase space> is still preserved.

= Energy balance for an autonomous Lagrangian
{parent=Hamiltonian conservation law}
{title2=$\dot H=-\dot x\,\delta A/\delta x$}

For an autonomous <Lagrangian> $L(x,\dot x)$, define its <action> $A=\int L\,dt$ and <Hamiltonian> $H=\dot xL_{\dot x}-L$. The <chain rule> gives
$$
\dot H=\dot x\left(\frac d{dt}L_{\dot x}-L_x\right)
=-\dot x\frac{\delta A}{\delta x}.
$$
Thus the identity holds along forced trajectories as well as solutions of the homogeneous <Euler-Lagrange equation>. Explicit time dependence adds $-\partial_tL$; stochastic work requires a consistent regularization or the <Stratonovich chain rule>.

= Phase-space dilation generator
{parent=Poisson bracket}

The function $D=q\cdot p$ generates the canonical scaling $(q,p)\mapsto(e^tq,e^{-t}p)$. An observable $F$ is invariant under this scaling exactly when
$$
\{F,D\}=q\cdot\nabla_qF-p\cdot\nabla_pF=0.
$$

= Poisson bracket in complex canonical coordinates
{parent=Poisson bracket}

For $z_j=q_j+ip_j$ and $\bar z_j=q_j-ip_j$,
$$
\{f,g\}
=-2i\frac{\partial f}{\partial z_j}
\frac{\partial g}{\partial\bar z_j}
+2i\frac{\partial g}{\partial z_j}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial\bar z_j}.
$$
This follows from $\partial_{q_j}=\partial_{z_j}+\partial_{\bar z_j}$ and $\partial_{p_j}=i\partial_{z_j}-i\partial_{\bar z_j}$.

= Anti-Hermitian quadratic Hamiltonian
{parent=Poisson bracket in complex canonical coordinates}

For an anti-Hermitian matrix $A$, the real quadratic Hamiltonian
$$
I_A=\frac1{2i}\bar z^TAz
$$
satisfies
$$
\{I_A,I_B\}=-I_{[A,B]}.
$$
Thus $A\mapsto-I_A$ realizes the matrix commutator as a Poisson bracket; it is the quadratic moment-map construction for the unitary action.

= Variational derivative
{parent=Hamiltonian mechanics}

For a functional $H[u]$, its variational derivative is defined by
$$
\left.\frac d{d\epsilon}H[u+\epsilon v]\right|_{\epsilon=0}
=\int\frac{\delta H}{\delta u}v\,dx
$$
after integrating derivatives of $v$ by parts and discarding the boundary terms.

= Hamiltonian field equation
{parent=Variational derivative}

For a complex field $\psi$, the canonical Hamiltonian evolution convention
$$
i\psi_t=\frac{\delta H}{\delta\overline\psi}
$$
is accompanied by its complex conjugate equation.

= Hamiltonian evolution equation
{parent=Hamiltonian field equation}
{title2=$u_t=J\,\delta H/\delta u$}

An evolution equation has Hamiltonian form when a Hamiltonian operator applied to the <variational derivative> of a functional gives its time <derivative>. The induced functional bracket must satisfy the Poisson properties, including Jacobi.

= Canonical transformation
{parent=Hamiltonian mechanics}
{wiki}

A canonical transformation preserves the symplectic form and hence all Poisson brackets. In one degree of freedom, an invertible map $(q,p)\mapsto(Q,P)$ is canonical exactly when $\{Q,P\}_{q,p}=1$.

= Exact quadratic normal form for a perturbed linear rotor
{parent=Canonical transformation}
{title2=$H\circ\Phi=E+\omega\cdot y'+\tfrac12Q+\epsilon^2\widetilde V$}

For $H=\omega\cdot y+\frac12|y|^2+\epsilon V(x)$ with a <Diophantine frequency vector>, solve $\mathcal D_\omega u=\langle V\rangle-V$ and apply <translated conjugacy of a Diophantine vector field> to $w=\epsilon\nabla u$. Set $\eta=\epsilon\nabla u+a$ and use the <symplectic cotangent lift with a closed momentum shift>. The transformed <Hamiltonian> is exactly $E+\omega\cdot y'+\frac12y'^T D\chi^{-1}D\chi^{-T}y'+\frac12|\eta\circ\chi|^2$, where $E=\epsilon\langle V\rangle+\omega\cdot a$. Since $a=O(\epsilon^2)$, the last term is an angular potential of order $\epsilon^2$ and the quadratic coefficient is identity plus $O(\epsilon)$. The construction is on a smaller angular strip and momentum ball.

= Canonical completion of independent commuting functions
{parent=Canonical transformation}
{title2=$\{Q_i,Q_j\}=0$}

Independent functions $Q_1,\ldots,Q_n$ on a $2n$-dimensional <symplectic manifold> can locally form the position half of canonical coordinates exactly when $\{Q_i,Q_j\}=0$ throughout a neighbourhood. Their commuting <Hamiltonian vector fields> span the fibres of $Q$. Flow from a transverse section gives coordinates $(Q,t)$ with $\omega=\sum dt_i\wedge dQ_i+\beta(Q)$. Write $\beta=d\alpha$ locally and take $P_i=-t_i-\alpha_i(Q)$. Then $\omega=\sum dQ_i\wedge dP_i$.

= Type-two generating function for a canonical transformation
{parent=Canonical transformation}

A function $S(q,P)$ generates a canonical transformation through
$$
p=\frac{\partial S}{\partial q},
\qquad
Q=\frac{\partial S}{\partial P}.
$$

= Type-one generating function for a canonical transformation
{parent=Canonical transformation}

A function $\Phi(q,Q)$ generates a canonical transformation through
$$
p=\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial q},
\qquad
P=-\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial Q}.
$$

= Integrable Hamiltonian system
{parent=Hamiltonian mechanics}

= Complete integrability
{synonym}

= Completely integrable Hamiltonian system
{synonym}

= Liouville integrability
{c}
{synonym}

= Liouville integrable
{c}
{synonym}

Liouville integrability means having $n$ almost-everywhere independent first integrals in mutual Poisson involution.

For a Hamiltonian phase space of dimension $2n$, Liouville integrability means $n$ functionally independent conserved quantities with pairwise vanishing <Poisson brackets>. This is the Hamiltonian specialization of <integrable systems>.

= Superintegrable Hamiltonian system
{parent=Integrable Hamiltonian system}

A <superintegrable Hamiltonian system> with $n$ degrees of freedom has more than $n$ functionally independent <integrals of motion>, including an integrable set of $n$ in involution. At a regular nonzero <Hamiltonian vector field>, at most $2n-1$ independent integral differentials can annihilate the flow. A maximally superintegrable system attains that bound. The three-dimensional <Kepler orbit> has five independent integrals through energy, angular momentum and the <Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector>. A closed orbit on one isolated resonance is weaker than maximal superintegrability on an open phase-space region.

= Integrals of an irrational two-frequency oscillator
{parent=Integrable Hamiltonian system}
{title2=$F=\phi(R_1,R_2)$}

On the regular set of two uncoupled oscillators with irrational frequency ratio, every continuous globally defined <integral of motion> is constant on each fixed-action <flat torus>, because every orbit is dense there. A differentiable integral is consequently a function of the two actions, so its <differential of a smooth map> lies in their span. Local angle combinations can still give extra local integrals; they fail to be single-valued on the torus.

= Hamiltonian period lattice
{parent=Integrable Hamiltonian system}
{c}
{title2=$\Lambda=\{t:\varphi^t(z)=z\}$}

For a compact connected regular level of $n$ independent <Poisson-commuting functions>, the periods of their joint flow form a full-rank discrete subgroup $\Lambda\subset\mathbb R^n$. The level is $\mathbb R^n/\Lambda$. A local smooth basis of these periods determines <action integrals> and the normalized periodic fields used to construct <action-angle variables>.

= Stäckel potential
{parent=Integrable Hamiltonian system}
{c}
{title2=$\psi=\frac{G(\lambda)-G(\mu)}{\lambda-\mu}$}

A <Stäckel potential> allows additive separation of the <Hamilton-Jacobi equation> in an orthogonal coordinate system. In axisymmetric <prolate spheroidal coordinates>, the relative-potential form is $\psi=[G(\lambda)-G(\mu)]/(\lambda-\mu)$. It gives <energy>, axial <angular momentum> and an independent third separation <integral>. Here $G$ is a one-variable generating function, not the gravitational constant. Generic bounded <orbits> are regular invariant-torus motions; resonance and coordinate degeneracy require separate treatment.

= Orbit volume in an axisymmetric Stäckel potential
{parent=Stäckel potential}
{title2=$p_\tau^2\geq0$}

For fixed $E,l,I$, separated motion obeys $p_\tau^2=[E\tau+G(\tau)-I+(\alpha-\beta)l^2/(2(\tau+\alpha))]/[2(\tau+\alpha)(\tau+\beta)]$. Allowed intervals are where this is nonnegative, with turning points and coordinate-fold endpoints treated separately. A generic nonresonant bound <orbit> with $l\ne0$ circulates azimuthally and densely samples a projected <volume> bounded by confocal spheroids and hyperboloids. It is not uniformly distributed in <volume>. Resonant or zero-angular-momentum <orbits> can project to lower-dimensional regions; integrability alone does not imply that every <orbit> fills a three-dimensional shell.

= Axisymmetric Stäckel third integral
{parent=Stäckel potential}
{title2=$I$}

For $H=\tfrac12\sum p_j^2/h_j^2-\psi$, $l=p_\phi$ and a <Stäckel potential>, multiplying the separated <Hamilton-Jacobi equation> by $\lambda-\mu$ gives $2(\tau+\alpha)(\tau+\beta)p_\tau^2-(\alpha-\beta)l^2/[2(\tau+\alpha)]-G(\tau)-E\tau=-I$ for both $\tau=\lambda,\mu$. Eliminating $E$ gives $I=\tfrac12[\mu v_\lambda^2+\lambda v_\mu^2+(\lambda+\mu+\alpha)v_\phi^2]-[\mu G(\lambda)-\lambda G(\mu)]/(\lambda-\mu)$. Physical components are $v_j=h_j\dot q_j$ and $p_j=h_jv_j$. Separation proves conservation locally; direct <Poisson brackets> extend it across regular coordinate patches.

= First integrals in involution
{parent=Integrable Hamiltonian system}

First integrals are in involution when every pair has zero <Poisson bracket>. An $n$-degree-of-freedom Hamiltonian system is <Liouville integrable> when it has $n$ almost-everywhere independent first integrals in involution.

= Invariant torus
{parent=Integrable Hamiltonian system}

An invariant torus is a subset homeomorphic to a <torus> and preserved by a <flow map>[flow]. In <action-angle variables>, an integrable Hamiltonian flow moves linearly in the angular coordinates of each regular invariant torus.

The torus is a particular <invariant manifold>; its toroidal topology is an extra condition beyond invariance.

= Quasiperiodic motion
{parent=Invariant torus}
{wiki}

= Quasiperiodic orbit
{synonym}

A quasiperiodic orbit on a torus has angular coordinates $\theta(t)=\theta(0)+\omega t$ modulo $2\pi$. It is <periodic orbit>[periodic] exactly when the nonzero frequencies are commensurate; rationally independent frequencies produce a dense orbit.

= Incommensurate harmonic-oscillator orbit
{parent=Quasiperiodic motion}

For two uncoupled <harmonic oscillators> with nonzero amplitudes and irrational frequency ratio $\omega_1/\omega_2$, the orbit is bounded but not <periodic orbit>[periodic]. It winds quasiperiodically on a two-dimensional <invariant torus>; a common period would force the frequency ratio to be rational.

= Direct product of integrable Hamiltonian systems
{parent=Integrable Hamiltonian system}

The direct product of integrable Hamiltonian systems is integrable. Pull each family's commuting <integrals of motion> back to the product <phase space>. Integrals from different factors have zero <Poisson bracket> because they use disjoint canonical coordinates, and their <total differential>[differentials] are independent wherever the differentials within each factor are independent.

= Liouville integrability of the isotropic harmonic oscillator
{parent=Integrable Hamiltonian system}

For
$$
H=\frac12\sum_{j=1}^n(q_j^2+p_j^2),
$$
the functions $I_j=(q_j^2+p_j^2)/2$ are independent almost everywhere, Poisson commute, and sum to $H$. A regular common level $I_j=c_j>0$ is the product of circles of radii $\sqrt{2c_j}$, hence an invariant $n$-torus. Allowing some $c_j=0$ gives lower-dimensional singular tori.

= Liouville–Arnold theorem
{c}
{parent=Integrable Hamiltonian system}
{wiki}

= Arnold-Liouville theorem
{c}
{synonym}

A compact connected regular common level of $n$ commuting integrals is an $n$-torus with local action-angle coordinates.

= Action-angle variables
{parent=Integrable Hamiltonian system}
{wiki}

= Action-angle coordinates
{synonym}

For a one-degree-of-freedom closed orbit, action-angle coordinates make the Hamiltonian a function $H(I)$ and give $\dot I=0$, $\dot\phi=dH/dI$.

= Delaunay variables
{c}
{parent=Action-angle variables}
{title2=$(\mathcal L,\mathcal G,\mathcal H;\ell,\omega,\Omega)$}

For a bound noncollision <Kepler orbit> with gravitational parameter $\mu$, the canonical actions per unit mass are $\mathcal L=\sqrt{\mu a}$, $\mathcal G=\mathcal L\sqrt{1-e^2}$ and $\mathcal H=\mathcal G\cos i$. Their angles are <mean anomaly> $\ell$, <argument of periapsis> $\omega$ and <longitude of ascending node> $\Omega$. The <Hamiltonian> is $-\mu^2/(2\mathcal L^2)$, so only $\ell$ advances in the unperturbed problem. In the prograde spherical-action chart, $J_r=\mathcal L-\mathcal G$, $J_\theta=\mathcal G-\mathcal H$, $J_\phi=\mathcal H$; the type-two <canonical transformation> is generated by $F_2=(\mathcal L-\mathcal G)w_r+(\mathcal G-\mathcal H)w_\theta+\mathcal H w_\phi$. Retrograde spherical actions have $J_\theta=\mathcal G-|\mathcal H|$, so the absolute value cannot be dropped globally. Circular and coplanar limits are singular angle charts, not singular orbits.

= Action integral
{parent=Action-angle variables}
{title2=$I_j=\frac1{2\pi}\oint_{\gamma_j}\lambda$}

Near a regular <Lagrangian torus>, choose a one-form $\lambda$ with $d\lambda=-\omega$ and a basis of fibre cycles $\gamma_j$. The action $I_j=(2\pi)^{-1}\int_{\gamma_j}\lambda$ has <differential of a smooth map> $dI_j=(2\pi)^{-1}\sum_i t_i^{(j)}dF_i$, where $t^{(j)}$ is the corresponding joint-flow period. Nonsingularity of the period matrix makes these actions local coordinates transverse to the tori.

= Action variable
{parent=Action-angle variables}

With angle period $2\pi$, the action is the enclosed phase-space area divided by $2\pi$:
$$I=(2\pi)^{-1}\oint p\,dq.$$

This is the action component of <action-angle variables>, rather than the entire coordinate construction.

= Radial action of a bound Kepler orbit
{c}
{parent=Action variable}
{title2=$I_r=k/\sqrt{-2E}-|\ell|$}

For $H=p_r^2/2+\ell^2/(2r^2)-k/r$, a noncollision bound orbit has $\ell\ne0$ and $-k^2/(2\ell^2)\leq E<0$. Its radial action is $\sqrt{-2E}/\pi$ times $\int_\alpha^\beta\sqrt{(r-\alpha)(\beta-r)}\,dr/r$, where $\alpha,\beta=[k\mp\sqrt{k^2+2E\ell^2}]/(-2E)$. Evaluating the integral gives the displayed action and $H=-k^2/[2(I_r+|\ell|)^2]$. The angular and radial frequencies have equal magnitudes, explaining closed orbits rather than generic quasiperiodicity. The circular limit has $I_r=0$; zero angular momentum gives collision in the unregularized phase space.

= Adiabatic invariance of the action
{parent=Action variable}

When a Hamiltonian parameter varies slowly compared with the orbital period and no separatrix is crossed, the action changes only at higher adiabatic order.

The action is an <adiabatic invariant> under these assumptions; other slowly varying systems can have different adiabatic invariants.

= Adiabatic particle between moving parallel walls
{parent=Adiabatic invariance of the action}

For a one-dimensional particle of energy $E$ between walls separated by $L$, the unnormalized action is
$$
\mathcal I=\oint p\,dq=2L\sqrt{2mE}.
$$
Adiabatic conservation gives $E(t)L(t)^2=\text{constant}$.

= Adiabatic parameter scaling from an action
{parent=Adiabatic invariance of the action}

If an action has parameter dependence $I\propto\sqrt{m/|E|}$, adiabatic conservation implies $|E|\propto m$.

= Adiabatic invariance of an orbital action
{parent=Adiabatic invariance of the action}

When a gravitational potential changes slowly relative to an orbital period, the orbital actions remain constant provided no separatrix is crossed. In a spherical potential, angular momentum is conserved exactly and the radial action is adiabatically conserved.

= Adiabatic actions do not preserve orbital closure
{parent=Adiabatic invariance of an orbital action}

<Adiabatic invariance of an orbital action> preserves action values during slow regular changes, but frequencies $\partial H/\partial J_i$ can vary. Closure requires rational frequency ratios, which need not persist. A slowly added harmonic component in a <Kepler orbit> potential produces generic apsidal precession.

= Radial action
{title2=$J_r$}
{parent=Action variable}

For radial motion between turning radii $r_-$ and $r_+$ in a central potential, the radial action per unit mass is
$$
J_r=\frac1\pi\int_{r_-}^{r_+}
\sqrt{2[E-\Phi(r)]-\frac{L^2}{r^2}}\,dr.
$$
It vanishes on a circular orbit and is canonically conjugate to the radial angle.

= Radial action of a Kepler orbit
{title2=$J_r$}
{parent=Radial action}

For a bound Kepler orbit of semimajor axis $a$ around mass $M$,
$$
J_r+L=\sqrt{GMa}.
$$
Adiabatic central-mass change therefore preserves $Ma$ and orbital eccentricity.

= Action of the one-dimensional Kepler collision orbit
{parent=Action variable}

For $H=p^2/(2m)-1/|q|=-|E|$, collision continuation between the two turning points gives $I=\sqrt{2m/|E|}$.

= Liouville's theorem
{disambiguate=Hamiltonian}
{c}
{parent=Hamiltonian mechanics}
{wiki}

= Liouville theorem in Hamiltonian mechanics
{c}
{synonym}

Hamiltonian flow has zero phase-space divergence and preserves phase volume.

= Particle dynamics
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

Particle dynamics studies motion determined by forces through Newton’s laws.

= Test particle
{parent=Particle dynamics}
{wiki}

A <test particle> is an idealized body whose own fields and effect on its environment are negligible. Its motion probes an externally prescribed field. A gravitational <test particle> follows a <timelike geodesic> when nongravitational forces are absent; appreciable self-gravity requires a more complete treatment.

= One-dimensional conservative motion
{parent=Particle dynamics}
{wiki}

For force minus the derivative of a potential, total kinetic plus potential energy is conserved.

= Period near a separatrix
{parent=One-dimensional conservative motion}
{wiki}

A trajectory approaching a <nondegenerate critical point>[nondegenerate] unstable maximum of its <potential energy> has a logarithmically divergent period.

= Simple harmonic motion
{parent=One-dimensional conservative motion}
{wiki}
{title2=$\ddot x+\omega^2x=0$}

= Simple harmonic oscillator
{synonym}

= Harmonic oscillator equation
{synonym}

= Harmonic oscillator
{synonym}

<Simple harmonic motion> is motion in which the <acceleration> is proportional to the <displacement> from <equilibrium> and points back towards it:
$$
\ddot x=-\omega^2x,\qquad \omega>0.
$$
Here $x$ is measured from <equilibrium>, and $\omega$ is the constant <angular frequency>. For a <point mass> attached to an ideal <spring>, <Hooke's law> gives the restoring <force> $F=-kx$, with stiffness $k>0$, and <Newton's second law> yields $\omega=\sqrt{k/m}$. The minus sign matters: for $x>0$ the <force> is negative, and for $x<0$ it is positive. A restoring <force> need not oppose <velocity>; on the return towards <equilibrium> it increases <speed>.

The general solution is
$$
x(t)=A\cos(\omega t+\varphi),\qquad
v(t)=-A\omega\sin(\omega t+\varphi),\qquad
P=\frac{2\pi}{\omega}.
$$
The <amplitude> $A\geq0$ and <oscillation phase> $\varphi$ encode the <initial conditions>. We use $P$ for the <oscillation period> to distinguish it from <kinetic energy> $T$. For $A>0$, the <oscillation period> is independent of <amplitude>, the property of <isochronous harmonic motion>. At $A=0$ the body remains at <equilibrium>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-01sc-classical-mechanics-fall-2016/mit8_01scs22_chapter23.pdf[MIT mechanics, Chapter 23, sections 23.2 and 23.3]{ref}

Choose $m=0.5\,\mathrm{kg}$, $k=2\,\mathrm{N/m}$, and release the <mass> from rest at <displacement> $A=0.3\,\mathrm m$. Then $\omega=2\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, $P=\pi\,\mathrm s$, and $\varphi=0$. Writing <time> $t$ in seconds,
$$
x(t)=0.3\cos(2t)\,\mathrm m,\qquad
v(t)=-0.6\sin(2t)\,\mathrm{m/s}.
$$
At $t=0$ the <velocity> vanishes, but the <acceleration> is $-1.2\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$: a <turning point> is not a state of rest that persists. At $t=\pi/4\,\mathrm s$, the body crosses <equilibrium> with <velocity> $-0.6\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and zero <acceleration>. At $t=\pi/2\,\mathrm s$ it reaches the opposite <turning point>, $x=-0.3\,\mathrm m$.

Taking <potential energy> zero at <equilibrium>, the same solution gives
$$
V(t)=\frac12kx(t)^2=0.09\cos^2(2t)\,\mathrm J,\qquad
T(t)=\frac12mv(t)^2=0.09\sin^2(2t)\,\mathrm J,
\qquad E=T+V=0.09\,\mathrm J.
$$
At each <turning point> all <mechanical energy> is <potential energy>; at each <equilibrium> crossing it is <kinetic energy>. Both repeat twice during one complete <position> cycle because squaring removes the sign. At $t=\pi/8\,\mathrm s$, for example, each contributes $0.045\,\mathrm J$.

\Image[mechanical-energy-oscillation.png]
{title=Position, velocity, phase ellipse and energy exchange for a mass of 0.5 kg on a spring of stiffness 2 N/m}

The physical <position> trace covers a line segment, $-0.3\leq x\leq0.3$ in metres. In the <phase plane>, the paired <position> and <velocity> instead trace the <ellipse>
$$
\frac{x^2}{(0.3\,\mathrm m)^2}+\frac{v^2}{(0.6\,\mathrm{m/s})^2}=1.
$$
This is precisely $mv^2/2+kx^2/2=E$, rewritten: the single conserved <mechanical energy> fixes the <amplitude> and the whole <ellipse>. It does not fix the starting <oscillation phase>. At $x=0$ both $v=-0.6\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and $v=+0.6\,\mathrm{m/s}$ lie on it; <position> alone cannot distinguish them. Replacing $v$ by <momentum> $p=mv$ gives the equivalent <phase-space trajectory>. The arrows show increasing <time>, clockwise in $(x,v)$ coordinates.

This closed <ellipse> assumes a time-independent <spring> and no <damping> or applied drive. Adding a resisting <force> $-bv$, $b>0$, gives $m\ddot x+b\dot x+kx=0$ and $dE/dt=-bv^2\leq0$. An underdamped <damped harmonic oscillator> spirals inward in the <phase plane>, crossing successively smaller <energy> contours. The ideal <harmonic oscillator> is also useful beyond <springs>: near a smooth <potential energy> minimum with $V''(0)>0$, its <Taylor expansion> begins with $V(0)+V''(0)x^2/2$. For sufficiently small <displacements>, this gives the same restoring <force> to leading order, explaining why <small oscillations> share one solvable pattern.

= Fixed-endpoint harmonic-oscillator action
{parent=Simple harmonic motion}
{title2=$S_{\rm cl}=\frac{\omega[(a^2+b^2)\cos\omega T-2ab]}{2\sin\omega T}$}

For $S=\tfrac12\int_0^T(\dot x^2-\omega^2x^2)dt$ and fixed endpoint values $a,b$, the <Euler-Lagrange equation> is $\ddot x+\omega^2x=0$. When $\sin\omega T\ne0$, solve the endpoint equations for the unique trajectory and use $\dot x^2-\omega^2x^2=(x\dot x)'$ on that trajectory to obtain the displayed <action>. For $\omega\ne0$, at $\omega T=k\pi$ a solution exists only if $b=(-1)^ka$; then there is a family of solutions and their action is zero. At $\omega=0$ the nonresonant formula has the limit $(b-a)^2/(2T)$.

= Quartic oscillator
{parent=Simple harmonic motion}

A quartic oscillator adds an anharmonic term to the quadratic potential, for example $H=(p^2+q^2)/2+\epsilon q^4$.

The quartic term is a specific <anharmonicity> of the oscillator potential.

= Isochronous harmonic motion
{parent=Simple harmonic motion}

Harmonic trajectories are isochronous: all nonconstant oscillations with a fixed frequency have the same period, regardless of amplitude.

= Collision mechanics
{parent=Particle dynamics}
{wiki}

Collision mechanics uses impulse together with conservation of momentum and, for elastic collisions, kinetic energy.

= Coefficient of restitution
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{wiki}
{title2=$e=\frac{\text{normal separation speed}}{\text{normal approach speed}}$}

The restitution law applies to velocities of the actual contact points, including any rotational velocities of rigid bodies. A perfectly <elastic collision> has $e=1$; $e=0$ gives zero immediate normal separation speed, but does not by itself require all parts of colliding bodies to share the same velocity.

// Target: classical-mechanics.bigb

= Geometric bounce sequence
{parent=Coefficient of restitution}
{title2=$h_n=\gamma^{2n}h_0,\quad T_n=2\gamma^n\sqrt{2h_0/g}$}

For repeated vertical collisions with a fixed plate and constant <coefficient of restitution> $0\le\gamma<1$, each collision multiplies the upward speed by $\gamma$, so successive peak heights form the displayed <geometric sequence>. The initial fall plus both legs of every rebound have total distance $h_0(1+\gamma^2)/(1-\gamma^2)$. The idealized impact times accumulate in finite time when $\gamma<1$; real contact mechanics replaces the limiting sequence by rest.

= Collision
{parent=Collision mechanics}

A collision is an interaction between approaching bodies or particles that changes their motion. <Momentum conservation> and <conservation of energy> constrain the outcomes; <inelastic collisions> can transfer mechanical energy into internal degrees of freedom.

= Collision cross-section
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{title2=$\sigma_c$}

A collision cross-section is the effective area that converts a target <number density> and incident particle speed into a <collision> rate. It can depend on particle species, relative speed, and long-range forces, and need not equal a purely <geometric collision cross-section>.

= Impact parameter
{title2=$b$}
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{wiki}

The impact parameter is the perpendicular distance between an incoming particle's asymptotic straight-line trajectory and the centre of a scattering potential. In central-force scattering it determines the deflection angle whenever the scattering map is single-valued.

= Geometric collision cross-section
{parent=Collision mechanics}

= Geometric cross-section
{synonym}

Two spheres of diameters $D_1,D_2$ collide geometrically when their projected centre separation is at most $(D_1+D_2)/2$, giving cross-section $\sigma=\pi(D_1+D_2)^2/4$.

= Cross-section per unit mass
{parent=Geometric collision cross-section}

For an equal-density sphere of diameter $D$, the ratio of geometric cross-section to mass is
$$
\frac{\pi D^2/4}{\pi\rho D^3/6}=\frac{3}{2\rho D}.
$$
Breaking a fixed mass into smaller pieces therefore increases its total cross-section in inverse proportion to fragment size.

= Gravitational focusing
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{wiki}

Gravitational focusing increases a physical collision cross-section because mutual gravity bends initially non-colliding trajectories toward one another.

= Elastic collision
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{wiki}

An <elastic collision> preserves total <kinetic energy> between the incoming and outgoing states. For isolated colliding bodies, <momentum conservation> also holds. The <kinetic energy> condition supplies information beyond <momentum conservation> alone. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-4-types-of-collisions[OpenStax University Physics Volume 1, section 9.4: Types of Collisions]{ref}

Consider a stipulated one-dimensional example with rightward positive: $m_1=2\,\mathrm{kg}$, $m_2=1\,\mathrm{kg}$, incoming <velocities> $u_1=3\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and $u_2=0$. The total <momentum> is $P=6\,\mathrm{kg\,m/s}$ and total <kinetic energy> is $K=9\,\mathrm J$. In <SI> numerical values, outgoing <velocities> $v_1,v_2$ satisfy
$$
2v_1+v_2=6,\qquad v_1^2+\frac12v_2^2=9.
$$
Substituting $v_2=6-2v_1$ gives $3(v_1-1)(v_1-3)=0$. The root $v_1=3$, $v_2=0$ repeats the incoming state; the outgoing separating state is $v_1=1\,\mathrm{m/s}$, $v_2=4\,\mathrm{m/s}$. Its <momenta> add to $2+4=6\,\mathrm{kg\,m/s}$ and its <kinetic energies> add to $1+8=9\,\mathrm J$. The incoming and outgoing <relative velocities> both have magnitude $3\,\mathrm{m/s}$, consistent with <coefficient of restitution> $e=1$.

\Image[momentum-collision-before-after.png]
{title=The same incoming momentum gives different elastic and sticking collision outcomes}

The <inelastic collision> example below uses exactly these incoming data. Both outcomes preserve total <momentum>, while only the <elastic collision> preserves total <kinetic energy>. These are ideal numerical examples, not experimental measurements.

= Inelastic collision
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{wiki}

An <inelastic collision> changes total <kinetic energy>. In the dissipative case it decreases, transferring <energy> to <internal energy>, including <deformation> and microscopic motion. Isolated colliding bodies still obey <momentum conservation>; <conservation of energy> requires accounting for the transferred <energy>. Bodies need not stick in every <inelastic collision>. The decrease in translational <kinetic energy> drives <dissipative spreading of a planetary ring>.

For the incoming <masses> and <velocities> in the <elastic collision> example, stipulate that the bodies stick. This <perfectly inelastic collision> has a shared outgoing <velocity>
$$
v=\frac{m_1u_1+m_2u_2}{m_1+m_2}
=\frac{6\,\mathrm{kg\,m/s}}{3\,\mathrm{kg}}
=2\,\mathrm{m/s}.
$$
The outgoing <momenta> are $4$ and $2\,\mathrm{kg\,m/s}$, totaling $6\,\mathrm{kg\,m/s}$. The outgoing <kinetic energies> are $4$ and $2\,\mathrm J$, totaling $6\,\mathrm J$. Thus $9-6=3\,\mathrm J$ of <kinetic energy> becomes <internal energy> within the isolated pair. Nothing in <momentum conservation> requires this $3\,\mathrm J$ to remain as <kinetic energy>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-4-types-of-collisions[OpenStax University Physics Volume 1, section 9.4: elastic and perfectly inelastic outcomes]{ref}

= Perfectly inelastic collision
{parent=Inelastic collision}

A <perfectly inelastic collision> is an <inelastic collision> in which the bodies stick and share one outgoing <velocity>. For two isolated <point masses>, <momentum conservation> gives $v=(m_1u_1+m_2u_2)/(m_1+m_2)$. At fixed total <momentum>, common <velocity> minimizes the outgoing translational <kinetic energy>, so sticking gives the greatest loss of that <kinetic energy> for the specified incoming state.

= Oblique collision
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{wiki}

In a frictionless oblique collision, impulse acts along the common normal and tangential velocity components remain unchanged.

= Impulse
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{title2=$\mathbf J$}
{wiki}

The <impulse> of a <force> over a <time> interval is its <vector> <integral>:
$$
\mathbf J=\int_{t_i}^{t_f}\mathbf F(t)\,dt.
$$
Its <International System of Units> unit is $\mathrm{N\,s}=\mathrm{kg\,m/s}$. The <impulse-momentum theorem> equates the net <impulse> to the change in <momentum>. For one fixed-<mass> <point mass>, integrate its net <force>; for a closed collection obeying <Newton's third law>, integrate the sum of external <forces>. In one dimension, the signed area under the <force> versus <time> graph gives the <impulse>; negative <force> contributes negative area. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-2-impulse-and-collisions[OpenStax University Physics Volume 1, section 9.2: Impulse and Collisions]{ref}

Three stipulated rightward net <force> pulses illustrate the same $J=6\,\mathrm{N\,s}$: constant $12\,\mathrm N$ for $0.5\,\mathrm s$, a triangle rising from zero to $24\,\mathrm N$ at $0.25\,\mathrm s$ and falling to zero at $0.5\,\mathrm s$, and constant $3\,\mathrm N$ for $2\,\mathrm s$. Their areas are
$$
(12\,\mathrm N)(0.5\,\mathrm s)
=\frac12(24\,\mathrm N)(0.5\,\mathrm s)
=(3\,\mathrm N)(2\,\mathrm s)
=6\,\mathrm{N\,s}.
$$
Applied separately to the same fixed <mass> $m=2\,\mathrm{kg}$, each gives $\Delta v=J/m=3\,\mathrm{m/s}$. Their peak <forces> differ: $12$, $24$, and $3\,\mathrm N$. Thus <impulse> fixes the final <momentum> change, while the <force> history determines how abruptly it occurs.

\Image[impulse-force-pulses.png]
{title=Three force pulses have the same impulse but different durations and peak forces}

The mean net <force> over the chosen interval is $\overline{\mathbf F}=\mathbf J/(t_f-t_i)$. Extending the interval for a fixed <impulse> reduces this mean <force>. A lower peak <force> additionally depends on the pulse shape: the two short pulses above have the same mean $12\,\mathrm N$ but different peaks. This explains why controlling the duration and shape of a stopping <impulse> matters in cushioning a <collision>.

= Impulse-momentum theorem
{parent=Impulse}
{title2=$\mathbf J_{\mathrm{ext}}=\Delta\mathbf P$}

Integrating the closed-collection balance in <momentum conservation> and applying the <fundamental theorem of calculus> gives
$$
\mathbf J_{\mathrm{ext}}
=\int_{t_i}^{t_f}\sum_i\mathbf F_i^{\mathrm{ext}}(t)\,dt
=\mathbf P(t_f)-\mathbf P(t_i).
$$
For one fixed-<mass> <point mass>, this becomes $\mathbf J_{\mathrm{net}}=\Delta\mathbf p=m\Delta\mathbf v$. Zero external <impulse> implies equal initial and final total <momenta>; <momentum conservation> throughout the interval requires zero net external <force> throughout it.

= Freely expanding rotating particle ring
{parent=Particle dynamics}

Particles initially on a circle of radius $R$ with common tangential speed $v$ move freely on tangent lines. They remain on a circle of radius
$$
R(t)=\sqrt{R^2+v^2t^2},
$$
rotated through $\theta(t)=\arctan(vt/R)$, while total angular momentum remains $mRv$.

= Relative velocity
{parent=Particle dynamics}
{wiki}

The relative velocity of body 1 with respect to body 2 is $\mathbf v_{12}=\mathbf v_1-\mathbf v_2$.

= Celestial mechanics
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

Celestial mechanics studies motion under gravitational forces.

= Radial orbit time transformation
{parent=Celestial mechanics}
{title2=$d\tau/dt=(q/r)dq/dr$}

For a central-force radial <energy> equation, choose a monotone radius $q=q(r)$ and clock $\tau$ such that $d\tau/dt=(q/r)dq/dr$. Then $q'=(r/q)\dot r$, and multiplying the old <energy> equation by $r^2/q^2$ preserves the centrifugal form $L^2/(2q^2)$. A split of the remaining terms into constant <energy> and a new <Newtonian gravitational potential> produces a transformed radial orbit.

= Angular reconstruction of a radial orbit transformation
{parent=Radial orbit time transformation}
{title2=$d\bar\theta/d\tau=L/q^2$}

A transformed radial equation containing $L^2/(2q^2)$ becomes a full planar central-force orbit by choosing $d\bar\theta/d\tau=L/q^2$. For $d\tau/dt=(q/r)dq/dr$ and old <angular velocity> $L/r^2$, this gives $d\bar\theta/d\theta=(r/q)dq/dr$. Keeping the old angle generally fails to preserve the stated transformed <angular momentum>.

= Kepler–isochrone radial transformation
{c}
{parent=Radial orbit time transformation}
{title2=$r=\sqrt{\bar E/(\lambda E)}(\sqrt{\bar r^2+b^2}-b)$}

With $a=(1-\lambda)GM$, $h=\lambda E>0$ and $\bar E>0$, set $\bar E q^2=hr^2+ar$. If $a\geq0$, its positive-radius root is $r=\sqrt{\bar E/h}(\sqrt{q^2+b^2}-b)$, with $b=a/(2\sqrt{h\bar E})$. The transformed <Newtonian gravitational potential> is an additive constant plus $-G\bar M/(b+\sqrt{q^2+b^2})$, the <spherical isochrone model>. The <mass> sign and branch conditions must be checked.

= Attractive branch of the Kepler–isochrone transformation
{parent=Kepler–isochrone radial transformation}

For bound old <Kepler orbits>, $E<0$, $\lambda<0$ and $\bar E>0$ give a positive radius, positive isochrone scale $b$, and positive transformed <mass>. The shifted <energy> is $\bar E-k=\bar E/\lambda<0$. For unbound old orbits with $E>0$, $1/2<\lambda\leq1$ gives an attractive unbound isochrone branch. The case $\lambda=0$ is handled by the separate <Kepler–harmonic radial duality>.

= Kepler–harmonic radial duality
{c}
{parent=Radial orbit time transformation}
{title2=$r=(\bar E/GM)\bar r^2$}

Taking $\bar E=GM\,r/q^2>0$ in a <radial orbit time transformation> gives $r=(\bar E/GM)q^2$ and $\bar\Phi(q)=-E(\bar E/GM)^2q^2$. Bound <Kepler orbits> with $E<0$ map to a confining <harmonic oscillator>; positive <energy> maps to an inverted oscillator. Full angular reconstruction halves the original orbital angle.

= Two-body problem
{parent=Celestial mechanics}
{wiki}

The two-body problem determines the motion of two particles interacting only with one another. For a central force it separates into uniform centre-of-mass motion and one relative-coordinate problem with the reduced mass.

= Equal-mass circular binary
{parent=Two-body problem}
{title2=$\omega^2=Gm/(4a^3)$}

Two equal point <masses> can move around the same fixed circle of radius $a$ in diametrically opposite positions. Their stationary <centre of mass> is the circle's center. A separation $2a$ gives gravitational <force> $Gm^2/(4a^2)$ on each <mass>, and <Newton's second law> equates it to $ma\omega^2$. The common <angular speed> is therefore fixed by the displayed relation. Opposite tangential <velocities> produce the same sense of angular rotation.

= Negative-energy gravitational collision
{parent=Two-body problem}
{title2=$t_{\mathrm{coll}}=\pi\sqrt{a^3/[8G(m_1+m_2)]}$}

Two point <masses> released from rest at separation $a$ have negative <mechanical energy> $-Gm_1m_2/a$ but collide in time $\pi\sqrt{a^3/[8G(m_1+m_2)]}$. Their separation approaches zero along a regular trajectory before collision. The <energy> sign alone therefore does not supply a positive lower separation bound.

= Kepler orbit
{parent=Celestial mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Keplerian orbit
{c}
{synonym}

An inverse-square central force produces conic-section trajectories with the force centre at a focus.

= Kepler velocity hodograph
{c}
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{title2=$\boldsymbol v=(\mu_g/h)(-\sin f,\cos f+e)$}

In axes directed along the <periapsis> and transverse to it, a <Kepler orbit> with gravitational parameter $\mu_g$ and <specific angular momentum> $h$ has the displayed <velocity>. The <velocity> tip traces a circle of radius $\mu_g/h$ centred at $(0,e\mu_g/h)$. To derive it, differentiate $r=h^2/[\mu_g(1+e\cos f)]$, use $r^2\dot f=h$, and resolve $\dot r\widehat{\boldsymbol r}+r\dot f\widehat{\boldsymbol\theta}$. A rotation of the spatial axes rotates the entire hodograph by the same angle.

= Apsidal speed sum for an attractive inverse-square orbit
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{title2=$v_{\min}+v_{\max}=2Q/h$}

For unit mass with <inverse-square potential> $-Q/r$, $Q>0$, and positive conserved <angular momentum> $h$, a bounded orbit has $-Q^2/(2h^2)\le E<0$. Its <periapsis distance> and <apoapsis distance> are roots of $|E|r^2-Qr+h^2/2=0$, so their sum is $Q/|E|$ and product $h^2/(2|E|)$. Speed is largest at the <periapsis> and smallest at the <apoapsis>, because $v^2=2(E+Q/r)$ decreases with $r$. Radial velocity is zero there, so the two speeds are $h/r_{\min}$ and $h/r_{\max}$. Their sum is $h(r_{\min}+r_{\max})/(r_{\min}r_{\max})=2Q/h$, independent of $E$, including the circular limit.

= Velocity kick on a circular Kepler orbit
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{title2=$a'/a=(1-2\gamma\cos\theta-\gamma^2)^{-1}$}

For an instantaneous in-plane change of <velocity> of magnitude $\gamma v_K$ on a <circular orbit>, with angle $\theta$ measured from the forward tangential direction, the new <specific orbital energy> gives $a'/a=(1-2\gamma\cos\theta-\gamma^2)^{-1}$. The <specific angular momentum> gives $e'^2=1-(1-2\gamma\cos\theta-\gamma^2)(1+\gamma\cos\theta)^2$. The signed <semi-major axis> becomes negative for an unbound <hyperbolic Kepler orbit>.

= Kick-orbit distribution
{parent=Velocity kick on a circular Kepler orbit}
{title2=$f_c(c)=1/(\pi\sqrt{1-c^2})$}

Uniform planar kick angle $\theta$ gives $c=\cos\theta$ with probability density $1/(\pi\sqrt{1-c^2})$. This maps onto a one-dimensional curve in <orbital eccentricity>–<semi-major axis> space. The two radial-kick signs share that curve but have opposite tangential components of the <eccentricity vector>. The resulting density is enhanced near tangential-kick endpoints.

= Kepler radial energy equation
{c}
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{title2=$\frac12\dot r^2+\frac{L^2}{2r^2}=E+GM/r$}

For unit <mass> in $\Phi=-GM/r$, <conservation of angular momentum> splits the <velocity> norm into $\dot r^2+L^2/r^2$. <Conservation of energy> then gives $\tfrac12\dot r^2+L^2/(2r^2)=E+GM/r$. It separates radial dynamics from the reconstruction of the orbital angle.

= Two-body orbital energy
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{title2=$E=\mu\varepsilon$}

= Binary orbital energy
{synonym}

In the centre-of-mass frame of two Newtonian point masses, total orbital energy is reduced mass times <specific orbital energy>: $E=\mu[|\dot{\mathbf r}|^2/2-GM/r]$. The factor $\mu$ multiplies both kinetic and potential contributions, while $GM$ is the relative gravitational parameter.

= Polar equation of a Kepler orbit
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{title2=$r=p/(1+e\cos f)$}

For an attractive <inverse-square force>, $r=p/(1+e\cos f)$, where $f$ is <true anomaly>, $e$ is <orbital eccentricity>, and $p=h^2/\mu$ is the semilatus rectum. The <specific angular momentum> relation $\dot f=h/r^2$ converts this geometric equation into an angular evolution law.

= Circumplanetary orbit
{parent=Kepler orbit}

An <orbit> about a <planet>. Stellar tides, <radiation pressure> and other perturbations can limit its stability; a sufficiently small <semi-major axis> compared with the <Hill sphere> permits a local approximately planet-centred <Kepler orbit>.

= Planet-synchronous orbit
{parent=Circumplanetary orbit}
{title2=$T_{\rm orb}=P_{\rm spin}$}

= Synchronous circumplanetary orbit
{synonym}

A <circumplanetary orbit> whose <orbital period> equals the central planet's spin period. Its <semi-major axis> follows from <Kepler's third law>. Matching periods alone does not fix the satellite above one ground location; that additionally requires a <planet-stationary orbit>.

= Planet-stationary orbit
{parent=Planet-synchronous orbit}

= Stationary circumplanetary orbit
{synonym}

A prograde, equatorial <circular orbit> with <orbital period> equal to the planet's spin period. In the ideal model its satellite remains above one equatorial ground location. Nonzero <orbital inclination> or <orbital eccentricity> destroys exact stationarity even when the periods agree.

= Radiation pressure on circumplanetary dust
{parent=Circumplanetary orbit}

The leading uniform stellar <radiation pressure> is a differential <acceleration> relative to a <planet>, of scale $\beta GM_\star/a_p^2$. Relative to planetocentric gravity at dust radius $a$, its magnitude is
$$
\eta_{\rm rad}=\beta\frac{M_\star}{M_p}\left(\frac a{a_p}\right)^2.
$$
Thus lying inside a <Hill sphere> alone does not guarantee small radiative perturbations. Approximately circular tightly bound dust requires this ratio sufficiently small, as well as weak tides; the uniform radiative <force> can otherwise excite <orbital eccentricity> or unbind grains.

= Shadowing of circumplanetary dust
{parent=Radiation pressure on circumplanetary dust}

A <planet> can block stellar radiation during part of a <circumplanetary orbit>. Then the full-orbit constant-flux averages for <radiation pressure> and <Poynting–Robertson drag> require modification. The detailed shadow duty cycle depends on the <orbital inclination>, stellar angular extent and dust radius.

= Circumstellar orbit
{parent=Kepler orbit}

An <orbit> about a <star>. Dust on such an <orbit> can experience <radiation pressure> and <Poynting–Robertson drag>, in addition to gravitational perturbations.

= Binet equation
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{c}
{wiki}

Binet’s equation rewrites a central-force orbit using $u(\theta)=1/r$. If $\ell=mr^2\dot\theta$ is angular momentum and the potential is $V(r)$, then
$$
u''+u=\frac{m}{\ell^2u^2}\frac{dV}{dr}\bigg|_{r=1/u}.
$$

= Circular orbit through an inverse-fifth-power singularity
{parent=Binet equation}
{title2=$c=8mh^2R^2,\quad T=2\pi R^2/|h|$}

For an attractive <central force> of magnitude $c/r^5$, a circle of radius $R$ through the force center has polar equation $r=2R\cos\theta$. With <specific angular momentum> $h$, substitution into the <Binet equation> gives $c=8mh^2R^2$. Consequently $R$ is inversely proportional to the magnitude of the <angular momentum>, and the angular-time integral gives $T=2\pi R^2/|h|\propto R^3$. This is the finite travel time along the punctured circle from one approach to the singular center to the next; the force law alone does not specify continuation through the collision.

= Orbit equation for combined inverse-square and inverse-cube attraction
{parent=Binet equation}
{title2=$u''+(1-B/l^2)u=A/l^2$}

For radial acceleration $-A/r^2-B/r^3$, conserved <specific angular momentum> $l$ and $u=1/r$ reduce the <Binet equation> to the displayed linear equation. If $l^2>B$, its angular oscillation frequency is $\sqrt{1-B/l^2}$; attraction proportional to $r^{-3}$ changes the apsidal angle. A zero of $u$ ends the physical positive-radius branch and corresponds to escape, not passage to a negative radius. A spatial self-intersection need not be a periodic return because <velocity> may differ.

= Apsidal invariant on an unbound central-force branch
{parent=Orbit equation for combined inverse-square and inverse-cube attraction}
{title2=$\psi_{\rm peri}=\psi-\operatorname{Arg}(u-u_c-iu'/\nu)/\nu$}

For $u=u_c[1+e\cos\nu(\psi-\psi_0)]$ with $\nu>0$ and $e\geq1$, the positive-radius scattering branch has $|\nu(\psi-\psi_0)|<\arccos(-1/e)\leq\pi$. Thus the principal complex argument in the displayed expression is continuous throughout that branch, unlike at an apocentre of a bound orbit. Its derivative is $\nu$, so the angle modulo $2\pi$, or the associated unit apsidal vector in the orbital plane, is a stationary <integral of motion> fixing the periapsis direction. Together with energy and three components of <specific angular momentum>, it gives five regular scattering labels even for irrational $\nu$. Periodicity is therefore necessary for five orbit labels in recurrent bound motion, not in general for escaping trajectories.

= Closed orbits with an inverse-cube perturbation
{parent=Orbit equation for combined inverse-square and inverse-cube attraction}
{title2=$\nu=\sqrt{1-2\mu a/L^2}\in\mathbb Q_{>0}$}

In the <gravitational potential> $-\mu(1/r+a/r^2)$, a regular noncircular bound <orbit> requires $L^2>2\mu a$ and has $u=\mu(L^2-2\mu a)^{-1}[1+e\cos\nu(\psi-\psi_0)]$ with $0<e<1$. Each radial period advances the angle by $2\pi/\nu$. The full position and <velocity> return after finitely many radial periods precisely when $\nu$ is rational. Irrational $\nu$ gives a dense rosette on the two-frequency <invariant torus>. Rationality permits a fifth <orbit> label fixing the apsidal phase on that resonant <angular momentum> family, but does not give global superintegrability for all nearby angular momenta. When $a=0$, every regular bound <Kepler orbit> is closed and the <Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector> supplies the globally defined additional integral.

= Inverse-cube perturbation of Kepler apsidal motion
{parent=Orbit equation for combined inverse-square and inverse-cube attraction}
{title2=$\Delta\varpi\simeq-\pi C/[\mu a(1-e^2)]$}

For radial acceleration $-\mu/r^2+C/r^3$, the <Binet equation> gives $u''+(1+C/h^2)u=\mu/h^2$, where $h$ is <specific angular momentum>. A regular bound orbit has $h^2+C>0$, $a=-\mu/(2\mathcal E)$ and $e^2=1+2\mathcal E(h^2+C)/\mu^2$. Its radius is $a(1-e^2)/[1+e\cos(\alpha\theta)]$, with $\alpha=\sqrt{1+C/h^2}$. The <apsidal precession> per radial period is $2\pi(\alpha^{-1}-1)$, which reduces to the displayed expression for a small perturbation. Negative $C$ produces prograde precession; positive $C$ produces retrograde precession. The dimensionless coefficient is $C/(\mu a)$, since $C$ has units of length to the fourth power divided by time squared.

= Inferring an inverse-square force from Kepler laws
{parent=Binet equation}
{title2=$a_r=-l^2/(Ar^2)$}

Constant <areal velocity> gives constant specific <angular momentum> $l=r^2\dot\theta$, hence zero transverse acceleration and a <central force>. For a focus-based <ellipse> $u=1/r=(1+\varepsilon\cos\theta)/A$, one has $u_{\theta\theta}+u=1/A$. The <Binet equation> then gives the displayed radial acceleration, proving an attractive <inverse-square law>. An orbit's shape alone does not specify its acceleration without a time law.

= Damped Binet equation
{parent=Binet equation}
{title2=$u^{\prime\prime}+\frac{k}{h}u^{\prime}+u=\frac{p}{h^2}$}

An attractive <inverse-square force> together with radial drag $-k\dot r/r^2$ preserves <angular momentum> $h=r^2\dot\theta$ but modifies the <Binet equation> for $u=1/r$ by a first derivative. For $k/|h|<2$ and $h\ne0$, the reciprocal radius is a decaying oscillation about $p/h^2$. Forward angular distance $\operatorname{sgn}(h)(\theta-\theta_0)$ makes its damping sign explicit for either orbital orientation.

= Circularization under radial inverse-square drag
{parent=Damped Binet equation}
{title2=$r\longrightarrow h^2/p,\quad\dot E=-k\dot r^2/r^2$}

A bounded trajectory in the <damped Binet equation> with nonzero <angular momentum> asymptotically becomes circular. Energy loss is nonnegative in magnitude and vanishes only for zero radial velocity. The centrifugal effective <potential energy> excludes collision; bounded radius forces indefinitely increasing forward angular distance, so the reciprocal-radius oscillation decays. Escaping trajectories are excluded by the boundedness hypothesis.

= Shifted inverse-radius potential
{parent=Binet equation}

For
$$
V(r)=-\frac{km}{r-r_0},
\qquad r>r_0,
$$
Binet’s equation is
$$
u''+u=\frac{K}{(1-r_0u)^2},
\qquad K=\frac{km^2}{\ell^2}.
$$
Its circular orbits solve $K=u(1-r_0u)^2$; the branch $u<1/(3r_0)$ is stable and the branch $u>1/(3r_0)$ is unstable.

= Orbital eccentricity
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{wiki}

Orbital eccentricity distinguishes circular, elliptic, parabolic, and hyperbolic conics.

= Eccentricity vector
{title2=$\mathbf e$}
{parent=Orbital eccentricity}
{wiki}

For an attractive <Kepler orbit> with gravitational parameter $GM$, <velocity> $\mathbf v$ and <specific angular momentum> $\mathbf h=\mathbf r\times\mathbf v$, the eccentricity vector is
$$
\mathbf e=\frac{\mathbf v\times\mathbf h}{GM}-\widehat{\mathbf r}.
$$
Its magnitude is the <orbital eccentricity>, and it points toward <periapsis> for a noncircular orbit. It is a normalized attractive <Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector>; it remains constant under the pure inverse-square force.

= Perturbed Kepler-orbit conservation laws
{parent=Eccentricity vector}

If the relative acceleration is $\ddot{\mathbf r}=-GM\mathbf r/r^3+\mathbf f$, with constant masses, then the <specific orbital energy> $\varepsilon$, <specific angular momentum> $\mathbf h$ and <eccentricity vector> obey
$$
\dot\varepsilon=\mathbf v\mathbin\cdot\mathbf f,\qquad
\dot{\mathbf h}=\mathbf r\times\mathbf f,\qquad
GM\dot{\mathbf e}=\mathbf f\times\mathbf h+\mathbf v\times(\mathbf r\times\mathbf f).
$$
The inverse-square-force terms cancel in each derivative. These identities express perturbing work and torque, and track the instantaneous change of the ellipse's shape and orientation.

= Thermal eccentricity distribution
{title2=$n(e)=2e$}
{parent=Orbital eccentricity}

An isotropic population of bound Kepler orbits has eccentricity probability density $n(e)=2e$ on $0\leq e<1$. It follows from the fixed-energy angular-momentum measure $n(L)dL\propto LdL$ and $L=L_c\sqrt{1-e^2}$.

= Delaunay phase-volume proof of the thermal eccentricity distribution
{c}
{parent=Thermal eccentricity distribution}
{title2=$P(e'<e)=e^2$}

For bound <Kepler orbits>, the <Delaunay variables> obey $0<G<L$, $-G<H<G$, and three periodic angles. A <canonical transformation> preserves <phase space> volume. At fixed $L$, integrating $H$ supplies the measure $2G\,dG$. Since $G=L\sqrt{1-e^2}$, the fraction with <orbital eccentricity> less than $e$ is $L^{-2}\int_{L\sqrt{1-e^2}}^L2G\,dG=e^2$. Thus the <probability density function> is $2e$, independently of any normalizable energy-only <phase-space distribution function>. A <Boltzmann distribution> is unnecessary. Even the time-dependent density $F(E(L))[1+\eta\cos(l-n(L)t)]$, $|\eta|<1$, has this same marginal because integrating the <mean anomaly> removes its oscillatory part; an eccentricity marginal alone does not imply equilibrium.

= Finite normalization of an energy-only Kepler distribution
{parent=Delaunay phase-volume proof of the thermal eccentricity distribution}
{title2=$N=(2\pi)^3\int_0^\infty L^2F(-\mu^2/(2L^2))\,dL$}

For bound <Kepler orbits> with gravitational parameter $\mu$, integrating the <Delaunay variables> over $0<G<L$, $-G<H<G$ and their three full angle periods gives the displayed population. A globally defined <thermal eccentricity distribution> requires this integral to converge, or an explicit finite action range. Merely having finite $F(E)$ at every energy does not suffice: $F=1$ yields an infinite integral at large $L$. At each fixed energy, or on every finite normalized action band, the cumulative eccentricity fraction remains $e^2$.

= Elliptic orbit
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{wiki}

= Elliptic Kepler orbit
{synonym}

An elliptic orbit is a bound orbit shaped as an ellipse. A Kepler ellipse has negative <specific orbital energy> and eccentricity $0\leq e<1$.

= Hyperbolic Kepler orbit
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{wiki}

A positive-energy inverse-square orbit is a hyperbola with two scattering asymptotes.

= Gravitational scattering angle
{parent=Hyperbolic Kepler orbit}
{wiki}

The gravitational scattering angle is the change between incoming and outgoing asymptotic velocity directions.

= Non-colliding gravitational velocity-kick bound
{parent=Gravitational scattering angle}
{title2=$|\Delta\boldsymbol v|_{\max}$}

For a local two-body hyperbolic encounter with central parameter $\mu$ and allowed centre separation $r_{\min}\geq r_0$, <conservation of energy> gives equal asymptotic speeds. The <gravitational scattering angle> therefore implies
$$
|\Delta\boldsymbol v|=\frac{2v_\infty}{1+r_{\min}v_\infty^2/\mu}
\leq\sqrt{\frac{\mu}{r_0}}=\frac{v_{\rm esc}(r_0)}{\sqrt2}.
$$
Put $z=v_\infty\sqrt{r_{\min}/\mu}$ and use $2z/(1+z^2)\leq1$. The optimizing grazing <orbit> has $z=1$, <orbital eccentricity> two and deflection $\pi/3$. For finite spherical bodies $r_0$ is the sum of their radii; excluding actual contact makes the upper bound a supremum. Without a finite exclusion radius the ideal point-mass problem has no finite bound.

= Gravitational assist
{parent=Hyperbolic Kepler orbit}
{wiki}

In a gravitational assist, a small body's speed relative to a moving massive body is deflected with nearly unchanged magnitude. Transforming back to the inertial frame can transfer orbital energy between the bodies.

= Parabolic trajectory
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{wiki}

= Parabolic Kepler orbit
{synonym}

A zero-energy inverse-square orbit is parabolic and has eccentricity one.

= Barker equation
{parent=Parabolic trajectory}
{c}

For a <parabolic trajectory> with <periapsis> radius $r_p$, put $D=\tan(f/2)$, where $f$ is <true anomaly>. The Barker equation is $t-t_p=\sqrt{2r_p^3/(GM)}(D+D^3/3)$. Since $r=r_p(1+D^2)$, it is equivalent to the <radius-time relation for a parabolic Kepler orbit>. The sign of $D$ distinguishes approach from recession; $t_p$ is the <periapsis> time.

= Radius-time relation for a parabolic Kepler orbit
{parent=Barker equation}
{title2=$(r-r_p)(r+2r_p)^2=\tfrac92GM(t-t_p)^2$}

On a nonradial zero-<energy> <Kepler orbit>, conserved <specific angular momentum> $h$ gives periapsis $r_p=h^2/(2GM)$ and $\dot r^2=2GM(r-r_p)/r^2$. Integrating with $s=\sqrt{r-r_p}$ yields $t-t_p=\pm\sqrt{2/(GM)}(r+2r_p)\sqrt{r-r_p}/3$. Squaring gives the displayed version of the <Barker equation>, valid on both incoming and outgoing branches. The time origin $t_p$ is passage through <periapsis>, not an arbitrary point on the orbit.

= Pericentre-to-crossover flight time
{parent=Barker equation}
{title2=$n_pt_x\simeq2^{7/8}(q/a_p)^{3/8}/3$}

A <parabolic Kepler orbit> obeys $t=\sqrt{2q^3/(GM)}(D+D^3/3)$ with $D=\tan(f/2)$. At the <high-eccentricity angular-speed crossover>, $q\ll r_x$, so $n_pt_x\simeq(\sqrt2/3)(r_x/a_p)^{3/2}=2^{7/8}(q/a_p)^{3/8}/3$. The small time fraction explains why an eccentric orbit turns through almost $\pi$ of <true anomaly> while a distant perturber barely moves.

= Periapsis
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{wiki}

= Pericentre
{synonym}

= Periapses
{synonym}

The periapsis is the point of an orbit nearest its force centre.

= Pericentre distance
{parent=Periapsis}
{title2=$q$}

= Periapsis distance
{synonym}

= Apoapsis
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{wiki}

= Apocentre
{synonym}

The apoapsis is the point of an orbit farthest from its force centre.

= Apocentre distance
{parent=Apoapsis}
{title2=$Q$}

= Apoapsis distance
{synonym}

= Semi-major axis
{title2=$a$}
{parent=Kepler orbit}

= Semi-major axes
{synonym}

= Semimajor axis
{synonym}

The semi-major axis is one half of an ellipse's longest diameter. For a Kepler ellipse with apsidal distances $r_1,r_2$, it is $a=(r_1+r_2)/2$.

This is one of the <semi-major and semi-minor axes>; the two-axis geometry is the broader concept.

= Semi-minor axis
{title2=$b$}
{parent=Kepler orbit}

For an ellipse of eccentricity $e$, the semi-minor axis is $b=a\sqrt{1-e^2}$.

This is one of the <semi-major and semi-minor axes>; the two-axis geometry is the broader concept.

= Orbital element
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{wiki}

An orbital element is one of a set of parameters specifying an orbit at a chosen epoch. For a Kepler orbit, the semi-major axis and eccentricity determine its size and shape, while angular elements determine its orientation and the body's position.

= Argument of periapsis
{parent=Orbital element}
{title2=$\omega$}
{wiki}

= Argument of pericentre
{synonym}

= Argument of pericenter
{synonym}

The <argument of periapsis> is the angle in the <orbital plane> from the <ascending node> to <periapsis>, measured in the direction of motion. It differs from the <longitude of periapsis> $\varpi=\Omega+\omega$. For an inclined orbit this sum is a conventional longitude, not generally the azimuth of the projected periapsis. The argument is undefined for a circular orbit and the node-based description is singular when the orbit and reference plane coincide.

= True longitude
{parent=Orbital element}
{title2=$\ell_{\rm true}=\Omega+\omega+f$}

True longitude is the conventional orbital angle formed from the <longitude of ascending node>, argument of <periapsis> and <true anomaly>. For a coplanar <Kepler orbit> it is the actual polar longitude of the position vector. For an inclined <orbital plane> the sum of angles is generally not the projected azimuth. The <mean longitude> uses <mean anomaly> $M$ in place of $f$, so $\ell_{\rm true}-\lambda=f-M$. For small <orbital eccentricity>, <Kepler's equation> gives $E=M+e\sin M+O(e^2)$, while the eccentric-to-true anomaly relation gives $f=E+e\sin E+O(e^2)$; hence $f-M=2e\sin M+O(e^2)$. This periodic difference matters when separating a slow <resonant argument> from a rapidly varying geometric angle.

= Orbital plane
{parent=Orbital element}

The plane containing a central-force <Kepler orbit>, perpendicular to its conserved <specific angular momentum>. Its orientation can be specified by an <orbital inclination> and an <orbital node>. Perturbing <forces> can change that orientation.

= Osculating orbital element
{parent=Orbital element}

An <orbital element> of the <Kepler orbit> having the same instantaneous position and <velocity> as a perturbed trajectory. Its elements vary as the perturbing <force> acts. Their interpretation depends on the central <gravitational parameter> used, for example whether static <radiation pressure> is included in the unperturbed attraction.

= Astrocentric osculating-element oscillations
{parent=Osculating orbital element}
{title2=$\delta a/a=2\varepsilon\cos\Delta,\quad(e_r,e_\theta)=(2\varepsilon\cos\Delta,\varepsilon\sin\Delta)$}

A circular distant barycentric <Kepler orbit> around a circular star–planet binary need not have constant star-centred <osculating orbital elements>. Let $\mu$ be the planet's fraction of the binary <mass>, $\alpha=a_{\rm pl}/a\ll1$, and $\varepsilon=\mu/\sqrt\alpha\ll1$. Subtracting the stellar reflex <velocity> produces radial and tangential perturbations $(-v_0\varepsilon\sin\Delta,v_0\varepsilon\cos\Delta)$, where $\Delta$ is the planet's longitude minus the particle's longitude. The <vis-viva equation> gives the displayed <semi-major axis> variation. The <eccentricity vector> components follow from $e_r=rv_\theta^2/(GM_\star)-1$ and $e_\theta=-rv_rv_\theta/(GM_\star)$. The errors are $O(\mu+\varepsilon^2)$; the spatial translation itself is only $O(\mu\alpha)$. These are changes of instantaneous orbital description, even when the assumed barycentric trajectory is fixed.

= True anomaly
{title2=$f$}
{parent=Orbital element}
{wiki}

The true anomaly is the angle at the focus from periapsis to the current position of an orbiting body, measured in the direction of motion.

= Orbital phase
{parent=Orbital element}

Orbital phase specifies where a body lies within its orbital cycle relative to a chosen reference event.

= Phase-mixed orbit
{parent=Orbital phase}

A phase-mixed orbit has a probability distribution uniform in its canonical angle variable. For a Kepler ellipse, this means uniform mean anomaly and therefore probability proportional to time spent in each orbital segment.

= Line density on a Kepler orbit
{parent=Phase-mixed orbit}
{title2=$\lambda_\ell$}

= Line densities on a Kepler orbit
{synonym}

Uniform <mean anomaly> implies a line probability inversely proportional to the orbital speed. For small <orbital eccentricity> and <true anomaly> $f$, it is proportional to $1-e\cos f$.

= Cross-sectional-area current
{parent=Line density on a Kepler orbit}
{title2=$J_\sigma$}

The <geometric cross-section> passing a fixed point of a steady orbital wire per unit time is $J_\sigma=\lambda_\sigma v$, where $\lambda_\sigma=d\sigma/ds$. Conservation of fragments requires $J_\sigma$ constant and $\lambda_\sigma\propto1/v$. For total area $\sigma_{\rm tot}$ and <orbital period> $P$,
$$
J_\sigma=\frac{\sigma_{\rm tot}}P.
$$
With the <fractional luminosity of a phase-mixed eccentric wire>, this becomes $J_\sigma=2fh$. The current describes transported area, not the swept area enclosed by the <orbit>.

= Phase-mixed radial probability of a Kepler orbit
{parent=Phase-mixed orbit}
{title2=$p_r(r)$}

For a <Kepler orbit> with <pericentre distance> $q$, <apocentre distance> $Q$, and <semi-major axis> $a=(q+Q)/2$, a uniform <mean anomaly> gives
$$
p_r(r)=\frac{r}{\pi a\sqrt{(r-q)(Q-r)}},\qquad q<r<Q.
$$
The two radial passages per <orbital period> are included, and $\int_q^Qp_r(r)\,dr=1$.

= Mean anomaly
{title2=$M$}
{parent=Orbital element}
{wiki}

Mean anomaly is the uniformly increasing phase $M=n(t-\tau)$ of a Kepler orbit, measured from periapsis time $\tau$.

= Kepler's equation
{c}
{parent=Mean anomaly}
{wiki}

= Kepler equation
{c}
{synonym}

For an elliptic <Kepler orbit>, <Kepler's equation> relates <mean anomaly> $M$ and <eccentric anomaly> $E$ by
$$
M=E-e\sin E.
$$

= Eccentric anomaly
{title2=$E$}
{parent=Orbital element}
{wiki}

The eccentric anomaly parametrizes a Kepler ellipse through the corresponding point on its auxiliary circle. It gives radius $r=a(1-e\cos E)$.

= Orbital node
{parent=Orbital element}
{wiki}

An orbital node is an intersection of an orbital plane with its reference plane.

= Ascending node
{parent=Orbital node}

The ascending node is the orbital node where a body crosses from the negative to the positive side of the reference plane.

= Longitude of ascending node
{title2=$\Omega$}
{parent=Ascending node}
{wiki}

The longitude of ascending node is the reference-plane angle from a fixed direction to the ascending node.

= Orbital inclination
{title2=$I$}
{parent=Orbital element}
{wiki}

Orbital inclination is the angle between an orbital plane and a reference plane.

= Orbital-frame rotation from inclination and node
{parent=Orbital inclination}
{title2=$R_z(\Omega)R_x(I)$}

In a right-handed reference frame, the <rotation matrix> from an <orbital plane> frame whose first axis points along the <ascending node> is $R_z(\Omega)R_x(I)$. Its columns are the orbital frame's <orthonormal basis> expressed in reference coordinates. The <unit vector> normal is $(\sin\Omega\sin I,-\cos\Omega\sin I,\cos I)$; its <dot product> with another such normal gives the cosine of their <mutual inclination>.

= Mutual inclination
{title2=$I_m$}
{parent=Orbital inclination}

The mutual inclination is the angle between two orbital planes, equivalently the angle between consistently oriented orbital normal vectors.

= Mutual inclination of nearly edge-on orbits
{parent=Mutual inclination}
{title2=$I_m^2\simeq(\Delta I)^2+(\Delta\Omega)^2$}

For two nearly <edge-on orbits> with small <orbital inclination> difference $\Delta I$ and small <longitude of ascending node> difference $\Delta\Omega$, expanding the <dot product> of their normals gives $I_m^2=(\Delta I)^2+(\Delta\Omega)^2+O(\delta^4)$, with angles in radians and all offsets from edge-on of order $\delta$. Thus equality of observed sky-plane inclinations alone does not imply aligned <orbital planes>.

= Invariable plane
{parent=Orbital inclination}
{wiki}

The invariable plane is perpendicular to a system's total <angular momentum>. For an isolated planetary system, it is fixed while individual orbital planes precess.

= Inclination to the invariable plane of two orbital planes
{parent=Invariable plane}

For two groups carrying <angular momenta> $L_a,L_b$ with <mutual inclination> $\Delta I$, resolving total <angular momentum> parallel and perpendicular to the second group's normal gives its <orbital inclination> relative to the <invariable plane>:
$$
I_b=\operatorname{atan2}(L_a\sin\Delta I,L_b+L_a\cos\Delta I).
$$
For a positive denominator, $\tan I_b=\sin\Delta I/(L_b/L_a+\cos\Delta I)$. The larger-angular-momentum group lies closer to the <invariable plane>.

= Edge-on orbit
{parent=Orbital inclination}

An orbit is edge-on to an observer when its orbital plane contains the line of sight, so its inclination to the plane of the sky is $90^\circ$.

= Orbital-plane orientation degeneracy
{parent=Orbital inclination}

Projected observations that do not identify which node is ascending may determine an orbital plane only up to reversal of its normal or addition of $\pi$ to its nodal longitude.

= Longitude of periapsis
{title2=$\varpi$}
{parent=Orbital element}
{wiki}

= Longitudes of periapsis
{synonym}

= Longitude of pericentre
{synonym}

The longitude of periapsis gives the angular direction of periapsis in the reference plane.

= Mean longitude
{title2=$\lambda$}
{parent=Orbital element}
{wiki}

Mean longitude is an angle that advances uniformly at the mean motion in an unperturbed Kepler orbit.

= Mean motion
{title2=$n$}
{parent=Orbital element}
{wiki}

Mean motion is the average orbital angular frequency. A Kepler orbit about gravitational parameter $\mu$ has $n^2a^3=\mu$.

= Orbital period
{title2=$T$}
{parent=Mean motion}
{wiki}

The orbital period is the time required to complete one orbit. It is related to <mean motion> by $T=2\pi/n$.

= Synodic period
{title2=$T_{\rm syn}$}
{parent=Mean motion}
{wiki}

The synodic period is the interval between successive repetitions of the relative orbital configuration of two bodies. For coplanar prograde mean motions $n_1,n_2$, it is $2\pi/|n_1-n_2|$.

= Specific orbital energy
{title2=$\varepsilon$}
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{wiki}

The specific orbital energy is the mechanical energy per unit mass,
$$
\varepsilon=\frac{v^2}{2}-\frac{\mu}{r}=-\frac{\mu}{2a}.
$$
Its sign distinguishes bound elliptic, marginal parabolic, and unbound hyperbolic Kepler orbits.

= Orbit-averaged drag work
{parent=Specific orbital energy}

A weak perturbing relative acceleration $\mathbf f$ changes <specific orbital energy> at rate $\dot\varepsilon=\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf f$. Since $\varepsilon=-\mu/(2a)$ for a <Kepler orbit>,
$$
\dot a=\frac{2a^2}{\mu}\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf f.
$$
For a circular <Kepler orbit> under $\mathbf f=-\kappa\mathbf v+\mathbf g$ with constant $\mathbf g$, the constant-force term averages to zero over an unperturbed orbit. Thus $\langle\dot a\rangle=-2\kappa a$. This is a leading-order orbital average; a strong constant force can distort the orbit instead.

= Vis-viva equation
{parent=Specific orbital energy}
{wiki}

For a <Kepler orbit> with <semi-major axis> $a$, the <vis-viva equation> is
$$
v^2=\mu\left(\frac2r-\frac1a\right).
$$
For the convention that a <hyperbolic Kepler orbit> has positive semi-major-axis magnitude $a$, its final term instead has a plus sign.

= Specific angular momentum
{title2=$h$}
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{wiki}

The specific angular momentum is angular momentum per unit mass, $\mathbf h=\mathbf r\times\mathbf v$. For a planar Kepler orbit its magnitude is $h=r^2\dot\theta=\sqrt{\mu a(1-e^2)}$.

= High-eccentricity angular-speed crossover
{parent=Specific angular momentum}
{title2=$r_x=\sqrt{h/n_p}$}

For a nearly radial <Kepler orbit> with <pericentre> $q$, <specific angular momentum> is $h\simeq\sqrt{2GMq}$. Relative to a circular reference orbit of radius $a_p$ and <mean motion> $n_p$, the instantaneous azimuthal rate changes from faster to slower at $r_x=\sqrt{h/n_p}\simeq a_p(2q/a_p)^{1/4}$. In a <rotating reference frame> the polar angle has a turning point at this radius. The <true anomaly> there differs from $\pi$ by approximately $2\sqrt{q/r_x}$.

= Periapsis angular speed
{title2=$\dot\theta_{\rm peri}$}
{parent=Specific angular momentum}

The <specific angular momentum> of an elliptic <Kepler orbit> is $h=\sqrt{\mu a(1-e^2)}$. At <periapsis>, $r=a(1-e)$, so
$$
\dot\theta_{\rm peri}=\frac h{r^2}=n\frac{\sqrt{1+e}}{(1-e)^{3/2}}.
$$
The <mean motion> understates instantaneous angular speed near <periapsis>. A particle in an <exterior mean-motion resonance> can briefly overtake the planet in angle despite completing full orbits more slowly.

= Circular Kepler orbit
{parent=Kepler orbit}

A circular Kepler orbit at radius $r$ has speed $v_K=\sqrt{\mu/r}$ and mean motion $n=\sqrt{\mu/r^3}$.

= Synchronous circular orbit
{parent=Circular Kepler orbit}
{title2=$r=(GMT^2/4\pi^2)^{1/3}$}

A satellite remaining above a fixed point of a uniformly rotating spherical planet must have a prograde equatorial <circular Kepler orbit> with angular speed $2\pi/T$. Balancing gravitational acceleration and centripetal acceleration gives the displayed radius and speed $v=2\pi r/T$. The satellite's own mass cancels. A physically realizable external orbit additionally requires this radius to exceed the planet's radius.

= Hohmann transfer orbit
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{c}
{wiki}

= Hohmann transfer
{c}
{synonym}

A Hohmann transfer uses two tangential impulses and one half of an elliptic Kepler orbit to move between two coplanar circular orbits.

= Restricted three-body problem
{parent=Celestial mechanics}
{wiki}

The restricted three-body problem follows a negligibly massive particle in the gravitational field of two massive bodies whose motion is unaffected by the particle.

= Circular restricted three-body problem
{parent=Restricted three-body problem}
{wiki}

In the circular restricted three-body problem the two massive bodies move on circular orbits about their barycentre. The problem is autonomous in their uniformly rotating frame.

= Hill equations
{c}
{parent=Circular restricted three-body problem}

The unforced planar <equation of motion in a rotating frame> near a circular <Kepler orbit> has components $\ddot x-2n\dot y-3n^2x=0$ and $\ddot y+2n\dot x=0$. They describe <epicyclic motion> and <Keplerian shear>; mutual gravity adds close-encounter forcing.

= Free solution of Hill equations
{parent=Hill equations}

= Orbital elements from Hill coordinates
{parent=Free solution of Hill equations}

The oscillatory integration constants determine the <orbital eccentricity> vector, the constant radial offset determines the <semi-major axis>, and the constant along-track offset fixes the <mean longitude>. The radial and tangential epicycle amplitudes have ratio $1:2$.

= Photogravitational restricted three-body problem
{parent=Circular restricted three-body problem}

The photogravitational restricted three-body problem adds radial <radiation pressure> from one or both luminous primaries. If the pressure-to-gravity ratio of primary $i$ is $\beta_i$, its effective attraction on the test particle is multiplied by $1-\beta_i$.

= Lagrange point
{c}
{parent=Circular restricted three-body problem}
{wiki}

A Lagrange point is an equilibrium of the <circular restricted three-body problem> in the frame rotating with the two massive bodies.

= Effective-potential geometry of Lagrange points
{parent=Lagrange point}

The <rotating-frame effective potential> of the <circular restricted three-body problem> has negative singular wells at both primaries. Its three <collinear Lagrange points> are planar saddles, and its two <triangular Lagrange points> are planar local maxima for nonzero primary masses. These geometric labels do not replace a dynamical stability analysis including the <Coriolis force>.

= Collinear Lagrange point
{c}
{parent=Lagrange point}

The three collinear Lagrange points $L_1,L_2,L_3$ lie on the line through the two massive bodies.

= Small-mass-ratio collinear Lagrange points
{parent=Collinear Lagrange point}
{title2=$x_{L_1,L_2}=1\mp(\alpha/3)^{1/3}+O(\alpha^{2/3})$}

In barycentric coordinates with unit primary separation, a small secondary mass fraction $\alpha$ gives distances $(\alpha/3)^{1/3}$ from that secondary to the two neighboring <Lagrange points> at leading order. This is the dimensionless <Hill radius>. The opposite exterior <L3 Lagrange point> has coordinate $-1-5\alpha/12+O(\alpha^2)$.

= Inner Lagrange point
{parent=Collinear Lagrange point}
{title2=$L_1$}

= L1 point
{c}
{synonym}

The <Lagrange point> lying between the components of a <circular restricted three-body problem>. The critical effective potential through this saddle defines their <Roche lobes>; gas passing through it can initiate <Roche-lobe overflow>.

= L2 Lagrange point
{parent=Collinear Lagrange point}
{title2=$L_2$}
{c}

The collinear <Lagrange point> beyond the smaller primary, away from the larger one, is $L_2$. For a small mass ratio it lies approximately one <Hill radius> from the secondary and has a growing linear mode.

= Linearization at L2
{parent=L2 Lagrange point}
{title2=$B=\mu_1/r_1^3+\mu_2/r_2^3$}

In unit binary separation and mean motion, displacements obey $\ddot X-2\dot Y=(1+2B)X$ and $\ddot Y+2\dot X=(1-B)Y$, where $B=\mu_1/r_1^3+\mu_2/r_2^3$ at $L_2$. The characteristic polynomial is $\lambda^4+(2-B)\lambda^2+(1+2B)(1-B)$. The small-secondary limit has $B\to4$.

= L2 escape e-folding time
{parent=Linearization at L2}
{title2=$\tau=[n_{\rm bin}\sqrt{1+2\sqrt7}]^{-1}$}
{c}

For a small secondary-to-primary mass ratio, the unstable exponent at the outer collinear equilibrium is $n_{\rm bin}\sqrt{1+2\sqrt7}$. Its inverse is approximately $0.0634$ binary periods. Reaching a finite target distance takes this time multiplied by the logarithm of the ratio of final to initial unstable amplitudes.

= L3 Lagrange point
{title2=$L_3$}
{c}
{parent=Collinear Lagrange point}

The $L_3$ Lagrange point lies beyond the more massive primary, on the side opposite the secondary.

= Triangular Lagrange point
{c}
{parent=Lagrange point}

The triangular Lagrange points $L_4,L_5$ complete equilateral triangles with the two massive bodies.

= Hill sphere
{c}
{parent=Circular restricted three-body problem}
{wiki}

The Hill sphere is the neighborhood of a smaller body in which its gravity can dominate tidal perturbations from a larger primary.

= Hill radius
{title2=$r_H$}
{c}
{parent=Hill sphere}

For a body of mass $m$ on a circular orbit of radius $a$ about mass $M\gg m$, the Hill radius is approximately
$$
r_H=a\left(\frac{m}{3M}\right)^{1/3}.
$$

= Jacobi constant
{c}
{parent=Circular restricted three-body problem}
{wiki}

The Jacobi constant is the conserved energy-like integral of the circular restricted three-body problem in the rotating frame.

= Orbital resonance
{parent=Celestial mechanics}
{wiki}

An orbital resonance occurs when orbital frequencies satisfy an approximately integer relation, allowing perturbations to accumulate coherently.

= Mean-motion resonance
{parent=Orbital resonance}
{wiki}

A mean-motion resonance has $jn_2-kn_1\simeq0$ for small integers $j,k$. Its slow resonant angle combines mean longitudes with apsidal or nodal longitudes.

= Interior eccentricity-type mean-motion resonance
{parent=Mean-motion resonance}

For an eccentric inner body and circular outer perturber near the reduced $(m+n):m$ period ratio, the leading <resonant argument> is $\phi=(m+n)\lambda_{\rm out}-m\lambda_{\rm in}-n\varpi_{\rm in}$. Its <disturbing function> amplitude is of order $e_{\rm in}^n$. At a mean <conjunction>, $\phi=n(\Lambda-\varpi_{\rm in})$; at actual alignment the exact expression is $(m+n)x-my$ with $x=f_{\rm in}$ and $y=M_{\rm in}(x)$.

= Inclination-type mean-motion resonance
{parent=Mean-motion resonance}

= Inclination resonance
{synonym}

An inclination-type mean-motion resonance has a leading <resonant argument> containing a <longitude of ascending node>. Reflection symmetry forces the total nodal coefficient in each disturbing-function harmonic to be even, so the leading resonant strength is an even power of $\sin(I/2)$.

= Exterior mean-motion resonance
{parent=Mean-motion resonance}

In an exterior mean-motion resonance, the resonant small body orbits outside the perturbing planet and therefore has lower mean motion. A $(p+q):p$ exterior resonance has $n/n_p\simeq p/(p+q)$.

= Resonant semi-major axis
{parent=Exterior mean-motion resonance}
{title2=$a_r=a_p((p+q)/p)^{2/3}$}

The nominal <semi-major axis> of an exterior $(p+q):p$ <mean-motion resonance> follows from $(p+q)n_r=pn_p$ and <Kepler's third law>. Apsidal precession displaces the actual libration center because stationarity of the <resonant argument> requires $(p+q)n-pn_p-q\dot\varpi=0$.

= Radiation-pressure shift of a mean-motion resonance
{parent=Exterior mean-motion resonance}
{title2=$a/a_p=(1-\beta)^{1/3}((j+1)/j)^{2/3}$}

A grain with <radiation-pressure coefficient> $\beta<1$ feels effective central <gravitational parameter> $GM_\star(1-\beta)$. Combining its <mean motion> with the planet's <mean motion> at an exterior-type $(j+1):j$ frequency commensurability gives the displayed <semi-major axis>. The commensurability lies geometrically exterior only for $j<\sqrt{1-\beta}/(1-\sqrt{1-\beta})$. Radiation changes the grain's attraction, so the same frequency ratio need not imply the usual radial ordering.

= First-order mean-motion resonance
{parent=Mean-motion resonance}

A first-order mean-motion resonance has $|j-k|=1$. For small eccentricity its leading eccentric resonant term is linear in eccentricity.

= Resonance-overlap encounter estimate
{parent=First-order mean-motion resonance}
{title2=$j_c\sim\mu^{-2/7}$}

A local weak <gravitational assist> estimate for a nonradiating circular grain gives $\delta\epsilon\simeq32\mu^2\epsilon^{-5}$, where $\mu=M_p/M_\star$. <Conjunction-longitude sensitivity to an encounter> then gives $|\delta\Lambda|\simeq128\pi\mu^2/(3\epsilon^7)$. Requiring the change to exceed one full turn and using $\epsilon\simeq2/(3j)$ gives $j_c\simeq(2/729)^{1/7}\mu^{-2/7}$. This motivates loss of coherent resonant conjunctions, rather than proving that every initial phase escapes. The coefficient depends on the encounter convention and the approximations; near-planet <Hill equations> require a consistent rotating-frame shear instead of mixing local inertial and rotating velocities.

= Resonant argument
{parent=Mean-motion resonance}

= Resonant angle
{synonym}

A resonant argument is a slow angular combination whose libration diagnoses resonance. For an exterior $j:(j-1)$ eccentric resonance it can be $\phi=j\lambda-(j-1)\lambda_p-\varpi$.

A resonant argument is an integer combination of orbital angles whose slow variation can diagnose <mean-motion resonance>. Its libration, rather than circulation through every angle, characterizes trapping in the resonance.

= Resonant conjunction geometry
{parent=Resonant argument}

For an eccentric outer body and circular inner perturber, the exterior <resonant argument> is $\phi=(p+q)\lambda_{\rm out}-p\lambda_{\rm in}-q\varpi$. At equal <mean longitudes> $\Lambda$, <conjunction> phases are
$$
\Lambda_j=\varpi+\frac{\phi+2\pi j}{q},\qquad j=0,\ldots,q-1.
$$
The <synodic period> and coprime $p,q$ determine the visitation order, with advance $2\pi p/q$. For actual true alignment, write $x=f$ and $y=M(f)$ for the eccentric body's <true anomaly> and <mean anomaly>. Then $\phi=(p+q)y-px$ instead of $qx$. The evenly spaced directions are a leading-small-eccentricity approximation.

= Phase protection of interior integer resonances
{parent=Resonant conjunction geometry}
{title2=$\phi=k\lambda_p-\lambda-(k-1)\varpi$}

In a high-<orbital eccentricity> interior $k:1$ <mean-motion resonance>, the <resonant argument> $\phi=k\lambda_p-\lambda-(k-1)\varpi$ places successive rotating-frame <apocentres> at $\pi-[\phi+(2j+1)\pi]/k$. Avoiding <astronomical conjunction> at these slow outer excursions favors $\phi=0$ for $k=2$ and $\phi=\pi$ for $k=3$. This geometrical phase protection suggests stable <resonant-argument libration> centres; actual dynamical stability requires the resonant perturbation problem.

= Conjunction-pattern precession near a mean-motion resonance
{parent=Resonant conjunction geometry}
{title2=$\dot\Lambda$}

With constant <mean motions> and $P_c/P_b=(1+\delta)(p+q)/p$, mean <conjunctions> advance by $\Delta\Lambda=2\pi/(P_c/P_b-1)$ each <synodic period>. Tracking one branch every $q$ <conjunctions> and subtracting $2\pi p$ gives
$$
\dot\Lambda=\frac{q\Delta\Lambda-2\pi p}{qP_{\rm syn}}
=-\frac{2\pi p\delta}{qP_b(1+\delta)}.
$$
The usual formula $-2\pi p\delta/(qP_b)$ keeps only first order in $\delta$. A labelled branch takes $qP_b(1+\delta)/(p|\delta|)$ to turn once, whereas the full set of $q$ interchangeable directions repeats after $P_b(1+\delta)/(p|\delta|)$. Finite <orbital eccentricity> introduces true-conjunction corrections.

= Conjunction-longitude sensitivity to an encounter
{parent=Conjunction-pattern precession near a mean-motion resonance}
{title2=$\delta\Lambda=\Lambda'(\epsilon)\delta\epsilon$}

For an exterior circular grain with <radiation-pressure coefficient> $\beta$, set $q=\sqrt{1-\beta}$ and $\epsilon=a/a_p-1$. Its next <conjunction> occurs after the <synodic period>, during which the planet's unwrapped <mean longitude> advances by $\Lambda=2\pi/[1-q(1+\epsilon)^{-3/2}]$. A weak <planetary scattering> encounter changes this by $\delta\Lambda\simeq-3\pi q(1+\epsilon)^{-5/2}\delta\epsilon/[1-q(1+\epsilon)^{-3/2}]^2$. Unwrapped longitude counts turns; reducing it modulo $2\pi$ before comparison would erase this measure of timing sensitivity.

= Resonant-argument libration
{parent=Resonant argument}

Resonant-argument libration is bounded oscillation of a resonant argument about a stable equilibrium value instead of circulation through all angles.

= Pendulum approximation of a mean-motion resonance
{parent=Resonant-argument libration}
{title2=$\ddot\phi=K\sin\phi$}

= Resonant pendulum approximation
{synonym}

Keeping one resonant harmonic and treating <orbital eccentricity> as fixed reduces <mean-motion resonance> dynamics to a <physical pendulum> equation. For the inner first-order term $\mathcal R=(GM_2/a_2)f(\alpha)e\cos\phi$, leading <Lagrange planetary equations> give $\ddot\phi\simeq3j^2\mu_2n^2\alpha f e\sin\phi$. Around its stable phase, the small-amplitude libration frequency is $jn\sqrt{3\mu_2\alpha|f|e}$. Evolution of eccentricity, other harmonics, and approach to a <separatrix> change this approximation.

= Resonant pendulum energy
{parent=Pendulum approximation of a mean-motion resonance}
{title2=$E=\dot\phi^2/2+\omega_0^2(1+\cos\phi)$}

For $\ddot\phi=\omega_0^2\sin\phi$ with constant $\omega_0$, multiplication by $\dot\phi$ gives the conserved energy $E=\dot\phi^2/2+\omega_0^2(1+\cos\phi)$. The stable center is $\pi$; the <separatrix> has $E=2\omega_0^2$. Below it the <resonant argument> librates and above it the angle circulates. This is an invariant of the constant-coefficient <pendulum approximation of a mean-motion resonance>, not of arbitrary coupled <orbital element> evolution.

= Asymmetric resonant-argument libration
{parent=Resonant-argument libration}

Libration about a resonant phase distinct from the symmetric values zero and $\pi$. For example, in a negative-Kepler-curvature reduction with disturbing potential $R=A\cos\phi+B\cos2\phi$, $A>0$, $B>A/4$, stationary points obey $\sin\phi(A+4B\cos\phi)=0$. At $\pi$, $R''=A-4B<0$, so it is unstable, whereas at $\cos\phi=-A/(4B)$ one has $R''=4B-A^2/(4B)>0$, giving two stable asymmetric centres. Actual branch thresholds depend on the resonance coefficients and evolving <orbital elements>.

= Symmetric libration centres of an eccentricity resonance
{parent=Resonant-argument libration}

In the weak, non-crossing, leading-eccentricity planar model, exterior orders one to three have positive resonant <disturbing function> coefficients $B_q$. Interior eccentricity-type coefficients of these orders have sign $(-1)^q$. A local resonant <Hamiltonian> $H=-AJ^2/2-Be^q\cos\phi$, with $A>0$, gives $\ddot\phi=ABe^q\sin\phi$. <Linear stability analysis> therefore selects $\phi=\pi$ for $B>0$ and $\phi=0$ for $B<0$. Exterior symmetric centres are $\pi$; interior centres are $0,\pi,0$ for orders one, two and three. These are conditional leading-harmonic predictions, not universal centres at arbitrary <orbital eccentricity>; <asymmetric resonant-argument libration> changes the picture.

= Low-eccentricity correction to a first-order resonance center
{parent=Symmetric libration centres of an eccentricity resonance}
{title2=$3(p+1)^2ne^3-C_r$}

In the planar exterior first-order constant-coefficient model, differentiating the apsidal-precession term retains a contribution proportional to $C_r^2/e^2$. At the zero-phase fixed point its linear angular curvature is $3(p+1)^2nC_re-C_r^2/e^2$. Hence zero is a saddle for $3(p+1)^2ne^3>C_r$, but is a center when this inequality reverses. The usual constant-eccentricity <pendulum approximation of a mean-motion resonance> omits this term and requires $C_r/(ne^3)\ll1$.

= Libration amplitude of a resonant argument
{title2=$\Delta\phi$}
{parent=Resonant-argument libration}

= Libration amplitudes of a resonant argument
{synonym}

The libration amplitude is the maximum angular displacement of a librating resonant argument from its equilibrium value.

= Resonance protection
{parent=Resonant-argument libration}

Resonance protection confines conjunctions to orbital phases that avoid close encounters even when the osculating orbits geometrically cross.

= Resonance capture
{parent=Mean-motion resonance}
{wiki}

Resonance capture occurs when slow orbital migration carries a body into a resonance and the resonant argument begins to librate, so resonant torque can balance the migration torque.

= Rigid body dynamics
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

= Rigid body
{synonym}

Rigid body dynamics studies translation and rotation of bodies whose internal distances are fixed.

= Two hinged rods with both ends sliding on a floor
{parent=Rigid body dynamics}
{title2=$v_{\rm hinge}=\sqrt{6gl\sin\alpha}$}

Two identical uniform <rigid bodies> of rod shape, each of length $2l$ and mass $M$, form a symmetric arch with a free common hinge and both outer ends sliding without friction on a horizontal floor. At angle $\theta$, each center has <speed> $l|\dot\theta|$, giving total <kinetic energy> $4Ml^2\dot\theta^2/3$. The total gravitational <potential energy> is $2Mgl\sin\theta$. <Conservation of energy> from rest at $\alpha$ gives the displayed hinge impact <speed>. Unlike fixing one outer endpoint, allowing both endpoints to slide keeps the arch center horizontally fixed. The floor reaction on each rod is $Mg[1+9\sin^2\theta-6\sin\alpha\sin\theta]/4$, nonnegative throughout this fall.

= Endpoint collision of two freely rotating equal rods
{parent=Rigid body dynamics}
{title2=$J=\frac{mV(1+e)}2,\quad |\omega|=\frac{V(1+e)}{4\ell}$}

Each massless rod has endpoint masses $m$ at distance $\ell$ from its centre, hence total mass $2m$ and <moment of inertia> $2m\ell^2$. In a symmetric opposite-end collision with centres approaching at speed $V$, a horizontal <impulse> $J$ changes each centre speed by $J/(2m)$ and each contact-point speed by $J/m$. The <coefficient of restitution> gives $2J/m-2V=2eV$. Both rods rotate in the same sense for the staggered vertical configuration; their centre speeds after impact are $V(3-e)/4$.

// Target: electromagnetism.bigb

= Two hinged rods sliding on a floor
{parent=Rigid body dynamics}
{title2=$\dot\theta^2=\frac{3g}{2l}\frac{\sin\alpha-\sin\theta}{1+3\sin^2\theta}$}

Two equal uniform rods of length $2l$ and mass $M$, one pivoted to the floor and the other endpoint sliding on it, have arch coordinates $B=(2l\cos\theta,2l\sin\theta)$ and $C=(4l\cos\theta,0)$. The <kinetic energy decomposition about the center of mass> gives $T=\tfrac43Ml^2(1+3\sin^2\theta)\dot\theta^2$, while gravitational <potential energy> is $2Mgl\sin\theta$. <Conservation of energy> from rest at angle $\alpha$ gives the displayed equation and hinge impact <speed> $\sqrt{6gl\sin\alpha}$, provided contact is maintained. The endpoint's normal reaction becomes negative during this constrained fall if $\sin\alpha>35/54$; a unilateral floor then permits detachment and the constrained formula no longer describes the entire motion.

= Solid-body rotation
{parent=Rigid body dynamics}
{title2=$\Omega=\mathrm{constant}$}

In solid-body rotation the <angular velocity> is independent of distance from the rotation axis, so tangential speed is proportional to cylindrical radius. A galactic region with this <galaxy rotation curve> has <radial epicyclic frequency> $\kappa=2\Omega$.

= Symmetric top
{parent=Rigid body dynamics}
{wiki}

A symmetric top is a <rigid body> with two equal principal moments of inertia, conventionally $I_1=I_2$. Its third principal axis is its symmetry axis.

= Heavy symmetric top
{parent=Symmetric top}
{wiki}

A heavy symmetric top is a <symmetric top> with one point fixed in a uniform gravitational field. Its vertical and body-axis rotational symmetries give two cyclic Euler angles.

= Nutation
{parent=Heavy symmetric top}
{title2=$\theta(t)$}
{wiki}

Variation in the inclination of a rotating body's axis, distinguished from its azimuthal <precession>. For a <heavy symmetric top>, the cyclic angular momenta reduce the motion to $\dot\mu^2=F(\mu)$ with $\mu=\cos\theta$ and $F$ cubic. Its admissible interval and turning points describe the nutation.

= Uniform precession of a heavy symmetric top
{parent=Heavy symmetric top}
{title2=$I_1\cos\theta\,\Omega^2-I_3\omega_3\Omega+Mgl=0$}

A <heavy symmetric top> precesses uniformly when its inclination is constant and its azimuthal speed is constant. For a nonvertical inclination, the condition is $I_1\cos\theta\,\Omega^2-I_3\omega_3\Omega+Mgl=0$. The real-root condition depends on whether the body axis points above or below the horizontal.

= Precession
{parent=Rigid body dynamics}
{wiki}

Precession is the rotation of a body's spin axis about another direction, such as the vertical or the conserved angular-momentum vector.

= Angular velocity
{title2=$\omega$}
{parent=Rigid body dynamics}
{wiki}

Angular velocity is the vector whose direction gives the instantaneous rotation axis and whose magnitude gives the angular speed.

= Angular acceleration
{parent=Angular velocity}
{title2=$\boldsymbol\alpha=d\boldsymbol\omega/dt$}

Angular acceleration is the time derivative of <angular velocity>, $\boldsymbol\alpha=d\boldsymbol\omega/dt$. For rotation about a fixed axis, its signed scalar component obeys $\tau=I\alpha$ when the axial <moment of inertia> is constant and $\tau$ is the total applied <torque> about that axis.

= Angular speed
{parent=Angular velocity}
{title2=$\omega=|\boldsymbol\omega|$}

Angular speed is the nonnegative magnitude of <angular velocity>: $\omega=|\boldsymbol\omega|$. It measures the rate of rotation about an instantaneous axis without specifying its direction. For uniform <circular motion> of radius $R$, the ordinary <speed> is $v=R\omega$.

= Fixed space frame
{parent=Rigid body dynamics}

A fixed space frame is an inertial orthonormal basis whose axes do not rotate with the body.

= Principal body frame
{parent=Rigid body dynamics}

A principal body frame is an orthonormal basis fixed in the body and aligned with the principal axes of its inertia tensor. In this frame the inertia tensor is diagonal with principal moments $I_1,I_2,I_3$.

= Principal axis
{parent=Principal body frame}

= Principal axes
{synonym}

A principal axis is an <eigenvector> direction of the <inertia tensor>. Choosing three orthogonal principal axes diagonalizes the inertia tensor.

= Derivative of a body-fixed basis vector
{parent=Principal body frame}

If $e_i$ is fixed in a rigid body with <angular velocity> $\omega$, then its derivative in the fixed space frame is
$$
\dot e_i=\omega\times e_i.
$$

= Body-frame derivative of a space-fixed vector
{parent=Principal body frame}

If a vector $k$ is fixed in space while a body frame rotates with <angular velocity> $\omega$, its body-frame components satisfy
$$
\left(\frac{dk}{dt}\right)_{\rm body}=-\omega\times k.
$$

= Euler's equations
{c}
{disambiguate=rigid body dynamics}
{parent=Rigid body dynamics}
{title2=$I\dot\omega+\omega\times(I\omega)=\tau$}
{wiki}

In principal body coordinates, <Euler's equations (rigid body dynamics)> express <angular momentum> balance as $I\dot\omega+\omega\times(I\omega)=\tau$, with applied <torque> $\tau$. Setting the torque to zero gives the following torque-free specialization.

= Euler equations for a torque-free rigid body
{parent=Euler's equations (rigid body dynamics)}
{c}

In principal-axis body coordinates, torque-free angular velocity satisfies
$$
I_1\dot\omega_1=(I_2-I_3)\omega_2\omega_3,
\quad
I_2\dot\omega_2=(I_3-I_1)\omega_3\omega_1,
\quad
I_3\dot\omega_3=(I_1-I_2)\omega_1\omega_2.
$$

= Torque-free rigid-body invariants
{parent=Euler equations for a torque-free rigid body}

The <Euler equations for a torque-free rigid body> conserve rotational kinetic energy and squared angular momentum,
$$
E=\frac12\sum_{i=1}^3I_i\omega_i^2,
\qquad
L^2=\sum_{i=1}^3I_i^2\omega_i^2.
$$
Their common level sets are the intersection of two ellipsoids in angular-velocity space.

= Quartic equation for one component of torque-free angular velocity
{parent=Torque-free rigid-body invariants}

Eliminating $\omega_1^2$ and $\omega_2^2$ between the conserved energy and squared angular momentum gives
$$
\dot\omega_3^2=-\frac{
\left[L^2-2EI_2+I_3(I_2-I_3)\omega_3^2\right]
\left[L^2-2EI_1+I_3(I_1-I_3)\omega_3^2\right]
}{I_1I_2I_3^2}.
$$
The right-hand side is a quartic polynomial in $\omega_3$; its real turning points delimit the nutation of the body-frame angular velocity.

= Coplanarity in a torque-free axisymmetric rigid body
{parent=Euler equations for a torque-free rigid body}

For principal moments $I_1=I_2=I$ and symmetry-axis unit vector $e_3$, angular momentum and angular velocity obey
$$
L=I\omega+(I_3-I)\omega_3e_3.
$$
Thus $L$, $\omega$, and the symmetry axis are coplanar at every instant.

= Intermediate axis theorem
{parent=Euler equations for a torque-free rigid body}
{wiki}

For $I_1<I_2<I_3$, steady torque-free rotation about the first or third principal axis is stable, while rotation about the intermediate principal axis is unstable.

= Linear stability of principal-axis rotation
{parent=Intermediate axis theorem}

Linearize the <Euler equations for a torque-free rigid body> about rotation with angular speed $\Omega$ along the third principal axis. The transverse perturbations obey
$$
\dot\omega_1=\frac{I_2-I_3}{I_1}\Omega\omega_2,
\qquad
\dot\omega_2=\frac{I_3-I_1}{I_2}\Omega\omega_1,
$$
and hence
$$
\ddot\omega_1
=-\Omega^2\frac{(I_3-I_2)(I_3-I_1)}{I_1I_2}\omega_1.
$$
Cyclic permutation gives the corresponding equations about the other two axes. For $I_1<I_2<I_3$, the squared frequency is positive about axes one and three, whereas the intermediate-axis equation has a positive exponential-growth coefficient.

= Intermediate-axis separatrix
{parent=Intermediate axis theorem}

On the invariant level $L^2=2EI_2$, the torque-free rigid-body orbit is a separatrix joining the two intermediate-axis rotations. With
$$
\mu=\sqrt{\frac{2E}{I_2}},
\qquad
\lambda=\mu\sqrt{\frac{(I_3-I_2)(I_2-I_1)}{I_1I_3}},
$$
one orientation has $\omega_2(t)=\mu\tanh(\lambda(t-t_0))$; the other components are constant multiples of $\operatorname{sech}(\lambda(t-t_0))$.

= Moment of inertia
{title2=$I$}
{parent=Rigid body dynamics}
{wiki}

= Moments of inertia
{synonym}

The <moment of inertia> about a specified axis measures how a <mass> distribution resists changes of <angular velocity> about that axis. For <point masses> $m_i$ at perpendicular distances $r_{\perp i}$, or a continuous distribution with <mass density> $\rho$,
$$
I=\sum_i m_i r_{\perp i}^2,
\qquad I=\int r_\perp^2\,dm=\int r_\perp^2\rho\,dV.
$$
Its <SI> unit is $\mathrm{kg\,m^2}$. The distance is to the entire axis, not to one point on it: moving a <point mass> along the axis leaves its contribution unchanged. The <moment of inertia> depends on both the distribution and the chosen axis; it is not determined by total <mass> alone. About an axis fixed in an <inertial frame>, a <rigid body> with larger <moment of inertia> has smaller <angular acceleration> under the same net axial <torque>, and greater <rotational kinetic energy> at the same <angular speed>.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume I by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 19: Center of Mass; Moment of Inertia>{ref}

For a simple model, put four equal <point masses> of total <mass> $M$ on light rigid spokes at radius $a$. Their <moment of inertia> is $Ma^2$. Doubling $a$ quadruples the <moment of inertia> without changing $M$. The illustrated placements $a=R/3$ and $a=R$ give $MR^2/9$ and $MR^2$. Actual spokes and finite-sized weights contribute their own <moments of inertia>; the <point mass> model neglects them. Repositioning weights during rotation also breaks the fixed-distribution assumption, so it requires a separate <work> and <angular momentum> calculation.

\Image[rotational-inertia-energy-mass-distribution.png]
{title=Same total <mass>, different radial placement and <moment of inertia>}
{description=Original model diagrams, not experimental data. The upper pair has four equal <point masses> on negligible-mass spokes, at $R/3$ and $R$ from a perpendicular central axis. The lower pair compares a uniform disc with an ideal thin ring of the same <mass> $M$ and outer radius $R$. The displayed <moments of inertia> determine <rotational kinetic energy> at a shared <angular speed>. The visible thickness of the ring and size of the point markers are only drawing conventions.}

The <moment of inertia of a uniform disc> follows by dividing it into concentric <annuli>: $dm=(2M/R^2)r\,dr$ and $I=\int_0^R r^2(2M/R^2)r\,dr=MR^2/2$. For an ideal thin ring every <mass> element is at $R$, so $I=MR^2$. A ring of finite radial thickness instead uses the <moment of inertia of a uniform annulus>, $I=M(R^2+b^2)/2$, with inner radius $b$. A <moment of inertia> is thus a compact summary of radial placement rather than a complete description of shape. For several joined pieces, their <moments of inertia> add only when each is calculated about the same axis.<University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others/10.5 Calculating Moments of Inertia>[§10.5]{ref}

A <physical pendulum> makes the <moment of inertia> measurable through timing. Martín Monteiro, Cecilia Stari, Cecilia Cabeza and Arturo C. Martí used a metal bar with suspension holes $1.0\,\mathrm{cm}$ apart and an attached smartphone. They report bar length $1.199\,\mathrm m$, bar <mass> $0.2518\,\mathrm{kg}$ and phone <mass> $0.1311\,\mathrm{kg}$. Changing the suspension hole changes the pivot axis. Their readouts were phone <gyroscope> <angular velocity> records, analyzed by <autocorrelation> or a fit to a <sinusoid>, and an independent photogate; the logged records for the <sinusoid> fits lasted $10\,\mathrm s$. They observed agreement between the three timing methods and the geometry-based model, including a minimum of <oscillation period> as the suspension point varied. These are reported apparatus details and observations, not measurements made for this article.<Experimental analysis of a physical pendulum with variable suspension point by Martín Monteiro and others>{ref}

To infer a <moment of inertia> from such a measurement, determine the total <mass> $M_{\mathrm{tot}}$ including every attached part, locate the assembly's <centre of mass> $G$, and measure its distance $h>0$ below the horizontal pivot $O$. Record successive crossings in the same direction, or the elapsed <time> $t_N$ for $N$ complete <oscillations>, giving <oscillation period> $P=t_N/N$. The <physical pendulum> model with a fixed pivot, a <uniform gravitational field>, negligible <friction> and the <small-angle approximation> gives
$$
I_O\ddot\theta=-M_{\mathrm{tot}}gh\sin\theta,
\qquad P\simeq2\pi\sqrt{\frac{I_O}{M_{\mathrm{tot}}gh}},
\qquad I_{O,\mathrm{inferred}}=M_{\mathrm{tot}}gh\left(\frac{P}{2\pi}\right)^2.
$$
The crossing times, <masses> and geometric distances are the measured inputs; $I_{O,\mathrm{inferred}}$ is an inference conditional on this model, while an <integral> over the assumed shape supplies an independent model prediction. The <parallel axis theorem> converts the inferred pivot <moment of inertia> to one about a parallel axis through $G$. The controls and interpretation of that comparison are described there. An axial measurement determines one scalar <moment of inertia>, not the complete <inertia tensor> needed for general rotation.

= Moment of inertia of a uniform solid hemisphere
{parent=Moment of inertia}
{title2=$I_G=83ma^2/320$}

The centroid of a uniform solid hemisphere of radius $a$ lies $3a/8$ from its flat face. About a diameter of the underlying sphere parallel to that face, its <moment of inertia> is $2ma^2/5$. The <parallel axis theorem> therefore gives $I_G=2ma^2/5-m(3a/8)^2=83ma^2/320$ about the parallel centroidal axis. A thin hemispherical shell has a different centroid and inertia.

= Moment of inertia of a uniform solid cylinder
{parent=Moment of inertia}
{title2=$I=ma^2/2$}

For a uniform solid cylinder of radius $a$, length $L$ and <mass density> $\rho$, a coaxial shell carries <mass> $dm=2\pi\rho Lr\,dr$. The axial <moment of inertia> is $I=\int_0^a r^2dm=\pi\rho La^4/2=ma^2/2$. The axial <moment of inertia> is independent of the length once <mass> and radius are fixed. A thin cylindrical shell instead has $I=ma^2$, demonstrating why the uniform solid assumption matters.

= Reflected inertia of a no-slip wheel and roller
{parent=Moment of inertia}
{title2=$K=I+J(R/S)^2$}

If a fixed-axis wheel of radius $R$ drives a fixed-axis roller of radius $S$ without slip, their angular-speed magnitudes satisfy $\sigma=(R/S)\omega$. Their <kinetic energy> is $\tfrac12[I+J(R/S)^2]\omega^2$, so the displayed reflected <moment of inertia> governs the wheel coordinate. A roller resisting torque of magnitude $\gamma\sigma^2$ consumes power $\gamma\sigma^3$ and therefore reflects to wheel torque $\gamma(R/S)^3\omega^2$. The squared ratio belongs to inertia; the cubed ratio belongs to this quadratic resisting torque.

= Moment of inertia of a uniform annulus
{parent=Moment of inertia}
{title2=$I=\frac M2(a^2+b^2)$}

A uniform planar annulus of outer radius $a$, inner radius $b<a$ and mass $M$ has surface density $M/[\pi(a^2-b^2)]$. Integrating $r^2$ over its mass gives the axial <moment of inertia> $I=M(a^2+b^2)/2$. Subtracting the inner disc from a complete disc gives the same result, provided their surface densities match.

// Target: electromagnetism.bigb

= Moment of inertia of a uniform disc
{parent=Moment of inertia}
{title2=$I=Ma^2/2$}

For a uniform disc of radius $a$ and mass $M$, annuli of radius $r$ and thickness $dr$ have mass $(M/\pi a^2)2\pi r\,dr$. Their axial <moment of inertia> sums to $I=\int_0^a r^2\,dm=Ma^2/2$.

= Moment of inertia of a uniform solid sphere
{parent=Moment of inertia}
{title2=$I=\frac25Ma^2$}

A uniform solid sphere of mass $M$ and radius $a$ has the indicated <moment of inertia> about any diameter. Integrating the squared perpendicular distance against uniform <mass density> gives $I=8\pi\rho a^5/15=2Ma^2/5$. It is different from the value for a thin spherical shell and determines the rotational contribution to <kinetic energy> during <rolling without slipping>.

= Mass second-moment tensor
{title2=$I_{jk}$}
{parent=Moment of inertia}

The mass second-moment tensor about a <centre of mass> is $I_{jk}=\int y_jy_k\,dm$. It measures the shape of a mass distribution and appears in <tidal torque theory>. It differs from the mechanical <inertia tensor>, which is $\mathcal I_{jk}=\operatorname{tr}(I)\delta_{jk}-I_{jk}$. For a uniform <ellipsoid> of mass $M$ and semiaxes $a_1,a_2,a_3$, its principal values are $Ma_j^2/5$.

= Inertia tensor
{title2=$I_{ij}$}
{parent=Moment of inertia}
{wiki}

The <inertia tensor> packages the <moments of inertia> about every axis through a specified point $O$ into one <symmetric matrix>. An axis still needs a direction: if $\mathbf n$ is a <unit vector>, its scalar <moment of inertia> is $I_{O,\mathbf n}=\mathbf n^T\mathbf I_O\mathbf n$. The same <mass> layout can therefore be easy to spin about one direction and harder about another. The <inertia tensor> also determines <rotational kinetic energy> and relates <angular velocity> to <angular momentum> in the appropriate <rigid body> model.

Take <position> vectors $\mathbf r=(x,y,z)$ relative to $O$, expressed in an <orthonormal basis>. For <point masses> $m_a$, or a continuous distribution with <mass density> $\rho$ and $dm=\rho\,dV$,
$$
(\mathbf I_O)_{ij}=\sum_a m_a\bigl(|\mathbf r_a|^2\delta_{ij}-r_{ai}r_{aj}\bigr),
\qquad
(\mathbf I_O)_{ij}=\int\bigl(|\mathbf r|^2\delta_{ij}-r_ir_j\bigr)\,dm.
$$
Here $\delta_{ij}$ is the <Kronecker delta>. Equivalently, using the <identity matrix> $\mathbf1$ and an <outer product>,
$$
\mathbf I_O=\int\bigl(|\mathbf r|^2\mathbf1-\mathbf r\mathbf r^T\bigr)\,dm
=\int\begin{pmatrix}
y^2+z^2&-xy&-xz\\
-xy&x^2+z^2&-yz\\
-xz&-yz&x^2+y^2
\end{pmatrix}\,dm.
$$
Every entry has <SI> units $\mathrm{kg\,m^2}$. The diagonal entries are axial <moments of inertia>; the off-diagonal entries carry a minus sign in this convention. Some texts instead define the integrals without the prefixed minus sign $\int xy\,dm$, $\int xz\,dm$, $\int yz\,dm$ as their named off-diagonal quantities and put the minus signs into the displayed <matrix>. Check that convention before combining formulas. <Newtonian Dynamics by Richard Fitzpatrick/Moment of Inertia Tensor>{ref} <16.07 Dynamics/Lecture L26 - 3D Rigid Body Dynamics: The Inertia Tensor>{ref}

The <symmetric matrix> is <positive semidefinite>, because its <quadratic form> is
$$
\mathbf u^T\mathbf I_O\mathbf u
=\int\bigl(|\mathbf u|^2|\mathbf r|^2-(\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf r)^2\bigr)\,dm
=\int|\mathbf u\times\mathbf r|^2\,dm\geq0.
$$
The <dot product> and <cross product> identify this as a sum of squared distances when $\mathbf u$ is a <unit vector>. A zero <moment of inertia> occurs precisely when all the <mass> lies on that axis, up to sets of zero <mass>. A genuine three-dimensional body with positive <mass density> in a volume has strictly positive axial <moments of inertia>. Do not confuse this <inertia tensor> with the <mass second-moment tensor> $\mathbf S=\int\mathbf r\mathbf r^T\,dm$: they obey $\mathbf I_O=\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf S)\mathbf1-\mathbf S$, where $\operatorname{tr}$ denotes the <matrix trace>.

If $O$ is fixed in an <inertial frame> and each <velocity> is $\mathbf v=\boldsymbol\omega\times\mathbf r$, summing the individual <kinetic energies> and <angular momenta> gives
$$
\mathbf L_O=\mathbf I_O\boldsymbol\omega,
\qquad
K=\frac12\boldsymbol\omega^T\mathbf I_O\boldsymbol\omega
=\frac12\boldsymbol\omega\cdot\mathbf L_O.
$$
These formulas also describe <angular momentum> and <rotational kinetic energy> relative to the <centre of mass> $G$ of a translating <rigid body>. In that case the total <kinetic energy> in the <inertial frame> is $K_{\mathrm{total}}=\tfrac12M|\mathbf V_G|^2+\tfrac12\boldsymbol\omega^T\mathbf I_G\boldsymbol\omega$. For a general moving point $O$, the body's actual <velocities> include $\mathbf V_O$, so $\mathbf I_O\boldsymbol\omega$ alone need not be its <angular momentum> about $O$. <Newtonian Dynamics by Richard Fitzpatrick/Rotational Kinetic Energy>{ref} <16.07 Dynamics/Lecture L26 - 3D Rigid Body Dynamics: The Inertia Tensor>{ref}

Changing the <orthonormal basis> changes components, rather than the physical distribution: if the columns of an <orthogonal matrix> $Q$ are the new basis vectors expressed in the old basis, then $\mathbf I'_O=Q^T\mathbf I_OQ$. Changing the reference point is a different operation, governed by the <tensor parallel-axis theorem>. Both <principal axes> and <principal moments of inertia> must therefore be quoted with their reference point.

= Inertia tensor of paired point masses
{parent=Inertia tensor}

An exact planar example makes the off-diagonal entries tangible. Join four equal <point masses> $m$ with negligible-mass rigid connectors at $a(2,1,0)$, $a(-2,-1,0)$, $a(1,-1,0)$ and $a(-1,1,0)$, where $a>0$ is a length. Their <centre of mass> is $O=(0,0,0)$. Summing $x^2$, $y^2$ and $xy$ gives $10a^2$, $4a^2$ and $2a^2$, respectively, so
$$
\mathbf I_{\mathrm{planar}}=ma^2\begin{pmatrix}4&-2&0\\-2&10&0\\0&0&14\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The $z$ axis is a <principal axis>, and $I_{zz}=I_{xx}+I_{yy}$ illustrates the <perpendicular axis theorem>. The $x$ axis is not a <principal axis>: <angular velocity> $\boldsymbol\omega=(\Omega,0,0)$ gives <angular momentum> $ma^2\Omega(4,-2,0)$, which points in a different direction. A bearing can enforce fixed-axis rotation despite this difference, supplying the necessary transverse <torque>.

\Image[inertia-tensor-paired-masses-rotational-energy.png]
{title=Paired <point masses> and direction-dependent <rotational kinetic energy>}
{description=Original exact model. Four equal <point masses> lie in the $xy$ plane with negligible-mass connectors. The curves use <angular velocity> $\boldsymbol\omega=\Omega(\cos\theta,\sin\theta,0)$ and plot $2K/(ma^2\Omega^2)$. Adding two more equal <point masses> at $a(0,0,\pm1)$ raises this curve by two. Markers depict ideal <point masses>, not finite-sized weights; no experimental data are plotted.}

For an in-plane <unit vector> $\mathbf n=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta,0)$, the planar model has
$$
\frac{I_{O,\mathbf n}}{ma^2}=4\cos^2\theta+10\sin^2\theta-4\sin\theta\cos\theta.
$$
Its extrema are the <principal moments of inertia> $(7-\sqrt{13})ma^2$ and $(7+\sqrt{13})ma^2$; the remaining <principal moment of inertia> is $14ma^2$. These values follow from the two-by-two <characteristic polynomial> $(4-\lambda)(10-\lambda)-4$, so they can be checked without trusting a numerical <eigenvalue> routine. At a stipulated <angular speed> $\Omega=1\,\mathrm{rad/s}$, with $m=1\,\mathrm{kg}$ and $a=1\,\mathrm m$, rotation about $x$, $y$ or $z$ has <rotational kinetic energy> $2$, $5$ or $7\,\mathrm J$, respectively.

A truly three-dimensional example adds two more equal <point masses> at $a(0,0,1)$ and $a(0,0,-1)$. Each contributes $ma^2\operatorname{diag}(1,1,0)$ to the <inertia tensor>, giving
$$
\mathbf I_{\mathrm{3D}}=ma^2\begin{pmatrix}6&-2&0\\-2&12&0\\0&0&14\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
(I_1,I_2,I_3)=ma^2(9-\sqrt{13},9+\sqrt{13},14).
$$
The in-plane <principal axes> stay the same while their <principal moments of inertia> each increase by $2ma^2$. The $z$ <moment of inertia> stays unchanged because the added <point masses> lie on that axis. This comparison changes the total <mass> from $4m$ to $6m$; it holds the original four <point masses> fixed rather than redistributing a fixed total <mass>.

= Rotational inertia measurement with a falling mass
{parent=Inertia tensor}

PASCO's <PASPORT Rotary Motion Sensor Experiment Guide>, Experiment 1, specifies a PS-2120A sensor, a rod carrying two equal-radius weights, a three-step drive wheel, a thread and hanging <mass>, a clamp-on guide wheel, a support, a balance and calipers. The thread leaves the drive wheel tangentially. The sensor records <angular velocity> against <time>; a <linear regression> over the accelerating interval supplies <angular acceleration> $\alpha$. The guide measures the drive radius with calipers and repeats the run with the weights removed to subtract the apparatus's own <moment of inertia>. Its tables are blank: it prescribes a measurement and does not report an observed numerical agreement. <PASPORT Rotary Motion Sensor Experiment Guide>{ref}

With hanging <mass> $m_h$ including its hanger, effective drive radius $b$, no thread slip and negligible guide-wheel inertia, <Newton's second law> gives thread <tension> $F=m_h(g-b\alpha)$. The axial <torque> is $bF$. Under negligible <friction>, the inference is therefore
$$
I_{\mathrm{loaded}}=\frac{m_hgb}{\alpha}-m_hb^2.
$$
Repeat the inference for the unloaded assembly and subtract: $I_{\mathrm{weights}}=I_{\mathrm{loaded}}-I_{\mathrm{empty}}$. Omitting the $m_hb^2$ term incorrectly treats thread <tension> as the hanging weight. Comparing with $(M_1+M_2)R^2$ tests the <point mass> approximation; finite-sized weights also have their own <moments of inertia> about their <centres of mass>. <PASPORT Rotary Motion Sensor Experiment Guide>{ref}

Useful proposed checks go beyond that ideal inference. Verify the <rotary encoder>'s angle scale by turning the shaft through a marked full revolution; check the recorded <time> against an independent clock and the balance against a known <mass>. Measure $b$ at the thread's running surface, including thread thickness; avoid overlapping wraps that change $b$. Release without a push, inspect the <angular velocity> record for a straight interval, and stop before the hanger lands or the thread runs out. Repeat with several hanging <masses>, weight radii and rotation directions. A consistent inferred <moment of inertia> across drive <torques> supports the model; curvature, direction dependence or drift indicates a limitation rather than a new <principal moment of inertia>.

For approximately constant resisting <torque> $\tau_f\geq0$ in one rotation direction, an original calibration model is
$$
m_hgb-m_hb^2\alpha=I\alpha+\tau_f.
$$
Fit the left-hand quantity against $\alpha$ across several drives: the slope estimates the axial <moment of inertia> and the intercept estimates $\tau_f$. This calibration is conditional on <friction> remaining constant over the fitted <angular speeds>; a freely slowing run can expose speed dependence. Sensor differentiation noise, guide-wheel <moment of inertia>, thread slip, flexing connectors and uncertain weight radii limit the inference. Removing weights can also change bearing loads, so empty-apparatus subtraction does not automatically cancel <friction>. These are proposed controls and a model extension, not results claimed by PASCO.

A fixed-axis readout measures $\mathbf n^T\mathbf I_O\mathbf n$, not the full <inertia tensor>. In a proposed generalization, repeat about $x$, $y$, $z$ and the three diagonal directions $(\mathbf e_x+\mathbf e_y)/\sqrt2$, $(\mathbf e_x+\mathbf e_z)/\sqrt2$, $(\mathbf e_y+\mathbf e_z)/\sqrt2$, all through the same $O$. For example,
$$
I_{xy}=I_{O,(\mathbf e_x+\mathbf e_y)/\sqrt2}-\frac{I_{xx}+I_{yy}}2.
$$
Six independent scalar readings then determine the six entries of a <symmetric matrix>. Each mounting needs its own apparatus correction, and different reference points require the <tensor parallel-axis theorem> before the readings can be combined.

= Rotary encoder
{parent=Rotational inertia measurement with a falling mass}
{wiki}

A <rotary encoder> converts shaft rotation into a readout of <angle> or changes in <angle>. The PS-2120A uses interruptions of a <light> beam to encode shaft motion; its product specification gives 4000 divisions per revolution. That count is a resolution specification, not a guarantee of the precision of an inferred <angular acceleration> or <moment of inertia>. <PASPORT Rotary Motion Sensor - PS-2120A>{ref}

= Tensor parallel-axis theorem
{parent=Inertia tensor}
{title2=$I(P)=I(G)+M(|X|^2I-XX^T)$}

If $G$ is the <center of mass> and $X=P-G$, expanding the defining <inertia tensor> about $P$ gives the displayed formula. The cross terms vanish because $\int r\,dm=0$ in coordinates centred at $G$. Contracting with a unit axis $u$ gives the <parallel axis theorem>, with the squared distance between the parallel axes equal to $|X|^2-(u\cdot X)^2$.

= Principal moment of inertia
{title2=$I_i$}
{parent=Inertia tensor}

= Principal moments of inertia
{synonym}

The <principal moments of inertia> about a specified point $O$ are the <eigenvalues> $I_1,I_2,I_3$ of the <inertia tensor> $\mathbf I_O$. Their associated <unit vectors> that are <eigenvectors> specify <principal axes> through $O$. The <spectral theorem for real symmetric matrices> guarantees an <orthonormal basis> of these <eigenvectors>, even for an irregular <rigid body>. Choose their signs to make a right-handed basis and collect them as the columns of an <orthogonal matrix> $Q$:
$$
\mathbf I_O\mathbf e_i=I_i\mathbf e_i,
\qquad Q^T\mathbf I_OQ=\operatorname{diag}(I_1,I_2,I_3).
$$
Thus <diagonalization> removes the off-diagonal coupling in the <rotational kinetic energy>. The <eigenvalues> belong to the <mass> distribution and reference point, independently of which <orthonormal basis> initially describes them. <Newtonian Dynamics by Richard Fitzpatrick/Principal Axes of Rotation>{ref}

In this <principal body frame>, write <angular velocity> as $\boldsymbol\omega=\sum_i\omega_i\mathbf e_i$. Then
$$
\mathbf L_O=\sum_i I_i\omega_i\mathbf e_i,
\qquad
K=\frac12\sum_i I_i\omega_i^2.
$$
Use these as total <angular momentum> and <kinetic energy> only under the reference-point conditions stated under <inertia tensor>. Along a single <principal axis> with $I_i>0$, <angular momentum> is parallel to <angular velocity>. For a mixture of directions with different <principal moments of inertia>, it generally is not. A <principal axis> need not be a geometric symmetry axis: it is selected by the complete <mass> distribution. A <principal axis> also need not give stable free rotation; for three distinct <principal moments of inertia>, the <intermediate axis theorem> supplies the stability distinction.

For any <unit vector> $\mathbf n=\sum_i n_i\mathbf e_i$,
$$
I_{O,\mathbf n}=\sum_i I_i n_i^2,
\qquad \sum_i n_i^2=1,
\qquad \min_i I_i\leq I_{O,\mathbf n}\leq\max_i I_i.
$$
The axial <moment of inertia> is a weighted average of the <principal moments of inertia>. At fixed <angular speed>, the least and greatest <rotational kinetic energies> occur in the corresponding <eigenspaces>. At fixed nonzero <angular momentum> magnitude $L$, instead, $K=\tfrac12\sum_i L_i^2/I_i$, so the largest <principal moment of inertia> gives the smallest <rotational kinetic energy>. These comparisons hold the reference point fixed.

There is an additional physical restriction beyond <positive semidefiniteness>. In <principal axes> coordinates,
$$
I_1+I_2-I_3=2\int z^2\,dm\geq0,
$$
with two analogous cyclic inequalities. Therefore three arbitrary positive numbers do not always represent a possible <inertia tensor>. For a distribution in the $xy$ plane through $O$, $I_3=I_1+I_2$, as in the planar <inertia tensor of paired point masses>. A uniform solid <ellipsoid> of <mass> $M$ and semiaxes $a,b,c$ supplies a continuous three-dimensional example: using its <mass second-moment tensor>, its central <principal moments of inertia> are $M(b^2+c^2)/5$, $M(a^2+c^2)/5$, $M(a^2+b^2)/5$. Setting $a=b=c=R$ recovers the <moment of inertia of a uniform solid sphere>.

= Rotational energy ellipsoid
{parent=Principal moment of inertia}
{title2=$\boldsymbol\omega^T\mathbf I_O\boldsymbol\omega=2K_0$}

For strictly positive <principal moments of inertia> and a fixed <rotational kinetic energy> $K_0>0$, the allowed <angular velocity> components form an <ellipsoid>:
$$
I_1\omega_1^2+I_2\omega_2^2+I_3\omega_3^2=2K_0,
\qquad
s_i=\sqrt{\frac{2K_0}{I_i}}.
$$
Its semiaxis lengths $s_i$ have units of <angular speed>. The longest semiaxis corresponds to the smallest <principal moment of inertia>: that direction permits the greatest <angular speed> for the same <rotational kinetic energy>. This is a surface in <angular velocity> space, not the physical outline of the body. If one <principal moment of inertia> vanishes in an ideal line-mass model, this constant-energy surface is unbounded in that direction and is no longer an <ellipsoid>.

\Image[inertia-tensor-principal-axis-energy-ellipsoid.png]
{title=<Principal axes> and the constant-<rotational kinetic energy> <ellipsoid>}
{description=Original exact model using the six <point masses> in <inertia tensor of paired point masses>. With $K_0=ma^2\Omega_0^2$, the displayed surface in $\boldsymbol\omega/\Omega_0$ has semiaxes $\sqrt{2/(9-\sqrt{13})}$, $\sqrt{2/(9+\sqrt{13})}$ and $\sqrt{2/14}$. The left view uses the original coordinate directions and the right view the <principal body frame>. Colors identify the same <principal axes> in both views; the surface describes possible <angular velocities>, not a material shell or a measured trajectory.}

The <rotational energy ellipsoid> gives the geometry behind <Poinsot's ellipsoid>, but a constant-energy surface alone does not determine a free-rotation trajectory. A torque-free trajectory must also satisfy the conserved <angular momentum> constraint and the <Euler equations for a torque-free rigid body>.

= Degeneracy of principal moments of inertia
{parent=Principal moment of inertia}

If two <principal moments of inertia> coincide, for example $I_1=I_2\ne I_3$, every direction in their two-dimensional <eigenspace> is a <principal axis>. The perpendicular axis is determined, but there is no privileged orthogonal pair within that plane. Rotating the pair in the plane leaves the diagonal <inertia tensor> unchanged. If all three <principal moments of inertia> coincide, $\mathbf I_O=I_0\mathbf1$ and every axis through $O$ is principal. This follows from the <eigenvalue multiplicity> and <eigenspace>, rather than from a failure of <diagonalization>. <Newtonian Dynamics by Richard Fitzpatrick/Matrix Eigenvalue Theory>{ref}

Equal <principal moments of inertia> do not prove that a body's outline has continuous rotational symmetry. For example, six equal <point masses> at $(\pm a,0,0)$, $(0,\pm a,0)$ and $(0,0,\pm a)$ have $\mathbf I_O=4ma^2\mathbf1$. This discrete layout has the same axial <moment of inertia> in every direction, although its actual <mass> distribution changes under most rotations. Conversely, even when all three <principal moments of inertia> are distinct, <principal axes> are unoriented lines: reversing an <eigenvector>'s sign gives the same axis. Near repeated <eigenvalues>, small measurement errors can rotate fitted <principal axes> substantially within the nearly degenerate <eigenspace>, so quoted directions need an uncertainty assessment as well as quoted <principal moments of inertia>.

= Poinsot's ellipsoid
{c}
{parent=Inertia tensor}
{wiki}

= Poinsot construction
{c}
{synonym}

For torque-free rigid-body motion, the kinetic-energy ellipsoid fixed in the body rolls without slipping on the invariable plane perpendicular to the conserved angular momentum. Its point of contact is the angular-velocity vector.

= Parallel axis theorem
{parent=Moment of inertia}
{wiki}

The <parallel axis theorem> transfers a <moment of inertia> from an axis through the <centre of mass> $G$ to a parallel axis through $O$:
$$
I_O=I_G+Md^2.
$$
Here $M$ is the total <mass> and $d$ is the shortest perpendicular distance between the two axes. Both <moments of inertia> concern the same <mass> distribution and the same axis direction. The axis for $I_G$ must pass through the <centre of mass>; $I_O$ may refer to an axis outside the body. No uniform-density or planar-shape assumption is needed. Within any family of parallel axes, the <centre of mass> axis minimizes the <moment of inertia>.<University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others/10.5 Calculating Moments of Inertia>[§10.5]{ref}

A short proof isolates why the <centre of mass> matters. Choose parallel axes perpendicular to an $xy$ plane, put $G$ at the origin, and place $O$ at $(d,0)$. Then
$$
I_O=\int\bigl[(x-d)^2+y^2\bigr]\,dm
=I_G-2d\int x\,dm+d^2\int dm
=I_G+Md^2,
$$
because $\int x\,dm=0$ in <centre of mass> coordinates. Individual <mass> elements can move closer to the new axis, but their weighted cross terms cancel. For arbitrary parallel axes $A$ and $B$, use $I_B-I_A=M(d_B^2-d_A^2)$, where each distance is measured from the parallel <centre of mass> axis; adding $M$ times the squared separation of $A$ and $B$ is generally incorrect. A displacement along the axis has $d=0$. Changing the axis direction instead requires the <inertia tensor> or, in its applicable planar setting, the <perpendicular axis theorem>.

For a uniform disc, <mass> $M$ and radius $R$, the central perpendicular <moment of inertia> is $I_G=MR^2/2$. Shifting that axis to the rim gives $d=R$ and $I_O=3MR^2/2$, three times $I_G$. In the computed $M=2\,\mathrm{kg}$, $R=0.30\,\mathrm m$ example, $I_G=0.090\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2}$, $Md^2=0.180\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2}$ and $I_O=0.270\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2}$. At <angular speed> $10\,\mathrm{rad/s}$ the rim-pivoted disc has <rotational kinetic energy> $13.5\,\mathrm J$. Its <centre of mass> now moves, so $\tfrac12I_O\omega^2$ already includes that translation; adding a separate <centre of mass> translation term to it would count that contribution twice.

\Image[rotational-inertia-energy-parallel-axis-shift.png]
{title=<Parallel axis theorem>: a uniform disc's central and rim axes}
{description=Original geometric and numerical model, not measured data. Both axes are perpendicular to the disc; the separation is $d=R$. The bar chart separates $I_G=MR^2/2$ from the additional $Md^2=MR^2$ in $I_O=3MR^2/2$, normalized by $MR^2$. All parts of the disc remain in the calculation, including those nearer to the shifted axis.}

The <physical pendulum> measurement described under <moment of inertia> provides a practical check: infer $I_O=Mgh(P/2\pi)^2$ at several pivot positions, then subtract $Mh^2$ to obtain $I_G$. Keep all pivot axes parallel and measure $h$ to the <centre of mass> of the entire moving assembly. The same assembly should yield the same $I_G$ within measurement precision. If a smartphone is attached, its <mass> and its own <moment of inertia> belong in both the geometric prediction and the <centre of mass> calculation. The published bar-and-phone study explicitly adds both contributions using the <parallel axis theorem>.<Experimental analysis of a physical pendulum with variable suspension point by Martín Monteiro and others>[equations 2-4]{ref}

For a careful replication, release small swings without a push, repeat timings at several small <amplitudes>, and check that the extrapolated <oscillation period> is stable as <amplitude> decreases. Time many complete <oscillations> and repeat the run; two crossings of a photogate per cycle must not be mistaken for two cycles. Inspect decay of <amplitude> and compare early and late timings to detect significant pivot <friction> or air <drag>. Mark the bar and phone mount to detect relative slip, tighten the attachment, and check that the pivot support does not rock or migrate between holes. Repeat with an independent photogate or video readout to check the phone's timing. These are proposed controls for the replication, not additional results reported by the authors. The ideal model excludes appreciable <damping>, large swings and flexing; subtraction of $Mh^2$ also magnifies relative errors when $I_G$ is much smaller than $I_O$.

The <oscillation period> need not increase monotonically with pivot distance: the shift raises the <moment of inertia> but also increases the gravitational restoring <torque>. For a computed slender uniform bar model of length $L$, $I_G=ML^2/12$ and
$$
\frac{P^2}{4\pi^2}=\frac1g\left(h+\frac{L^2}{12h}\right),
\qquad h>0.
$$
This expression has its minimum at $h=L/\sqrt{12}$. With $L=1\,\mathrm m$, $h=0.50\,\mathrm m$ and stipulated $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$, the prediction is $P\simeq1.638\,\mathrm s$; it is not a recorded timing or a prediction for the bar with a phone attached. The competing terms explain why a timing experiment can reveal a <mass> distribution rather than merely its weight.

= Perpendicular axis theorem
{parent=Moment of inertia}
{wiki}

For a planar lamina in the $xy$-plane, the moments about three mutually perpendicular axes through one point satisfy
$$
I_z=I_x+I_y.
$$
This follows pointwise from $x^2+y^2=y^2+x^2$ in the defining mass integrals.

= Angular rocket equation
{parent=Moment of inertia}

For a uniformly filled, negligibly light disc of fixed radius $R$, remaining mass $m(t)$, angular velocity $\omega(t)$, and tangential exhaust speed $u$ relative to its rim, angular-momentum balance gives
$$
m\dot\omega=\dot m\left(\omega+\frac{2u}{R}\right).
$$
At zero relative exhaust speed, $\omega/m$ is constant.

= Moment of inertia of a solid cone about its vertex
{parent=Moment of inertia}

For a uniform solid cone of height $l$, base radius $R$, and mass $M$, whose symmetry axis points from vertex to base, the centre of mass lies at $3l/4$ from the vertex. The principal moments at the vertex are
$$I_1=I_2=\frac{3M}{20}(R^2+4l^2),
\qquad I_3=\frac{3MR^2}{10}.$$

= Moment of inertia of a pointed cubic-profile solid of revolution
{parent=Moment of inertia}

Consider the uniform <solid of revolution> about the $x_3$-axis whose boundary is
$$
x_1^2+x_2^2=x_3^2-\frac{x_3^3}{h},
\qquad 0\leq x_3\leq h.
$$
For total mass $M$, the center of mass lies a distance $3h/5$ from the pointed origin, and the principal moments about that origin are
$$
I_1=I_2=\frac37Mh^2,
\qquad
I_3=\frac2{35}Mh^2.
$$

= Lagrange, Euler, and Kovalevskaya tops
{c}
{parent=Rigid body dynamics}
{wiki}

The <Lagrange, Euler, and Kovalevskaya tops> are three integrable cases of fixed-point <rigid body dynamics>. The Euler top has no external torque; the Lagrange top has axial symmetry and its center of mass on the symmetry axis; the Kovalevskaya top has two equal principal moments twice the third and its center of mass in the corresponding principal plane.

= Euler angles
{parent=Rigid body dynamics}
{c}
{wiki}
{title2=$(\phi,\theta,\psi)$}

<Euler angles> describe the orientation of a <rigid body> through three successive angular rotations. Their convention specifies the axes and order of the rotations. For a symmetric top, a common convention takes $\theta$ as the inclination, $\phi$ as precession and $\psi$ as spin about the body axis.

= Euler angles for a symmetric top
{parent=Euler angles}
{c}

With the convention in which $\theta$ is inclination, $\phi$ is precession, and $\psi$ is body-axis spin, an axisymmetric body's kinetic energy is
$$T=\frac I2(\dot\theta^2+\sin^2\theta\dot\phi^2)
+\frac J2(\dot\psi+\cos\theta\dot\phi)^2.$$

= Heavy symmetric top Lagrangian
{parent=Euler angles for a symmetric top}

For a fixed point a distance $a$ from the centre of mass and inclination measured from the upward vertical,
$$L=T-Mga\cos\theta.$$
The spin and precession angles are cyclic.

This is the Lagrangian of a <Lagrange top>, one of the <Lagrange, Euler, and Kovalevskaya tops>.

= Lagrange top
{c}
{parent=Heavy symmetric top Lagrangian}

A Lagrange top is an axisymmetric rigid body with one point fixed on its symmetry axis, moving in a uniform gravitational field with its center of mass on that axis.

= Steady precession of a Lagrange top
{c}
{parent=Lagrange top}

For a Lagrange top whose inclination $\theta$ is measured from the upward vertical, steady precession at angular speed $\Omega$ obeys
$$
I_1\Omega^2\cos\theta-p_\psi\Omega+Mgl=0.
$$
Thus steady precession at a prescribed $0<\theta<\pi/2$ exists precisely when
$$
p_\psi^2\geq4MglI_1\cos\theta.
$$

= Heavy symmetric top Hamiltonian
{parent=Heavy symmetric top Lagrangian}

With transverse and axial moments $I,J$, the Hamiltonian is
$$H=\frac{p_\theta^2}{2I}
+\frac{(p_\phi-p_\psi\cos\theta)^2}{2I\sin^2\theta}
+\frac{p_\psi^2}{2J}+Mga\cos\theta.$$

= Heavy symmetric top reduction
{parent=Heavy symmetric top Hamiltonian}

Fixing the cyclic momenta $p_\psi=\lambda$ and $p_\phi=\mu$ leaves the canonical pair $(\theta,p_\theta)$ and a one-degree-of-freedom Hamiltonian with effective potential
$$V_{\lambda,\mu}(\theta)
=\frac{(\mu-\lambda\cos\theta)^2}{2I\sin^2\theta}
+\frac{\lambda^2}{2J}+Mga\cos\theta.$$

= Gyroscopic stabilization of an inverted symmetric top
{parent=Heavy symmetric top reduction}

At the upright spinning state, $p_\phi=p_\psi=\lambda$. The reduced potential is locally minimized at zero inclination when
$$\lambda^2>4IMga,$$
so sufficiently large axial angular momentum stabilizes the gravitationally inverted top.

= Rolling without slipping
{parent=Rigid body dynamics}
{wiki}

Rolling without slipping imposes that the contact point is instantaneously at rest, giving v=R omega.

= Rolling-time factor on a straight incline
{parent=Rolling without slipping}
{title2=$t_{\rm roll}/t_{\rm slide}=\sqrt{1+I/(MR^2)}$}

For a rigid body rolling from rest down a straight incline with fixed rolling radius $R$ and axial <moment of inertia> $I$, <translation> and <rotation> give $(M+I/R^2)\ddot s=Mg\sin\theta$. A smooth slide has $\ddot s=g\sin\theta$. Travel over the same distance from rest therefore gives the displayed time ratio. <Static friction> must suffice for <rolling without slipping>; its contact point is at rest, so this ideal friction does no work. A uniform solid cylinder has $I=MR^2/2$ and ratio $\sqrt{3/2}$.

= Rolling uniform solid hemisphere
{parent=Rolling without slipping}
{title2=$\dot\theta^2=15g\cos\theta/[a(28-15\cos\theta)]$}

Let the flat face make angle $\theta$ with horizontal while the rounded face contacts a horizontal plane. The centroid has height $a-(3a/8)\cos\theta$ and contact distance squared $b^2=a^2+(3a/8)^2-2a(3a/8)\cos\theta$. For <rolling without slipping>, its speed is $b|\dot\theta|$. Combining translation and rotation gives kinetic energy $\tfrac12(I_G+mb^2)\dot\theta^2$. Conservation from rest at $\theta=\pi/2$ gives the displayed result until the flat-face/contact geometry changes.

= Slipping solid cylinder on an incline
{parent=Rolling without slipping}
{title2=$\tan\alpha>3\mu$}

For a uniform solid cylinder, take $s$ positive uphill and $\omega$ positive for uphill <rolling without slipping>. The velocity of the contact point is $w=\dot s-a\omega$. Uphill <kinetic friction> gives $\ddot s=-g(\sin\alpha-\mu\cos\alpha)$ and $a\dot\omega=-2\mu g\cos\alpha$. Starting from $\dot s(0)=a\omega(0)$, one obtains $w=-gt(\sin\alpha-3\mu\cos\alpha)$. Thus $\tan\alpha>3\mu$ makes the assumed uphill <kinetic friction> self-consistent for the entire ascent and descent. Without slip the required uphill <static friction> is $mg\sin\alpha/3$; it is available exactly when $\tan\alpha\leq3\mu$ under the same limiting coefficient.

= Rolling inside a fixed circular cylinder
{parent=Rolling without slipping}
{title2=$\Omega=-\frac{R_1-R_0}{R_0}\dot\theta$}

A round body of radius $R_0$ in a fixed cylinder of radius $R_1$ has <center of mass> path radius $a=R_1-R_0$. With orbital angle $\theta$ and spin <angular velocity> $\Omega$ both positive about the same axis, <rolling without slipping> gives $a\dot\theta+R_0\Omega=0$. For <moment of inertia> $I=\gamma mR_0^2$, its <kinetic energy> is $\frac12ma^2(1+\gamma)\dot\theta^2$. Release from rest at angle $\alpha$ gives, by <conservation of energy>, a descent time $\sqrt{1+\gamma}$ times that of frictionless sliding along the same <center of mass> path. The spin sign reverses if its positive direction is chosen opposite to the orbital angle.

= Lagrangian mechanics
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

Lagrangian mechanics derives motion from the stationary action of $L=T-V$ through the Euler--Lagrange equations.

= Cartan form of a regular mechanical Lagrangian
{c}
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}
{title2=$\theta_L=L_{v^i}\,dq^i,\quad\omega_L=-d\theta_L$}

A <regular Lagrangian> $L:TQ\to\mathbb R$ defines a fibre derivative $\mathbb FL:TQ\to T^*Q$ and the Cartan one-form $\theta_L=(\mathbb FL)^*\theta$. Its two-form $\omega_L=-d\theta_L$ is nondegenerate when the velocity Hessian is invertible. The energy is $E_L=v^iL_{v^i}-L$. The equation $\iota_{\Gamma_L}\omega_L=dE_L$ determines the Euler–Lagrange vector field. A <hyperregular Lagrangian> makes the fibre derivative a global diffeomorphism and transports this dynamics to Hamiltonian phase space.

= Helical symmetry of a potential
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

A potential $V(r,\phi,z)=f(r,\phi-kz)$ is invariant under the screw transformation $(\phi,z)\mapsto(\phi+ks,z+s)$. The <Noether conserved quantity for a mechanical point symmetry> is
$$
p_z+kp_\phi,
$$
a linear combination of axial <linear momentum> and axial <angular momentum>.

= Cyclic coordinate
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}
{wiki}

A generalized coordinate absent from the <Lagrangian> is cyclic. Its conjugate <generalized momentum> is conserved by the <Euler-Lagrange equations>.

= Action
{title2=$S$}
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}
{wiki=Action_(physics)}

= Action functional
{synonym}

The action of a path is the time integral of its <Lagrangian>. The <principle of stationary action> requires its first variation to vanish for every admissible fixed-endpoint variation.

= Principle of stationary action
{parent=Action}
{wiki}

The principle of stationary action says that the physical path between fixed endpoints is a <stationary point> of the <action>.

= Total-time-derivative invariance of a Lagrangian
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

Replacing a Lagrangian by
$$
L'(q,\dot q,t)=L(q,\dot q,t)+\frac d{dt}F(q,t)
$$
adds only the endpoint term $F(q(t_2),t_2)-F(q(t_1),t_1)$ to the action. Fixed-endpoint variations therefore give the same Euler--Lagrange equations.

= Charged-particle electromagnetic Lagrangian
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

For electromagnetic scalar and vector potentials $\phi$ and $A$,
$$
L=\frac12m\dot r^2-q\phi+q\dot r\cdot A.
$$
With
$$
E=-\nabla\phi-\partial_tA,
\qquad
B=\nabla\times A,
$$
the Euler--Lagrange equations give the Lorentz force $m\ddot r=q(E+\dot r\times B)$, while the canonical momentum is $p=m\dot r+qA$.

= Hamiltonian of a charged particle in an electromagnetic field
{parent=Charged-particle electromagnetic Lagrangian}

The <Legendre transform> of the <charged-particle electromagnetic Lagrangian> is
$$
H(\mathbf x,\mathbf p,t)=\frac{[\mathbf p-q\mathbf A(\mathbf x,t)]^2}{2m}+q\phi(\mathbf x,t).
$$

= Gauge variation of the charged-particle Lagrangian
{parent=Charged-particle electromagnetic Lagrangian}

Under $\phi\mapsto\phi-\partial_tf$ and $A\mapsto A+\nabla f$, the charged-particle Lagrangian changes by
$$
L' - L=q\bigl(\partial_tf+\dot r\cdot\nabla f\bigr)
=q\frac{df(r(t),t)}{dt}.
$$
It therefore has unchanged equations of motion by total-time-derivative invariance.

= Canonical momentum parallel to a uniform magnetic field
{parent=Charged-particle electromagnetic Lagrangian}

For a constant uniform magnetic field $B$, choose the symmetric gauge $A=\tfrac12B\times r$. The coordinate parallel to $B$ is cyclic, so its canonical momentum is conserved. Since $A\cdot B=0$, this component is also the mechanical momentum $m\dot r\cdot\widehat B$.

= Generalized momentum
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

The generalized, or conjugate, momentum associated with a coordinate $q_i$ is
$$
p_i=\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q_i}.
$$

In <Lagrangian mechanics>, the coordinate variable describes the configuration; its conjugate momentum need not equal the Cartesian mechanical momentum.

= Ignorable coordinate
{parent=Generalized momentum}

A coordinate $q_i$ is ignorable, or cyclic, when $\partial L/\partial q_i=0$. The Euler--Lagrange equation then gives $\dot p_i=0$, so its generalized momentum is conserved.

= Conserved quantities from cyclic coordinates
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

If a Lagrangian is independent of a coordinate, the conjugate momentum to that coordinate is conserved by the Euler--Lagrange equations.

= Axially symmetric isotropic-mass Lagrangian
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

If $L=|\dot q|^2/2-V(q)$ and $V$ depends only on $|q|^2$ and $(q\cdot n)^2$, then rotations about the axis $n$ leave $L$ invariant. The quantity supplied by <Noether theorem> is the axial angular momentum
$$
J_n=n\cdot(q\times\dot q).
$$
If $L$ also has no explicit time dependence, the energy $|\dot q|^2/2+V(q)$ is conserved independently.

= Double pendulum
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}
{wiki}

A double pendulum consists of two pendula coupled because the second pivot moves with the first mass.

= Double pendulum Lagrangian
{parent=Double pendulum}

The kinetic energy contains the coupling $ml_1l_2\cos(\theta_1-\theta_2)\dot\theta_1\dot\theta_2$, while gravity supplies one cosine potential for each mass height.

= Double pendulum equations of motion
{parent=Double pendulum}

The nonlinear Euler--Lagrange equations couple both angular accelerations and contain centrifugal terms quadratic in angular velocity.

= Kinetic energy from Cartesian position vectors
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

For point masses with generalized coordinates, kinetic energy follows by differentiating their Cartesian position vectors and summing $m_i|\dot r_i|^2/2$.

= Small oscillation
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

Near a stable equilibrium, retaining quadratic Lagrangian terms gives a linear system $\mathsf M\ddot z+\mathsf Kz=0$.

The resulting linearized motion decomposes into <normal modes> near the stable equilibrium.

= Stable equilibrium in Lagrangian mechanics
{parent=Small oscillation}

At an equilibrium, a positive-definite <Hessian matrix> of the potential gives stable <small oscillations>. If a continuous symmetry supplies zero modes, positive definiteness on the directions transverse to the symmetry gives stability of the relative configuration.

= Angular frequency
{parent=Small oscillation}
{wiki}

Angular frequency measures phase advance per unit time. For $x(t)=A\cos(\omega t+\varphi)$ it is $\omega=2\pi/T$, where $T$ is the period.

= Two vertically suspended springs
{parent=Small oscillation}

For two masses hung in series from springs of natural length $\ell$, the lower equilibrium spring tension is $m_2g$ and the upper tension is $(m_1+m_2)g$. Hence their equilibrium lengths are
$$
L_2=\ell+\frac{m_2g}{k_2},
\qquad
L_1=\ell+\frac{(m_1+m_2)g}{k_1}.
$$

= Vertical normal modes of two equal suspended masses
{parent=Two vertically suspended springs}

For equal masses $m$ and spring constants $k$, vertical displacements from equilibrium have stiffness and mass matrices
$$
K=k\begin{pmatrix}2&-1\\-1&1\end{pmatrix},
\qquad M=mI.
$$
The two squared frequencies and displacement ratios are
$$
\omega_\pm^2=\frac{k}{2m}(3\pm\sqrt5),
\qquad
\frac{\eta_2}{\eta_1}=\frac{1\mp\sqrt5}{2}.
$$
Gravity shifts the equilibrium but does not enter these vertical frequencies.

= Small-oscillation mass and stiffness matrices
{parent=Small oscillation}

The Hessians of kinetic and potential energy at equilibrium give the symmetric mass matrix $\mathsf M$ and stiffness matrix $\mathsf K$.

= Generalized eigenvalue problem for small oscillations
{parent=Small oscillation}

Normal frequencies satisfy $\det(\mathsf K-\Omega^2\mathsf M)=0$, and each null vector gives the fixed amplitude ratio of a mode.

= Mass-matrix orthogonality of normal modes
{parent=Generalized eigenvalue problem for small oscillations}

For symmetric stiffness matrix $K$ and positive-definite mass matrix $M$, eigenvectors $a_i,a_j$ of
$$
Ka=\omega^2Ma
$$
with distinct squared frequencies satisfy
$$
a_i^TMa_j=0.
$$
Thus normal modes are orthogonal in the kinetic-energy inner product.

= Normal modes of a double pendulum
{parent=Generalized eigenvalue problem for small oscillations}

For equal lengths and mass ratio $\mu=m/M$, the squared frequencies are $\omega_0^2(1+\mu\pm\sqrt{\mu(1+\mu)})$.

= Square-root splitting of nearly degenerate frequencies
{parent=Generalized eigenvalue problem for small oscillations}

A weakly coupled degenerate pair can split by order $\sqrt\mu$ when the small parameter enters a singular mass or stiffness matrix.

= Degenerate normal modes from permutation symmetry
{parent=Generalized eigenvalue problem for small oscillations}

If the mass and stiffness matrices commute with a <permutation representation>, each symmetry subspace is invariant under the <generalized eigenvalue problem for small oscillations>. On an irreducible subspace, <Schur lemma> forces a commuting operator to be scalar, producing a degenerate <normal mode> multiplet.

= Normal modes of three equal masses with a symmetric gap potential
{parent=Degenerate normal modes from permutation symmetry}

Let three equal masses move on a circle of radius $r$, and let the potential be $U(\alpha)+U(\beta)+U(\gamma)$ with $\alpha+\beta+\gamma=2\pi$. At equal spacing $s_0=2\pi/3$, angular displacements have a zero-frequency rigid-rotation mode $(1,1,1)$ and a two-dimensional sum-zero eigenspace with
$$
\omega^2=\frac{3U''(s_0)}{mr^2}.
$$
The two vibrational modes are degenerate by the permutation symmetry.

= Normal modes of three symmetrically coupled trapped masses
{parent=Degenerate normal modes from permutation symmetry}

For three unit masses with unit springs between every pair and one unit spring anchoring each mass to the origin,
$$
M=I,\qquad K=4I-J,
$$
where $J$ is the all-ones matrix. The collective mode $(1,1,1)$ has angular frequency $1$, while the two-dimensional sum-zero subspace has angular frequency $2$.

= Normal modes of two equal masses between three springs
{parent=Generalized eigenvalue problem for small oscillations}

For equal masses $m$, wall-spring constants $k_1,k_2$, and coupling-spring constant $k_3$, the <small-oscillation mass and stiffness matrices> are
$$
\mathsf M=mI,
\qquad
\mathsf K=
\begin{pmatrix}
k_1+k_3&-k_3\\
-k_3&k_2+k_3
\end{pmatrix}.
$$
If $k_1=k(1+\varepsilon\delta)$, $k_2=k(1-\varepsilon\delta)$, and $k_3=k\varepsilon$, the squared normal frequencies are
$$
\omega_\pm^2=\frac{k}{m}
\left[1+\varepsilon\left(1\pm\sqrt{1+\delta^2}\right)\right].
$$

= Conservation of energy from time-translation invariance
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

A Lagrangian with no explicit time dependence conserves $E=\sum_i\dot q_i\,\partial L/\partial\dot q_i-L$.

= Center of mass
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

= Centres of mass
{synonym}

= Centre of mass
{synonym}

For masses $m_i$ at positions $\mathbf r_i$, the center of mass is $\mathbf R=(\sum_i m_i\mathbf r_i)/(\sum_i m_i)$, with the analogous mass-density integral for a continuous body.

= Barycenter
{disambiguate=astronomy}
{parent=Center of mass}
{wiki}

= Astronomical barycenter
{synonym}

The <centre of mass> of an astronomical system. In an isolated <two-body problem>, both bodies follow <orbits> around their shared astronomical barycenter; the <Kepler orbit> of their relative displacement has a <semi-major axis> equal to the sum of their individual <semi-major axes>.

= Centre of mass after removing material
{parent=Center of mass}
{title2=$\mathbf R=(M_0\mathbf R_0-M_c\mathbf R_c)/(M_0-M_c)$}

Suppose a body of <mass> $M_0$ has <centre of mass> $\mathbf R_0$, and material of <mass> $M_c<M_0$ with <centre of mass> $\mathbf R_c$ is removed. Additivity of the first <mass> moment gives the remaining <centre of mass>
$$
\mathbf R=\frac{M_0\mathbf R_0-M_c\mathbf R_c}{M_0-M_c}.
$$
When the original <centre of mass> is at the origin, the remaining <centre of mass> points opposite the removed material's first <mass> moment. For a constrained <rigid body> rotating about a fixed axis, this displaced <centre of mass> undergoes <centripetal acceleration> even if the applied axial <torque> is zero.

= Center-of-mass motion
{parent=Center of mass}

The center of mass moves as if the total external force acted on the total mass; internal forces cancel in its equation.

= Center-of-mass reduction
{parent=Center-of-mass motion}

Center-of-mass reduction separates translational motion from internal coordinates by using the conserved or externally forced center coordinate.

= Kinetic-energy decomposition about the centre of mass
{parent=Center-of-mass reduction}
{title2=$T=\tfrac12M|\dot{\mathbf R}|^2+\tfrac12\sum_i m_i|\dot{\mathbf y}_i|^2$}

For fixed particle <masses> and positive total <mass>, write $\mathbf x_i=\mathbf R+\mathbf y_i$ with $\mathbf R$ the <centre of mass>. The weighted relative <velocities> sum to zero, so expanding the <kinetic energy> eliminates the mixed term and yields the displayed decomposition. For a <rigid body>, the relative contribution is the <rotational kinetic energy> $I\omega^2/2$ about the chosen centre-of-mass axis.

= Jacobi coordinates
{c}
{parent=Center-of-mass reduction}
{wiki}

For particles with masses $m_j$ and positions $r_j$, <Jacobi coordinates> consist of the total <center of mass> and successive relative coordinates $\rho_j=r_{j+1}-(\sum_{l=1}^j m_l r_l)/(\sum_{l=1}^j m_l)$. They separate translation from internal motion and diagonalize the <kinetic energy> with the corresponding reduced masses.

= Jacobi coordinates for three particles
{c}
{parent=Jacobi coordinates}

For three particles, choose the relative position of particles one and two, the position of their center of mass relative to particle three, and the total center of mass. The kinetic energy diagonalizes with masses
$$
\frac{m_1m_2}{m_1+m_2},
\qquad
\frac{(m_1+m_2)m_3}{m_1+m_2+m_3},
\qquad
m_1+m_2+m_3.
$$

= Angular momentum decomposition in three-body Jacobi coordinates
{parent=Jacobi coordinates for three particles}

For Jacobi coordinates $\mathbf a,\mathbf b,\mathbf c$ and their corresponding reduced masses $\alpha,\beta,\gamma$, total angular momentum is
$$
\mathbf L
=\alpha\mathbf a\times\dot{\mathbf a}
+\beta\mathbf b\times\dot{\mathbf b}
+\gamma\mathbf c\times\dot{\mathbf c}.
$$

= Collective rotation and gap coordinates on a circle
{title2=$\Phi$}
{parent=Center-of-mass reduction}

For three ordered angular positions $q_1,q_2,q_3$, define consecutive gaps
$$
\alpha=q_2-q_1,
\qquad
\beta=q_3-q_2,
\qquad
\gamma=2\pi-q_3+q_1
$$
and the collective angle $\Phi=(q_1+q_2+q_3)/3$. Then $\alpha+\beta+\gamma=2\pi$. For three equal masses on a circle of radius $r$,
$$
T=\frac{3mr^2}{2}\dot\Phi^2
+\frac{mr^2}{3}
\left(\dot\alpha^2+\dot\alpha\dot\beta+\dot\beta^2\right).
$$
Thus the <ignorable coordinate> $\Phi$ decouples from the relative gap dynamics whenever the potential depends only on the gaps.

= Reduced mass
{title2=$\mu$}
{parent=Center of mass}
{wiki}

The <reduced mass> is the effective <mass> for the <two-particle relative coordinate> of two bodies with constant positive <masses> $m_1,m_2$:
$$
\mu=\frac{m_1m_2}{m_1+m_2},\qquad \frac1\mu=\frac1{m_1}+\frac1{m_2}.
$$
Both bodies can respond to their mutual <force>, so their relative <acceleration> is greater than if one were fixed. Consequently $0<\mu<\min(m_1,m_2)$. This explains why using either individual <mass>, or their sum, generally gives the wrong internal <oscillation> rate. The same <reduced mass> describes a <two-body problem>, a <free two-mass spring>, and the approximate <molecular vibration> of a <diatomic molecule>.<Newtonian Dynamics by Richard Fitzpatrick>[Reduced Mass, equations 322–328: equal-opposite forces, relative acceleration and reduced mass]{ref}

For equal <masses> $m_1=m_2=m$, $\mu=m/2$. Two $1\,\mathrm{kg}$ bodies therefore have $\mu=0.5\,\mathrm{kg}$: in their stationary <centre of mass> frame a relative <speed> $2\,\mathrm{m/s}$ means individual <speeds> $1\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and total <kinetic energy> $1\,\mathrm J$. With $m_1=1\,\mathrm{kg}$ and $m_2=100\,\mathrm{kg}$, instead $\mu=100/101\,\mathrm{kg}\approx0.990099\,\mathrm{kg}$. At the same relative <speed>, the individual <speeds> are $200/101$ and $2/101\,\mathrm{m/s}$, and the total <kinetic energy> is $200/101\,\mathrm J$. These are calculations for stipulated <masses> and <velocities>.

For fixed $m_1$ and $q=m_2/m_1>0$, $\mu/m_1=q/(1+q)$. The heavy-partner limit $q\to\infty$ gives $\mu\to m_1$ from below; the relative error incurred by replacing $\mu$ with $m_1$ is $(m_1-\mu)/\mu=1/q$. At $q=100$ this is $1\%$. Treating the heavy body as fixed is thus a controlled approximation to internal motion, rather than an exact consequence of its being heavier.

= Two-particle relative coordinate
{parent=Reduced mass}
{title2=$\mathbf r=\mathbf r_2-\mathbf r_1$}

Choose <positions> in one <inertial frame> and let $M=m_1+m_2$. The <centre of mass> and <two-particle relative coordinate> are
$$
\mathbf R=\frac{m_1\mathbf r_1+m_2\mathbf r_2}{M},\qquad \mathbf r=\mathbf r_2-\mathbf r_1,\qquad
\mathbf r_1=\mathbf R-\frac{m_2}{M}\mathbf r,\quad \mathbf r_2=\mathbf R+\frac{m_1}{M}\mathbf r.
$$
The lighter body is farther from the <centre of mass>. Reconstructing both <positions> from these equations is part of solving the <two-body problem>; $\mathbf r$ alone describes their separation, not either body's laboratory <position>.<Newtonian Dynamics by Richard Fitzpatrick>[Reduced Mass, equations 324–326: centre and reconstruction of both particle positions]{ref}

\Image[reduced-mass-center-of-mass-relative-motion.png]
{id=image-reduced-mass-center-of-mass-relative-motion}
{title=<Centre of mass> translation and internal <two-particle relative coordinate> for a pair with mass ratio three}
{description=Original illustrative <free two-mass spring> model, with $m_2=3m_1$, equilibrium separation $\ell$, relative extension $s/\ell=0.3\cos\tau$, $\tau=\omega t$, and $R/\ell=0.08\tau$. The right plot subtracts both the translating <centre of mass> and each body's equilibrium offset. It shows internal <displacements>, not full distances from the <centre of mass>.}

Substitute the reconstructed <velocities> into the <kinetic energy>. The result is the exact two-particle instance of <kinetic-energy decomposition about the centre of mass>:
$$
T=\frac12m_1|\dot{\mathbf r}_1|^2+\frac12m_2|\dot{\mathbf r}_2|^2
=\frac12M|\dot{\mathbf R}|^2+\frac12\mu|\dot{\mathbf r}|^2.
$$
The mixed <dot product> has coefficient $-m_1m_2/M+m_2m_1/M=0$; the coefficient of $|\dot{\mathbf r}|^2$ is $(m_1m_2^2+m_2m_1^2)/M^2=\mu$. This algebra needs constant positive <masses> but no particular <force> law. In the stationary <centre of mass> frame, the fraction of internal <kinetic energy> carried by body 1 is $m_2/M$, and by body 2 is $m_1/M$: the lighter body carries more.

For a closed pair with no external <forces>, write $\mathbf F$ for the <force> on body 2 and $-\mathbf F$ for that on body 1, as required by <Newton's third law>. Adding and subtracting their <Newton's second law> equations yields
$$
M\ddot{\mathbf R}=\mathbf0,\qquad
\ddot{\mathbf r}=\left(\frac1{m_2}+\frac1{m_1}\right)\mathbf F,\qquad
\mu\ddot{\mathbf r}=\mathbf F.
$$
Thus the <centre of mass> moves uniformly while the <two-particle relative coordinate> behaves like one body of <reduced mass>. Equal-opposite internal <forces> suffice for these equations. For an internal <central force>, $\mathbf F=f(r)\widehat{\mathbf r}$ with $r=|\mathbf r|>0$, the internal <torque> vanishes and <conservation of angular momentum> gives constant $\mathbf L_{\rm rel}=\mu\mathbf r\times\dot{\mathbf r}$. If the <central force> comes from a time-independent <potential energy> $U(r)$, then $f(r)=-dU/dr$ and the internal <mechanical energy> $E_{\rm rel}=\mu|\dot{\mathbf r}|^2/2+U(r)$ is conserved. The <kinetic energy> split alone does not establish either conservation law.

= Free two-mass spring
{parent=Reduced mass}
{title2=$\omega=\sqrt{k/\mu}$}

Take two <point masses> joined by a massless <spring> of <spring constant> $k>0$ and equilibrium length $\ell>0$. Assume one-dimensional motion along the <spring>, no external <force>, no <damping>, and an extension $s=x_2-x_1-\ell$ satisfying <Hooke's law>; keep $x_2>x_1$. For a real <spring> this is a model within its elastic range. Its <potential energy> is $U=ks^2/2$, so
$$
\mu\ddot s=-ks,\qquad s(t)=A\cos(\omega t+\phi),\qquad
\omega=\sqrt{\frac{k}{\mu}}=\sqrt{k\left(\frac1{m_1}+\frac1{m_2}\right)},\qquad \nu=\frac\omega{2\pi}.
$$
Here $\omega$ is <angular frequency> and $\nu$ is <frequency>. Choose $|A|<\ell$ so the model bodies do not cross. The internal <normal mode> has <displacements> $u_1=-m_2s/M$ and $u_2=m_1s/M$ about equilibrium offsets from the <centre of mass>; its <amplitude> ratio is $|u_1|/|u_2|=m_2/m_1$. The other <normal mode> is collective translation with zero <frequency> and no <spring> extension. This free pair has different boundary conditions from <normal modes of two equal masses between three springs>.

For $k=10\,\mathrm{N/m}$ and two $1\,\mathrm{kg}$ <masses>, $\omega=\sqrt{20}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}\approx4.47214\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ and $\nu\approx0.711763\,\mathrm{Hz}$. Replacing the second <mass> by $100\,\mathrm{kg}$ gives $\omega=\sqrt{10.1}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}\approx3.17805\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ and $\nu\approx0.505802\,\mathrm{Hz}$. Fixing the second endpoint instead would give $\sqrt{10}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$: the free heavy partner still moves. The free pair's <angular frequency> exceeds that fixed-end limit by the factor $\sqrt{1+1/q}$, about $1.004988$ at $q=100$.

\Image[reduced-mass-ratio-spring-frequency.png]
{id=image-reduced-mass-ratio-spring-frequency}
{title=<Reduced mass> and <free two-mass spring> <angular frequency> as the partner becomes heavier}
{description=Original dimensionless model with $m_1$ and $k$ held fixed, $q=m_2/m_1>0$, $\mu/m_1=q/(1+q)$ and $\omega/\sqrt{k/m_1}=\sqrt{1+1/q}$. The marks at $q=1$ and $q=100$ correspond to the computed examples; curves are predictions, not measured data.}

= Isotope scaling of diatomic vibration
{parent=Reduced mass}
{title2=$\nu'/\nu\simeq\sqrt{\mu/\mu'}$}

Near a stable equilibrium <bond length> $r_e$, a <Taylor expansion> of a <diatomic molecule>'s <potential energy> gives $U(r)\simeq U(r_e)+k(r-r_e)^2/2$, with $k=U''(r_e)>0$. Treat the internal stretch as a <harmonic oscillator> of <reduced mass>, initially omitting <molecular rotation>. If replacing an <isotope> leaves this local <potential energy> curvature unchanged, then
$$
\frac{\nu'}\nu\simeq\sqrt{\frac\mu{\mu'}},\qquad
\widetilde\nu_{\rm harm}=\frac1{2\pi c}\sqrt{\frac{k}{\mu}}.
$$
Here $c$ is the <speed of light> and $\widetilde\nu_{\rm harm}$ a <spectroscopic wavenumber>; use consistent length units for $c$ and $\widetilde\nu_{\rm harm}$. The <quantum harmonic oscillator> gives adjacent <energy eigenvalues> separated by $h\nu$, with $h$ the <Planck constant>. Consequently a vibrational <photon absorption> can probe the same <frequency> scale, subject to <electric-dipole selection rules>.

For an explicitly approximate <isotope> example, assign the <atomic masses> of $^{1}\mathrm H$, $^{2}\mathrm H$, $^{35}\mathrm{Cl}$, and $^{37}\mathrm{Cl}$ the mass-number values $1u,2u,35u,37u$, respectively, with $u$ the <unified atomic mass unit>. These are stipulated approximations to individual <atomic masses>, not measured <masses> of <molecules> or natural-abundance averages. They give
$$
\mu_{\mathrm H^{35}\mathrm{Cl}}=\frac{35}{36}u,\quad
\mu_{\mathrm D^{35}\mathrm{Cl}}=\frac{70}{37}u,\quad
\frac{\nu_{\mathrm D^{35}\mathrm{Cl}}}{\nu_{\mathrm H^{35}\mathrm{Cl}}}\simeq\sqrt{\frac{37}{72}}\approx0.716860,
\qquad
\frac{\nu_{\mathrm H^{37}\mathrm{Cl}}}{\nu_{\mathrm H^{35}\mathrm{Cl}}}\simeq\sqrt{\frac{665}{666}}\approx0.999249.
$$
The symbol $\mathrm D$ means <deuterium>, $^{2}\mathrm H$. Doubling the light partner's approximate <atomic mass> therefore produces a large predicted shift, whereas changing the already heavy <chlorine> partner by two mass units produces a much smaller one. A quantitative comparison with <infrared spectroscopy> requires actual <isotope>-specific <atomic masses>, fitted band parameters, and uncertainties, rather than identifying any arbitrary peak with $\widetilde\nu_{\rm harm}$.

= HCl and DCl vibrational isotope experiment
{c}
{parent=Isotope scaling of diatomic vibration}

An actual <infrared spectroscopy> route is to compare gaseous <hydrogen chloride> containing ordinary <hydrogen> with its <deuterium> counterpart. A documented teaching experiment uses a stopcock-equipped gas cell with potassium bromide windows and a Nicolet Avatar instrument for <Fourier transform spectroscopy>. A nitrogen-purged flask and drying tube supply the mixture; the sample also contains sulfur dioxide. An empty sample-chamber background precedes the filled-cell scan. The <absorbance> trace and peak-picked <spectroscopic wavenumbers> are the readout. The analysis assigns and indexes $^{1}\mathrm H^{35}\mathrm{Cl}$ and $^{2}\mathrm H^{35}\mathrm{Cl}$ transitions separately and propagates transition uncertainties. The instructions distinguish resolvable <chlorine> <isotope> peaks from adequate precision for the intended parameter analysis. <Vibrational-Rotational Spectroscopy of HCl by James M. McCormick>[McCormick, Introduction; Experimental, Laboratory Component, Figures 1–2 and background/peak-picking paragraphs; Analysis and Discussion, first three paragraphs]{ref}

A separate published teaching dataset reports a Nicolet Nexus 670 instrument, a $10\,\mathrm{cm}$ gas cell at $20\,\mathrm{Torr}$, and <chlorine> <isotope> peaks resolved to baseline. These apparatus details describe that dataset, not the preceding experiment. <HCl and DCl IR absorption spectra by Jonathan Gutow>[Gutow, HCl and DCl IR absorption spectra, dataset description: instrument, path length, pressure and resolved chlorine isotope peaks]{ref}

The inference connects several models. Fit correctly assigned transitions to separate <molecular rotation> from <molecular vibration>, then compare the inferred vibrational scales using <isotope scaling of diatomic vibration>. Agreement supports the approximate separation between a nearly unchanged <chemical bond> stiffness and a changed nuclear <mass>. It does not make a real <molecule> an exact classical <spring>: <anharmonicity> shifts the fundamental transition away from the local <harmonic oscillator> scale, and coupling to <molecular rotation> shifts individual lines.

For an independent test, calculate the <reduced masses> from the specified <isotopes> and compare the inferred values $k=\mu(2\pi c\widetilde\nu_{\rm harm})^2$. Agreement within uncertainty tests the common-curvature assumption; choosing separate $k$ values just to fit each <frequency> would not test it. The documented background addresses instrument and ambient contributions, but does not by itself subtract window absorption, identify contaminating species, or calibrate the <spectroscopic wavenumber> scale. Those require separate controls. Finite spectral resolution, blended peaks, incorrect <isotope> assignments, and departure from the <harmonic approximation near a potential minimum> limit the comparison. No numerical measured line positions are assumed here.

= Uniform gravitational field
{parent=Classical mechanics}

In a uniform gravitational field, every freely falling center of mass has constant acceleration $-g$.

= Normal coordinate
{parent=Classical mechanics}

A normal coordinate diagonalizes the linearized kinetic and potential forms so that its motion decouples as a harmonic oscillator.

= Antisymmetric molecular vibration
{parent=Normal coordinate}

In an antisymmetric vibration of a symmetric linear triatomic molecule, the outer atoms move oppositely and the central atom does not couple to that mode.

= Virtual work
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

= Principle of virtual work
{synonym}

Virtual work is the work of applied forces through an infinitesimal displacement compatible with the system's instantaneous constraints. Equilibrium or overdamped force balance requires the total virtual work to vanish for every admissible virtual displacement.
